window.IELTS_DATA.items.push(...[{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Game shows and whether adverts get noticed","prompt":"Do you think there are too many game shows on TV nowadays and why?\nDo you think that people pay attention to adverts on TV and why?","answer":"Q: Do you think there are too many game shows on TV nowadays and why?\nA: Yes, there are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far too many</strong> game shows on TV for my liking. I suppose the channels show these programmes because they&#x27;re popular, and they must be cheap to make compared with drama, which is why they&#x27;ve <strong class=\"key-phrase\">multiplied so quickly</strong>. A good example is &#x27;Who Wants to Be a Millionaire?&#x27;, which has been sold to broadcasters across the world and repeated in dozens of local versions. Personally, I&#x27;m not a fan, and I&#x27;d much rather watch a film or an original drama series. That said, I&#x27;d make an exception for the handful that test real knowledge rather than luck, since at least those <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reward something more than guessing</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you think that people pay attention to adverts on TV and why?\nA: Yes, I think we&#x27;re all influenced <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to some extent</strong> by television advertising; otherwise companies wouldn&#x27;t spend so much money on it. Advertisers create memorable images and link products with desirable lifestyles, so a brand starts to feel familiar before you&#x27;ve even used it. Even when viewers don&#x27;t buy immediately, an advert can plant a seed and affect a later decision — that&#x27;s the whole point of a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long-running campaign</strong>. How closely people pay attention obviously varies, though, and most of us skip adverts whenever we can, which is exactly why advertisers have moved towards sponsorship and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">product placement</strong>, since you can&#x27;t skip what&#x27;s built into the programme itself.","topic":"Advertising","phrases":[{"en":"far too many","context":"Q: Do you think there are too many game shows on TV nowadays and why?\nA: Yes, there are far too many game shows on TV for my liking. I suppose the channels show these programmes because they're popular, and they must be cheap to make compared with drama, which is why they've multiplied so quickly. A good example is 'Who Wants to Be a Millionaire?', which has been sold to broadcasters across the world and repeated in dozens of local versions. Personally, I'm not a fan, and I'd much rather watch a film or an original drama series. That said, I'd make an exception for the handful that test real knowledge rather than luck, since at least those reward something more than guessing."},{"en":"multiplied so quickly","context":"Q: Do you think there are too many game shows on TV nowadays and why?\nA: Yes, there are far too many game shows on TV for my liking. I suppose the channels show these programmes because they're popular, and they must be cheap to make compared with drama, which is why they've multiplied so quickly. A good example is 'Who Wants to Be a Millionaire?', which has been sold to broadcasters across the world and repeated in dozens of local versions. Personally, I'm not a fan, and I'd much rather watch a film or an original drama series. That said, I'd make an exception for the handful that test real knowledge rather than luck, since at least those reward something more than guessing."},{"en":"reward something more than guessing","context":"Q: Do you think there are too many game shows on TV nowadays and why?\nA: Yes, there are far too many game shows on TV for my liking. I suppose the channels show these programmes because they're popular, and they must be cheap to make compared with drama, which is why they've multiplied so quickly. A good example is 'Who Wants to Be a Millionaire?', which has been sold to broadcasters across the world and repeated in dozens of local versions. Personally, I'm not a fan, and I'd much rather watch a film or an original drama series. That said, I'd make an exception for the handful that test real knowledge rather than luck, since at least those reward something more than guessing."},{"en":"to some extent","context":"Q: Do you think that people pay attention to adverts on TV and why?\nA: Yes, I think we're all influenced to some extent by television advertising; otherwise companies wouldn't spend so much money on it. Advertisers create memorable images and link products with desirable lifestyles, so a brand starts to feel familiar before you've even used it. Even when viewers don't buy immediately, an advert can plant a seed and affect a later decision — that's the whole point of a long-running campaign. How closely people pay attention obviously varies, though, and most of us skip adverts whenever we can, which is exactly why advertisers have moved towards sponsorship and product placement, since you can't skip what's built into the programme itself."},{"en":"long-running campaign","context":"Q: Do you think that people pay attention to adverts on TV and why?\nA: Yes, I think we're all influenced to some extent by television advertising; otherwise companies wouldn't spend so much money on it. Advertisers create memorable images and link products with desirable lifestyles, so a brand starts to feel familiar before you've even used it. Even when viewers don't buy immediately, an advert can plant a seed and affect a later decision — that's the whole point of a long-running campaign. How closely people pay attention obviously varies, though, and most of us skip adverts whenever we can, which is exactly why advertisers have moved towards sponsorship and product placement, since you can't skip what's built into the programme itself."},{"en":"product placement","context":"Q: Do you think that people pay attention to adverts on TV and why?\nA: Yes, I think we're all influenced to some extent by television advertising; otherwise companies wouldn't spend so much money on it. Advertisers create memorable images and link products with desirable lifestyles, so a brand starts to feel familiar before you've even used it. Even when viewers don't buy immediately, an advert can plant a seed and affect a later decision — that's the whole point of a long-running campaign. How closely people pay attention obviously varies, though, and most of us skip adverts whenever we can, which is exactly why advertisers have moved towards sponsorship and product placement, since you can't skip what's built into the programme itself."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Advertising","id":"speaking-part-3-001","_displayNo":"#001","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-001-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-001-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, I think there are too many. Game shows are popular and cheaper to make than dramas, so channels keep producing similar versions. For example, Who Wants to Be a Millionaire? has appeared in many countries. I'd rather watch a film or a new drama, although I do enjoy some shows that test knowledge rather than depend mainly on luck.","phrases":[{"en":"depend mainly on luck","meaning":"Be decided mostly by chance rather than ability."}],"question":"Do you think there are too many game shows on TV nowadays and why?","html":"Yes, I think there are too many. Game shows are popular and cheaper to make than dramas, so channels keep producing similar versions. For example, Who Wants to Be a Millionaire? has appeared in many countries. I&#x27;d rather watch a film or a new drama, although I do enjoy some shows that test knowledge rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depend mainly on luck</strong>."},{"text":"Yes, at least to some degree. Adverts make products familiar and connect them with a lifestyle people want. We may not buy immediately, but remember the name later. Many viewers skip adverts, so companies also pay to show products inside programmes. That way, the message can reach people even when they are not actively watching an advert.","phrases":[{"en":"to some degree","meaning":"Partly, although not completely."}],"question":"Do you think that people pay attention to adverts on TV and why?","html":"Yes, at least <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to some degree</strong>. Adverts make products familiar and connect them with a lifestyle people want. We may not buy immediately, but remember the name later. Many viewers skip adverts, so companies also pay to show products inside programmes. That way, the message can reach people even when they are not actively watching an advert."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-001-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-001-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Whether adverts change what people buy","prompt":"Do you think advertisements influence what people buy?\nWhat criteria should be used to assess a good advertisement?","answer":"Q: Do you think advertisements influence what people buy?\nA: Yes, though not in the direct way people imagine. Very few adverts make somebody buy something immediately. What they do is build familiarity, so when you&#x27;re standing in front of two similar products, one <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feels known and therefore safer</strong>. That&#x27;s a subtle advantage but a valuable one, which is why brands spend so much simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeping their name in front of you</strong>. Advertising also attaches products to an image — a car sold as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">evidence of success</strong> rather than as transport — and that works even on people who insist it doesn&#x27;t. The exception is a genuinely new product with no reputation yet; there, word of mouth often does more than the advert itself.\n\nQ: What criteria should be used to assess a good advertisement?\nA: Whether it&#x27;s memorable is the first test, since an advert nobody recalls has failed regardless of how elegant it is. Clarity comes next: you should know what&#x27;s being sold and why it might matter to you. But the criterion I&#x27;d weight most heavily is truthfulness. An advertisement that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exaggerates may work once</strong>, and then the customer discovers the product doesn&#x27;t match the claim, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">damages the brand far more</strong> than the sale was worth. Occasionally a company gets away with mild exaggeration for years, so it isn&#x27;t an absolute rule — but it&#x27;s a risk that rarely pays off in the long run.","topic":"Advertising","phrases":[{"en":"feels known and therefore safer","context":"Q: Do you think advertisements influence what people buy?\nA: Yes, though not in the direct way people imagine. Very few adverts make somebody buy something immediately. What they do is build familiarity, so when you're standing in front of two similar products, one feels known and therefore safer. That's a subtle advantage but a valuable one, which is why brands spend so much simply keeping their name in front of you. Advertising also attaches products to an image — a car sold as evidence of success rather than as transport — and that works even on people who insist it doesn't. The exception is a genuinely new product with no reputation yet; there, word of mouth often does more than the advert itself."},{"en":"keeping their name in front of you","context":"Q: Do you think advertisements influence what people buy?\nA: Yes, though not in the direct way people imagine. Very few adverts make somebody buy something immediately. What they do is build familiarity, so when you're standing in front of two similar products, one feels known and therefore safer. That's a subtle advantage but a valuable one, which is why brands spend so much simply keeping their name in front of you. Advertising also attaches products to an image — a car sold as evidence of success rather than as transport — and that works even on people who insist it doesn't. The exception is a genuinely new product with no reputation yet; there, word of mouth often does more than the advert itself."},{"en":"evidence of success","context":"Q: Do you think advertisements influence what people buy?\nA: Yes, though not in the direct way people imagine. Very few adverts make somebody buy something immediately. What they do is build familiarity, so when you're standing in front of two similar products, one feels known and therefore safer. That's a subtle advantage but a valuable one, which is why brands spend so much simply keeping their name in front of you. Advertising also attaches products to an image — a car sold as evidence of success rather than as transport — and that works even on people who insist it doesn't. The exception is a genuinely new product with no reputation yet; there, word of mouth often does more than the advert itself."},{"en":"exaggerates may work once","context":"Q: What criteria should be used to assess a good advertisement?\nA: Whether it's memorable is the first test, since an advert nobody recalls has failed regardless of how elegant it is. Clarity comes next: you should know what's being sold and why it might matter to you. But the criterion I'd weight most heavily is truthfulness. An advertisement that exaggerates may work once, and then the customer discovers the product doesn't match the claim, which damages the brand far more than the sale was worth. Occasionally a company gets away with mild exaggeration for years, so it isn't an absolute rule — but it's a risk that rarely pays off in the long run."},{"en":"damages the brand far more","context":"Q: What criteria should be used to assess a good advertisement?\nA: Whether it's memorable is the first test, since an advert nobody recalls has failed regardless of how elegant it is. Clarity comes next: you should know what's being sold and why it might matter to you. But the criterion I'd weight most heavily is truthfulness. An advertisement that exaggerates may work once, and then the customer discovers the product doesn't match the claim, which damages the brand far more than the sale was worth. Occasionally a company gets away with mild exaggeration for years, so it isn't an absolute rule — but it's a risk that rarely pays off in the long run."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Advertising","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-002","_displayNo":"#002","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-002-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-002-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, but they usually work slowly. Seeing a brand repeatedly makes it familiar, so it may feel safer than another product when we shop. Adverts can also suggest that owning something shows success. However, for a completely new product, people may trust recommendations from friends more than the advert itself.","phrases":[{"en":"recommendations","meaning":"Suggestions about what someone should try or choose."}],"question":"Do you think advertisements influence what people buy?","html":"Yes, but they usually work slowly. Seeing a brand repeatedly makes it familiar, so it may feel safer than another product when we shop. Adverts can also suggest that owning something shows success. However, for a completely new product, people may trust <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recommendations</strong> from friends more than the advert itself."},{"text":"A good advert should be easy to remember and explain clearly what the product offers. Most importantly, it should be honest. An exciting promise may attract a buyer once, but people lose trust if the product disappoints them. Some exaggeration might succeed for a while, but it can damage the business in the long term.","phrases":[{"en":"in the long term","meaning":"Over a long period rather than immediately."}],"question":"What criteria should be used to assess a good advertisement?","html":"A good advert should be easy to remember and explain clearly what the product offers. Most importantly, it should be honest. An exciting promise may attract a buyer once, but people lose trust if the product disappoints them. Some exaggeration might succeed for a while, but it can damage the business <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the long term</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-002-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-002-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Popular forms of advertising, and working in the industry","prompt":"What's the most popular form of advertising in your country?\nWould you like to work for an advertising company?\nDo you agree that advertisements for smoking should be banned?","answer":"Q: What&#x27;s the most popular form of advertising in your country?\nA: Social media advertising, without much doubt, and short video particularly. It&#x27;s overtaken television because it&#x27;s far cheaper, it can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">targeted at very specific groups</strong>, and people share it themselves, which effectively makes the advertising free. Influencer marketing is a large part of it, where a recommendation looks like a personal opinion rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a paid promotion</strong>. That&#x27;s precisely what makes it effective, and also what makes a lot of people uncomfortable about it — you often can&#x27;t tell whether someone genuinely likes a product or was simply paid to say so.\n\nQ: Would you like to work for an advertising company?\nA: Possibly, depending on the clients. The work itself looks genuinely interesting — it combines research into how people think, writing and visual design, and you see results quickly. What would put me off is the type of product. I&#x27;d have no difficulty advertising something useful, but a good deal of the industry works by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">creating insecurity and then selling the remedy</strong> — skincare that promises to fix a flaw you didn&#x27;t know you had, for instance — and I don&#x27;t think I could keep doing that for long. That said, plenty of agencies work purely on brand image or public information campaigns, so it wouldn&#x27;t necessarily be every client.\n\nQ: Do you agree that advertisements for smoking should be banned?\nA: Yes, and the evidence supports it fairly clearly. Tobacco&#x27;s unusual in that there&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no safe level of use</strong>, and the public health costs fall on everybody through the health system. Advertising&#x27;s main effect was normalising it and recruiting new smokers, who are almost always teenagers. Countries that banned it saw rates fall within a few years. It does restrict commercial freedom, certainly, and I wouldn&#x27;t apply that argument to every product. But with tobacco the harm&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">severe enough to justify it</strong>.","topic":"Advertising","phrases":[{"en":"targeted at very specific groups","context":"Q: What's the most popular form of advertising in your country?\nA: Social media advertising, without much doubt, and short video particularly. It's overtaken television because it's far cheaper, it can be targeted at very specific groups, and people share it themselves, which effectively makes the advertising free. Influencer marketing is a large part of it, where a recommendation looks like a personal opinion rather than a paid promotion. That's precisely what makes it effective, and also what makes a lot of people uncomfortable about it — you often can't tell whether someone genuinely likes a product or was simply paid to say so."},{"en":"a paid promotion","context":"Q: What's the most popular form of advertising in your country?\nA: Social media advertising, without much doubt, and short video particularly. It's overtaken television because it's far cheaper, it can be targeted at very specific groups, and people share it themselves, which effectively makes the advertising free. Influencer marketing is a large part of it, where a recommendation looks like a personal opinion rather than a paid promotion. That's precisely what makes it effective, and also what makes a lot of people uncomfortable about it — you often can't tell whether someone genuinely likes a product or was simply paid to say so."},{"en":"creating insecurity and then selling the remedy","context":"Q: Would you like to work for an advertising company?\nA: Possibly, depending on the clients. The work itself looks genuinely interesting — it combines research into how people think, writing and visual design, and you see results quickly. What would put me off is the type of product. I'd have no difficulty advertising something useful, but a good deal of the industry works by creating insecurity and then selling the remedy — skincare that promises to fix a flaw you didn't know you had, for instance — and I don't think I could keep doing that for long. That said, plenty of agencies work purely on brand image or public information campaigns, so it wouldn't necessarily be every client."},{"en":"no safe level of use","context":"Q: Do you agree that advertisements for smoking should be banned?\nA: Yes, and the evidence supports it fairly clearly. Tobacco's unusual in that there's no safe level of use, and the public health costs fall on everybody through the health system. Advertising's main effect was normalising it and recruiting new smokers, who are almost always teenagers. Countries that banned it saw rates fall within a few years. It does restrict commercial freedom, certainly, and I wouldn't apply that argument to every product. But with tobacco the harm's severe enough to justify it."},{"en":"severe enough to justify it","context":"Q: Do you agree that advertisements for smoking should be banned?\nA: Yes, and the evidence supports it fairly clearly. Tobacco's unusual in that there's no safe level of use, and the public health costs fall on everybody through the health system. Advertising's main effect was normalising it and recruiting new smokers, who are almost always teenagers. Countries that banned it saw rates fall within a few years. It does restrict commercial freedom, certainly, and I wouldn't apply that argument to every product. But with tobacco the harm's severe enough to justify it."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Advertising","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-003","_displayNo":"#003","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-003-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-003-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-003-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I'd say social media adverts, especially short videos, are very common. They cost less than television adverts and can reach particular groups. People may also share them with friends. Influencers are important too, but their recommendations can be confusing because viewers may not know whether the opinion is honest or paid for.","phrases":[{"en":"particular groups","meaning":"Specific types of people with shared interests or characteristics."}],"question":"What's the most popular form of advertising in your country?","html":"I&#x27;d say social media adverts, especially short videos, are very common. They cost less than television adverts and can reach <strong class=\"key-phrase\">particular groups</strong>. People may also share them with friends. Influencers are important too, but their recommendations can be confusing because viewers may not know whether the opinion is honest or paid for."},{"text":"Possibly, if I believed in the products. The mix of research, writing and design sounds interesting, and you can see the results quickly. However, I would dislike making people feel bad about themselves just to sell something. Advertising useful products or sharing public information would feel more suitable for me.","phrases":[{"en":"believed in","meaning":"Trusted or thought something had real value."}],"question":"Would you like to work for an advertising company?","html":"Possibly, if I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">believed in</strong> the products. The mix of research, writing and design sounds interesting, and you can see the results quickly. However, I would dislike making people feel bad about themselves just to sell something. Advertising useful products or sharing public information would feel more suitable for me."},{"text":"Yes, I think they should be banned because smoking causes serious harm. Adverts can make it seem normal or attractive, especially to young people. The health costs also affect society, not only smokers. Although businesses normally need freedom to advertise, I think protecting people from this level of harm is more important in this case.","phrases":[{"en":"banned","meaning":"Officially not allowed."}],"question":"Do you agree that advertisements for smoking should be banned?","html":"Yes, I think they should be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">banned</strong> because smoking causes serious harm. Adverts can make it seem normal or attractive, especially to young people. The health costs also affect society, not only smokers. Although businesses normally need freedom to advertise, I think protecting people from this level of harm is more important in this case."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-003-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-003-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-003-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Advice from friends or from family","prompt":"Is it better to get advice from a friend or from a family member?\nWhat would you say are the characteristics of a good adviser?\nShould people make their own work and career decisions, or is it a good idea to ask for advice about this?","answer":"Q: Is it better to get advice from a friend or from a family member?\nA: I think it depends on the kind of advice that you need. Parents and grandparents probably have more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">life experience</strong> than a friend, so you might get a wiser, more considered answer from them. Friends, on the other hand, are less likely to get anxious if you go to them with a problem — I probably wouldn&#x27;t want to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">burden my parents with</strong> a financial worry, for instance, when a friend would just help me think it through calmly. It&#x27;s not a hard rule, though; some parents are far more relaxed than some friends, so it really comes down to the individual person, not the category they fall into.\n\nQ: What would you say are the characteristics of a good adviser?\nA: I&#x27;d say the most important quality is being <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a good listener</strong> — someone who takes the time to actually understand the situation instead of jumping straight to a solution. That matters because most people who ask for advice have already half-decided what they want to hear, so a good adviser needs to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ask the right questions</strong> rather than just handing out opinions. My aunt&#x27;s like this: she almost never tells you what to do, she just asks questions until you work it out yourself, which is far more useful than being told outright. The one limitation is that even a good adviser can only draw on their own experience, so it&#x27;s worth being wary of anyone who&#x27;s certain their solution fits every situation.\n\nQ: Should people make their own work and career decisions, or is it a good idea to ask for advice about this?\nA: I&#x27;d say it&#x27;s a combination of both. Most people benefit from discussing career choices with family, teachers or experienced colleagues, because someone outside the situation often notices risks or opportunities you&#x27;ve missed yourself. I know someone who almost turned down a job abroad because her parents were nervous about the move, and it turned out to be the best decision she&#x27;s made. That said, the final decision has to rest with the individual, who must <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take ultimate responsibility for the consequences</strong>. Advice is also worth weighing by where it comes from — parents often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prize security above everything</strong>, which is understandable but can just as easily steer someone away from a risk that was actually worth taking.","topic":"Advice","phrases":[{"en":"life experience","context":"Q: Is it better to get advice from a friend or from a family member?\nA: I think it depends on the kind of advice that you need. Parents and grandparents probably have more life experience than a friend, so you might get a wiser, more considered answer from them. Friends, on the other hand, are less likely to get anxious if you go to them with a problem — I probably wouldn't want to burden my parents with a financial worry, for instance, when a friend would just help me think it through calmly. It's not a hard rule, though; some parents are far more relaxed than some friends, so it really comes down to the individual person, not the category they fall into."},{"en":"burden my parents with","context":"Q: Is it better to get advice from a friend or from a family member?\nA: I think it depends on the kind of advice that you need. Parents and grandparents probably have more life experience than a friend, so you might get a wiser, more considered answer from them. Friends, on the other hand, are less likely to get anxious if you go to them with a problem — I probably wouldn't want to burden my parents with a financial worry, for instance, when a friend would just help me think it through calmly. It's not a hard rule, though; some parents are far more relaxed than some friends, so it really comes down to the individual person, not the category they fall into."},{"en":"a good listener","context":"Q: What would you say are the characteristics of a good adviser?\nA: I'd say the most important quality is being a good listener — someone who takes the time to actually understand the situation instead of jumping straight to a solution. That matters because most people who ask for advice have already half-decided what they want to hear, so a good adviser needs to ask the right questions rather than just handing out opinions. My aunt's like this: she almost never tells you what to do, she just asks questions until you work it out yourself, which is far more useful than being told outright. The one limitation is that even a good adviser can only draw on their own experience, so it's worth being wary of anyone who's certain their solution fits every situation."},{"en":"ask the right questions","context":"Q: What would you say are the characteristics of a good adviser?\nA: I'd say the most important quality is being a good listener — someone who takes the time to actually understand the situation instead of jumping straight to a solution. That matters because most people who ask for advice have already half-decided what they want to hear, so a good adviser needs to ask the right questions rather than just handing out opinions. My aunt's like this: she almost never tells you what to do, she just asks questions until you work it out yourself, which is far more useful than being told outright. The one limitation is that even a good adviser can only draw on their own experience, so it's worth being wary of anyone who's certain their solution fits every situation."},{"en":"take ultimate responsibility for the consequences","context":"Q: Should people make their own work and career decisions, or is it a good idea to ask for advice about this?\nA: I'd say it's a combination of both. Most people benefit from discussing career choices with family, teachers or experienced colleagues, because someone outside the situation often notices risks or opportunities you've missed yourself. I know someone who almost turned down a job abroad because her parents were nervous about the move, and it turned out to be the best decision she's made. That said, the final decision has to rest with the individual, who must take ultimate responsibility for the consequences. Advice is also worth weighing by where it comes from — parents often prize security above everything, which is understandable but can just as easily steer someone away from a risk that was actually worth taking."},{"en":"prize security above everything","context":"Q: Should people make their own work and career decisions, or is it a good idea to ask for advice about this?\nA: I'd say it's a combination of both. Most people benefit from discussing career choices with family, teachers or experienced colleagues, because someone outside the situation often notices risks or opportunities you've missed yourself. I know someone who almost turned down a job abroad because her parents were nervous about the move, and it turned out to be the best decision she's made. That said, the final decision has to rest with the individual, who must take ultimate responsibility for the consequences. Advice is also worth weighing by where it comes from — parents often prize security above everything, which is understandable but can just as easily steer someone away from a risk that was actually worth taking."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Advice","id":"speaking-part-3-004","_displayNo":"#004","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-004-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-004-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-004-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"It depends on the problem and the person. Older relatives may have useful experience, while a friend might discuss a worry more calmly without becoming too anxious. For example, I might discuss a money problem with a friend first. Still, some parents are very relaxed, so I would choose someone I trust rather than follow a fixed rule.","phrases":[{"en":"a fixed rule","meaning":"An instruction that is expected to apply in every situation."}],"question":"Is it better to get advice from a friend or from a family member?","html":"It depends on the problem and the person. Older relatives may have useful experience, while a friend might discuss a worry more calmly without becoming too anxious. For example, I might discuss a money problem with a friend first. Still, some parents are very relaxed, so I would choose someone I trust rather than follow <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fixed rule</strong>."},{"text":"A good adviser listens carefully before suggesting a solution. They ask questions to understand the situation and help you think for yourself. My aunt does this: she asks questions until I work out my own answer. They should also accept that their experience may not fit every problem, instead of acting certain about everything.","phrases":[{"en":"work out my own answer","meaning":"Find a solution by thinking for myself."}],"question":"What would you say are the characteristics of a good adviser?","html":"A good adviser listens carefully before suggesting a solution. They ask questions to understand the situation and help you think for yourself. My aunt does this: she asks questions until I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work out my own answer</strong>. They should also accept that their experience may not fit every problem, instead of acting certain about everything."},{"text":"People should ask for advice, but make the final choice themselves. Family or colleagues may notice a risk or opportunity they have missed. However, advice can reflect the adviser's priorities, such as wanting a secure job. The person choosing must consider that and take responsibility, because they will live with the result.","phrases":[{"en":"take responsibility","meaning":"Accept that you are answerable for a decision and its effects."}],"question":"Should people make their own work and career decisions, or is it a good idea to ask for advice about this?","html":"People should ask for advice, but make the final choice themselves. Family or colleagues may notice a risk or opportunity they have missed. However, advice can reflect the adviser&#x27;s priorities, such as wanting a secure job. The person choosing must consider that and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take responsibility</strong>, because they will live with the result."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-004-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-004-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-004-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Learning from other people's experience","prompt":"Does that person still give you advice?\nIs it helpful to learn from other people's experiences?\nWhat kind of advice do parents give to their children?","answer":"Q: Does that person still give you advice?\nA: Yes, though the nature of it&#x27;s changed completely. When I was younger I&#x27;d ask what to do and just expect an answer. Now I ask more for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">perspective rather than instructions</strong> — when I was deciding whether to change jobs last year, for example, I didn&#x27;t want to be told what to pick, I wanted to know what they&#x27;d be worried about, or what I might not be seeing. That&#x27;s more useful, because the decision stays mine. It doesn&#x27;t always work, though — sometimes I still just want someone to tell me the answer, and perspective isn&#x27;t much comfort when you&#x27;re exhausted and need a decision made quickly. But mostly they&#x27;ve adapted too, offering far less <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unsolicited advice</strong> than they used to.\n\nQ: Is it helpful to learn from other people&#x27;s experiences?\nA: Definitely, because otherwise everyone has to make every mistake personally, which is a slow and expensive way to learn. Somebody who&#x27;s already done what you&#x27;re attempting can warn you about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the predictable problems</strong> — a friend who&#x27;d already done a master&#x27;s abroad warned me about visa delays I&#x27;d never have thought to plan for. The limitation is that circumstances differ: advice that worked twenty years ago in a different economy might not transfer at all, and people tend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">over-generalise from their own case</strong>. So I treat experience as evidence rather than instruction.\n\nQ: What kind of advice do parents give to their children?\nA: Mostly about education, money, relationships and personal safety — the areas where a mistake has lasting consequences. What I&#x27;ve noticed is that good advice changes shape as children grow. With a young child it&#x27;s fairly direct instruction, because they can&#x27;t assess risk themselves — &#x27;don&#x27;t cross without looking,&#x27; not a discussion. With a teenager the same parent has to shift towards <strong class=\"key-phrase\">asking questions rather than issuing rules</strong>, otherwise it simply gets ignored. That shift doesn&#x27;t happen at the same pace for every family, though — some teenagers still want, and get, fairly direct guidance on the big decisions, like which subject to study.","topic":"Advice","phrases":[{"en":"perspective rather than instructions","context":"Q: Does that person still give you advice?\nA: Yes, though the nature of it's changed completely. When I was younger I'd ask what to do and just expect an answer. Now I ask more for perspective rather than instructions — when I was deciding whether to change jobs last year, for example, I didn't want to be told what to pick, I wanted to know what they'd be worried about, or what I might not be seeing. That's more useful, because the decision stays mine. It doesn't always work, though — sometimes I still just want someone to tell me the answer, and perspective isn't much comfort when you're exhausted and need a decision made quickly. But mostly they've adapted too, offering far less unsolicited advice than they used to."},{"en":"unsolicited advice","context":"Q: Does that person still give you advice?\nA: Yes, though the nature of it's changed completely. When I was younger I'd ask what to do and just expect an answer. Now I ask more for perspective rather than instructions — when I was deciding whether to change jobs last year, for example, I didn't want to be told what to pick, I wanted to know what they'd be worried about, or what I might not be seeing. That's more useful, because the decision stays mine. It doesn't always work, though — sometimes I still just want someone to tell me the answer, and perspective isn't much comfort when you're exhausted and need a decision made quickly. But mostly they've adapted too, offering far less unsolicited advice than they used to."},{"en":"the predictable problems","context":"Q: Is it helpful to learn from other people's experiences?\nA: Definitely, because otherwise everyone has to make every mistake personally, which is a slow and expensive way to learn. Somebody who's already done what you're attempting can warn you about the predictable problems — a friend who'd already done a master's abroad warned me about visa delays I'd never have thought to plan for. The limitation is that circumstances differ: advice that worked twenty years ago in a different economy might not transfer at all, and people tend to over-generalise from their own case. So I treat experience as evidence rather than instruction."},{"en":"over-generalise from their own case","context":"Q: Is it helpful to learn from other people's experiences?\nA: Definitely, because otherwise everyone has to make every mistake personally, which is a slow and expensive way to learn. Somebody who's already done what you're attempting can warn you about the predictable problems — a friend who'd already done a master's abroad warned me about visa delays I'd never have thought to plan for. The limitation is that circumstances differ: advice that worked twenty years ago in a different economy might not transfer at all, and people tend to over-generalise from their own case. So I treat experience as evidence rather than instruction."},{"en":"asking questions rather than issuing rules","context":"Q: What kind of advice do parents give to their children?\nA: Mostly about education, money, relationships and personal safety — the areas where a mistake has lasting consequences. What I've noticed is that good advice changes shape as children grow. With a young child it's fairly direct instruction, because they can't assess risk themselves — 'don't cross without looking,' not a discussion. With a teenager the same parent has to shift towards asking questions rather than issuing rules, otherwise it simply gets ignored. That shift doesn't happen at the same pace for every family, though — some teenagers still want, and get, fairly direct guidance on the big decisions, like which subject to study."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Advice","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-005","_displayNo":"#005","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-005-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-005-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-005-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, but I ask for a different kind of help now. When I was younger, I wanted instructions. Now I want another opinion about risks I might have missed, such as when I considered changing jobs. That leaves the choice with me. Sometimes I still want a quick answer, but usually discussion is more helpful.","phrases":[{"en":"another opinion","meaning":"A different person's view of a situation."}],"question":"Does that person still give you advice?","html":"Yes, but I ask for a different kind of help now. When I was younger, I wanted instructions. Now I want <strong class=\"key-phrase\">another opinion</strong> about risks I might have missed, such as when I considered changing jobs. That leaves the choice with me. Sometimes I still want a quick answer, but usually discussion is more helpful."},{"text":"Yes, it can save us from repeating mistakes. Someone who has already done something can warn us about problems; a friend studying abroad, for example, might explain visa delays. However, their situation may differ from ours. I would use their experience as useful information, rather than assume I must do exactly what they did.","phrases":[{"en":"assume","meaning":"Believe something is true without checking it fully."}],"question":"Is it helpful to learn from other people's experiences?","html":"Yes, it can save us from repeating mistakes. Someone who has already done something can warn us about problems; a friend studying abroad, for example, might explain visa delays. However, their situation may differ from ours. I would use their experience as useful information, rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assume</strong> I must do exactly what they did."},{"text":"Parents often advise children about school, money, relationships and safety because these affect their future. Young children need simple instructions, such as looking before crossing a road. As children grow, parents can explain choices and ask questions instead. The right approach depends on the child, since some teenagers still want direct help with important decisions.","phrases":[{"en":"direct help","meaning":"Clear guidance about what to do."}],"question":"What kind of advice do parents give to their children?","html":"Parents often advise children about school, money, relationships and safety because these affect their future. Young children need simple instructions, such as looking before crossing a road. As children grow, parents can explain choices and ask questions instead. The right approach depends on the child, since some teenagers still want <strong class=\"key-phrase\">direct help</strong> with important decisions."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-005-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-005-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-005-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How humans differ from animals, and what animals are for","prompt":"In what ways are humans different from other animals?\nWhat are the main roles of animals in your country?","answer":"Q: In what ways are humans different from other animals?\nA: The fundamental difference is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cumulative culture</strong>. Other animals learn, and some use tools, but each generation largely starts again, whereas humans pass down knowledge in a form that accumulates, so we build on what previous generations worked out — nobody has to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reinvent the wheel</strong> every generation. Complex language is what makes that possible. Long-term planning is the other major difference: humans will plant an orchard they won&#x27;t see fruit from, or save for a pension decades away, which almost no other species does <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in any meaningful sense</strong> — though a few animals, like some whales, do pass on narrow local traditions of their own, so the line isn&#x27;t quite absolute.\n\nQ: What are the main roles of animals in your country?\nA: Agriculture&#x27;s still the largest, providing meat, eggs, milk and fish, though mechanisation has removed most of the working roles that buffalo and horses once had. Then there&#x27;s the ecological function, which people rarely think about — pollination, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pest control</strong>, maintaining soil — and agriculture depends on it entirely. The fastest-growing role is companionship: urban families increasingly keep pets, and in smaller households the animal&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treated much like a family member</strong>. It&#x27;s a slightly uneven picture, though — people who&#x27;d never eat their own dog give barely a thought to the animals raised for their dinner, which shows how much the &quot;role&quot; of an animal depends on which category we&#x27;ve mentally put it in.","topic":"Animals","phrases":[{"en":"cumulative culture","context":"Q: In what ways are humans different from other animals?\nA: The fundamental difference is cumulative culture. Other animals learn, and some use tools, but each generation largely starts again, whereas humans pass down knowledge in a form that accumulates, so we build on what previous generations worked out — nobody has to reinvent the wheel every generation. Complex language is what makes that possible. Long-term planning is the other major difference: humans will plant an orchard they won't see fruit from, or save for a pension decades away, which almost no other species does in any meaningful sense — though a few animals, like some whales, do pass on narrow local traditions of their own, so the line isn't quite absolute."},{"en":"reinvent the wheel","context":"Q: In what ways are humans different from other animals?\nA: The fundamental difference is cumulative culture. Other animals learn, and some use tools, but each generation largely starts again, whereas humans pass down knowledge in a form that accumulates, so we build on what previous generations worked out — nobody has to reinvent the wheel every generation. Complex language is what makes that possible. Long-term planning is the other major difference: humans will plant an orchard they won't see fruit from, or save for a pension decades away, which almost no other species does in any meaningful sense — though a few animals, like some whales, do pass on narrow local traditions of their own, so the line isn't quite absolute."},{"en":"in any meaningful sense","context":"Q: In what ways are humans different from other animals?\nA: The fundamental difference is cumulative culture. Other animals learn, and some use tools, but each generation largely starts again, whereas humans pass down knowledge in a form that accumulates, so we build on what previous generations worked out — nobody has to reinvent the wheel every generation. Complex language is what makes that possible. Long-term planning is the other major difference: humans will plant an orchard they won't see fruit from, or save for a pension decades away, which almost no other species does in any meaningful sense — though a few animals, like some whales, do pass on narrow local traditions of their own, so the line isn't quite absolute."},{"en":"pest control","context":"Q: What are the main roles of animals in your country?\nA: Agriculture's still the largest, providing meat, eggs, milk and fish, though mechanisation has removed most of the working roles that buffalo and horses once had. Then there's the ecological function, which people rarely think about — pollination, pest control, maintaining soil — and agriculture depends on it entirely. The fastest-growing role is companionship: urban families increasingly keep pets, and in smaller households the animal's treated much like a family member. It's a slightly uneven picture, though — people who'd never eat their own dog give barely a thought to the animals raised for their dinner, which shows how much the \"role\" of an animal depends on which category we've mentally put it in."},{"en":"treated much like a family member","context":"Q: What are the main roles of animals in your country?\nA: Agriculture's still the largest, providing meat, eggs, milk and fish, though mechanisation has removed most of the working roles that buffalo and horses once had. Then there's the ecological function, which people rarely think about — pollination, pest control, maintaining soil — and agriculture depends on it entirely. The fastest-growing role is companionship: urban families increasingly keep pets, and in smaller households the animal's treated much like a family member. It's a slightly uneven picture, though — people who'd never eat their own dog give barely a thought to the animals raised for their dinner, which shows how much the \"role\" of an animal depends on which category we've mentally put it in."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Animals","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-006","_displayNo":"#006","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-006-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-006-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"One important difference is how humans share knowledge across generations. We use complex language to record discoveries, so each generation can build on earlier work. We also plan far into the future, such as saving for retirement. Some animals learn traditions too, so the difference is not complete, but human knowledge develops on a much larger scale.","phrases":[{"en":"across generations","meaning":"From older people to younger people over time."}],"question":"In what ways are humans different from other animals?","html":"One important difference is how humans share knowledge <strong class=\"key-phrase\">across generations</strong>. We use complex language to record discoveries, so each generation can build on earlier work. We also plan far into the future, such as saving for retirement. Some animals learn traditions too, so the difference is not complete, but human knowledge develops on a much larger scale."},{"text":"Animals provide food, including meat, eggs, milk and fish. They also support farming and nature by spreading pollen and controlling pests. Machines have replaced many jobs once done by working animals. Meanwhile, more families keep pets for company. People often care deeply about pets while thinking less about farm animals, which shows that our attitudes can be inconsistent.","phrases":[{"en":"inconsistent","meaning":"Not following the same ideas or standards in different situations."}],"question":"What are the main roles of animals in your country?","html":"Animals provide food, including meat, eggs, milk and fish. They also support farming and nature by spreading pollen and controlling pests. Machines have replaced many jobs once done by working animals. Meanwhile, more families keep pets for company. People often care deeply about pets while thinking less about farm animals, which shows that our attitudes can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">inconsistent</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-006-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-006-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Caring for pets and changing attitudes to animals","prompt":"What is involved in taking care of a pet?\nHow do you think people's attitudes towards animals will change in the future?\nWhat do you think should be done about poaching endangered animals?","answer":"Q: What is involved in taking care of a pet?\nA: More than most people expect when they take one on. There&#x27;s the daily routine — feeding, exercise, cleaning up — which sounds simple enough but has to happen every single day for years on end. Then vet care, which is genuinely expensive and unpredictable. Beyond the practical side, animals need attention and company, since a dog left alone for ten hours becomes distressed. What people underestimate most is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the length of the commitment</strong>. That&#x27;s not true of every animal, though — a cat manages considerably better on its own, which is one reason cats have overtaken dogs as the more popular pet in a lot of cities.\n\nQ: How do you think people&#x27;s attitudes towards animals will change in the future?\nA: I&#x27;d expect concern about welfare to keep growing, particularly around intensive farming, which is already driving legislation on cage sizes and transport in several countries. At the same time, pets are increasingly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treated as family members</strong> rather than animals, with specialised food and even insurance. Those two trends coexist a little uncomfortably, since people who adore their own dog will still eat industrially farmed pork <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without much reflection</strong> about the difference — it&#x27;s not that they don&#x27;t care, it&#x27;s that the connection just doesn&#x27;t occur to them at the supermarket.\n\nQ: What do you think should be done about poaching endangered animals?\nA: Enforcement matters, and rangers need proper funding and equipment, since they&#x27;re often outmatched by organised criminal groups. But enforcement alone doesn&#x27;t work — you have to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce demand at the other end</strong>, through prosecution and campaigns in the countries actually buying the products. And crucially, local communities need a reason to protect wildlife: if an elephant destroys your crops and brings you nothing, protecting it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">asking a great deal</strong>. It&#x27;s not a quick fix in any case; even where all three strands work together, populations of the worst-affected species can take decades to recover.","topic":"Animals","phrases":[{"en":"the length of the commitment","context":"Q: What is involved in taking care of a pet?\nA: More than most people expect when they take one on. There's the daily routine — feeding, exercise, cleaning up — which sounds simple enough but has to happen every single day for years on end. Then vet care, which is genuinely expensive and unpredictable. Beyond the practical side, animals need attention and company, since a dog left alone for ten hours becomes distressed. What people underestimate most is the length of the commitment. That's not true of every animal, though — a cat manages considerably better on its own, which is one reason cats have overtaken dogs as the more popular pet in a lot of cities."},{"en":"treated as family members","context":"Q: How do you think people's attitudes towards animals will change in the future?\nA: I'd expect concern about welfare to keep growing, particularly around intensive farming, which is already driving legislation on cage sizes and transport in several countries. At the same time, pets are increasingly treated as family members rather than animals, with specialised food and even insurance. Those two trends coexist a little uncomfortably, since people who adore their own dog will still eat industrially farmed pork without much reflection about the difference — it's not that they don't care, it's that the connection just doesn't occur to them at the supermarket."},{"en":"without much reflection","context":"Q: How do you think people's attitudes towards animals will change in the future?\nA: I'd expect concern about welfare to keep growing, particularly around intensive farming, which is already driving legislation on cage sizes and transport in several countries. At the same time, pets are increasingly treated as family members rather than animals, with specialised food and even insurance. Those two trends coexist a little uncomfortably, since people who adore their own dog will still eat industrially farmed pork without much reflection about the difference — it's not that they don't care, it's that the connection just doesn't occur to them at the supermarket."},{"en":"reduce demand at the other end","context":"Q: What do you think should be done about poaching endangered animals?\nA: Enforcement matters, and rangers need proper funding and equipment, since they're often outmatched by organised criminal groups. But enforcement alone doesn't work — you have to reduce demand at the other end, through prosecution and campaigns in the countries actually buying the products. And crucially, local communities need a reason to protect wildlife: if an elephant destroys your crops and brings you nothing, protecting it is asking a great deal. It's not a quick fix in any case; even where all three strands work together, populations of the worst-affected species can take decades to recover."},{"en":"asking a great deal","context":"Q: What do you think should be done about poaching endangered animals?\nA: Enforcement matters, and rangers need proper funding and equipment, since they're often outmatched by organised criminal groups. But enforcement alone doesn't work — you have to reduce demand at the other end, through prosecution and campaigns in the countries actually buying the products. And crucially, local communities need a reason to protect wildlife: if an elephant destroys your crops and brings you nothing, protecting it is asking a great deal. It's not a quick fix in any case; even where all three strands work together, populations of the worst-affected species can take decades to recover."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Animals","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-007","_displayNo":"#007","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-007-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-007-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-007-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"A pet needs food, cleaning, exercise and medical care, and those needs continue every day for years. Vet bills can also be expensive. Beyond basic care, animals need attention and company. People should think about the long commitment before getting a pet. Different animals have different needs, though; some cope better alone than others.","phrases":[{"en":"long commitment","meaning":"A responsibility that you agree to continue for a long time."}],"question":"What is involved in taking care of a pet?","html":"A pet needs food, cleaning, exercise and medical care, and those needs continue every day for years. Vet bills can also be expensive. Beyond basic care, animals need attention and company. People should think about the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long commitment</strong> before getting a pet. Different animals have different needs, though; some cope better alone than others."},{"text":"I think people will care more about how animals are treated, including animals on large farms. Pets may also receive more special food, insurance and attention as families treat them like members of the household. However, people may still care more about their own pets than farm animals because they do not connect the two situations.","phrases":[{"en":"how animals are treated","meaning":"The way people care for, handle or behave towards animals."}],"question":"How do you think people's attitudes towards animals will change in the future?","html":"I think people will care more about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">how animals are treated</strong>, including animals on large farms. Pets may also receive more special food, insurance and attention as families treat them like members of the household. However, people may still care more about their own pets than farm animals because they do not connect the two situations."},{"text":"We need trained, well-equipped people to protect endangered animals and enforce the law. But we also need to reduce demand for animal products and help local communities benefit from protecting wildlife. Otherwise, people may see the animals only as a threat to crops or income. These steps should work together, and recovery may still take many years.","phrases":[{"en":"reduce demand","meaning":"Make fewer people want to buy something."}],"question":"What do you think should be done about poaching endangered animals?","html":"We need trained, well-equipped people to protect endangered animals and enforce the law. But we also need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce demand</strong> for animal products and help local communities benefit from protecting wildlife. Otherwise, people may see the animals only as a threat to crops or income. These steps should work together, and recovery may still take many years."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-007-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-007-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-007-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Reading at speed, and jobs that involve heavy reading","prompt":"Are there any occasions when reading at speed is a useful skill?\nAre there any jobs in which people need to read a lot? Which ones?\nDo you think reading novels is more interesting than reading factual books, and why?","answer":"Q: Are there any occasions when reading at speed is a useful skill?\nA: Certainly, though I&#x27;d separate it from proper reading. Skimming&#x27;s genuinely useful when you need one specific fact, or want to know whether a document&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth reading at all</strong> — bills, timetables, long work emails, the opening pages of a report. What it doesn&#x27;t suit is anything you actually want to understand or enjoy. If a book&#x27;s good, racing through it seems to me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">defeat the entire point</strong> of having picked it up in the first place.\n\nQ: Are there any jobs in which people need to read a lot? Which ones?\nA: Lawyers are the obvious example, since a single case can involve thousands of pages and missing one clause has real consequences. Researchers and academics read constantly, as do journalists and anyone working in policy. What&#x27;s less obvious is that most ordinary office jobs now involve enormous amounts of reading — emails, reports, specifications. Nobody describes it as a reading job, but people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spend hours a day doing exactly that</strong>, which says something about how narrowly we still define what counts as real work. It&#x27;s just not the kind of reading anyone finds relaxing, which is probably why it doesn&#x27;t get counted.\n\nQ: Do you think reading novels is more interesting than reading factual books, and why?\nA: I don&#x27;t think either&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">inherently more interesting</strong>; they satisfy different appetites. A good novel creates a world convincing enough that the characters feel like people you know, and that&#x27;s an experience non-fiction can&#x27;t really provide. A well-written factual book gives you something quite different — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine insight into how something works</strong>, whether that&#x27;s the human brain or an ancient civilisation. Which one appeals depends entirely on my mood, and on a stressful week I&#x27;ll admit the novel usually wins, since fact-heavy books take more concentration than I&#x27;ve got left by the evening.","topic":"Books & Reading","phrases":[{"en":"worth reading at all","context":"Q: Are there any occasions when reading at speed is a useful skill?\nA: Certainly, though I'd separate it from proper reading. Skimming's genuinely useful when you need one specific fact, or want to know whether a document's worth reading at all — bills, timetables, long work emails, the opening pages of a report. What it doesn't suit is anything you actually want to understand or enjoy. If a book's good, racing through it seems to me to defeat the entire point of having picked it up in the first place."},{"en":"defeat the entire point","context":"Q: Are there any occasions when reading at speed is a useful skill?\nA: Certainly, though I'd separate it from proper reading. Skimming's genuinely useful when you need one specific fact, or want to know whether a document's worth reading at all — bills, timetables, long work emails, the opening pages of a report. What it doesn't suit is anything you actually want to understand or enjoy. If a book's good, racing through it seems to me to defeat the entire point of having picked it up in the first place."},{"en":"spend hours a day doing exactly that","context":"Q: Are there any jobs in which people need to read a lot? Which ones?\nA: Lawyers are the obvious example, since a single case can involve thousands of pages and missing one clause has real consequences. Researchers and academics read constantly, as do journalists and anyone working in policy. What's less obvious is that most ordinary office jobs now involve enormous amounts of reading — emails, reports, specifications. Nobody describes it as a reading job, but people spend hours a day doing exactly that, which says something about how narrowly we still define what counts as real work. It's just not the kind of reading anyone finds relaxing, which is probably why it doesn't get counted."},{"en":"inherently more interesting","context":"Q: Do you think reading novels is more interesting than reading factual books, and why?\nA: I don't think either's inherently more interesting; they satisfy different appetites. A good novel creates a world convincing enough that the characters feel like people you know, and that's an experience non-fiction can't really provide. A well-written factual book gives you something quite different — a genuine insight into how something works, whether that's the human brain or an ancient civilisation. Which one appeals depends entirely on my mood, and on a stressful week I'll admit the novel usually wins, since fact-heavy books take more concentration than I've got left by the evening."},{"en":"a genuine insight into how something works","context":"Q: Do you think reading novels is more interesting than reading factual books, and why?\nA: I don't think either's inherently more interesting; they satisfy different appetites. A good novel creates a world convincing enough that the characters feel like people you know, and that's an experience non-fiction can't really provide. A well-written factual book gives you something quite different — a genuine insight into how something works, whether that's the human brain or an ancient civilisation. Which one appeals depends entirely on my mood, and on a stressful week I'll admit the novel usually wins, since fact-heavy books take more concentration than I've got left by the evening."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Books & Reading","id":"speaking-part-3-008","_displayNo":"#008","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-008-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-008-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-008-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, when you need to find one fact quickly or decide whether a document is useful. For example, you might scan a timetable, bill or long email. However, fast reading is less useful when you need a deep understanding. If I enjoy a book, I would rather take my time than rush through it.","phrases":[{"en":"take my time","meaning":"Do something without hurrying."}],"question":"Are there any occasions when reading at speed is a useful skill?","html":"Yes, when you need to find one fact quickly or decide whether a document is useful. For example, you might scan a timetable, bill or long email. However, fast reading is less useful when you need a deep understanding. If I enjoy a book, I would rather <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take my time</strong> than rush through it."},{"text":"Yes, lawyers, researchers and journalists read a great deal. They need to understand documents accurately because missing a detail can cause problems. Many office workers also spend hours reading emails and reports, although we don't usually call theirs reading jobs. It is often necessary reading rather than something they do to relax.","phrases":[{"en":"accurately","meaning":"Correctly and with attention to important details."}],"question":"Are there any jobs in which people need to read a lot? Which ones?","html":"Yes, lawyers, researchers and journalists read a great deal. They need to understand documents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accurately</strong> because missing a detail can cause problems. Many office workers also spend hours reading emails and reports, although we don&#x27;t usually call theirs reading jobs. It is often necessary reading rather than something they do to relax."},{"text":"Neither is always more interesting; they offer different experiences. A novel lets you imagine a world and understand its characters. A factual book explains real subjects, such as history or how the brain works. My choice depends on my mood. After a stressful week, I often choose a novel because facts can require more concentration.","phrases":[{"en":"factual book","meaning":"A book about real information rather than an invented story."}],"question":"Do you think reading novels is more interesting than reading factual books, and why?","html":"Neither is always more interesting; they offer different experiences. A novel lets you imagine a world and understand its characters. A <strong class=\"key-phrase\">factual book</strong> explains real subjects, such as history or how the brain works. My choice depends on my mood. After a stressful week, I often choose a novel because facts can require more concentration."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-008-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-008-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-008-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"The books people read and encouraging children to read","prompt":"What kind of books do you read?\nWhat types of books do children like?\nShould we encourage children to read books and why?","answer":"Q: What kind of books do you read?\nA: Mostly non-fiction — psychology, education and technology are the areas I keep coming back to — with a novel now and then. The non-fiction&#x27;s partly practical, since I want ideas I can actually use. But I&#x27;ve come to think fiction does something non-fiction can&#x27;t: spending four hundred pages <strong class=\"key-phrase\">inside somebody else&#x27;s reasoning</strong> is genuinely different from reading about people from the outside. I try to keep a balance for that reason, though I&#x27;ll admit some months the non-fiction pile wins completely and I don&#x27;t touch a novel at all, which probably isn&#x27;t the healthiest ratio.\n\nQ: What types of books do children like?\nA: It changes fast with age. Young children want pictures, repetition and humour, and will happily hear the same story fifty times without getting bored of it. Slightly older ones move to adventure and anything a little frightening. Teenagers tend towards fantasy and mystery, or books connected to films and games they already know. What holds across all ages is that children read far more when they&#x27;ve <strong class=\"key-phrase\">chosen the book themselves</strong>, even if the choice seems unambitious to an adult — a graphic novel or a joke book still counts as reading, and insisting on something more &quot;worthy&quot; usually just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">kills the habit</strong>.\n\nQ: Should we encourage children to read books and why?\nA: Yes, but how you encourage matters enormously. Reading builds vocabulary and concentration in a way screens generally don&#x27;t, and children who read well find every other subject easier. The mistake is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turning it into an obligation</strong> — comprehension tests, required pages, reading logs — because that reliably makes children associate books with work rather than pleasure. What works is keeping books within reach, reading alongside them, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">letting them abandon anything they are not enjoying</strong> without any sense of failure.","topic":"Books & Reading","phrases":[{"en":"inside somebody else's reasoning","context":"Q: What kind of books do you read?\nA: Mostly non-fiction — psychology, education and technology are the areas I keep coming back to — with a novel now and then. The non-fiction's partly practical, since I want ideas I can actually use. But I've come to think fiction does something non-fiction can't: spending four hundred pages inside somebody else's reasoning is genuinely different from reading about people from the outside. I try to keep a balance for that reason, though I'll admit some months the non-fiction pile wins completely and I don't touch a novel at all, which probably isn't the healthiest ratio."},{"en":"chosen the book themselves","context":"Q: What types of books do children like?\nA: It changes fast with age. Young children want pictures, repetition and humour, and will happily hear the same story fifty times without getting bored of it. Slightly older ones move to adventure and anything a little frightening. Teenagers tend towards fantasy and mystery, or books connected to films and games they already know. What holds across all ages is that children read far more when they've chosen the book themselves, even if the choice seems unambitious to an adult — a graphic novel or a joke book still counts as reading, and insisting on something more \"worthy\" usually just kills the habit."},{"en":"kills the habit","context":"Q: What types of books do children like?\nA: It changes fast with age. Young children want pictures, repetition and humour, and will happily hear the same story fifty times without getting bored of it. Slightly older ones move to adventure and anything a little frightening. Teenagers tend towards fantasy and mystery, or books connected to films and games they already know. What holds across all ages is that children read far more when they've chosen the book themselves, even if the choice seems unambitious to an adult — a graphic novel or a joke book still counts as reading, and insisting on something more \"worthy\" usually just kills the habit."},{"en":"turning it into an obligation","context":"Q: Should we encourage children to read books and why?\nA: Yes, but how you encourage matters enormously. Reading builds vocabulary and concentration in a way screens generally don't, and children who read well find every other subject easier. The mistake is turning it into an obligation — comprehension tests, required pages, reading logs — because that reliably makes children associate books with work rather than pleasure. What works is keeping books within reach, reading alongside them, and letting them abandon anything they are not enjoying without any sense of failure."},{"en":"letting them abandon anything they are not enjoying","context":"Q: Should we encourage children to read books and why?\nA: Yes, but how you encourage matters enormously. Reading builds vocabulary and concentration in a way screens generally don't, and children who read well find every other subject easier. The mistake is turning it into an obligation — comprehension tests, required pages, reading logs — because that reliably makes children associate books with work rather than pleasure. What works is keeping books within reach, reading alongside them, and letting them abandon anything they are not enjoying without any sense of failure."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Books & Reading","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-009","_displayNo":"#009","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-009-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-009-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-009-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I mainly read factual books about psychology, education and technology because they give me useful ideas. I also read novels sometimes. Stories help me understand a person's thoughts from the inside, which is different from reading explanations about people. I try to include both, although in some months I read almost only non-fiction.","phrases":[{"en":"non-fiction","meaning":"Writing about real people, events or information."}],"question":"What kind of books do you read?","html":"I mainly read factual books about psychology, education and technology because they give me useful ideas. I also read novels sometimes. Stories help me understand a person&#x27;s thoughts from the inside, which is different from reading explanations about people. I try to include both, although in some months I read almost only <strong class=\"key-phrase\">non-fiction</strong>."},{"text":"It depends on age. Young children often enjoy pictures, funny stories and repetition. Older children may prefer adventures, while teenagers choose fantasy or mysteries. Across these ages, children usually read more when they can choose for themselves. Even a joke book or comic can help; forcing a more serious choice may reduce their interest.","phrases":[{"en":"repetition","meaning":"Something happening or being said again several times."}],"question":"What types of books do children like?","html":"It depends on age. Young children often enjoy pictures, funny stories and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">repetition</strong>. Older children may prefer adventures, while teenagers choose fantasy or mysteries. Across these ages, children usually read more when they can choose for themselves. Even a joke book or comic can help; forcing a more serious choice may reduce their interest."},{"text":"Yes, reading helps children build vocabulary and concentrate, which supports other school subjects too. However, we should make it enjoyable rather than another duty. Keeping books nearby, reading together and letting children choose are useful approaches. If they dislike a book, allowing them to try another may help them keep enjoying reading.","phrases":[{"en":"vocabulary","meaning":"The words a person knows and uses."}],"question":"Should we encourage children to read books and why?","html":"Yes, reading helps children build <strong class=\"key-phrase\">vocabulary</strong> and concentrate, which supports other school subjects too. However, we should make it enjoyable rather than another duty. Keeping books nearby, reading together and letting children choose are useful approaches. If they dislike a book, allowing them to try another may help them keep enjoying reading."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-009-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-009-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-009-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Why people write books, and books against films","prompt":"Why do you think some people write books?\nWhat type of books do you read often?\nDo you prefer to read a book or watch a movie on the same subject and why?","answer":"Q: Why do you think some people write books?\nA: Different motives, and they rarely overlap much. Some write to tell a story they can&#x27;t stop thinking about. Others want to preserve knowledge or influence an argument, which accounts for most non-fiction. A great deal of writing is also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a way of working something out</strong> — people write about a difficult period of their life partly in order to understand it, the way keeping a diary forces you to name what you&#x27;re actually feeling. What almost none of them do it for is money, though, since very few books earn much at all.\n\nQ: What type of books do you read often?\nA: Mostly research-based non-fiction, particularly on education, psychology and technology, because I want ideas I can apply to what I&#x27;m doing. But I read novels regularly too, and I&#x27;ve come to value them differently. Non-fiction tells you about people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">from the outside</strong>, whereas fiction puts you inside someone&#x27;s reasoning for several hundred pages — a novel about a difficult marriage will teach you something a relationship self-help book simply can&#x27;t, because you feel the decision being made rather than being told the theory behind it. I&#x27;d find it hard to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give up either</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you prefer to read a book or watch a movie on the same subject and why?\nA: The book, generally, if I want to understand something properly. A novel can spend a whole chapter inside a character&#x27;s thinking, whereas a film has to convey the same thing through an expression lasting two seconds. Films also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compress ruthlessly</strong> — a five-hundred-page plot becomes two hours, and most of the internal reasoning simply can&#x27;t survive that cut. That said, film does things prose can&#x27;t: landscape, music and performance carry <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an immediate emotional force</strong> that writing has to work considerably harder to achieve, so I wouldn&#x27;t say one form is simply better than the other.","topic":"Books & Reading","phrases":[{"en":"a way of working something out","context":"Q: Why do you think some people write books?\nA: Different motives, and they rarely overlap much. Some write to tell a story they can't stop thinking about. Others want to preserve knowledge or influence an argument, which accounts for most non-fiction. A great deal of writing is also a way of working something out — people write about a difficult period of their life partly in order to understand it, the way keeping a diary forces you to name what you're actually feeling. What almost none of them do it for is money, though, since very few books earn much at all."},{"en":"from the outside","context":"Q: What type of books do you read often?\nA: Mostly research-based non-fiction, particularly on education, psychology and technology, because I want ideas I can apply to what I'm doing. But I read novels regularly too, and I've come to value them differently. Non-fiction tells you about people from the outside, whereas fiction puts you inside someone's reasoning for several hundred pages — a novel about a difficult marriage will teach you something a relationship self-help book simply can't, because you feel the decision being made rather than being told the theory behind it. I'd find it hard to give up either."},{"en":"give up either","context":"Q: What type of books do you read often?\nA: Mostly research-based non-fiction, particularly on education, psychology and technology, because I want ideas I can apply to what I'm doing. But I read novels regularly too, and I've come to value them differently. Non-fiction tells you about people from the outside, whereas fiction puts you inside someone's reasoning for several hundred pages — a novel about a difficult marriage will teach you something a relationship self-help book simply can't, because you feel the decision being made rather than being told the theory behind it. I'd find it hard to give up either."},{"en":"compress ruthlessly","context":"Q: Do you prefer to read a book or watch a movie on the same subject and why?\nA: The book, generally, if I want to understand something properly. A novel can spend a whole chapter inside a character's thinking, whereas a film has to convey the same thing through an expression lasting two seconds. Films also compress ruthlessly — a five-hundred-page plot becomes two hours, and most of the internal reasoning simply can't survive that cut. That said, film does things prose can't: landscape, music and performance carry an immediate emotional force that writing has to work considerably harder to achieve, so I wouldn't say one form is simply better than the other."},{"en":"an immediate emotional force","context":"Q: Do you prefer to read a book or watch a movie on the same subject and why?\nA: The book, generally, if I want to understand something properly. A novel can spend a whole chapter inside a character's thinking, whereas a film has to convey the same thing through an expression lasting two seconds. Films also compress ruthlessly — a five-hundred-page plot becomes two hours, and most of the internal reasoning simply can't survive that cut. That said, film does things prose can't: landscape, music and performance carry an immediate emotional force that writing has to work considerably harder to achieve, so I wouldn't say one form is simply better than the other."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Books & Reading","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-010","_displayNo":"#010","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-010-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-010-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-010-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"People write for different reasons. Some have a story they want to tell; others want to share knowledge or change people's opinions. Writing can also help someone understand a difficult experience, much like a diary does. Money is not the main reward for most writers, because relatively few books earn a large amount.","phrases":[{"en":"reward","meaning":"Something valuable or satisfying you get from an activity."}],"question":"Why do you think some people write books?","html":"People write for different reasons. Some have a story they want to tell; others want to share knowledge or change people&#x27;s opinions. Writing can also help someone understand a difficult experience, much like a diary does. Money is not the main <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reward</strong> for most writers, because relatively few books earn a large amount."},{"text":"I often read books based on research, especially about education, psychology and technology. I want ideas I can use. I also value novels because they let me experience a character's thoughts and decisions, rather than just read advice about behaviour. Both types teach me something, so I would find it difficult to stop reading either.","phrases":[{"en":"based on research","meaning":"Using information gathered through careful study."}],"question":"What type of books do you read often?","html":"I often read books <strong class=\"key-phrase\">based on research</strong>, especially about education, psychology and technology. I want ideas I can use. I also value novels because they let me experience a character&#x27;s thoughts and decisions, rather than just read advice about behaviour. Both types teach me something, so I would find it difficult to stop reading either."},{"text":"I generally prefer a book when I want to understand something in detail. It has more time to explain a character's thoughts, while a film must shorten the story. However, films use music, scenery and acting to create feelings very quickly. So I prefer books for depth, but I don't think films are always less valuable.","phrases":[{"en":"in detail","meaning":"With a lot of information about the different parts."}],"question":"Do you prefer to read a book or watch a movie on the same subject and why?","html":"I generally prefer a book when I want to understand something <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in detail</strong>. It has more time to explain a character&#x27;s thoughts, while a film must shorten the story. However, films use music, scenery and acting to create feelings very quickly. So I prefer books for depth, but I don&#x27;t think films are always less valuable."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-010-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-010-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-010-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Why parties matter, and why some people avoid them","prompt":"What types of parties do people have, and why are parties important?\nWhy do you think some people like parties but others hate them?\nDo you think parties will become more popular in the future?","answer":"Q: What types of parties do people have, and why are parties important?\nA: People have parties to celebrate special occasions like birthdays, weddings, or the start of a new year. I think it&#x27;s important to mark these things because they&#x27;re landmarks in our lives — a wedding, for instance, is really the one time an entire scattered family ends up in the same room. Parties are a good way to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring people together</strong>, and they&#x27;re an opportunity to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">let off some steam</strong>. Without occasions like these, families and old friends would probably drift apart, since everyone&#x27;s scattered across different cities and nobody would otherwise make the effort. That said, not everyone enjoys them equally — plenty of people find a big, noisy gathering more draining than joyful.\n\nQ: Why do you think some people like parties but others hate them?\nA: Most people like parties because they have a good time - eating a nice meal, chatting to friends, or having a dance. People who don&#x27;t like them might find social situations difficult because they&#x27;re shy, or maybe they don&#x27;t enjoy having to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make small talk with</strong> people they don&#x27;t know. I&#x27;d also say a lot depends on the size of the party. Plenty of people who dread a room of two hundred strangers are perfectly happy at a dinner with six friends.\n\nQ: Do you think parties will become more popular in the future?\nA: I don&#x27;t think parties will necessarily become more popular; they&#x27;ve always been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a basic form of social life</strong>, so there isn&#x27;t really room for them to grow much further. The format may well change, but people will keep celebrating and socialising together one way or another. If anything, I&#x27;d expect gatherings to get smaller and less formal — a barbecue with ten friends rather than a hall booked for two hundred. Big parties are expensive and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take a great deal of organising</strong>, and younger people I know increasingly prefer seeing a few friends regularly to hosting one enormous event a year.","topic":"Celebrations","phrases":[{"en":"bring people together","context":"Q: What types of parties do people have, and why are parties important?\nA: People have parties to celebrate special occasions like birthdays, weddings, or the start of a new year. I think it's important to mark these things because they're landmarks in our lives — a wedding, for instance, is really the one time an entire scattered family ends up in the same room. Parties are a good way to bring people together, and they're an opportunity to let off some steam. Without occasions like these, families and old friends would probably drift apart, since everyone's scattered across different cities and nobody would otherwise make the effort. That said, not everyone enjoys them equally — plenty of people find a big, noisy gathering more draining than joyful."},{"en":"let off some steam","context":"Q: What types of parties do people have, and why are parties important?\nA: People have parties to celebrate special occasions like birthdays, weddings, or the start of a new year. I think it's important to mark these things because they're landmarks in our lives — a wedding, for instance, is really the one time an entire scattered family ends up in the same room. Parties are a good way to bring people together, and they're an opportunity to let off some steam. Without occasions like these, families and old friends would probably drift apart, since everyone's scattered across different cities and nobody would otherwise make the effort. That said, not everyone enjoys them equally — plenty of people find a big, noisy gathering more draining than joyful."},{"en":"make small talk with","context":"Q: Why do you think some people like parties but others hate them?\nA: Most people like parties because they have a good time - eating a nice meal, chatting to friends, or having a dance. People who don't like them might find social situations difficult because they're shy, or maybe they don't enjoy having to make small talk with people they don't know. I'd also say a lot depends on the size of the party. Plenty of people who dread a room of two hundred strangers are perfectly happy at a dinner with six friends."},{"en":"a basic form of social life","context":"Q: Do you think parties will become more popular in the future?\nA: I don't think parties will necessarily become more popular; they've always been a basic form of social life, so there isn't really room for them to grow much further. The format may well change, but people will keep celebrating and socialising together one way or another. If anything, I'd expect gatherings to get smaller and less formal — a barbecue with ten friends rather than a hall booked for two hundred. Big parties are expensive and take a great deal of organising, and younger people I know increasingly prefer seeing a few friends regularly to hosting one enormous event a year."},{"en":"take a great deal of organising","context":"Q: Do you think parties will become more popular in the future?\nA: I don't think parties will necessarily become more popular; they've always been a basic form of social life, so there isn't really room for them to grow much further. The format may well change, but people will keep celebrating and socialising together one way or another. If anything, I'd expect gatherings to get smaller and less formal — a barbecue with ten friends rather than a hall booked for two hundred. Big parties are expensive and take a great deal of organising, and younger people I know increasingly prefer seeing a few friends regularly to hosting one enormous event a year."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Celebrations","id":"speaking-part-3-011","_displayNo":"#011","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-011-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-011-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-011-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"People hold parties for birthdays, weddings and other important occasions. They bring family and friends together, including people who normally live far apart. They also give everyone a chance to relax and have fun. Without these occasions, people might meet less often. However, large, noisy parties can be tiring for people who prefer quieter gatherings.","phrases":[{"en":"gatherings","meaning":"Events where people meet and spend time together."}],"question":"What types of parties do people have, and why are parties important?","html":"People hold parties for birthdays, weddings and other important occasions. They bring family and friends together, including people who normally live far apart. They also give everyone a chance to relax and have fun. Without these occasions, people might meet less often. However, large, noisy parties can be tiring for people who prefer quieter <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gatherings</strong>."},{"text":"Some people enjoy the food, conversation and dancing. Others feel shy or dislike talking to strangers, so the same party may feel stressful. Size matters too: someone who dislikes a crowded hall may enjoy dinner with six close friends. I think the type of party is as important as whether a person enjoys socialising.","phrases":[{"en":"socialising","meaning":"Spending time with people for enjoyment."}],"question":"Why do you think some people like parties but others hate them?","html":"Some people enjoy the food, conversation and dancing. Others feel shy or dislike talking to strangers, so the same party may feel stressful. Size matters too: someone who dislikes a crowded hall may enjoy dinner with six close friends. I think the type of party is as important as whether a person enjoys <strong class=\"key-phrase\">socialising</strong>."},{"text":"I don't expect a big increase because parties are already common. People will continue celebrating, but gatherings may become smaller and more casual. A meal or barbecue with a few friends costs less and is easier to organise than a huge event. People may prefer meeting regularly instead of hosting one large party each year.","phrases":[{"en":"casual","meaning":"Relaxed and not very formal."}],"question":"Do you think parties will become more popular in the future?","html":"I don&#x27;t expect a big increase because parties are already common. People will continue celebrating, but gatherings may become smaller and more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">casual</strong>. A meal or barbecue with a few friends costs less and is easier to organise than a huge event. People may prefer meeting regularly instead of hosting one large party each year."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-011-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-011-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-011-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Festivals in the working year, and new ones","prompt":"Do you think new festivals will be introduced in the future?\nWhy do you think festivals are important in the working year?\nWould you agree that the original significance of the festivals is often lost today?","answer":"Q: Do you think new festivals will be introduced in the future?\nA: Almost certainly, because it&#x27;s happened continuously throughout history — most traditions people assume are ancient were actually invented at a specific point. I&#x27;d expect new ones around environmental themes and around local identity, as places try to distinguish themselves. Many will begin commercially, like Black Friday, which was straightforwardly invented to sell things. Whether any of them becomes genuinely meaningful depends on whether communities <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take them over and add their own customs</strong>, which is exactly how the older festivals came about in the first place. Plenty will just fade out again, though — most invented celebrations never catch on beyond a marketing campaign.\n\nQ: Why do you think festivals are important in the working year?\nA: They interrupt routine, which matters more than it sounds. Without fixed occasions people simply don&#x27;t stop, since there&#x27;s always a reason to keep working. A festival gives everyone permission to rest <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at the same time</strong>, which is the key point, because a holiday is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far less valuable</strong> if everyone you&#x27;d want to see is stuck at work instead. They also bring extended families together, often the only time all year it happens. Not everyone gets that rest equally, though — people in hospitals, transport and hospitality often work through the festival precisely so everyone else can enjoy it.\n\nQ: Would you agree that the original significance of the festivals is often lost today?\nA: Partly, though I&#x27;d be careful with the word &quot;lost&quot;. Commercial promotion certainly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overshadows the original meaning</strong> — plenty of people now think of a festival mainly in terms of the sales and the food rather than the event it commemorates. But traditions have always changed, and the version people call authentic was itself a modification of something earlier. What survives in practice is usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the social function</strong> of bringing families together, and that seems remarkably resistant to commercialisation, even when everything wrapped around it has become thoroughly commercial.","topic":"Celebrations","phrases":[{"en":"take them over and add their own customs","context":"Q: Do you think new festivals will be introduced in the future?\nA: Almost certainly, because it's happened continuously throughout history — most traditions people assume are ancient were actually invented at a specific point. I'd expect new ones around environmental themes and around local identity, as places try to distinguish themselves. Many will begin commercially, like Black Friday, which was straightforwardly invented to sell things. Whether any of them becomes genuinely meaningful depends on whether communities take them over and add their own customs, which is exactly how the older festivals came about in the first place. Plenty will just fade out again, though — most invented celebrations never catch on beyond a marketing campaign."},{"en":"at the same time","context":"Q: Why do you think festivals are important in the working year?\nA: They interrupt routine, which matters more than it sounds. Without fixed occasions people simply don't stop, since there's always a reason to keep working. A festival gives everyone permission to rest at the same time, which is the key point, because a holiday is far less valuable if everyone you'd want to see is stuck at work instead. They also bring extended families together, often the only time all year it happens. Not everyone gets that rest equally, though — people in hospitals, transport and hospitality often work through the festival precisely so everyone else can enjoy it."},{"en":"far less valuable","context":"Q: Why do you think festivals are important in the working year?\nA: They interrupt routine, which matters more than it sounds. Without fixed occasions people simply don't stop, since there's always a reason to keep working. A festival gives everyone permission to rest at the same time, which is the key point, because a holiday is far less valuable if everyone you'd want to see is stuck at work instead. They also bring extended families together, often the only time all year it happens. Not everyone gets that rest equally, though — people in hospitals, transport and hospitality often work through the festival precisely so everyone else can enjoy it."},{"en":"overshadows the original meaning","context":"Q: Would you agree that the original significance of the festivals is often lost today?\nA: Partly, though I'd be careful with the word \"lost\". Commercial promotion certainly overshadows the original meaning — plenty of people now think of a festival mainly in terms of the sales and the food rather than the event it commemorates. But traditions have always changed, and the version people call authentic was itself a modification of something earlier. What survives in practice is usually the social function of bringing families together, and that seems remarkably resistant to commercialisation, even when everything wrapped around it has become thoroughly commercial."},{"en":"the social function","context":"Q: Would you agree that the original significance of the festivals is often lost today?\nA: Partly, though I'd be careful with the word \"lost\". Commercial promotion certainly overshadows the original meaning — plenty of people now think of a festival mainly in terms of the sales and the food rather than the event it commemorates. But traditions have always changed, and the version people call authentic was itself a modification of something earlier. What survives in practice is usually the social function of bringing families together, and that seems remarkably resistant to commercialisation, even when everything wrapped around it has become thoroughly commercial."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Celebrations","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-012","_displayNo":"#012","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-012-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-012-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-012-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, new festivals will probably appear because traditions have always developed over time. Some may focus on the environment or local identity; others may begin as ways to sell products. To last, a festival needs people to give it their own customs and meaning. Many new celebrations will disappear if communities never become interested in them.","phrases":[{"en":"local identity","meaning":"The qualities and traditions that make a place and its people distinctive."}],"question":"Do you think new festivals will be introduced in the future?","html":"Yes, new festivals will probably appear because traditions have always developed over time. Some may focus on the environment or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">local identity</strong>; others may begin as ways to sell products. To last, a festival needs people to give it their own customs and meaning. Many new celebrations will disappear if communities never become interested in them."},{"text":"Festivals give people a shared break from work. That matters because free time is more enjoyable when family and friends are available too. They also help relatives meet when they live far apart. However, not everyone can rest: hospital, transport and restaurant workers may need to work so that others can enjoy the celebration.","phrases":[{"en":"a shared break","meaning":"Time off that people can enjoy at the same time."}],"question":"Why do you think festivals are important in the working year?","html":"Festivals give people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a shared break</strong> from work. That matters because free time is more enjoyable when family and friends are available too. They also help relatives meet when they live far apart. However, not everyone can rest: hospital, transport and restaurant workers may need to work so that others can enjoy the celebration."},{"text":"Partly. Shopping and advertising can receive more attention than the event a festival originally remembered. However, traditions have always changed, so change does not necessarily mean everything valuable is lost. Families still come together, and that social purpose often remains strong. I think the original meaning may become weaker while some important benefits continue.","phrases":[{"en":"original meaning","meaning":"The purpose or importance something had when it began."}],"question":"Would you agree that the original significance of the festivals is often lost today?","html":"Partly. Shopping and advertising can receive more attention than the event a festival originally remembered. However, traditions have always changed, so change does not necessarily mean everything valuable is lost. Families still come together, and that social purpose often remains strong. I think the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">original meaning</strong> may become weaker while some important benefits continue."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-012-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-012-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-012-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Parties in your country and what happens at them","prompt":"What types of parties are popular in your country?\nWhat sort of activities do people do at parties?\nWhat do you think of attending parties?","answer":"Q: What types of parties are popular in your country?\nA: Weddings are the biggest by some distance — they&#x27;re often enormous, with hundreds of guests, and families save for years to hold them. Birthday parties are common for children, rather less so for adults. Then there are family gatherings around New Year and traditional festivals, which matter more than birthdays to most people. Companies hold year-end celebrations too, which are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">half social and half obligation</strong> — you&#x27;re expected to show up even when you&#x27;d rather not. Younger people increasingly prefer small informal gatherings to large formal ones, partly because the enormous weddings are getting harder to afford.\n\nQ: What sort of activities do people do at parties?\nA: Eating&#x27;s central, since most gatherings are built around a meal and the food&#x27;s often the point rather than an accompaniment. Beyond that, people talk, take an enormous number of photographs, and there&#x27;s usually music, sometimes karaoke. At weddings there are speeches and formal rituals too. The best parties in my experience have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">just enough structure</strong> to stop people standing around awkwardly, without scheduling every minute so tightly that nobody can simply sit and talk. Get that balance wrong in either direction and people start <strong class=\"key-phrase\">checking their phones</strong>.\n\nQ: What do you think of attending parties?\nA: I enjoy them when they&#x27;re small enough for actual conversation, perhaps ten or fifteen people. Large parties I find harder work: the music&#x27;s too loud to talk properly, and you end up having <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the same brief exchange twenty times</strong> without really speaking to anyone. I&#x27;m glad I went, usually, but I often leave feeling I&#x27;ve <strong class=\"key-phrase\">been social rather than connected with anybody</strong> in particular, which is a slightly hollow feeling. Small gatherings don&#x27;t have that problem, so it really comes down to numbers rather than parties as a whole.","topic":"Celebrations","phrases":[{"en":"half social and half obligation","context":"Q: What types of parties are popular in your country?\nA: Weddings are the biggest by some distance — they're often enormous, with hundreds of guests, and families save for years to hold them. Birthday parties are common for children, rather less so for adults. Then there are family gatherings around New Year and traditional festivals, which matter more than birthdays to most people. Companies hold year-end celebrations too, which are half social and half obligation — you're expected to show up even when you'd rather not. Younger people increasingly prefer small informal gatherings to large formal ones, partly because the enormous weddings are getting harder to afford."},{"en":"just enough structure","context":"Q: What sort of activities do people do at parties?\nA: Eating's central, since most gatherings are built around a meal and the food's often the point rather than an accompaniment. Beyond that, people talk, take an enormous number of photographs, and there's usually music, sometimes karaoke. At weddings there are speeches and formal rituals too. The best parties in my experience have just enough structure to stop people standing around awkwardly, without scheduling every minute so tightly that nobody can simply sit and talk. Get that balance wrong in either direction and people start checking their phones."},{"en":"checking their phones","context":"Q: What sort of activities do people do at parties?\nA: Eating's central, since most gatherings are built around a meal and the food's often the point rather than an accompaniment. Beyond that, people talk, take an enormous number of photographs, and there's usually music, sometimes karaoke. At weddings there are speeches and formal rituals too. The best parties in my experience have just enough structure to stop people standing around awkwardly, without scheduling every minute so tightly that nobody can simply sit and talk. Get that balance wrong in either direction and people start checking their phones."},{"en":"the same brief exchange twenty times","context":"Q: What do you think of attending parties?\nA: I enjoy them when they're small enough for actual conversation, perhaps ten or fifteen people. Large parties I find harder work: the music's too loud to talk properly, and you end up having the same brief exchange twenty times without really speaking to anyone. I'm glad I went, usually, but I often leave feeling I've been social rather than connected with anybody in particular, which is a slightly hollow feeling. Small gatherings don't have that problem, so it really comes down to numbers rather than parties as a whole."},{"en":"been social rather than connected with anybody","context":"Q: What do you think of attending parties?\nA: I enjoy them when they're small enough for actual conversation, perhaps ten or fifteen people. Large parties I find harder work: the music's too loud to talk properly, and you end up having the same brief exchange twenty times without really speaking to anyone. I'm glad I went, usually, but I often leave feeling I've been social rather than connected with anybody in particular, which is a slightly hollow feeling. Small gatherings don't have that problem, so it really comes down to numbers rather than parties as a whole."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Celebrations","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-013","_displayNo":"#013","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-013-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-013-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-013-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Weddings are especially important and can have hundreds of guests. Children's birthdays, New Year gatherings and company celebrations are common too. However, younger people often prefer small, relaxed events. Large parties, particularly weddings, can cost a lot, so meeting a few friends may be a more practical choice for them.","phrases":[{"en":"practical choice","meaning":"An option that works well with the time, money or resources available."}],"question":"What types of parties are popular in your country?","html":"Weddings are especially important and can have hundreds of guests. Children&#x27;s birthdays, New Year gatherings and company celebrations are common too. However, younger people often prefer small, relaxed events. Large parties, particularly weddings, can cost a lot, so meeting a few friends may be a more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical choice</strong> for them."},{"text":"Most parties involve a meal, conversation, photos and music, sometimes including karaoke. Weddings may also have speeches and traditional activities. I think a good party needs some organisation so guests feel comfortable, but enough free time for them to talk. If every minute is planned, people may not really enjoy being together.","phrases":[{"en":"free time","meaning":"Time without a fixed task or organised activity."}],"question":"What sort of activities do people do at parties?","html":"Most parties involve a meal, conversation, photos and music, sometimes including karaoke. Weddings may also have speeches and traditional activities. I think a good party needs some organisation so guests feel comfortable, but enough <strong class=\"key-phrase\">free time</strong> for them to talk. If every minute is planned, people may not really enjoy being together."},{"text":"I enjoy smaller parties where I can have a proper conversation, perhaps with ten or fifteen people. At a huge party, loud music and many short introductions make it harder to connect. I may be glad I attended, but still feel that I barely spoke to anyone. So the size matters more than the party itself.","phrases":[{"en":"connect","meaning":"Build a meaningful or friendly relationship with someone."}],"question":"What do you think of attending parties?","html":"I enjoy smaller parties where I can have a proper conversation, perhaps with ten or fifteen people. At a huge party, loud music and many short introductions make it harder to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">connect</strong>. I may be glad I attended, but still feel that I barely spoke to anyone. So the size matters more than the party itself."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-013-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-013-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-013-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Why older people resist change","prompt":"Why do some older people resist change?\nIs technological change always positive?","answer":"Q: Why do some older people resist change?\nA: I think it&#x27;s less about age than about competence. Someone who&#x27;s spent forty years becoming genuinely skilled at something has a great deal to lose when the method changes overnight — they go from being the expert to being the person who needs help, and that&#x27;s uncomfortable at any age. It&#x27;s also often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">handled badly</strong>: new systems get introduced with no training and no explanation of why the old way was inadequate, which naturally feels like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a judgement on everything they have done</strong>. Age plays some part too, admittedly — younger people have simply had more practice adapting to new tools throughout their lives, so it isn&#x27;t purely about who&#x27;s more competent.\n\nQ: Is technological change always positive?\nA: No, and I think we&#x27;re too quick to assume it is. Technology&#x27;s clearly improved healthcare, communication and access to information — nobody wants to give up video calls with family abroad. But it&#x27;s also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">eliminated jobs faster than people can retrain</strong>, weakened privacy considerably, and created dependencies we don&#x27;t fully understand. The test should be the outcome and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">who benefits from it</strong>, not simply whether something&#x27;s new. And the people who benefit are rarely the people who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carry the cost</strong>, which is why the argument gets so heated.","topic":"Change","phrases":[{"en":"handled badly","context":"Q: Why do some older people resist change?\nA: I think it's less about age than about competence. Someone who's spent forty years becoming genuinely skilled at something has a great deal to lose when the method changes overnight — they go from being the expert to being the person who needs help, and that's uncomfortable at any age. It's also often handled badly: new systems get introduced with no training and no explanation of why the old way was inadequate, which naturally feels like a judgement on everything they have done. Age plays some part too, admittedly — younger people have simply had more practice adapting to new tools throughout their lives, so it isn't purely about who's more competent."},{"en":"a judgement on everything they have done","context":"Q: Why do some older people resist change?\nA: I think it's less about age than about competence. Someone who's spent forty years becoming genuinely skilled at something has a great deal to lose when the method changes overnight — they go from being the expert to being the person who needs help, and that's uncomfortable at any age. It's also often handled badly: new systems get introduced with no training and no explanation of why the old way was inadequate, which naturally feels like a judgement on everything they have done. Age plays some part too, admittedly — younger people have simply had more practice adapting to new tools throughout their lives, so it isn't purely about who's more competent."},{"en":"eliminated jobs faster than people can retrain","context":"Q: Is technological change always positive?\nA: No, and I think we're too quick to assume it is. Technology's clearly improved healthcare, communication and access to information — nobody wants to give up video calls with family abroad. But it's also eliminated jobs faster than people can retrain, weakened privacy considerably, and created dependencies we don't fully understand. The test should be the outcome and who benefits from it, not simply whether something's new. And the people who benefit are rarely the people who carry the cost, which is why the argument gets so heated."},{"en":"who benefits from it","context":"Q: Is technological change always positive?\nA: No, and I think we're too quick to assume it is. Technology's clearly improved healthcare, communication and access to information — nobody wants to give up video calls with family abroad. But it's also eliminated jobs faster than people can retrain, weakened privacy considerably, and created dependencies we don't fully understand. The test should be the outcome and who benefits from it, not simply whether something's new. And the people who benefit are rarely the people who carry the cost, which is why the argument gets so heated."},{"en":"carry the cost","context":"Q: Is technological change always positive?\nA: No, and I think we're too quick to assume it is. Technology's clearly improved healthcare, communication and access to information — nobody wants to give up video calls with family abroad. But it's also eliminated jobs faster than people can retrain, weakened privacy considerably, and created dependencies we don't fully understand. The test should be the outcome and who benefits from it, not simply whether something's new. And the people who benefit are rarely the people who carry the cost, which is why the argument gets so heated."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Change","id":"speaking-part-3-014","_displayNo":"#014","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-014-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-014-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"They may worry about losing skills they have spent years developing. When a familiar method changes, an expert suddenly needs help, which can feel uncomfortable. Changes are also harder when nobody provides training or explains the reason. Younger people may have more practice with new technology, but resistance is not simply about being old.","phrases":[{"en":"resistance","meaning":"Unwillingness to accept or support a change."}],"question":"Why do some older people resist change?","html":"They may worry about losing skills they have spent years developing. When a familiar method changes, an expert suddenly needs help, which can feel uncomfortable. Changes are also harder when nobody provides training or explains the reason. Younger people may have more practice with new technology, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resistance</strong> is not simply about being old."},{"text":"No. Technology has improved healthcare and made it easier to contact family abroad, but it also creates problems. People can lose jobs before learning new skills, and privacy may become weaker. We should ask whether a change improves life and who pays the cost, rather than assume everything new is automatically better.","phrases":[{"en":"privacy","meaning":"Control over your personal information and private life."}],"question":"Is technological change always positive?","html":"No. Technology has improved healthcare and made it easier to contact family abroad, but it also creates problems. People can lose jobs before learning new skills, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">privacy</strong> may become weaker. We should ask whether a change improves life and who pays the cost, rather than assume everything new is automatically better."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-014-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-014-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Toys for boys and girls, and what children should try","prompt":"Do you think it is important for boys to play with boys' toys and for girls to only play with girls' toys?\nDo you think it is good if parents only buy educational toys for their children?","answer":"Q: Do you think it is important for boys to play with boys&#x27; toys and for girls to only play with girls&#x27; toys?\nA: I think boys do <strong class=\"key-phrase\">naturally seem to prefer</strong> playing with &quot;boys&#x27; toys&quot; and girls with &quot;girls&#x27; toys&quot;, though how much of that is innate and how much is just modelled by parents, adverts and other children is genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hard to untangle</strong>. When I was young I had toy cars, trucks and toy guns, whereas my sister always wanted dolls, and neither of us was ever pushed towards those choices that I remember. I don&#x27;t suppose it really matters if a boy plays with a doll or a girl plays with a toy car, but children pick up very quickly what they&#x27;re &quot;supposed&quot; to like, and that&#x27;s the part I&#x27;d want to loosen up.\n\nQ: Do you think it is good if parents only buy educational toys for their children?\nA: No. Educational toys can be useful in moderation, but children also learn a great deal through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unstructured play</strong>. If every toy&#x27;s chosen for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an obvious academic purpose</strong>, play can turn into another lesson rather than a chance to experiment, invent rules and just enjoy themselves. Children lose interest fast, too, once they sense a toy&#x27;s really a test in disguise. In my experience the things they come back to are simple and open-ended, like building blocks or a box of old clothes, because those can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">become something different every time</strong> — a purpose-built &quot;educational&quot; toy usually can&#x27;t offer that same flexibility.","topic":"Children","phrases":[{"en":"naturally seem to prefer","context":"Q: Do you think it is important for boys to play with boys' toys and for girls to only play with girls' toys?\nA: I think boys do naturally seem to prefer playing with \"boys' toys\" and girls with \"girls' toys\", though how much of that is innate and how much is just modelled by parents, adverts and other children is genuinely hard to untangle. When I was young I had toy cars, trucks and toy guns, whereas my sister always wanted dolls, and neither of us was ever pushed towards those choices that I remember. I don't suppose it really matters if a boy plays with a doll or a girl plays with a toy car, but children pick up very quickly what they're \"supposed\" to like, and that's the part I'd want to loosen up."},{"en":"hard to untangle","context":"Q: Do you think it is important for boys to play with boys' toys and for girls to only play with girls' toys?\nA: I think boys do naturally seem to prefer playing with \"boys' toys\" and girls with \"girls' toys\", though how much of that is innate and how much is just modelled by parents, adverts and other children is genuinely hard to untangle. When I was young I had toy cars, trucks and toy guns, whereas my sister always wanted dolls, and neither of us was ever pushed towards those choices that I remember. I don't suppose it really matters if a boy plays with a doll or a girl plays with a toy car, but children pick up very quickly what they're \"supposed\" to like, and that's the part I'd want to loosen up."},{"en":"unstructured play","context":"Q: Do you think it is good if parents only buy educational toys for their children?\nA: No. Educational toys can be useful in moderation, but children also learn a great deal through unstructured play. If every toy's chosen for an obvious academic purpose, play can turn into another lesson rather than a chance to experiment, invent rules and just enjoy themselves. Children lose interest fast, too, once they sense a toy's really a test in disguise. In my experience the things they come back to are simple and open-ended, like building blocks or a box of old clothes, because those can become something different every time — a purpose-built \"educational\" toy usually can't offer that same flexibility."},{"en":"an obvious academic purpose","context":"Q: Do you think it is good if parents only buy educational toys for their children?\nA: No. Educational toys can be useful in moderation, but children also learn a great deal through unstructured play. If every toy's chosen for an obvious academic purpose, play can turn into another lesson rather than a chance to experiment, invent rules and just enjoy themselves. Children lose interest fast, too, once they sense a toy's really a test in disguise. In my experience the things they come back to are simple and open-ended, like building blocks or a box of old clothes, because those can become something different every time — a purpose-built \"educational\" toy usually can't offer that same flexibility."},{"en":"become something different every time","context":"Q: Do you think it is good if parents only buy educational toys for their children?\nA: No. Educational toys can be useful in moderation, but children also learn a great deal through unstructured play. If every toy's chosen for an obvious academic purpose, play can turn into another lesson rather than a chance to experiment, invent rules and just enjoy themselves. Children lose interest fast, too, once they sense a toy's really a test in disguise. In my experience the things they come back to are simple and open-ended, like building blocks or a box of old clothes, because those can become something different every time — a purpose-built \"educational\" toy usually can't offer that same flexibility."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Children","id":"speaking-part-3-015","_displayNo":"#015","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-015-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-015-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"No, I don't think those limits are necessary. Boys and girls may show different preferences, but parents, adverts and other children can influence them. I had cars and my sister liked dolls, yet that does not mean everyone should choose the same way. Children should feel free to try toys without worrying about what they are supposed to like.","phrases":[{"en":"preferences","meaning":"The things people like or choose more than others."}],"question":"Do you think it is important for boys to play with boys' toys and for girls to only play with girls' toys?","html":"No, I don&#x27;t think those limits are necessary. Boys and girls may show different <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preferences</strong>, but parents, adverts and other children can influence them. I had cars and my sister liked dolls, yet that does not mean everyone should choose the same way. Children should feel free to try toys without worrying about what they are supposed to like."},{"text":"No. Educational toys can help, but children also need play without a fixed lesson. Simple things such as blocks or old clothes can become many different games. If every toy feels like a test, children may lose interest. I would offer a mixture and let them experiment, imagine and enjoy themselves.","phrases":[{"en":"experiment","meaning":"Try different things to discover what happens or works."}],"question":"Do you think it is good if parents only buy educational toys for their children?","html":"No. Educational toys can help, but children also need play without a fixed lesson. Simple things such as blocks or old clothes can become many different games. If every toy feels like a test, children may lose interest. I would offer a mixture and let them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">experiment</strong>, imagine and enjoy themselves."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-015-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-015-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Encouraging children to try new things","prompt":"Do you think children today are encouraged to try new things?\nWhat can people do to make the best use of their life?\nWhat is the most important event for a teenager?","answer":"Q: Do you think children today are encouraged to try new things?\nA: Less than they should be, I&#x27;d say. Schools have become quite exam-focused, so anything that isn&#x27;t assessed gets squeezed out, and parents are more anxious about safety than they were a generation ago. There&#x27;s also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fear-of-failure problem</strong>: children praised for being clever often avoid anything they might be bad at, in case it proves them wrong. What works is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">low-stakes encouragement</strong> — letting a child try something with no expectation of being immediately good at it. Some children need none of this pushed on them, of course; a naturally curious kid will try new things regardless of how cautiously they&#x27;re raised.\n\nQ: What can people do to make the best use of their life?\nA: That&#x27;s a large question, but I&#x27;d start with health, because everything else depends on it and it&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far easier to protect than to recover</strong> once it&#x27;s gone. Then close relationships — the long-running research is fairly consistent that these <strong class=\"key-phrase\">predict life satisfaction better than income does</strong>; someone with a huge salary and nobody to share dinner with usually isn&#x27;t the happier one. And work that gives some sense of direction, which needn&#x27;t be a grand purpose. None of this is a fixed order for everyone, though — someone in real financial hardship reasonably has to prioritise income for a while before any of the rest becomes possible.\n\nQ: What is the most important event for a teenager?\nA: There&#x27;s no single event that applies to everybody, but finishing school and making the first real educational choice is significant for most. It&#x27;s usually the first decision with genuine long-term consequences that a young person makes largely alone. What makes it difficult is that they&#x27;re asked to choose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with very little information</strong> about what any of those paths actually involve day to day — nobody really knows what studying engineering is like until they&#x27;re two years into the degree. For others the defining event is more personal, like a first job or moving away from home for the first time, so it really varies by what a particular teenager&#x27;s life looks like.","topic":"Children","phrases":[{"en":"a fear-of-failure problem","context":"Q: Do you think children today are encouraged to try new things?\nA: Less than they should be, I'd say. Schools have become quite exam-focused, so anything that isn't assessed gets squeezed out, and parents are more anxious about safety than they were a generation ago. There's also a fear-of-failure problem: children praised for being clever often avoid anything they might be bad at, in case it proves them wrong. What works is low-stakes encouragement — letting a child try something with no expectation of being immediately good at it. Some children need none of this pushed on them, of course; a naturally curious kid will try new things regardless of how cautiously they're raised."},{"en":"low-stakes encouragement","context":"Q: Do you think children today are encouraged to try new things?\nA: Less than they should be, I'd say. Schools have become quite exam-focused, so anything that isn't assessed gets squeezed out, and parents are more anxious about safety than they were a generation ago. There's also a fear-of-failure problem: children praised for being clever often avoid anything they might be bad at, in case it proves them wrong. What works is low-stakes encouragement — letting a child try something with no expectation of being immediately good at it. Some children need none of this pushed on them, of course; a naturally curious kid will try new things regardless of how cautiously they're raised."},{"en":"far easier to protect than to recover","context":"Q: What can people do to make the best use of their life?\nA: That's a large question, but I'd start with health, because everything else depends on it and it's far easier to protect than to recover once it's gone. Then close relationships — the long-running research is fairly consistent that these predict life satisfaction better than income does; someone with a huge salary and nobody to share dinner with usually isn't the happier one. And work that gives some sense of direction, which needn't be a grand purpose. None of this is a fixed order for everyone, though — someone in real financial hardship reasonably has to prioritise income for a while before any of the rest becomes possible."},{"en":"predict life satisfaction better than income does","context":"Q: What can people do to make the best use of their life?\nA: That's a large question, but I'd start with health, because everything else depends on it and it's far easier to protect than to recover once it's gone. Then close relationships — the long-running research is fairly consistent that these predict life satisfaction better than income does; someone with a huge salary and nobody to share dinner with usually isn't the happier one. And work that gives some sense of direction, which needn't be a grand purpose. None of this is a fixed order for everyone, though — someone in real financial hardship reasonably has to prioritise income for a while before any of the rest becomes possible."},{"en":"with very little information","context":"Q: What is the most important event for a teenager?\nA: There's no single event that applies to everybody, but finishing school and making the first real educational choice is significant for most. It's usually the first decision with genuine long-term consequences that a young person makes largely alone. What makes it difficult is that they're asked to choose with very little information about what any of those paths actually involve day to day — nobody really knows what studying engineering is like until they're two years into the degree. For others the defining event is more personal, like a first job or moving away from home for the first time, so it really varies by what a particular teenager's life looks like."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Children","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-016","_displayNo":"#016","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-016-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-016-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-016-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Not enough, in my opinion. Exams can leave little room for other activities, and adults may worry about safety or failure. Children need chances to try something without having to be good immediately. Praising effort can help. Of course, some children are naturally curious and will explore even without much encouragement.","phrases":[{"en":"encouragement","meaning":"Support that helps someone feel willing or confident to try."}],"question":"Do you think children today are encouraged to try new things?","html":"Not enough, in my opinion. Exams can leave little room for other activities, and adults may worry about safety or failure. Children need chances to try something without having to be good immediately. Praising effort can help. Of course, some children are naturally curious and will explore even without much <strong class=\"key-phrase\">encouragement</strong>."},{"text":"I'd start with health, close relationships and work that gives life some direction. These can make everyday life more satisfying than simply earning more money. However, people have different needs. Someone struggling to pay for food and housing may reasonably focus on income first, before they have the freedom to think about other goals.","phrases":[{"en":"income","meaning":"Money received from work or other sources."}],"question":"What can people do to make the best use of their life?","html":"I&#x27;d start with health, close relationships and work that gives life some direction. These can make everyday life more satisfying than simply earning more money. However, people have different needs. Someone struggling to pay for food and housing may reasonably focus on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">income</strong> first, before they have the freedom to think about other goals."},{"text":"Finishing school and choosing further education is a major event for many teenagers. It can affect their future, although they often know little about what each course or career is really like. For others, a first job or moving away matters more. I don't think one event is equally important for every young person.","phrases":[{"en":"further education","meaning":"Study or training after finishing school."}],"question":"What is the most important event for a teenager?","html":"Finishing school and choosing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">further education</strong> is a major event for many teenagers. It can affect their future, although they often know little about what each course or career is really like. For others, a first job or moving away matters more. I don&#x27;t think one event is equally important for every young person."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-016-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-016-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-016-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Children's activities in a city and getting them interested","prompt":"What kinds of children's activities are there available in your city?\nWhat do you need to do to interest a child in such activities?\nHow do you think adults should improve their relations with children?","answer":"Q: What kinds of children&#x27;s activities are there available in your city?\nA: Quite a lot — sports clubs, swimming pools, music and art classes, libraries with children&#x27;s sections, and public parks. Larger cities also have science museums and coding workshops, which have become popular recently. The problem isn&#x27;t availability but access, though: plenty of the good ones charge fees a low-income family can&#x27;t manage, and they&#x27;re <strong class=\"key-phrase\">concentrated in central districts</strong>, so a child in an outer suburb <strong class=\"key-phrase\">faces a long journey</strong> just to get there, on top of the fee itself.\n\nQ: What do you need to do to interest a child in such activities?\nA: Start with something connected to what they already like, rather than what you think they should enjoy — a child obsessed with dinosaurs will happily sit through a museum trip that a &quot;get some culture&quot; lecture would never achieve. Then remove the pressure, because a child who feels they&#x27;re being assessed will usually refuse, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">praising effort rather than results</strong> matters a great deal, since a child praised for being talented stops attempting anything difficult. It doesn&#x27;t always work straightaway, though — plenty of children need to see a friend enjoying something first before they&#x27;re willing to try it themselves.\n\nQ: How do you think adults should improve their relations with children?\nA: The most effective thing is listening properly — not just waiting for your turn to reply, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taking what they say seriously</strong>, including complaints that seem trivial to an adult. Explaining rules helps too, since &quot;because I said so&quot; only produces compliance while an adult&#x27;s actually watching. And keeping promises matters, because children remember broken ones for years. None of this means removing boundaries, though; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consistent limits</strong> are part of what makes a child feel secure in the first place.","topic":"Children","phrases":[{"en":"concentrated in central districts","context":"Q: What kinds of children's activities are there available in your city?\nA: Quite a lot — sports clubs, swimming pools, music and art classes, libraries with children's sections, and public parks. Larger cities also have science museums and coding workshops, which have become popular recently. The problem isn't availability but access, though: plenty of the good ones charge fees a low-income family can't manage, and they're concentrated in central districts, so a child in an outer suburb faces a long journey just to get there, on top of the fee itself."},{"en":"faces a long journey","context":"Q: What kinds of children's activities are there available in your city?\nA: Quite a lot — sports clubs, swimming pools, music and art classes, libraries with children's sections, and public parks. Larger cities also have science museums and coding workshops, which have become popular recently. The problem isn't availability but access, though: plenty of the good ones charge fees a low-income family can't manage, and they're concentrated in central districts, so a child in an outer suburb faces a long journey just to get there, on top of the fee itself."},{"en":"praising effort rather than results","context":"Q: What do you need to do to interest a child in such activities?\nA: Start with something connected to what they already like, rather than what you think they should enjoy — a child obsessed with dinosaurs will happily sit through a museum trip that a \"get some culture\" lecture would never achieve. Then remove the pressure, because a child who feels they're being assessed will usually refuse, and praising effort rather than results matters a great deal, since a child praised for being talented stops attempting anything difficult. It doesn't always work straightaway, though — plenty of children need to see a friend enjoying something first before they're willing to try it themselves."},{"en":"taking what they say seriously","context":"Q: How do you think adults should improve their relations with children?\nA: The most effective thing is listening properly — not just waiting for your turn to reply, but taking what they say seriously, including complaints that seem trivial to an adult. Explaining rules helps too, since \"because I said so\" only produces compliance while an adult's actually watching. And keeping promises matters, because children remember broken ones for years. None of this means removing boundaries, though; consistent limits are part of what makes a child feel secure in the first place."},{"en":"consistent limits","context":"Q: How do you think adults should improve their relations with children?\nA: The most effective thing is listening properly — not just waiting for your turn to reply, but taking what they say seriously, including complaints that seem trivial to an adult. Explaining rules helps too, since \"because I said so\" only produces compliance while an adult's actually watching. And keeping promises matters, because children remember broken ones for years. None of this means removing boundaries, though; consistent limits are part of what makes a child feel secure in the first place."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Children","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-017","_displayNo":"#017","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-017-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-017-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-017-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"There are sports clubs, swimming pools, art and music classes, libraries and parks. Some larger cities also have science museums and computer workshops. However, not every child can use them. Fees may be too high, and many activities are far from outer neighbourhoods, so travel time and money can limit access.","phrases":[{"en":"access","meaning":"The opportunity or ability to use something."}],"question":"What kinds of children's activities are there available in your city?","html":"There are sports clubs, swimming pools, art and music classes, libraries and parks. Some larger cities also have science museums and computer workshops. However, not every child can use them. Fees may be too high, and many activities are far from outer neighbourhoods, so travel time and money can limit <strong class=\"key-phrase\">access</strong>."},{"text":"I'd begin with something the child already enjoys. A child interested in dinosaurs may like a museum more than a general lecture. It also helps to remove pressure and praise trying rather than winning. Some children need time, or may become interested only after seeing a friend enjoy the same activity.","phrases":[{"en":"remove pressure","meaning":"Stop making someone feel that they must succeed or perform."}],"question":"What do you need to do to interest a child in such activities?","html":"I&#x27;d begin with something the child already enjoys. A child interested in dinosaurs may like a museum more than a general lecture. It also helps to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remove pressure</strong> and praise trying rather than winning. Some children need time, or may become interested only after seeing a friend enjoy the same activity."},{"text":"Adults should listen seriously, explain rules and keep promises. Something that seems small to an adult may be important to a child, so dismissing it can damage trust. Listening does not mean allowing everything, though. Clear, consistent limits also help children feel safe because they know what to expect.","phrases":[{"en":"consistent limits","meaning":"Rules that stay clear and are applied in a similar way each time."}],"question":"How do you think adults should improve their relations with children?","html":"Adults should listen seriously, explain rules and keep promises. Something that seems small to an adult may be important to a child, so dismissing it can damage trust. Listening does not mean allowing everything, though. Clear, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consistent limits</strong> also help children feel safe because they know what to expect."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-017-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-017-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-017-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"City life against rural life, and making cities easier","prompt":"How is life different in cities compared to rural areas?\nHow do you think life in big cities could be made easier?\nWhat do you think cities will be like in 50 years time?","answer":"Q: How is life different in cities compared to rural areas?\nA: I&#x27;d point to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cost of living</strong> first — housing alone is far more expensive in cities than in rural areas, and pretty much everything else costs more too. There&#x27;s also more competition, for jobs and for space generally, simply because there are more people chasing the same things. Rural life moves a lot slower, people know their neighbours, and there&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of community</strong> that&#x27;s harder to find in a big, anonymous city. It&#x27;s not all one-sided, though — rural areas usually have far fewer services, from hospitals to decent internet, and isolation and lack of opportunity are real problems there too, just quieter ones than city crime.\n\nQ: How do you think life in big cities could be made easier?\nA: In my opinion cities need to be well-planned, above all. Good public transport can definitely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make life easier</strong>, because with so many people around, just getting from one side of the city to the other can be genuinely stressful otherwise. Public parks and green spaces matter too, since people need somewhere to actually relax rather than just commute and work. Affordable housing has to be part of it as well, because when ordinary workers can&#x27;t live anywhere near their jobs, everything else gets harder. None of this is quick or cheap, though — a proper transit system takes decades and enormous budgets to build, so most cities are stuck living with compromises for a long time before things genuinely improve.\n\nQ: What do you think cities will be like in 50 years time?\nA: I imagine cities will be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less polluted</strong> because we&#x27;ll have electric cars and far better public transport by then. More people might work from home too, so maybe cities won&#x27;t feel quite so packed during the day. But I think there&#x27;ll probably still be real problems, because more and more people are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">migrating to cities</strong> in search of work, so I&#x27;m not especially optimistic about issues like crime, homelessness and unemployment going away on their own. I&#x27;d also expect cities to be a good deal hotter, which is something planners are only just beginning to design for.","topic":"Cities","phrases":[{"en":"the cost of living","context":"Q: How is life different in cities compared to rural areas?\nA: I'd point to the cost of living first — housing alone is far more expensive in cities than in rural areas, and pretty much everything else costs more too. There's also more competition, for jobs and for space generally, simply because there are more people chasing the same things. Rural life moves a lot slower, people know their neighbours, and there's a sense of community that's harder to find in a big, anonymous city. It's not all one-sided, though — rural areas usually have far fewer services, from hospitals to decent internet, and isolation and lack of opportunity are real problems there too, just quieter ones than city crime."},{"en":"a sense of community","context":"Q: How is life different in cities compared to rural areas?\nA: I'd point to the cost of living first — housing alone is far more expensive in cities than in rural areas, and pretty much everything else costs more too. There's also more competition, for jobs and for space generally, simply because there are more people chasing the same things. Rural life moves a lot slower, people know their neighbours, and there's a sense of community that's harder to find in a big, anonymous city. It's not all one-sided, though — rural areas usually have far fewer services, from hospitals to decent internet, and isolation and lack of opportunity are real problems there too, just quieter ones than city crime."},{"en":"make life easier","context":"Q: How do you think life in big cities could be made easier?\nA: In my opinion cities need to be well-planned, above all. Good public transport can definitely make life easier, because with so many people around, just getting from one side of the city to the other can be genuinely stressful otherwise. Public parks and green spaces matter too, since people need somewhere to actually relax rather than just commute and work. Affordable housing has to be part of it as well, because when ordinary workers can't live anywhere near their jobs, everything else gets harder. None of this is quick or cheap, though — a proper transit system takes decades and enormous budgets to build, so most cities are stuck living with compromises for a long time before things genuinely improve."},{"en":"less polluted","context":"Q: What do you think cities will be like in 50 years time?\nA: I imagine cities will be less polluted because we'll have electric cars and far better public transport by then. More people might work from home too, so maybe cities won't feel quite so packed during the day. But I think there'll probably still be real problems, because more and more people are migrating to cities in search of work, so I'm not especially optimistic about issues like crime, homelessness and unemployment going away on their own. I'd also expect cities to be a good deal hotter, which is something planners are only just beginning to design for."},{"en":"migrating to cities","context":"Q: What do you think cities will be like in 50 years time?\nA: I imagine cities will be less polluted because we'll have electric cars and far better public transport by then. More people might work from home too, so maybe cities won't feel quite so packed during the day. But I think there'll probably still be real problems, because more and more people are migrating to cities in search of work, so I'm not especially optimistic about issues like crime, homelessness and unemployment going away on their own. I'd also expect cities to be a good deal hotter, which is something planners are only just beginning to design for."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Cities","id":"speaking-part-3-018","_displayNo":"#018","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-018-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-018-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-018-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"City life is usually more expensive and competitive, especially for housing and jobs. Rural areas often feel slower and more connected because neighbours know one another. However, they may have fewer hospitals, jobs and reliable internet services. Both places have difficulties; country problems may simply be less visible than those in a crowded city.","phrases":[{"en":"Rural areas","meaning":"Places in the countryside, outside towns and cities."}],"question":"How is life different in cities compared to rural areas?","html":"City life is usually more expensive and competitive, especially for housing and jobs. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Rural areas</strong> often feel slower and more connected because neighbours know one another. However, they may have fewer hospitals, jobs and reliable internet services. Both places have difficulties; country problems may simply be less visible than those in a crowded city."},{"text":"Cities need good public transport, green spaces and housing that ordinary workers can afford. These make travel easier and give people somewhere to relax. Better planning can bring homes and jobs closer together. However, major improvements cost a lot and take years, so residents may have to live with imperfect solutions for some time.","phrases":[{"en":"can afford","meaning":"Have enough money to pay for something."}],"question":"How do you think life in big cities could be made easier?","html":"Cities need good public transport, green spaces and housing that ordinary workers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">can afford</strong>. These make travel easier and give people somewhere to relax. Better planning can bring homes and jobs closer together. However, major improvements cost a lot and take years, so residents may have to live with imperfect solutions for some time."},{"text":"I expect cleaner vehicles and better transport, and perhaps more people working from home. That could reduce some pollution and crowding. However, growing populations may mean that homelessness, unemployment and crime remain problems. Cities may also become hotter, so I think future planning will need to deal with both population growth and heat.","phrases":[{"en":"unemployment","meaning":"The situation of people being unable to find work."}],"question":"What do you think cities will be like in 50 years time?","html":"I expect cleaner vehicles and better transport, and perhaps more people working from home. That could reduce some pollution and crowding. However, growing populations may mean that homelessness, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unemployment</strong> and crime remain problems. Cities may also become hotter, so I think future planning will need to deal with both population growth and heat."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-018-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-018-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-018-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"What makes a city worth living in","prompt":"What makes a city a good place to live?\nHow can crowded cities improve residents' living standards?","answer":"Q: What makes a city a good place to live?\nA: The basics come first — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">affordable housing</strong>, safe streets, transport that actually runs on time, and decent access to work and healthcare. Beyond that, I&#x27;d say the best cities manage to feel convenient without feeling anonymous. Somewhere with green space, local shops and places where people bump into each other tends to feel like a community rather than just a collection of buildings, and that&#x27;s what makes people want to stay long-term rather than just pass through. There&#x27;s often a trade-off, though — the neighbourhoods with the strongest sense of community are frequently the ones where housing&#x27;s least affordable, precisely because everyone wants to live there.\n\nQ: How can crowded cities improve residents&#x27; living standards?\nA: The most effective investment is usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mass transit</strong>, because it determines how far people can live from work without spending three hours a day commuting. After that I&#x27;d prioritise <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mixed-use neighbourhoods</strong>, so shops, schools and workplaces are all within walking distance, along with public housing and local parks. The underlying principle should be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spreading opportunity across every district</strong> rather than concentrating everything in one expensive centre — otherwise you just push the problem outwards instead of solving it. It&#x27;s rarely painless, though: better transport and new parks tend to raise property prices nearby, so the very investment meant to help existing residents can end up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pricing some of them out</strong>.","topic":"Cities","phrases":[{"en":"affordable housing","context":"Q: What makes a city a good place to live?\nA: The basics come first — affordable housing, safe streets, transport that actually runs on time, and decent access to work and healthcare. Beyond that, I'd say the best cities manage to feel convenient without feeling anonymous. Somewhere with green space, local shops and places where people bump into each other tends to feel like a community rather than just a collection of buildings, and that's what makes people want to stay long-term rather than just pass through. There's often a trade-off, though — the neighbourhoods with the strongest sense of community are frequently the ones where housing's least affordable, precisely because everyone wants to live there."},{"en":"mass transit","context":"Q: How can crowded cities improve residents' living standards?\nA: The most effective investment is usually mass transit, because it determines how far people can live from work without spending three hours a day commuting. After that I'd prioritise mixed-use neighbourhoods, so shops, schools and workplaces are all within walking distance, along with public housing and local parks. The underlying principle should be spreading opportunity across every district rather than concentrating everything in one expensive centre — otherwise you just push the problem outwards instead of solving it. It's rarely painless, though: better transport and new parks tend to raise property prices nearby, so the very investment meant to help existing residents can end up pricing some of them out."},{"en":"mixed-use neighbourhoods","context":"Q: How can crowded cities improve residents' living standards?\nA: The most effective investment is usually mass transit, because it determines how far people can live from work without spending three hours a day commuting. After that I'd prioritise mixed-use neighbourhoods, so shops, schools and workplaces are all within walking distance, along with public housing and local parks. The underlying principle should be spreading opportunity across every district rather than concentrating everything in one expensive centre — otherwise you just push the problem outwards instead of solving it. It's rarely painless, though: better transport and new parks tend to raise property prices nearby, so the very investment meant to help existing residents can end up pricing some of them out."},{"en":"spreading opportunity across every district","context":"Q: How can crowded cities improve residents' living standards?\nA: The most effective investment is usually mass transit, because it determines how far people can live from work without spending three hours a day commuting. After that I'd prioritise mixed-use neighbourhoods, so shops, schools and workplaces are all within walking distance, along with public housing and local parks. The underlying principle should be spreading opportunity across every district rather than concentrating everything in one expensive centre — otherwise you just push the problem outwards instead of solving it. It's rarely painless, though: better transport and new parks tend to raise property prices nearby, so the very investment meant to help existing residents can end up pricing some of them out."},{"en":"pricing some of them out","context":"Q: How can crowded cities improve residents' living standards?\nA: The most effective investment is usually mass transit, because it determines how far people can live from work without spending three hours a day commuting. After that I'd prioritise mixed-use neighbourhoods, so shops, schools and workplaces are all within walking distance, along with public housing and local parks. The underlying principle should be spreading opportunity across every district rather than concentrating everything in one expensive centre — otherwise you just push the problem outwards instead of solving it. It's rarely painless, though: better transport and new parks tend to raise property prices nearby, so the very investment meant to help existing residents can end up pricing some of them out."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Cities","id":"speaking-part-3-019","_displayNo":"#019","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-019-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-019-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"It needs affordable homes, safe streets, reliable transport, jobs and healthcare. Beyond those basics, local shops, parks and meeting places help people feel part of a community. A city should be convenient without making everyone feel like strangers. Unfortunately, the most attractive neighbourhoods often become expensive because so many people want to live there.","phrases":[{"en":"community","meaning":"People connected by living in the same place or sharing interests."}],"question":"What makes a city a good place to live?","html":"It needs affordable homes, safe streets, reliable transport, jobs and healthcare. Beyond those basics, local shops, parks and meeting places help people feel part of a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">community</strong>. A city should be convenient without making everyone feel like strangers. Unfortunately, the most attractive neighbourhoods often become expensive because so many people want to live there."},{"text":"Better public transport lets people reach work without very long journeys. Cities also need neighbourhoods where shops, schools and parks are close to homes, including affordable housing. Improvements should reach different districts rather than only the centre. However, new parks or stations can raise rents, so existing residents may need protection from being forced to move.","phrases":[{"en":"residents","meaning":"People who live in a particular place."}],"question":"How can crowded cities improve residents' living standards?","html":"Better public transport lets people reach work without very long journeys. Cities also need neighbourhoods where shops, schools and parks are close to homes, including affordable housing. Improvements should reach different districts rather than only the centre. However, new parks or stations can raise rents, so existing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">residents</strong> may need protection from being forced to move."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-019-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-019-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Migration from the countryside to the cities","prompt":"Do you think large-scale migration from the countryside to cities is positive or negative?\nDo you think that the possibility of working from home via the internet will lead to many people going back to the countryside?\nIn what ways do the new megacities of Asia, Africa, and South America differ from older ones such as London or New York?","answer":"Q: Do you think large-scale migration from the countryside to cities is positive or negative?\nA: On balance, positive for the individuals, and difficult for the places at both ends. People move because cities offer work, wages and schooling that simply don&#x27;t exist in rural areas, and it&#x27;d be patronising to say they shouldn&#x27;t. The problem&#x27;s speed: when a city grows faster than its housing and transport can keep up, migrants end up in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">informal settlements</strong> with poor sanitation, and rural areas lose exactly the young workers they needed to keep going.\n\nQ: Do you think that the possibility of working from home via the internet will lead to many people going back to the countryside?\nA: Some, but not the large-scale reversal people predicted. Remote work does let a certain group — office workers with decent salaries — choose cheaper housing and more space, and there was a visible movement in that direction just after the pandemic. But it stalls for practical reasons: schools, hospitals, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">patchy internet</strong>, and the fact that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">most jobs still cannot be done remotely</strong>. There&#x27;s also isolation, which people underestimate until they&#x27;ve actually experienced it, so the shift looks real but partial rather than any kind of wholesale return to rural life.\n\nQ: In what ways do the new megacities of Asia, Africa, and South America differ from older ones such as London or New York?\nA: The main difference is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the speed of growth relative to infrastructure</strong>. London expanded over centuries, so its transport and sanitation had time to develop alongside the population. Cities like Lagos or Dhaka have added millions of people within a few decades, so a large proportion of residents end up in informal housing that the city never really planned for. Older cities have serious inequality too, and it&#x27;d be wrong to romanticise them — but they generally do possess <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mature institutions</strong> and transport networks that the newer megacities are still very much in the middle of building.","topic":"Cities","phrases":[{"en":"informal settlements","context":"Q: Do you think large-scale migration from the countryside to cities is positive or negative?\nA: On balance, positive for the individuals, and difficult for the places at both ends. People move because cities offer work, wages and schooling that simply don't exist in rural areas, and it'd be patronising to say they shouldn't. The problem's speed: when a city grows faster than its housing and transport can keep up, migrants end up in informal settlements with poor sanitation, and rural areas lose exactly the young workers they needed to keep going."},{"en":"patchy internet","context":"Q: Do you think that the possibility of working from home via the internet will lead to many people going back to the countryside?\nA: Some, but not the large-scale reversal people predicted. Remote work does let a certain group — office workers with decent salaries — choose cheaper housing and more space, and there was a visible movement in that direction just after the pandemic. But it stalls for practical reasons: schools, hospitals, patchy internet, and the fact that most jobs still cannot be done remotely. There's also isolation, which people underestimate until they've actually experienced it, so the shift looks real but partial rather than any kind of wholesale return to rural life."},{"en":"most jobs still cannot be done remotely","context":"Q: Do you think that the possibility of working from home via the internet will lead to many people going back to the countryside?\nA: Some, but not the large-scale reversal people predicted. Remote work does let a certain group — office workers with decent salaries — choose cheaper housing and more space, and there was a visible movement in that direction just after the pandemic. But it stalls for practical reasons: schools, hospitals, patchy internet, and the fact that most jobs still cannot be done remotely. There's also isolation, which people underestimate until they've actually experienced it, so the shift looks real but partial rather than any kind of wholesale return to rural life."},{"en":"the speed of growth relative to infrastructure","context":"Q: In what ways do the new megacities of Asia, Africa, and South America differ from older ones such as London or New York?\nA: The main difference is the speed of growth relative to infrastructure. London expanded over centuries, so its transport and sanitation had time to develop alongside the population. Cities like Lagos or Dhaka have added millions of people within a few decades, so a large proportion of residents end up in informal housing that the city never really planned for. Older cities have serious inequality too, and it'd be wrong to romanticise them — but they generally do possess mature institutions and transport networks that the newer megacities are still very much in the middle of building."},{"en":"mature institutions","context":"Q: In what ways do the new megacities of Asia, Africa, and South America differ from older ones such as London or New York?\nA: The main difference is the speed of growth relative to infrastructure. London expanded over centuries, so its transport and sanitation had time to develop alongside the population. Cities like Lagos or Dhaka have added millions of people within a few decades, so a large proportion of residents end up in informal housing that the city never really planned for. Older cities have serious inequality too, and it'd be wrong to romanticise them — but they generally do possess mature institutions and transport networks that the newer megacities are still very much in the middle of building."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Cities","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-020","_displayNo":"#020","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-020-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-020-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-020-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"It can benefit individuals because cities offer better jobs, wages and education. However, rapid movement puts pressure on both places. Cities may not build homes and services quickly enough, while rural areas lose young workers. I think moving is a reasonable choice, but governments need to manage the speed of growth and support the communities affected.","phrases":[{"en":"puts pressure on","meaning":"Creates extra demands that are difficult to manage."}],"question":"Do you think large-scale migration from the countryside to cities is positive or negative?","html":"It can benefit individuals because cities offer better jobs, wages and education. However, rapid movement <strong class=\"key-phrase\">puts pressure on</strong> both places. Cities may not build homes and services quickly enough, while rural areas lose young workers. I think moving is a reasonable choice, but governments need to manage the speed of growth and support the communities affected."},{"text":"Some people will move back, but probably not most. Online work can give office workers cheaper homes and more space. However, many jobs require people to be present, and rural areas may lack schools, hospitals or good internet. Living far from others can also be lonely. So remote work creates more choice, rather than a complete return to country life.","phrases":[{"en":"remote work","meaning":"Work done away from a usual workplace, often using the internet."}],"question":"Do you think that the possibility of working from home via the internet will lead to many people going back to the countryside?","html":"Some people will move back, but probably not most. Online work can give office workers cheaper homes and more space. However, many jobs require people to be present, and rural areas may lack schools, hospitals or good internet. Living far from others can also be lonely. So <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remote work</strong> creates more choice, rather than a complete return to country life."},{"text":"Many newer megacities have grown much faster than their roads, transport and basic services. Cities such as Lagos or Dhaka have gained millions of residents in a short time. Older cities developed more gradually, giving systems longer to grow. They still have inequality, but usually have more established institutions and transport networks.","phrases":[{"en":"basic services","meaning":"Essential facilities such as clean water, sanitation and public transport."}],"question":"In what ways do the new megacities of Asia, Africa, and South America differ from older ones such as London or New York?","html":"Many newer megacities have grown much faster than their roads, transport and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">basic services</strong>. Cities such as Lagos or Dhaka have gained millions of residents in a short time. Older cities developed more gradually, giving systems longer to grow. They still have inequality, but usually have more established institutions and transport networks."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-020-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-020-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-020-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Collections and what they may become worth","prompt":"Do you think you will ever have the biggest collection in the world?\nHow valuable might your collection become in the future?\nWhy do you think some people collect things?","answer":"Q: Do you think you will ever have the biggest collection in the world?\nA: No, and honestly that&#x27;s never been the appeal. Once size becomes the objective you end up buying things indiscriminately just to push the count up, and the collection stops meaning anything. What I enjoy is the opposite — finding one item that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fills an obvious gap</strong>, or that comes with a story about how I got it. I&#x27;ll admit that&#x27;s a fairly personal definition of success, and a few serious collectors genuinely do build <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exhaustive collections</strong> that have real historical value — mine was just never going to be one of them.\n\nQ: How valuable might your collection become in the future?\nA: Genuinely impossible to predict, and I&#x27;d be suspicious of anyone who claims otherwise. Collectors&#x27; markets depend entirely on taste, and things that were valuable a generation ago can become almost worthless once the people who wanted them stop buying. So I treat any financial return as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a bonus rather than a plan</strong> — if I&#x27;d wanted a proper investment, I&#x27;d have bought something with a proven track record instead. That said, a handful of narrow categories, first editions of certain authors for instance, have held their value remarkably well, so it isn&#x27;t pure guesswork.\n\nQ: Why do you think some people collect things?\nA: A few different motives, really. Nostalgia is a common one — people collect objects from their childhood, and what they&#x27;re really holding onto is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a period rather than the objects</strong>. Others enjoy the systematic side: researching, organising, becoming genuinely expert in something narrow, and that need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">complete a set</strong> can be satisfying in itself. There&#x27;s a social element too, since collectors form strong communities. It becomes a problem when it turns compulsive and people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spend money they do not have</strong> chasing the next item.","topic":"Collecting","phrases":[{"en":"fills an obvious gap","context":"Q: Do you think you will ever have the biggest collection in the world?\nA: No, and honestly that's never been the appeal. Once size becomes the objective you end up buying things indiscriminately just to push the count up, and the collection stops meaning anything. What I enjoy is the opposite — finding one item that fills an obvious gap, or that comes with a story about how I got it. I'll admit that's a fairly personal definition of success, and a few serious collectors genuinely do build exhaustive collections that have real historical value — mine was just never going to be one of them."},{"en":"exhaustive collections","context":"Q: Do you think you will ever have the biggest collection in the world?\nA: No, and honestly that's never been the appeal. Once size becomes the objective you end up buying things indiscriminately just to push the count up, and the collection stops meaning anything. What I enjoy is the opposite — finding one item that fills an obvious gap, or that comes with a story about how I got it. I'll admit that's a fairly personal definition of success, and a few serious collectors genuinely do build exhaustive collections that have real historical value — mine was just never going to be one of them."},{"en":"a bonus rather than a plan","context":"Q: How valuable might your collection become in the future?\nA: Genuinely impossible to predict, and I'd be suspicious of anyone who claims otherwise. Collectors' markets depend entirely on taste, and things that were valuable a generation ago can become almost worthless once the people who wanted them stop buying. So I treat any financial return as a bonus rather than a plan — if I'd wanted a proper investment, I'd have bought something with a proven track record instead. That said, a handful of narrow categories, first editions of certain authors for instance, have held their value remarkably well, so it isn't pure guesswork."},{"en":"a period rather than the objects","context":"Q: Why do you think some people collect things?\nA: A few different motives, really. Nostalgia is a common one — people collect objects from their childhood, and what they're really holding onto is a period rather than the objects. Others enjoy the systematic side: researching, organising, becoming genuinely expert in something narrow, and that need to complete a set can be satisfying in itself. There's a social element too, since collectors form strong communities. It becomes a problem when it turns compulsive and people spend money they do not have chasing the next item."},{"en":"complete a set","context":"Q: Why do you think some people collect things?\nA: A few different motives, really. Nostalgia is a common one — people collect objects from their childhood, and what they're really holding onto is a period rather than the objects. Others enjoy the systematic side: researching, organising, becoming genuinely expert in something narrow, and that need to complete a set can be satisfying in itself. There's a social element too, since collectors form strong communities. It becomes a problem when it turns compulsive and people spend money they do not have chasing the next item."},{"en":"spend money they do not have","context":"Q: Why do you think some people collect things?\nA: A few different motives, really. Nostalgia is a common one — people collect objects from their childhood, and what they're really holding onto is a period rather than the objects. Others enjoy the systematic side: researching, organising, becoming genuinely expert in something narrow, and that need to complete a set can be satisfying in itself. There's a social element too, since collectors form strong communities. It becomes a problem when it turns compulsive and people spend money they do not have chasing the next item."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Collecting","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-021","_displayNo":"#021","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-021-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-021-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-021-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"No, and that is not my goal. I enjoy finding an item that fills a gap or has a personal story, rather than buying anything just to increase the number. Some very large collections do have historical value. Mine is mainly about personal interest, so becoming the biggest would not make it more enjoyable for me.","phrases":[{"en":"fills a gap","meaning":"Provides something that was missing from a group or collection."}],"question":"Do you think you will ever have the biggest collection in the world?","html":"No, and that is not my goal. I enjoy finding an item that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fills a gap</strong> or has a personal story, rather than buying anything just to increase the number. Some very large collections do have historical value. Mine is mainly about personal interest, so becoming the biggest would not make it more enjoyable for me."},{"text":"It is difficult to know because prices depend on what future collectors want. An object valued today may become unpopular later. I would see any profit as an extra benefit, not the main purpose. Some special items, such as certain first-edition books, keep their value well, but I would not assume that my whole collection will do the same.","phrases":[{"en":"keep their value","meaning":"Continue to be worth a similar amount of money."}],"question":"How valuable might your collection become in the future?","html":"It is difficult to know because prices depend on what future collectors want. An object valued today may become unpopular later. I would see any profit as an extra benefit, not the main purpose. Some special items, such as certain first-edition books, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep their value</strong> well, but I would not assume that my whole collection will do the same."},{"text":"Some people collect reminders of childhood. Others enjoy researching a subject, arranging objects or completing a set. Collecting can also introduce them to people with the same interests. These benefits can be enjoyable, but problems start if the desire for another item becomes too strong and people spend money they cannot afford.","phrases":[{"en":"completing a set","meaning":"Obtaining all the items that belong to a particular group."}],"question":"Why do you think some people collect things?","html":"Some people collect reminders of childhood. Others enjoy researching a subject, arranging objects or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completing a set</strong>. Collecting can also introduce them to people with the same interests. These benefits can be enjoyable, but problems start if the desire for another item becomes too strong and people spend money they cannot afford."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-021-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-021-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-021-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Ways of communicating, and how technology changed them","prompt":"What are the different ways people communicate?\nDo you prefer to write a letter to a friend or send an email and why?\nHow has technology affected the way people communicate?","answer":"Q: What are the different ways people communicate?\nA: The obvious ones are face-to-face conversation, phone calls, messaging apps, email and video calls, plus formal written documents at work. What interests me is that people have developed quite specific unwritten rules about which to use. A message is fine for arranging something, a phone call&#x27;s for when it&#x27;s urgent, and anything difficult or emotional really needs a face-to-face conversation. Using <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the wrong channel</strong> — resigning by email, for instance — is read as a statement in itself. Those rules aren&#x27;t fixed, though: video calls have blurred the line, and what counts as acceptable <strong class=\"key-phrase\">varies a lot by generation</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you prefer to write a letter to a friend or send an email and why?\nA: Email, for anything ordinary, because it&#x27;s instant and I can find it again months later. But I&#x27;d write an actual letter for something significant, like a condolence or properly thanking someone. The reason is that a letter takes effort, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the effort is part of the message</strong> — the person knows you sat down and gave them twenty minutes, not two. An email can carry that too, if you genuinely slow down and write it properly, but most of us don&#x27;t, which is exactly why the letter still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stands out</strong>.\n\nQ: How has technology affected the way people communicate?\nA: It&#x27;s made communication constant and global, which is largely positive, since families spread across countries can speak every day. The costs are subtler, though. We&#x27;ve become less patient, because anything slower than instant feels broken, and a lot of communication has moved to text, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strips out tone</strong> completely — so misunderstandings happen constantly over messages that would&#x27;ve been perfectly clear <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spoken aloud</strong>. Difficult conversations still need a real voice or a face, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no amount of messaging</strong> replaces that.","topic":"Communication","phrases":[{"en":"the wrong channel","context":"Q: What are the different ways people communicate?\nA: The obvious ones are face-to-face conversation, phone calls, messaging apps, email and video calls, plus formal written documents at work. What interests me is that people have developed quite specific unwritten rules about which to use. A message is fine for arranging something, a phone call's for when it's urgent, and anything difficult or emotional really needs a face-to-face conversation. Using the wrong channel — resigning by email, for instance — is read as a statement in itself. Those rules aren't fixed, though: video calls have blurred the line, and what counts as acceptable varies a lot by generation."},{"en":"varies a lot by generation","context":"Q: What are the different ways people communicate?\nA: The obvious ones are face-to-face conversation, phone calls, messaging apps, email and video calls, plus formal written documents at work. What interests me is that people have developed quite specific unwritten rules about which to use. A message is fine for arranging something, a phone call's for when it's urgent, and anything difficult or emotional really needs a face-to-face conversation. Using the wrong channel — resigning by email, for instance — is read as a statement in itself. Those rules aren't fixed, though: video calls have blurred the line, and what counts as acceptable varies a lot by generation."},{"en":"the effort is part of the message","context":"Q: Do you prefer to write a letter to a friend or send an email and why?\nA: Email, for anything ordinary, because it's instant and I can find it again months later. But I'd write an actual letter for something significant, like a condolence or properly thanking someone. The reason is that a letter takes effort, and the effort is part of the message — the person knows you sat down and gave them twenty minutes, not two. An email can carry that too, if you genuinely slow down and write it properly, but most of us don't, which is exactly why the letter still stands out."},{"en":"stands out","context":"Q: Do you prefer to write a letter to a friend or send an email and why?\nA: Email, for anything ordinary, because it's instant and I can find it again months later. But I'd write an actual letter for something significant, like a condolence or properly thanking someone. The reason is that a letter takes effort, and the effort is part of the message — the person knows you sat down and gave them twenty minutes, not two. An email can carry that too, if you genuinely slow down and write it properly, but most of us don't, which is exactly why the letter still stands out."},{"en":"strips out tone","context":"Q: How has technology affected the way people communicate?\nA: It's made communication constant and global, which is largely positive, since families spread across countries can speak every day. The costs are subtler, though. We've become less patient, because anything slower than instant feels broken, and a lot of communication has moved to text, which strips out tone completely — so misunderstandings happen constantly over messages that would've been perfectly clear spoken aloud. Difficult conversations still need a real voice or a face, and no amount of messaging replaces that."},{"en":"spoken aloud","context":"Q: How has technology affected the way people communicate?\nA: It's made communication constant and global, which is largely positive, since families spread across countries can speak every day. The costs are subtler, though. We've become less patient, because anything slower than instant feels broken, and a lot of communication has moved to text, which strips out tone completely — so misunderstandings happen constantly over messages that would've been perfectly clear spoken aloud. Difficult conversations still need a real voice or a face, and no amount of messaging replaces that."},{"en":"no amount of messaging","context":"Q: How has technology affected the way people communicate?\nA: It's made communication constant and global, which is largely positive, since families spread across countries can speak every day. The costs are subtler, though. We've become less patient, because anything slower than instant feels broken, and a lot of communication has moved to text, which strips out tone completely — so misunderstandings happen constantly over messages that would've been perfectly clear spoken aloud. Difficult conversations still need a real voice or a face, and no amount of messaging replaces that."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Communication","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-022","_displayNo":"#022","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-022-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-022-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-022-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"People communicate in person, by phone, messages, email and video calls, as well as through formal documents. They usually choose according to the situation: a text for plans, or a call for something urgent. Difficult conversations may need a face-to-face meeting. However, these habits vary between generations and change as new technology becomes normal.","phrases":[{"en":"face-to-face","meaning":"With people physically present together."}],"question":"What are the different ways people communicate?","html":"People communicate in person, by phone, messages, email and video calls, as well as through formal documents. They usually choose according to the situation: a text for plans, or a call for something urgent. Difficult conversations may need a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">face-to-face</strong> meeting. However, these habits vary between generations and change as new technology becomes normal."},{"text":"For ordinary news, I prefer email because it is quick and easy to find later. For something important, like expressing sympathy or giving special thanks, I might write a letter. The time and care involved make it feel more personal. An email can also be thoughtful, but people often write those much more quickly.","phrases":[{"en":"expressing sympathy","meaning":"Showing care for someone who is experiencing sadness or difficulty."}],"question":"Do you prefer to write a letter to a friend or send an email and why?","html":"For ordinary news, I prefer email because it is quick and easy to find later. For something important, like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expressing sympathy</strong> or giving special thanks, I might write a letter. The time and care involved make it feel more personal. An email can also be thoughtful, but people often write those much more quickly."},{"text":"Technology lets people communicate instantly across the world, which helps families stay close. However, people may expect immediate replies and become less patient. Written messages also hide tone, so misunderstandings are easier. For something sensitive or difficult, hearing a voice or seeing a face is still valuable; sending more messages may not solve the problem.","phrases":[{"en":"tone","meaning":"The feeling or attitude shown by how someone speaks."}],"question":"How has technology affected the way people communicate?","html":"Technology lets people communicate instantly across the world, which helps families stay close. However, people may expect immediate replies and become less patient. Written messages also hide <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tone</strong>, so misunderstandings are easier. For something sensitive or difficult, hearing a voice or seeing a face is still valuable; sending more messages may not solve the problem."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-022-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-022-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-022-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Competition in the classroom and prizes for children","prompt":"Why do you think some school teachers use competitions as class activities?\nIs it a good thing to give prizes to children who do well at school and why?\nWould you say that schools for young children have become more or less competitive since you were that age and why?","answer":"Q: Why do you think some school teachers use competitions as class activities?\nA: I think teachers use competitions mainly to motivate the children in their classes. I&#x27;m sure teachers try all kinds of activities to engage their pupils, and competitions might be one of the best ways to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep children interested</strong> or get them excited, because children love winning things and it changes the whole atmosphere of a lesson. A class that&#x27;s gone completely quiet will suddenly come alive if you turn the same exercise into a team game, even though the content&#x27;s identical. It doesn&#x27;t always work, though — a child who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never wins</strong> can switch off just as quickly as one who&#x27;s fired up by it.\n\nQ: Is it a good thing to give prizes to children who do well at school and why?\nA: It might be a good idea to reward children for doing well in games or sports, but I don&#x27;t think we should give prizes for their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">academic work</strong>. Children need to learn that the point of studying is to learn useful things that&#x27;ll help them later in life, not to collect prizes. I&#x27;ve seen what happens when rewards become routine: a child who&#x27;s used to getting a sticker every time stops trying the moment the stickers disappear, because the work itself was never the point. Occasional, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">low-key recognition</strong> for effort still seems far less risky than a formal prize system, though.\n\nQ: Would you say that schools for young children have become more or less competitive since you were that age and why?\nA: Schools for young children do seem more competitive than they were in the past. Testing begins earlier, parents compare results more closely, and some children start extra classes before they&#x27;ve even developed a proper attitude to learning — I&#x27;ve seen five-year-olds doing maths worksheets after school simply because a neighbour&#x27;s child is doing the same. That pressure can improve short-term performance, but it often comes at the cost of curiosity. Parents feel it too: they see other families paying for extra classes and worry their own child will <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fall behind</strong>, and that anxiety spreads fast, so it&#x27;s very hard for one family to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opt out alone</strong>.","topic":"Competition","phrases":[{"en":"keep children interested","context":"Q: Why do you think some school teachers use competitions as class activities?\nA: I think teachers use competitions mainly to motivate the children in their classes. I'm sure teachers try all kinds of activities to engage their pupils, and competitions might be one of the best ways to keep children interested or get them excited, because children love winning things and it changes the whole atmosphere of a lesson. A class that's gone completely quiet will suddenly come alive if you turn the same exercise into a team game, even though the content's identical. It doesn't always work, though — a child who never wins can switch off just as quickly as one who's fired up by it."},{"en":"never wins","context":"Q: Why do you think some school teachers use competitions as class activities?\nA: I think teachers use competitions mainly to motivate the children in their classes. I'm sure teachers try all kinds of activities to engage their pupils, and competitions might be one of the best ways to keep children interested or get them excited, because children love winning things and it changes the whole atmosphere of a lesson. A class that's gone completely quiet will suddenly come alive if you turn the same exercise into a team game, even though the content's identical. It doesn't always work, though — a child who never wins can switch off just as quickly as one who's fired up by it."},{"en":"academic work","context":"Q: Is it a good thing to give prizes to children who do well at school and why?\nA: It might be a good idea to reward children for doing well in games or sports, but I don't think we should give prizes for their academic work. Children need to learn that the point of studying is to learn useful things that'll help them later in life, not to collect prizes. I've seen what happens when rewards become routine: a child who's used to getting a sticker every time stops trying the moment the stickers disappear, because the work itself was never the point. Occasional, low-key recognition for effort still seems far less risky than a formal prize system, though."},{"en":"low-key recognition","context":"Q: Is it a good thing to give prizes to children who do well at school and why?\nA: It might be a good idea to reward children for doing well in games or sports, but I don't think we should give prizes for their academic work. Children need to learn that the point of studying is to learn useful things that'll help them later in life, not to collect prizes. I've seen what happens when rewards become routine: a child who's used to getting a sticker every time stops trying the moment the stickers disappear, because the work itself was never the point. Occasional, low-key recognition for effort still seems far less risky than a formal prize system, though."},{"en":"fall behind","context":"Q: Would you say that schools for young children have become more or less competitive since you were that age and why?\nA: Schools for young children do seem more competitive than they were in the past. Testing begins earlier, parents compare results more closely, and some children start extra classes before they've even developed a proper attitude to learning — I've seen five-year-olds doing maths worksheets after school simply because a neighbour's child is doing the same. That pressure can improve short-term performance, but it often comes at the cost of curiosity. Parents feel it too: they see other families paying for extra classes and worry their own child will fall behind, and that anxiety spreads fast, so it's very hard for one family to opt out alone."},{"en":"opt out alone","context":"Q: Would you say that schools for young children have become more or less competitive since you were that age and why?\nA: Schools for young children do seem more competitive than they were in the past. Testing begins earlier, parents compare results more closely, and some children start extra classes before they've even developed a proper attitude to learning — I've seen five-year-olds doing maths worksheets after school simply because a neighbour's child is doing the same. That pressure can improve short-term performance, but it often comes at the cost of curiosity. Parents feel it too: they see other families paying for extra classes and worry their own child will fall behind, and that anxiety spreads fast, so it's very hard for one family to opt out alone."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Competition","id":"speaking-part-3-023","_displayNo":"#023","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-023-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-023-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-023-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Competitions can make a lesson more exciting and encourage children to join in. The same exercise may feel more enjoyable as a team game because pupils want to win. However, teachers need to be careful. Children who repeatedly lose may stop trying, so competition should support learning rather than make some students feel incapable.","phrases":[{"en":"join in","meaning":"Take part in an activity with other people."}],"question":"Why do you think some school teachers use competitions as class activities?","html":"Competitions can make a lesson more exciting and encourage children to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">join in</strong>. The same exercise may feel more enjoyable as a team game because pupils want to win. However, teachers need to be careful. Children who repeatedly lose may stop trying, so competition should support learning rather than make some students feel incapable."},{"text":"Prizes may be useful in games or sport, but I would be careful about rewarding every good piece of schoolwork. Children should value learning itself. If they expect a prize each time, they may stop trying when rewards disappear. Occasional praise for effort seems more helpful than making academic work depend on winning something.","phrases":[{"en":"effort","meaning":"The energy and care you put into doing something."}],"question":"Is it a good thing to give prizes to children who do well at school and why?","html":"Prizes may be useful in games or sport, but I would be careful about rewarding every good piece of schoolwork. Children should value learning itself. If they expect a prize each time, they may stop trying when rewards disappear. Occasional praise for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">effort</strong> seems more helpful than making academic work depend on winning something."},{"text":"I think they have become more competitive. Testing starts earlier, parents compare results and children attend extra classes. This may improve marks for a while, but it can reduce curiosity and create anxiety. Parents also worry when other families do more, so it is difficult for one family to avoid the pressure alone.","phrases":[{"en":"curiosity","meaning":"The desire to learn or understand new things."}],"question":"Would you say that schools for young children have become more or less competitive since you were that age and why?","html":"I think they have become more competitive. Testing starts earlier, parents compare results and children attend extra classes. This may improve marks for a while, but it can reduce <strong class=\"key-phrase\">curiosity</strong> and create anxiety. Parents also worry when other families do more, so it is difficult for one family to avoid the pressure alone."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-023-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-023-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-023-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Entering competitions","prompt":"Do you enjoy entering competitions?\nHave you entered any other competitions?","answer":"Q: Do you enjoy entering competitions?\nA: I do, provided the competition <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tests a genuine skill</strong> and gives you something back for entering. What I like most is the deadline, because it forces a level of preparation I&#x27;d never manage on my own — I once signed up for a local photography competition and it was the only thing that got me out shooting every weekend for a month. I&#x27;m much less keen on events decided largely by luck, a prize draw, say, since there&#x27;s nothing to learn from the result either way. For me the point should be that you&#x27;re <strong class=\"key-phrase\">better afterwards than you were before</strong>, not simply where you finished.\n\nQ: Have you entered any other competitions?\nA: Yes, mostly academic and language competitions when I was at school — essay writing and public speaking, that sort of thing. Honestly, I didn&#x27;t win very often, but the preparation was where the real value lay. I learned to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">plan my time properly</strong>, to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">perform under pressure</strong>, and to look at a poor result and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work out what actually went wrong</strong> instead of simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feeling discouraged</strong>. Those habits turned out to matter far more than any of the results ever did, though I&#x27;ll admit it would&#x27;ve been nice to win occasionally too.","topic":"Competition","phrases":[{"en":"tests a genuine skill","context":"Q: Do you enjoy entering competitions?\nA: I do, provided the competition tests a genuine skill and gives you something back for entering. What I like most is the deadline, because it forces a level of preparation I'd never manage on my own — I once signed up for a local photography competition and it was the only thing that got me out shooting every weekend for a month. I'm much less keen on events decided largely by luck, a prize draw, say, since there's nothing to learn from the result either way. For me the point should be that you're better afterwards than you were before, not simply where you finished."},{"en":"better afterwards than you were before","context":"Q: Do you enjoy entering competitions?\nA: I do, provided the competition tests a genuine skill and gives you something back for entering. What I like most is the deadline, because it forces a level of preparation I'd never manage on my own — I once signed up for a local photography competition and it was the only thing that got me out shooting every weekend for a month. I'm much less keen on events decided largely by luck, a prize draw, say, since there's nothing to learn from the result either way. For me the point should be that you're better afterwards than you were before, not simply where you finished."},{"en":"plan my time properly","context":"Q: Have you entered any other competitions?\nA: Yes, mostly academic and language competitions when I was at school — essay writing and public speaking, that sort of thing. Honestly, I didn't win very often, but the preparation was where the real value lay. I learned to plan my time properly, to perform under pressure, and to look at a poor result and work out what actually went wrong instead of simply feeling discouraged. Those habits turned out to matter far more than any of the results ever did, though I'll admit it would've been nice to win occasionally too."},{"en":"perform under pressure","context":"Q: Have you entered any other competitions?\nA: Yes, mostly academic and language competitions when I was at school — essay writing and public speaking, that sort of thing. Honestly, I didn't win very often, but the preparation was where the real value lay. I learned to plan my time properly, to perform under pressure, and to look at a poor result and work out what actually went wrong instead of simply feeling discouraged. Those habits turned out to matter far more than any of the results ever did, though I'll admit it would've been nice to win occasionally too."},{"en":"work out what actually went wrong","context":"Q: Have you entered any other competitions?\nA: Yes, mostly academic and language competitions when I was at school — essay writing and public speaking, that sort of thing. Honestly, I didn't win very often, but the preparation was where the real value lay. I learned to plan my time properly, to perform under pressure, and to look at a poor result and work out what actually went wrong instead of simply feeling discouraged. Those habits turned out to matter far more than any of the results ever did, though I'll admit it would've been nice to win occasionally too."},{"en":"feeling discouraged","context":"Q: Have you entered any other competitions?\nA: Yes, mostly academic and language competitions when I was at school — essay writing and public speaking, that sort of thing. Honestly, I didn't win very often, but the preparation was where the real value lay. I learned to plan my time properly, to perform under pressure, and to look at a poor result and work out what actually went wrong instead of simply feeling discouraged. Those habits turned out to matter far more than any of the results ever did, though I'll admit it would've been nice to win occasionally too."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Competition","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-024","_displayNo":"#024","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-024-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-024-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, when a competition tests a skill and helps me improve. A deadline makes me prepare more seriously. For example, a photography competition once encouraged me to practise every weekend. I am less interested in events decided by luck because there is little to learn. Improving matters more to me than my final position.","phrases":[{"en":"deadline","meaning":"The time by which something must be finished."}],"question":"Do you enjoy entering competitions?","html":"Yes, when a competition tests a skill and helps me improve. A <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deadline</strong> makes me prepare more seriously. For example, a photography competition once encouraged me to practise every weekend. I am less interested in events decided by luck because there is little to learn. Improving matters more to me than my final position."},{"text":"Yes, I entered school competitions such as essay writing and public speaking. I did not often win, but preparing taught me to organise my time, handle pressure and learn from mistakes. Those skills have been more useful than the results themselves, although winning occasionally would also have been nice.","phrases":[{"en":"handle pressure","meaning":"Stay effective when a situation is demanding or stressful."}],"question":"Have you entered any other competitions?","html":"Yes, I entered school competitions such as essay writing and public speaking. I did not often win, but preparing taught me to organise my time, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">handle pressure</strong> and learn from mistakes. Those skills have been more useful than the results themselves, although winning occasionally would also have been nice."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-024-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-024-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Local goods against imported ones","prompt":"Why do some people prefer domestically produced goods to imported products?","answer":"Q: Why do some people prefer domestically produced goods to imported products?\nA: There are a few reasons. The most common is a sense of responsibility towards <strong class=\"key-phrase\">local employment</strong> — people feel that buying domestic keeps money circulating in the national economy and protects jobs in their own community. Trust is another factor, particularly with food: consumers understand their own country&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">safety standards</strong> and know that a problem can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">traced back to a specific farm or factory</strong>, which isn&#x27;t always the case with imports. There&#x27;s an emotional element too — for some people buying local is simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a form of national pride</strong>, regardless of price or quality. It isn&#x27;t always the rational choice, though: a domestic product is sometimes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more expensive for no real gain</strong> in quality, and buyers who insist on it anyway are paying for the principle, not the product.","topic":"Consumerism & Possessions","phrases":[{"en":"local employment","context":"Q: Why do some people prefer domestically produced goods to imported products?\nA: There are a few reasons. The most common is a sense of responsibility towards local employment — people feel that buying domestic keeps money circulating in the national economy and protects jobs in their own community. Trust is another factor, particularly with food: consumers understand their own country's safety standards and know that a problem can be traced back to a specific farm or factory, which isn't always the case with imports. There's an emotional element too — for some people buying local is simply a form of national pride, regardless of price or quality. It isn't always the rational choice, though: a domestic product is sometimes more expensive for no real gain in quality, and buyers who insist on it anyway are paying for the principle, not the product."},{"en":"safety standards","context":"Q: Why do some people prefer domestically produced goods to imported products?\nA: There are a few reasons. The most common is a sense of responsibility towards local employment — people feel that buying domestic keeps money circulating in the national economy and protects jobs in their own community. Trust is another factor, particularly with food: consumers understand their own country's safety standards and know that a problem can be traced back to a specific farm or factory, which isn't always the case with imports. There's an emotional element too — for some people buying local is simply a form of national pride, regardless of price or quality. It isn't always the rational choice, though: a domestic product is sometimes more expensive for no real gain in quality, and buyers who insist on it anyway are paying for the principle, not the product."},{"en":"traced back to a specific farm or factory","context":"Q: Why do some people prefer domestically produced goods to imported products?\nA: There are a few reasons. The most common is a sense of responsibility towards local employment — people feel that buying domestic keeps money circulating in the national economy and protects jobs in their own community. Trust is another factor, particularly with food: consumers understand their own country's safety standards and know that a problem can be traced back to a specific farm or factory, which isn't always the case with imports. There's an emotional element too — for some people buying local is simply a form of national pride, regardless of price or quality. It isn't always the rational choice, though: a domestic product is sometimes more expensive for no real gain in quality, and buyers who insist on it anyway are paying for the principle, not the product."},{"en":"a form of national pride","context":"Q: Why do some people prefer domestically produced goods to imported products?\nA: There are a few reasons. The most common is a sense of responsibility towards local employment — people feel that buying domestic keeps money circulating in the national economy and protects jobs in their own community. Trust is another factor, particularly with food: consumers understand their own country's safety standards and know that a problem can be traced back to a specific farm or factory, which isn't always the case with imports. There's an emotional element too — for some people buying local is simply a form of national pride, regardless of price or quality. It isn't always the rational choice, though: a domestic product is sometimes more expensive for no real gain in quality, and buyers who insist on it anyway are paying for the principle, not the product."},{"en":"more expensive for no real gain","context":"Q: Why do some people prefer domestically produced goods to imported products?\nA: There are a few reasons. The most common is a sense of responsibility towards local employment — people feel that buying domestic keeps money circulating in the national economy and protects jobs in their own community. Trust is another factor, particularly with food: consumers understand their own country's safety standards and know that a problem can be traced back to a specific farm or factory, which isn't always the case with imports. There's an emotional element too — for some people buying local is simply a form of national pride, regardless of price or quality. It isn't always the rational choice, though: a domestic product is sometimes more expensive for no real gain in quality, and buyers who insist on it anyway are paying for the principle, not the product."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Consumerism & Possessions","id":"speaking-part-3-025","_displayNo":"#025","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-025-q01.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Some people buy local products to support jobs and keep money in their own economy. They may also trust familiar safety standards, especially for food, or feel proud of their country. However, local does not always mean better. If a product costs more without offering extra quality, the buyer may be paying for a belief rather than a practical benefit.","phrases":[{"en":"local products","meaning":"Goods made in your own area or country."}],"question":"Why do some people prefer domestically produced goods to imported products?","html":"Some people buy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">local products</strong> to support jobs and keep money in their own economy. They may also trust familiar safety standards, especially for food, or feel proud of their country. However, local does not always mean better. If a product costs more without offering extra quality, the buyer may be paying for a belief rather than a practical benefit."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-025-q01.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Possessions as a mark of status","prompt":"In your country, what possessions show a higher status or position in society?\nDo you think we place too much importance on possessions?\nDo you think things were different 50 years ago?","answer":"Q: In your country, what possessions show a higher status or position in society?\nA: Property first — where you live and whether you own it says more than anything else. Then cars, luxury watches and designer clothing, and until recently the latest phone, though phones have become so universal now that they don&#x27;t signal much any more. What these have in common is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">visibility</strong>: a possession only works as a status marker if other people can see it and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recognise what it cost</strong>, which is exactly why the specific items shift so quickly — today&#x27;s must-have brand is often forgotten in five years.\n\nQ: Do you think we place too much importance on possessions?\nA: Yes, though I&#x27;d say the pressure&#x27;s manufactured rather than natural. Advertising works precisely by linking products to identity, so you&#x27;re not buying a jacket but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a version of yourself</strong>, and social media has widened the comparison enormously. It&#x27;s hardest on teenagers, who face <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuine social consequences</strong> for having the wrong brand — being excluded over a pair of trainers is a real thing — and whose families often can&#x27;t afford to prevent it. Not everyone falls for it equally, though; I know plenty of people who&#x27;ve simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opted out of the whole game</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you think things were different 50 years ago?\nA: Considerably. People owned far fewer things and got them repaired instead of replacing them, because goods were expensive relative to income and credit was much harder to obtain — you generally couldn&#x27;t buy what you hadn&#x27;t saved for. My grandfather still talks about having his shoes resoled rather than buying new ones, which sounds almost unthinkable now. Advertising existed then too, but it reached you a few times a day rather than constantly. Materialism isn&#x27;t new, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cycle of upgrading</strong> has accelerated beyond anything he&#x27;d recognise, which says as much about marketing as it does about human nature.","topic":"Consumerism & Possessions","phrases":[{"en":"visibility","context":"Q: In your country, what possessions show a higher status or position in society?\nA: Property first — where you live and whether you own it says more than anything else. Then cars, luxury watches and designer clothing, and until recently the latest phone, though phones have become so universal now that they don't signal much any more. What these have in common is visibility: a possession only works as a status marker if other people can see it and recognise what it cost, which is exactly why the specific items shift so quickly — today's must-have brand is often forgotten in five years."},{"en":"recognise what it cost","context":"Q: In your country, what possessions show a higher status or position in society?\nA: Property first — where you live and whether you own it says more than anything else. Then cars, luxury watches and designer clothing, and until recently the latest phone, though phones have become so universal now that they don't signal much any more. What these have in common is visibility: a possession only works as a status marker if other people can see it and recognise what it cost, which is exactly why the specific items shift so quickly — today's must-have brand is often forgotten in five years."},{"en":"a version of yourself","context":"Q: Do you think we place too much importance on possessions?\nA: Yes, though I'd say the pressure's manufactured rather than natural. Advertising works precisely by linking products to identity, so you're not buying a jacket but a version of yourself, and social media has widened the comparison enormously. It's hardest on teenagers, who face genuine social consequences for having the wrong brand — being excluded over a pair of trainers is a real thing — and whose families often can't afford to prevent it. Not everyone falls for it equally, though; I know plenty of people who've simply opted out of the whole game."},{"en":"genuine social consequences","context":"Q: Do you think we place too much importance on possessions?\nA: Yes, though I'd say the pressure's manufactured rather than natural. Advertising works precisely by linking products to identity, so you're not buying a jacket but a version of yourself, and social media has widened the comparison enormously. It's hardest on teenagers, who face genuine social consequences for having the wrong brand — being excluded over a pair of trainers is a real thing — and whose families often can't afford to prevent it. Not everyone falls for it equally, though; I know plenty of people who've simply opted out of the whole game."},{"en":"opted out of the whole game","context":"Q: Do you think we place too much importance on possessions?\nA: Yes, though I'd say the pressure's manufactured rather than natural. Advertising works precisely by linking products to identity, so you're not buying a jacket but a version of yourself, and social media has widened the comparison enormously. It's hardest on teenagers, who face genuine social consequences for having the wrong brand — being excluded over a pair of trainers is a real thing — and whose families often can't afford to prevent it. Not everyone falls for it equally, though; I know plenty of people who've simply opted out of the whole game."},{"en":"the cycle of upgrading","context":"Q: Do you think things were different 50 years ago?\nA: Considerably. People owned far fewer things and got them repaired instead of replacing them, because goods were expensive relative to income and credit was much harder to obtain — you generally couldn't buy what you hadn't saved for. My grandfather still talks about having his shoes resoled rather than buying new ones, which sounds almost unthinkable now. Advertising existed then too, but it reached you a few times a day rather than constantly. Materialism isn't new, but the cycle of upgrading has accelerated beyond anything he'd recognise, which says as much about marketing as it does about human nature."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Consumerism & Possessions","id":"speaking-part-3-026","_displayNo":"#026","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-026-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-026-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-026-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Homes and land are the clearest signs, followed by expensive cars, watches and designer clothes. These things show status because other people can see them and recognise their price. Phones used to do this too, but they are now so common that they show less. The particular products change as fashions and ideas of success change.","phrases":[{"en":"status","meaning":"A person's social position or level of respect in society."}],"question":"In your country, what possessions show a higher status or position in society?","html":"Homes and land are the clearest signs, followed by expensive cars, watches and designer clothes. These things show <strong class=\"key-phrase\">status</strong> because other people can see them and recognise their price. Phones used to do this too, but they are now so common that they show less. The particular products change as fashions and ideas of success change."},{"text":"Yes, I think adverts and social media encourage us to connect possessions with who we are. We compare ourselves with more people than before, and teenagers may feel pressure to wear certain brands. That can be hard for families with less money. Still, some people choose to ignore this competition and focus on what they actually need.","phrases":[{"en":"possessions","meaning":"The things a person owns."}],"question":"Do you think we place too much importance on possessions?","html":"Yes, I think adverts and social media encourage us to connect <strong class=\"key-phrase\">possessions</strong> with who we are. We compare ourselves with more people than before, and teenagers may feel pressure to wear certain brands. That can be hard for families with less money. Still, some people choose to ignore this competition and focus on what they actually need."},{"text":"Yes. People owned less and often repaired things because new goods were expensive and borrowing money was harder. My grandfather remembers repairing his shoes instead of replacing them. Advertising was also less constant. People still cared about possessions, but buying newer versions happened more slowly than it often does now.","phrases":[{"en":"replacing","meaning":"Getting something new to take the place of an older item."}],"question":"Do you think things were different 50 years ago?","html":"Yes. People owned less and often repaired things because new goods were expensive and borrowing money was harder. My grandfather remembers repairing his shoes instead of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">replacing</strong> them. Advertising was also less constant. People still cared about possessions, but buying newer versions happened more slowly than it often does now."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-026-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-026-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-026-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Valuable possessions and changing attitudes to keeping things","prompt":"What are the valuable personal possessions in your country?\nHow has your attitude towards keeping personal possessions changed?\nWhy do you think people keep valuable things?","answer":"Q: What are the valuable personal possessions in your country?\nA: Property&#x27;s by far the most valuable thing most families own — a house or a piece of land usually represents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a lifetime&#x27;s savings</strong>. Beyond that, gold and jewellery are traditionally important, partly because older generations trust them more than banks, and they&#x27;re often given as wedding gifts. Vehicles matter too, and increasingly phones and laptops, though those are valued for what they let you do rather than as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assets in themselves</strong>. That said, younger people often rate a good laptop above gold, so what counts as valuable is starting to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">split along generational lines</strong>.\n\nQ: How has your attitude towards keeping personal possessions changed?\nA: I&#x27;ve become far more selective. When I was younger I kept almost everything, partly on the assumption that it might be useful one day and partly because throwing things away felt wasteful. What changed my mind was moving house a few times and realising how much I was carrying around that I&#x27;d never even touched. Now I keep two categories only: things I actually use, and things that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carry real personal meaning</strong>, like photographs or gifts from family. I&#x27;ll admit I still break my own rule occasionally — I&#x27;ve kept a jacket I never wear simply because I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">can&#x27;t quite part with it</strong>.\n\nQ: Why do you think people keep valuable things?\nA: Several reasons, and they&#x27;re quite different from one another. Some people keep valuables purely as an investment, since gold and property hold their worth when currency doesn&#x27;t. Others keep <strong class=\"key-phrase\">family heirlooms</strong>, where the point isn&#x27;t the price at all but the connection to a grandparent or a particular period of family history — my mother still has her mother&#x27;s wedding ring and would never sell it whatever it was worth. And then there&#x27;s status: an expensive watch signals success to other people. I&#x27;d say the emotional and financial motives are strongest and they often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overlap</strong>, though buying purely for status, things you&#x27;ll never actually use, is a less healthy version of the same instinct.","topic":"Consumerism & Possessions","phrases":[{"en":"a lifetime's savings","context":"Q: What are the valuable personal possessions in your country?\nA: Property's by far the most valuable thing most families own — a house or a piece of land usually represents a lifetime's savings. Beyond that, gold and jewellery are traditionally important, partly because older generations trust them more than banks, and they're often given as wedding gifts. Vehicles matter too, and increasingly phones and laptops, though those are valued for what they let you do rather than as assets in themselves. That said, younger people often rate a good laptop above gold, so what counts as valuable is starting to split along generational lines."},{"en":"assets in themselves","context":"Q: What are the valuable personal possessions in your country?\nA: Property's by far the most valuable thing most families own — a house or a piece of land usually represents a lifetime's savings. Beyond that, gold and jewellery are traditionally important, partly because older generations trust them more than banks, and they're often given as wedding gifts. Vehicles matter too, and increasingly phones and laptops, though those are valued for what they let you do rather than as assets in themselves. That said, younger people often rate a good laptop above gold, so what counts as valuable is starting to split along generational lines."},{"en":"split along generational lines","context":"Q: What are the valuable personal possessions in your country?\nA: Property's by far the most valuable thing most families own — a house or a piece of land usually represents a lifetime's savings. Beyond that, gold and jewellery are traditionally important, partly because older generations trust them more than banks, and they're often given as wedding gifts. Vehicles matter too, and increasingly phones and laptops, though those are valued for what they let you do rather than as assets in themselves. That said, younger people often rate a good laptop above gold, so what counts as valuable is starting to split along generational lines."},{"en":"carry real personal meaning","context":"Q: How has your attitude towards keeping personal possessions changed?\nA: I've become far more selective. When I was younger I kept almost everything, partly on the assumption that it might be useful one day and partly because throwing things away felt wasteful. What changed my mind was moving house a few times and realising how much I was carrying around that I'd never even touched. Now I keep two categories only: things I actually use, and things that carry real personal meaning, like photographs or gifts from family. I'll admit I still break my own rule occasionally — I've kept a jacket I never wear simply because I can't quite part with it."},{"en":"can't quite part with it","context":"Q: How has your attitude towards keeping personal possessions changed?\nA: I've become far more selective. When I was younger I kept almost everything, partly on the assumption that it might be useful one day and partly because throwing things away felt wasteful. What changed my mind was moving house a few times and realising how much I was carrying around that I'd never even touched. Now I keep two categories only: things I actually use, and things that carry real personal meaning, like photographs or gifts from family. I'll admit I still break my own rule occasionally — I've kept a jacket I never wear simply because I can't quite part with it."},{"en":"family heirlooms","context":"Q: Why do you think people keep valuable things?\nA: Several reasons, and they're quite different from one another. Some people keep valuables purely as an investment, since gold and property hold their worth when currency doesn't. Others keep family heirlooms, where the point isn't the price at all but the connection to a grandparent or a particular period of family history — my mother still has her mother's wedding ring and would never sell it whatever it was worth. And then there's status: an expensive watch signals success to other people. I'd say the emotional and financial motives are strongest and they often overlap, though buying purely for status, things you'll never actually use, is a less healthy version of the same instinct."},{"en":"overlap","context":"Q: Why do you think people keep valuable things?\nA: Several reasons, and they're quite different from one another. Some people keep valuables purely as an investment, since gold and property hold their worth when currency doesn't. Others keep family heirlooms, where the point isn't the price at all but the connection to a grandparent or a particular period of family history — my mother still has her mother's wedding ring and would never sell it whatever it was worth. And then there's status: an expensive watch signals success to other people. I'd say the emotional and financial motives are strongest and they often overlap, though buying purely for status, things you'll never actually use, is a less healthy version of the same instinct."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Consumerism & Possessions","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-027","_displayNo":"#027","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-027-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-027-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-027-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"For most families, a home or land is the most valuable possession. Gold and jewellery also matter, often as savings or wedding gifts. Vehicles, phones and laptops are useful too. Younger people may value a good computer more than gold because of the work it allows them to do, so priorities differ between generations.","phrases":[{"en":"priorities","meaning":"The things people consider most important."}],"question":"What are the valuable personal possessions in your country?","html":"For most families, a home or land is the most valuable possession. Gold and jewellery also matter, often as savings or wedding gifts. Vehicles, phones and laptops are useful too. Younger people may value a good computer more than gold because of the work it allows them to do, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">priorities</strong> differ between generations."},{"text":"I keep fewer things now. When I was younger, I saved almost everything in case it became useful. Moving house showed me how much I never used. Now I mainly keep useful items and things with personal meaning, such as family gifts. I am not perfect, though; I still have an old jacket I cannot quite give away.","phrases":[{"en":"personal meaning","meaning":"Importance connected with your own memories or feelings."}],"question":"How has your attitude towards keeping personal possessions changed?","html":"I keep fewer things now. When I was younger, I saved almost everything in case it became useful. Moving house showed me how much I never used. Now I mainly keep useful items and things with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personal meaning</strong>, such as family gifts. I am not perfect, though; I still have an old jacket I cannot quite give away."},{"text":"People may keep valuable things as savings, as family memories or to show success. A family ring, for example, can matter because of who owned it rather than its price. These emotional and financial reasons often overlap. Buying things only to impress others seems less useful to me, especially if the owner never actually uses them.","phrases":[{"en":"overlap","meaning":"Be partly connected or have something in common."}],"question":"Why do you think people keep valuable things?","html":"People may keep valuable things as savings, as family memories or to show success. A family ring, for example, can matter because of who owned it rather than its price. These emotional and financial reasons often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overlap</strong>. Buying things only to impress others seems less useful to me, especially if the owner never actually uses them."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-027-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-027-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-027-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"What shows status now and in your grandparents' time","prompt":"What kinds of possessions show status in your country?\nDo you think it was different for your grandparents?\nModern society is often called 'materialistic'. Why do you think this is?","answer":"Q: What kinds of possessions show status in your country?\nA: Property first, then cars, then branded clothing, watches and the latest phone. What&#x27;s interesting is that the signal only works because everyone agrees it does — an expensive watch keeps time no better than a cheap one, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the entire value is social</strong>. The specific items shift quite quickly, too: something that signalled success ten years ago can look dated now, which is precisely what keeps people buying replacements they don&#x27;t really need. That&#x27;s starting to shift a little, though — plenty of younger buyers now signal status through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">experiences instead of objects</strong>, a good holiday rather than a good watch.\n\nQ: Do you think it was different for your grandparents?\nA: Yes, considerably. Their generation valued things that lasted — land, gold, solid furniture — partly because goods were genuinely scarce and partly because owning something durable meant security. Status came from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what you had accumulated over a lifetime</strong>. Now it&#x27;s much more visible and much faster-moving, largely because social media displays everyone&#x27;s purchases continuously: my grandparents&#x27; neighbours knew what they owned, and that was the whole audience, whereas <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the audience is effectively unlimited</strong> now. I wouldn&#x27;t call their era better exactly, though — they also had far less choice and far less means of fixing a bad purchase once they&#x27;d made it.\n\nQ: Modern society is often called &#x27;materialistic&#x27;. Why do you think this is?\nA: Partly because economies now depend on continuous consumption — if people stopped replacing things, a huge amount of employment would disappear, so there&#x27;s enormous pressure to keep buying. Advertising then does the work of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">linking products with identity</strong>, so you&#x27;re not purchasing a phone but a version of yourself. Comparison has widened, too: people once measured themselves against neighbours, and now it&#x27;s against everyone they follow online. A minority of people do consciously <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resist that pressure</strong>, though, and minimalism as a lifestyle has grown partly in response to it.","topic":"Consumerism & Possessions","phrases":[{"en":"the entire value is social","context":"Q: What kinds of possessions show status in your country?\nA: Property first, then cars, then branded clothing, watches and the latest phone. What's interesting is that the signal only works because everyone agrees it does — an expensive watch keeps time no better than a cheap one, so the entire value is social. The specific items shift quite quickly, too: something that signalled success ten years ago can look dated now, which is precisely what keeps people buying replacements they don't really need. That's starting to shift a little, though — plenty of younger buyers now signal status through experiences instead of objects, a good holiday rather than a good watch."},{"en":"experiences instead of objects","context":"Q: What kinds of possessions show status in your country?\nA: Property first, then cars, then branded clothing, watches and the latest phone. What's interesting is that the signal only works because everyone agrees it does — an expensive watch keeps time no better than a cheap one, so the entire value is social. The specific items shift quite quickly, too: something that signalled success ten years ago can look dated now, which is precisely what keeps people buying replacements they don't really need. That's starting to shift a little, though — plenty of younger buyers now signal status through experiences instead of objects, a good holiday rather than a good watch."},{"en":"what you had accumulated over a lifetime","context":"Q: Do you think it was different for your grandparents?\nA: Yes, considerably. Their generation valued things that lasted — land, gold, solid furniture — partly because goods were genuinely scarce and partly because owning something durable meant security. Status came from what you had accumulated over a lifetime. Now it's much more visible and much faster-moving, largely because social media displays everyone's purchases continuously: my grandparents' neighbours knew what they owned, and that was the whole audience, whereas the audience is effectively unlimited now. I wouldn't call their era better exactly, though — they also had far less choice and far less means of fixing a bad purchase once they'd made it."},{"en":"the audience is effectively unlimited","context":"Q: Do you think it was different for your grandparents?\nA: Yes, considerably. Their generation valued things that lasted — land, gold, solid furniture — partly because goods were genuinely scarce and partly because owning something durable meant security. Status came from what you had accumulated over a lifetime. Now it's much more visible and much faster-moving, largely because social media displays everyone's purchases continuously: my grandparents' neighbours knew what they owned, and that was the whole audience, whereas the audience is effectively unlimited now. I wouldn't call their era better exactly, though — they also had far less choice and far less means of fixing a bad purchase once they'd made it."},{"en":"linking products with identity","context":"Q: Modern society is often called 'materialistic'. Why do you think this is?\nA: Partly because economies now depend on continuous consumption — if people stopped replacing things, a huge amount of employment would disappear, so there's enormous pressure to keep buying. Advertising then does the work of linking products with identity, so you're not purchasing a phone but a version of yourself. Comparison has widened, too: people once measured themselves against neighbours, and now it's against everyone they follow online. A minority of people do consciously resist that pressure, though, and minimalism as a lifestyle has grown partly in response to it."},{"en":"resist that pressure","context":"Q: Modern society is often called 'materialistic'. Why do you think this is?\nA: Partly because economies now depend on continuous consumption — if people stopped replacing things, a huge amount of employment would disappear, so there's enormous pressure to keep buying. Advertising then does the work of linking products with identity, so you're not purchasing a phone but a version of yourself. Comparison has widened, too: people once measured themselves against neighbours, and now it's against everyone they follow online. A minority of people do consciously resist that pressure, though, and minimalism as a lifestyle has grown partly in response to it."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Consumerism & Possessions","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-028","_displayNo":"#028","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-028-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-028-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-028-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Homes, cars, expensive watches, branded clothes and new phones can all show status. They work only because other people recognise them as signs of success. A costly watch may not tell the time better, for example. These signals change quickly, and some younger people now prefer showing experiences, such as holidays, rather than possessions.","phrases":[{"en":"branded clothes","meaning":"Clothing sold under a recognised company or designer name."}],"question":"What kinds of possessions show status in your country?","html":"Homes, cars, expensive watches, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">branded clothes</strong> and new phones can all show status. They work only because other people recognise them as signs of success. A costly watch may not tell the time better, for example. These signals change quickly, and some younger people now prefer showing experiences, such as holidays, rather than possessions."},{"text":"Yes. My grandparents valued things that lasted, such as land, gold and strong furniture, because possessions were harder to obtain and offered security. Today, people replace things faster and show purchases online to a much wider audience. Their generation had less pressure of that kind, but also fewer choices, so I would not say everything was better.","phrases":[{"en":"security","meaning":"A feeling of safety and protection from future difficulties."}],"question":"Do you think it was different for your grandparents?","html":"Yes. My grandparents valued things that lasted, such as land, gold and strong furniture, because possessions were harder to obtain and offered <strong class=\"key-phrase\">security</strong>. Today, people replace things faster and show purchases online to a much wider audience. Their generation had less pressure of that kind, but also fewer choices, so I would not say everything was better."},{"text":"Modern economies depend on people continuing to buy things, so businesses strongly encourage spending. Adverts link products with identity, while social media lets us compare ourselves with many others. This can make possessions seem unusually important. However, some people resist by owning less and choosing carefully, so not everyone accepts the same values.","phrases":[{"en":"identity","meaning":"The qualities and ideas that form who a person is."}],"question":"Modern society is often called 'materialistic'. Why do you think this is?","html":"Modern economies depend on people continuing to buy things, so businesses strongly encourage spending. Adverts link products with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">identity</strong>, while social media lets us compare ourselves with many others. This can make possessions seem unusually important. However, some people resist by owning less and choosing carefully, so not everyone accepts the same values."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-028-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-028-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-028-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"The advantages of country living, and how it has changed","prompt":"What advantages does living in the countryside have, compared to living in a city?\nHave areas of countryside in your country changed since you were a child?\nDo you think that visits to the countryside should be something that schools offer to their children?","answer":"Q: What advantages does living in the countryside have, compared to living in a city?\nA: I think there are some clear advantages to living in the countryside. With the clean air and less traffic or industry, the countryside&#x27;s generally a much healthier place to live than the average city, and there&#x27;s a stronger sense of community too — in a small village everyone tends to know everyone else, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the pace of life</strong> is slower, with people more laid back and friendly, compared to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the hustle and bustle</strong> of city life. It&#x27;s not without its downsides, though: jobs are scarcer, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">basic services</strong> like hospitals or good schools are often a long drive away.\n\nQ: Have areas of countryside in your country changed since you were a child?\nA: Less than I expected, in the area I know best, because a good deal of it&#x27;s protected from development — the hills, footpaths and woodland are still pretty much as I remember them. What has changed is the infrastructure around the villages: better roads, a good many more cafes and guesthouses, and considerably more visitors at weekends than there used to be. That&#x27;s brought welcome income to places that were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">losing their young people</strong>, though a few villages now feel arranged for tourists rather than actually lived in.\n\nQ: Do you think that visits to the countryside should be something that schools offer to their children?\nA: Yes, I think it&#x27;s a great idea for schools to take children on trips to the countryside, especially if those children live in a large town or city. A visit like that can be both relaxing and educational — children get to walk in the fresh air, and see animals and plants they might never have come across otherwise. If schools don&#x27;t offer this, some children will never see life outside the city. The main obstacle is usually cost, though: a proper residential trip isn&#x27;t cheap, and schools in poorer areas often can&#x27;t manage more than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a single day out</strong> a year.","topic":"Countryside","phrases":[{"en":"the pace of life","context":"Q: What advantages does living in the countryside have, compared to living in a city?\nA: I think there are some clear advantages to living in the countryside. With the clean air and less traffic or industry, the countryside's generally a much healthier place to live than the average city, and there's a stronger sense of community too — in a small village everyone tends to know everyone else, and the pace of life is slower, with people more laid back and friendly, compared to the hustle and bustle of city life. It's not without its downsides, though: jobs are scarcer, and basic services like hospitals or good schools are often a long drive away."},{"en":"the hustle and bustle","context":"Q: What advantages does living in the countryside have, compared to living in a city?\nA: I think there are some clear advantages to living in the countryside. With the clean air and less traffic or industry, the countryside's generally a much healthier place to live than the average city, and there's a stronger sense of community too — in a small village everyone tends to know everyone else, and the pace of life is slower, with people more laid back and friendly, compared to the hustle and bustle of city life. It's not without its downsides, though: jobs are scarcer, and basic services like hospitals or good schools are often a long drive away."},{"en":"basic services","context":"Q: What advantages does living in the countryside have, compared to living in a city?\nA: I think there are some clear advantages to living in the countryside. With the clean air and less traffic or industry, the countryside's generally a much healthier place to live than the average city, and there's a stronger sense of community too — in a small village everyone tends to know everyone else, and the pace of life is slower, with people more laid back and friendly, compared to the hustle and bustle of city life. It's not without its downsides, though: jobs are scarcer, and basic services like hospitals or good schools are often a long drive away."},{"en":"losing their young people","context":"Q: Have areas of countryside in your country changed since you were a child?\nA: Less than I expected, in the area I know best, because a good deal of it's protected from development — the hills, footpaths and woodland are still pretty much as I remember them. What has changed is the infrastructure around the villages: better roads, a good many more cafes and guesthouses, and considerably more visitors at weekends than there used to be. That's brought welcome income to places that were losing their young people, though a few villages now feel arranged for tourists rather than actually lived in."},{"en":"a single day out","context":"Q: Do you think that visits to the countryside should be something that schools offer to their children?\nA: Yes, I think it's a great idea for schools to take children on trips to the countryside, especially if those children live in a large town or city. A visit like that can be both relaxing and educational — children get to walk in the fresh air, and see animals and plants they might never have come across otherwise. If schools don't offer this, some children will never see life outside the city. The main obstacle is usually cost, though: a proper residential trip isn't cheap, and schools in poorer areas often can't manage more than a single day out a year."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Countryside","id":"speaking-part-3-029","_displayNo":"#029","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-029-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-029-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-029-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"The countryside usually offers cleaner air, less traffic and a slower way of life. In small villages, neighbours often know each other, which can create a friendly community. However, it also has disadvantages. Jobs may be harder to find, and good schools or hospitals can be far away, so the benefits depend on a person's needs.","phrases":[{"en":"way of life","meaning":"The usual habits and conditions in which people live."}],"question":"What advantages does living in the countryside have, compared to living in a city?","html":"The countryside usually offers cleaner air, less traffic and a slower <strong class=\"key-phrase\">way of life</strong>. In small villages, neighbours often know each other, which can create a friendly community. However, it also has disadvantages. Jobs may be harder to find, and good schools or hospitals can be far away, so the benefits depend on a person&#x27;s needs."},{"text":"The protected hills, woods and paths I know have changed less than I expected. However, villages now have better roads, more cafes and guesthouses, and more weekend visitors. Tourism provides useful income, but some villages feel designed mainly for visitors rather than local families. So the landscape may stay familiar while daily life changes around it.","phrases":[{"en":"landscape","meaning":"The natural features and appearance of an area."}],"question":"Have areas of countryside in your country changed since you were a child?","html":"The protected hills, woods and paths I know have changed less than I expected. However, villages now have better roads, more cafes and guesthouses, and more weekend visitors. Tourism provides useful income, but some villages feel designed mainly for visitors rather than local families. So the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">landscape</strong> may stay familiar while daily life changes around it."},{"text":"Yes, especially for children who mainly know city life. They can exercise outdoors and learn about animals and plants directly. Some might never experience the countryside otherwise. The difficulty is cost, particularly for longer trips. Schools with limited money may only manage a short visit, so the opportunity is not equally available to everyone.","phrases":[{"en":"directly","meaning":"Through personal experience rather than through someone else's description."}],"question":"Do you think that visits to the countryside should be something that schools offer to their children?","html":"Yes, especially for children who mainly know city life. They can exercise outdoors and learn about animals and plants <strong class=\"key-phrase\">directly</strong>. Some might never experience the countryside otherwise. The difficulty is cost, particularly for longer trips. Schools with limited money may only manage a short visit, so the opportunity is not equally available to everyone."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-029-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-029-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-029-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Customer service and how companies could improve it","prompt":"How important is customer service for you?\nWhat can companies do to improve their customer service?\nWhy do you think employees sometimes fail to provide good customer service?","answer":"Q: How important is customer service for you?\nA: Customer service is really important for me because I like to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel welcome and valued</strong> as a customer. If companies want us to use their products or services, I believe they should treat us well and therefore encourage us to return. For example, I have a favourite cafe where I like to go, and the friendly staff are the main reason I&#x27;ve become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a loyal customer</strong>. Price matters, obviously, but I&#x27;ll pay slightly more to be treated well.\n\nQ: What can companies do to improve their customer service?\nA: I think managers need to train their employees to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deliver great customer service</strong>, because good intentions alone don&#x27;t achieve much without real skills behind them. There are lots of things staff can do, like greeting customers properly, handling problems quickly, and making sure they&#x27;re <strong class=\"key-phrase\">asked for feedback</strong>. I called my phone provider recently with a billing problem, and the difference was obvious: the first person I spoke to just read from a script, while the second actually solved it in five minutes because she took real ownership of the problem instead of passing it on. Training only goes so far, though, if staff are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stretched too thin</strong> — even the best-trained call-handler can&#x27;t do much when they&#x27;re rushing through fifty calls an hour.\n\nQ: Why do you think employees sometimes fail to provide good customer service?\nA: When employees&#x27; treatment of customers isn&#x27;t good, it&#x27;s usually because they&#x27;re unhappy doing the job itself. That might be because they&#x27;re treated badly by their own managers, or because they&#x27;ve never been encouraged to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take pride in</strong> their work. I remember my first ever job — I didn&#x27;t enjoy the work I&#x27;d been given, and I probably wasn&#x27;t as thoughtful or attentive as I should&#x27;ve been when I had to deal with customers. It isn&#x27;t always about the person, though; sometimes it really is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">just one bad day</strong>, not a sign of how they usually work.","topic":"Customer Service","phrases":[{"en":"feel welcome and valued","context":"Q: How important is customer service for you?\nA: Customer service is really important for me because I like to feel welcome and valued as a customer. If companies want us to use their products or services, I believe they should treat us well and therefore encourage us to return. For example, I have a favourite cafe where I like to go, and the friendly staff are the main reason I've become a loyal customer. Price matters, obviously, but I'll pay slightly more to be treated well."},{"en":"a loyal customer","context":"Q: How important is customer service for you?\nA: Customer service is really important for me because I like to feel welcome and valued as a customer. If companies want us to use their products or services, I believe they should treat us well and therefore encourage us to return. For example, I have a favourite cafe where I like to go, and the friendly staff are the main reason I've become a loyal customer. Price matters, obviously, but I'll pay slightly more to be treated well."},{"en":"deliver great customer service","context":"Q: What can companies do to improve their customer service?\nA: I think managers need to train their employees to deliver great customer service, because good intentions alone don't achieve much without real skills behind them. There are lots of things staff can do, like greeting customers properly, handling problems quickly, and making sure they're asked for feedback. I called my phone provider recently with a billing problem, and the difference was obvious: the first person I spoke to just read from a script, while the second actually solved it in five minutes because she took real ownership of the problem instead of passing it on. Training only goes so far, though, if staff are stretched too thin — even the best-trained call-handler can't do much when they're rushing through fifty calls an hour."},{"en":"asked for feedback","context":"Q: What can companies do to improve their customer service?\nA: I think managers need to train their employees to deliver great customer service, because good intentions alone don't achieve much without real skills behind them. There are lots of things staff can do, like greeting customers properly, handling problems quickly, and making sure they're asked for feedback. I called my phone provider recently with a billing problem, and the difference was obvious: the first person I spoke to just read from a script, while the second actually solved it in five minutes because she took real ownership of the problem instead of passing it on. Training only goes so far, though, if staff are stretched too thin — even the best-trained call-handler can't do much when they're rushing through fifty calls an hour."},{"en":"stretched too thin","context":"Q: What can companies do to improve their customer service?\nA: I think managers need to train their employees to deliver great customer service, because good intentions alone don't achieve much without real skills behind them. There are lots of things staff can do, like greeting customers properly, handling problems quickly, and making sure they're asked for feedback. I called my phone provider recently with a billing problem, and the difference was obvious: the first person I spoke to just read from a script, while the second actually solved it in five minutes because she took real ownership of the problem instead of passing it on. Training only goes so far, though, if staff are stretched too thin — even the best-trained call-handler can't do much when they're rushing through fifty calls an hour."},{"en":"take pride in","context":"Q: Why do you think employees sometimes fail to provide good customer service?\nA: When employees' treatment of customers isn't good, it's usually because they're unhappy doing the job itself. That might be because they're treated badly by their own managers, or because they've never been encouraged to take pride in their work. I remember my first ever job — I didn't enjoy the work I'd been given, and I probably wasn't as thoughtful or attentive as I should've been when I had to deal with customers. It isn't always about the person, though; sometimes it really is just one bad day, not a sign of how they usually work."},{"en":"just one bad day","context":"Q: Why do you think employees sometimes fail to provide good customer service?\nA: When employees' treatment of customers isn't good, it's usually because they're unhappy doing the job itself. That might be because they're treated badly by their own managers, or because they've never been encouraged to take pride in their work. I remember my first ever job — I didn't enjoy the work I'd been given, and I probably wasn't as thoughtful or attentive as I should've been when I had to deal with customers. It isn't always about the person, though; sometimes it really is just one bad day, not a sign of how they usually work."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Customer Service","id":"speaking-part-3-030","_displayNo":"#030","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-030-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-030-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-030-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"It matters a lot because I want to feel welcome and respected. Friendly service makes me more likely to return. For example, I regularly visit one cafe largely because of its staff. Price still matters, but I am willing to pay a little more when I know I will be treated well.","phrases":[{"en":"willing to","meaning":"Ready or happy to do something."}],"question":"How important is customer service for you?","html":"It matters a lot because I want to feel welcome and respected. Friendly service makes me more likely to return. For example, I regularly visit one cafe largely because of its staff. Price still matters, but I am <strong class=\"key-phrase\">willing to</strong> pay a little more when I know I will be treated well."},{"text":"Companies should train staff to welcome people, listen and solve problems. Employees also need enough freedom to take responsibility rather than pass a complaint around. When I called about a bill, one worker solved it quickly while another only followed a script. However, training cannot fix everything if there are too few staff and everyone is rushed.","phrases":[{"en":"take responsibility","meaning":"Accept the job of dealing with something properly."}],"question":"What can companies do to improve their customer service?","html":"Companies should train staff to welcome people, listen and solve problems. Employees also need enough freedom to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take responsibility</strong> rather than pass a complaint around. When I called about a bill, one worker solved it quickly while another only followed a script. However, training cannot fix everything if there are too few staff and everyone is rushed."},{"text":"Sometimes employees dislike their work because managers treat them badly or give them little support. That can affect how carefully they deal with customers. I was less attentive in my first job because I did not enjoy it. Still, poor service does not always show a permanent attitude; someone may simply be having a difficult day.","phrases":[{"en":"attentive","meaning":"Paying careful attention to people's needs."}],"question":"Why do you think employees sometimes fail to provide good customer service?","html":"Sometimes employees dislike their work because managers treat them badly or give them little support. That can affect how carefully they deal with customers. I was less <strong class=\"key-phrase\">attentive</strong> in my first job because I did not enjoy it. Still, poor service does not always show a permanent attitude; someone may simply be having a difficult day."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-030-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-030-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-030-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Popular dances and attitudes to dancing","prompt":"What dances are popular in your country?\nWhat is the general attitude towards dancing in your country?\nDo young people dance any differently from 10 years ago?","answer":"Q: What dances are popular in your country?\nA: There&#x27;s quite a range, and it splits fairly clearly by age. Traditional dances still appear at festivals and weddings, and older people take them seriously as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">part of cultural heritage</strong>. Among young people, styles picked up from social media dominate — K-pop choreography especially, which spread incredibly fast because anyone can learn it from a video. Then there&#x27;s dance purely as exercise: Zumba and similar classes are popular with adults who&#x27;d never call themselves dancers at all. The traditional forms are the ones losing ground, though — fewer young people are learning them properly, and a couple are kept alive mainly by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">festival performances</strong> rather than everyday practice.\n\nQ: What is the general attitude towards dancing in your country?\nA: It&#x27;s become noticeably more relaxed. Most people now see dancing as either entertainment or a form of exercise, and dance classes are common in cities. There&#x27;s still some shyness about dancing in public, especially among men of my parents&#x27; generation, who&#x27;d feel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">embarrassed doing it in front of others</strong>. Among younger people that&#x27;s largely disappeared, partly because posting dance videos online has made being seen doing it completely normal. That relaxed attitude is really a city thing, though; in smaller towns <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the old self-consciousness</strong> hasn&#x27;t shifted nearly as much.\n\nQ: Do young people dance any differently from 10 years ago?\nA: Yes, in two ways. First, they learn from global platforms rather than from family or local teachers, so the styles are far more international and they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mix influences freely</strong>. Second, a lot of dancing&#x27;s now done for the camera rather than for a room of people, which changes the movements — routines are shorter, more repetitive, and designed to look good in a fifteen-second clip. Something&#x27;s gained in creativity, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the link to local tradition</strong> has weakened, and that&#x27;s a genuine loss, not just a stylistic shift.","topic":"Dance","phrases":[{"en":"part of cultural heritage","context":"Q: What dances are popular in your country?\nA: There's quite a range, and it splits fairly clearly by age. Traditional dances still appear at festivals and weddings, and older people take them seriously as part of cultural heritage. Among young people, styles picked up from social media dominate — K-pop choreography especially, which spread incredibly fast because anyone can learn it from a video. Then there's dance purely as exercise: Zumba and similar classes are popular with adults who'd never call themselves dancers at all. The traditional forms are the ones losing ground, though — fewer young people are learning them properly, and a couple are kept alive mainly by festival performances rather than everyday practice."},{"en":"festival performances","context":"Q: What dances are popular in your country?\nA: There's quite a range, and it splits fairly clearly by age. Traditional dances still appear at festivals and weddings, and older people take them seriously as part of cultural heritage. Among young people, styles picked up from social media dominate — K-pop choreography especially, which spread incredibly fast because anyone can learn it from a video. Then there's dance purely as exercise: Zumba and similar classes are popular with adults who'd never call themselves dancers at all. The traditional forms are the ones losing ground, though — fewer young people are learning them properly, and a couple are kept alive mainly by festival performances rather than everyday practice."},{"en":"embarrassed doing it in front of others","context":"Q: What is the general attitude towards dancing in your country?\nA: It's become noticeably more relaxed. Most people now see dancing as either entertainment or a form of exercise, and dance classes are common in cities. There's still some shyness about dancing in public, especially among men of my parents' generation, who'd feel embarrassed doing it in front of others. Among younger people that's largely disappeared, partly because posting dance videos online has made being seen doing it completely normal. That relaxed attitude is really a city thing, though; in smaller towns the old self-consciousness hasn't shifted nearly as much."},{"en":"the old self-consciousness","context":"Q: What is the general attitude towards dancing in your country?\nA: It's become noticeably more relaxed. Most people now see dancing as either entertainment or a form of exercise, and dance classes are common in cities. There's still some shyness about dancing in public, especially among men of my parents' generation, who'd feel embarrassed doing it in front of others. Among younger people that's largely disappeared, partly because posting dance videos online has made being seen doing it completely normal. That relaxed attitude is really a city thing, though; in smaller towns the old self-consciousness hasn't shifted nearly as much."},{"en":"mix influences freely","context":"Q: Do young people dance any differently from 10 years ago?\nA: Yes, in two ways. First, they learn from global platforms rather than from family or local teachers, so the styles are far more international and they mix influences freely. Second, a lot of dancing's now done for the camera rather than for a room of people, which changes the movements — routines are shorter, more repetitive, and designed to look good in a fifteen-second clip. Something's gained in creativity, but the link to local tradition has weakened, and that's a genuine loss, not just a stylistic shift."},{"en":"the link to local tradition","context":"Q: Do young people dance any differently from 10 years ago?\nA: Yes, in two ways. First, they learn from global platforms rather than from family or local teachers, so the styles are far more international and they mix influences freely. Second, a lot of dancing's now done for the camera rather than for a room of people, which changes the movements — routines are shorter, more repetitive, and designed to look good in a fifteen-second clip. Something's gained in creativity, but the link to local tradition has weakened, and that's a genuine loss, not just a stylistic shift."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Dance","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-031","_displayNo":"#031","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-031-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-031-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-031-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Traditional dances appear at weddings and festivals, while younger people often learn international styles such as K-pop online. Adults also attend dance exercise classes like Zumba. Traditional forms seem less secure because fewer young people learn them fully. Some now survive mainly through festival performances rather than being part of ordinary community life.","phrases":[{"en":"Traditional forms","meaning":"Styles passed down through a culture over time."}],"question":"What dances are popular in your country?","html":"Traditional dances appear at weddings and festivals, while younger people often learn international styles such as K-pop online. Adults also attend dance exercise classes like Zumba. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Traditional forms</strong> seem less secure because fewer young people learn them fully. Some now survive mainly through festival performances rather than being part of ordinary community life."},{"text":"People seem more relaxed about dancing now, particularly in cities. They see it as entertainment or exercise, and classes are common. Some older people still feel embarrassed dancing in public. Younger people are often more comfortable because online dance videos have made it familiar, although smaller towns may remain more reserved.","phrases":[{"en":"reserved","meaning":"Not showing feelings or behaviour very openly."}],"question":"What is the general attitude towards dancing in your country?","html":"People seem more relaxed about dancing now, particularly in cities. They see it as entertainment or exercise, and classes are common. Some older people still feel embarrassed dancing in public. Younger people are often more comfortable because online dance videos have made it familiar, although smaller towns may remain more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reserved</strong>."},{"text":"Yes. Young people now learn styles from around the world through online videos, rather than mainly from local teachers. Many routines are also designed for short recordings, so movements are brief and repeated. This can encourage creativity, but it may weaken the connection to local dance traditions. I think there are gains and losses.","phrases":[{"en":"routines","meaning":"Planned sequences of movements in a dance."}],"question":"Do young people dance any differently from 10 years ago?","html":"Yes. Young people now learn styles from around the world through online videos, rather than mainly from local teachers. Many <strong class=\"key-phrase\">routines</strong> are also designed for short recordings, so movements are brief and repeated. This can encourage creativity, but it may weaken the connection to local dance traditions. I think there are gains and losses."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-031-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-031-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-031-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Choices at different stages of life","prompt":"What are the typical choices people make at different stages of their lives?\nShould important choices be made by parents rather than by young adults?\nWhy do some people like to discuss choices with other people?","answer":"Q: What are the typical choices people make at different stages of their lives?\nA: They follow a fairly predictable sequence. Children choose their friends and how much effort to put into school, which seems minor but shapes a lot more than people realise. Young adults face the biggest decisions — what to study, which career to enter, whether to move away — and they make these with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the least experience</strong> of anyone. Later choices, around housing, children and retirement, are financially bigger but usually much better informed, simply because by then people have some sense of how their own judgement performs. That&#x27;s not a fixed rule, though: some people are still making decisions <strong class=\"key-phrase\">just as badly informed</strong> at fifty as they were at twenty.\n\nQ: Should important choices be made by parents rather than by young adults?\nA: Parents should advise, and their experience is genuinely valuable, but the final decision has to belong to the young person. There&#x27;s a practical reason for this: someone pushed into a career they didn&#x27;t choose tends to resent it — I know someone who did medicine because his parents insisted, and he&#x27;s spent a decade being competent at a job he never wanted. Making a decision and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">living with the consequences</strong> is how judgement develops, and without that you get adults who&#x27;ve never really practised deciding anything. The exception is anything genuinely dangerous or irreversible, where <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a parent&#x27;s veto</strong> is reasonable whatever the teenager says.\n\nQ: Why do some people like to discuss choices with other people?\nA: Partly because talking forces you to actually articulate the reasoning, and you often notice a flaw the moment you say it out loud. Another person also brings options you hadn&#x27;t considered and questions <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the assumptions you&#x27;d stopped noticing</strong> — a friend once pointed out I was choosing a flat based purely on rent, having completely forgotten how long the commute would be. There&#x27;s an emotional element too, since a decision feels less frightening once someone else has heard it. The risk is asking people who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have a stake in the outcome</strong>, because their advice will quietly serve their own interests rather than yours.","topic":"Decisions","phrases":[{"en":"the least experience","context":"Q: What are the typical choices people make at different stages of their lives?\nA: They follow a fairly predictable sequence. Children choose their friends and how much effort to put into school, which seems minor but shapes a lot more than people realise. Young adults face the biggest decisions — what to study, which career to enter, whether to move away — and they make these with the least experience of anyone. Later choices, around housing, children and retirement, are financially bigger but usually much better informed, simply because by then people have some sense of how their own judgement performs. That's not a fixed rule, though: some people are still making decisions just as badly informed at fifty as they were at twenty."},{"en":"just as badly informed","context":"Q: What are the typical choices people make at different stages of their lives?\nA: They follow a fairly predictable sequence. Children choose their friends and how much effort to put into school, which seems minor but shapes a lot more than people realise. Young adults face the biggest decisions — what to study, which career to enter, whether to move away — and they make these with the least experience of anyone. Later choices, around housing, children and retirement, are financially bigger but usually much better informed, simply because by then people have some sense of how their own judgement performs. That's not a fixed rule, though: some people are still making decisions just as badly informed at fifty as they were at twenty."},{"en":"living with the consequences","context":"Q: Should important choices be made by parents rather than by young adults?\nA: Parents should advise, and their experience is genuinely valuable, but the final decision has to belong to the young person. There's a practical reason for this: someone pushed into a career they didn't choose tends to resent it — I know someone who did medicine because his parents insisted, and he's spent a decade being competent at a job he never wanted. Making a decision and living with the consequences is how judgement develops, and without that you get adults who've never really practised deciding anything. The exception is anything genuinely dangerous or irreversible, where a parent's veto is reasonable whatever the teenager says."},{"en":"a parent's veto","context":"Q: Should important choices be made by parents rather than by young adults?\nA: Parents should advise, and their experience is genuinely valuable, but the final decision has to belong to the young person. There's a practical reason for this: someone pushed into a career they didn't choose tends to resent it — I know someone who did medicine because his parents insisted, and he's spent a decade being competent at a job he never wanted. Making a decision and living with the consequences is how judgement develops, and without that you get adults who've never really practised deciding anything. The exception is anything genuinely dangerous or irreversible, where a parent's veto is reasonable whatever the teenager says."},{"en":"the assumptions you'd stopped noticing","context":"Q: Why do some people like to discuss choices with other people?\nA: Partly because talking forces you to actually articulate the reasoning, and you often notice a flaw the moment you say it out loud. Another person also brings options you hadn't considered and questions the assumptions you'd stopped noticing — a friend once pointed out I was choosing a flat based purely on rent, having completely forgotten how long the commute would be. There's an emotional element too, since a decision feels less frightening once someone else has heard it. The risk is asking people who have a stake in the outcome, because their advice will quietly serve their own interests rather than yours."},{"en":"have a stake in the outcome","context":"Q: Why do some people like to discuss choices with other people?\nA: Partly because talking forces you to actually articulate the reasoning, and you often notice a flaw the moment you say it out loud. Another person also brings options you hadn't considered and questions the assumptions you'd stopped noticing — a friend once pointed out I was choosing a flat based purely on rent, having completely forgotten how long the commute would be. There's an emotional element too, since a decision feels less frightening once someone else has heard it. The risk is asking people who have a stake in the outcome, because their advice will quietly serve their own interests rather than yours."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Decisions","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-032","_displayNo":"#032","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-032-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-032-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-032-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Children choose friends and how much effort to give school. Young adults make major choices about education, careers and moving away. Later, people decide about homes, children and retirement. Older adults often have more experience, but that does not guarantee better judgement. The order also varies, so not everyone follows exactly the same life path.","phrases":[{"en":"life path","meaning":"The course a person's life takes through choices and events."}],"question":"What are the typical choices people make at different stages of their lives?","html":"Children choose friends and how much effort to give school. Young adults make major choices about education, careers and moving away. Later, people decide about homes, children and retirement. Older adults often have more experience, but that does not guarantee better judgement. The order also varies, so not everyone follows exactly the same <strong class=\"key-phrase\">life path</strong>."},{"text":"Parents should offer advice, but young adults usually need to make the final choice. Being forced into a career can create lasting unhappiness. Deciding for yourself also develops judgement. However, parents should still step in when a choice is seriously dangerous or cannot easily be reversed, rather than leave a young person without protection.","phrases":[{"en":"reversed","meaning":"Changed back or undone."}],"question":"Should important choices be made by parents rather than by young adults?","html":"Parents should offer advice, but young adults usually need to make the final choice. Being forced into a career can create lasting unhappiness. Deciding for yourself also develops judgement. However, parents should still step in when a choice is seriously dangerous or cannot easily be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reversed</strong>, rather than leave a young person without protection."},{"text":"Talking helps people explain their reasoning and notice mistakes. Someone else may suggest an option they missed; a friend once reminded me to consider travel time, not just a flat's rent. Discussion can also make a decision feel less frightening. However, advice may be biased if the other person benefits from a particular result.","phrases":[{"en":"biased","meaning":"Unfairly influenced by one person's interests or preferences."}],"question":"Why do some people like to discuss choices with other people?","html":"Talking helps people explain their reasoning and notice mistakes. Someone else may suggest an option they missed; a friend once reminded me to consider travel time, not just a flat&#x27;s rent. Discussion can also make a decision feel less frightening. However, advice may be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">biased</strong> if the other person benefits from a particular result."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-032-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-032-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-032-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Why decisions matter and how to make good ones","prompt":"Why is decision-making important in our lives?\nHow can we make good decisions?\nDo you think parents should allow their children to make their own decisions?","answer":"Q: Why is decision-making important in our lives?\nA: Because the shape of your life is essentially <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the accumulation of decisions</strong> — what you study, where you work, who you spend time with, how you look after your health. Individually most of them seem small, but they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compound over decades</strong>; someone who exercises three times a week for twenty years ends up in a completely different place physically from someone who always meant to start next month. Good decision-making doesn&#x27;t guarantee a good outcome, since luck plays an enormous part, but it improves the odds and reduces the particular kind of regret that comes from never having thought about it at all.\n\nQ: How can we make good decisions?\nA: A few things help consistently. Being clear about what you&#x27;re actually trying to achieve, because a lot of bad decisions come from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">answering the wrong question</strong> — optimising for the cheapest option when what you actually wanted was reliability, for example. Gathering information from sources that don&#x27;t all agree with each other helps too, and so does deliberately asking what could go wrong, uncomfortable as that is. For anything major I&#x27;d add two more: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sleep on it</strong>, and ask someone with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no stake in the outcome</strong>. None of this removes risk entirely, though — even a carefully made decision can still turn out badly.\n\nQ: Do you think parents should allow their children to make their own decisions?\nA: Yes, but gradually, with the stakes matched to the age. A young child can choose their own clothes or how to spend their pocket money — if the choice is a poor one, the cost is small and the lesson&#x27;s real. A teenager can choose their subjects and manage their own time. What parents shouldn&#x27;t do is either decide everything, which produces adults who&#x27;ve never practised deciding anything, or step back entirely on things that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely cannot be undone</strong> — a tattoo, say, or dropping out of school at sixteen.","topic":"Decisions","phrases":[{"en":"the accumulation of decisions","context":"Q: Why is decision-making important in our lives?\nA: Because the shape of your life is essentially the accumulation of decisions — what you study, where you work, who you spend time with, how you look after your health. Individually most of them seem small, but they compound over decades; someone who exercises three times a week for twenty years ends up in a completely different place physically from someone who always meant to start next month. Good decision-making doesn't guarantee a good outcome, since luck plays an enormous part, but it improves the odds and reduces the particular kind of regret that comes from never having thought about it at all."},{"en":"compound over decades","context":"Q: Why is decision-making important in our lives?\nA: Because the shape of your life is essentially the accumulation of decisions — what you study, where you work, who you spend time with, how you look after your health. Individually most of them seem small, but they compound over decades; someone who exercises three times a week for twenty years ends up in a completely different place physically from someone who always meant to start next month. Good decision-making doesn't guarantee a good outcome, since luck plays an enormous part, but it improves the odds and reduces the particular kind of regret that comes from never having thought about it at all."},{"en":"answering the wrong question","context":"Q: How can we make good decisions?\nA: A few things help consistently. Being clear about what you're actually trying to achieve, because a lot of bad decisions come from answering the wrong question — optimising for the cheapest option when what you actually wanted was reliability, for example. Gathering information from sources that don't all agree with each other helps too, and so does deliberately asking what could go wrong, uncomfortable as that is. For anything major I'd add two more: sleep on it, and ask someone with no stake in the outcome. None of this removes risk entirely, though — even a carefully made decision can still turn out badly."},{"en":"sleep on it","context":"Q: How can we make good decisions?\nA: A few things help consistently. Being clear about what you're actually trying to achieve, because a lot of bad decisions come from answering the wrong question — optimising for the cheapest option when what you actually wanted was reliability, for example. Gathering information from sources that don't all agree with each other helps too, and so does deliberately asking what could go wrong, uncomfortable as that is. For anything major I'd add two more: sleep on it, and ask someone with no stake in the outcome. None of this removes risk entirely, though — even a carefully made decision can still turn out badly."},{"en":"no stake in the outcome","context":"Q: How can we make good decisions?\nA: A few things help consistently. Being clear about what you're actually trying to achieve, because a lot of bad decisions come from answering the wrong question — optimising for the cheapest option when what you actually wanted was reliability, for example. Gathering information from sources that don't all agree with each other helps too, and so does deliberately asking what could go wrong, uncomfortable as that is. For anything major I'd add two more: sleep on it, and ask someone with no stake in the outcome. None of this removes risk entirely, though — even a carefully made decision can still turn out badly."},{"en":"genuinely cannot be undone","context":"Q: Do you think parents should allow their children to make their own decisions?\nA: Yes, but gradually, with the stakes matched to the age. A young child can choose their own clothes or how to spend their pocket money — if the choice is a poor one, the cost is small and the lesson's real. A teenager can choose their subjects and manage their own time. What parents shouldn't do is either decide everything, which produces adults who've never practised deciding anything, or step back entirely on things that genuinely cannot be undone — a tattoo, say, or dropping out of school at sixteen."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Decisions","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-033","_displayNo":"#033","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-033-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-033-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-033-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Our lives develop through many decisions about work, relationships and health. Small choices can have large effects when repeated for years, such as exercising regularly. Making good decisions cannot remove bad luck, but it improves our chances. It also helps avoid the regret of realising we never properly considered something important.","phrases":[{"en":"regret","meaning":"Sadness about a choice or action you wish had been different."}],"question":"Why is decision-making important in our lives?","html":"Our lives develop through many decisions about work, relationships and health. Small choices can have large effects when repeated for years, such as exercising regularly. Making good decisions cannot remove bad luck, but it improves our chances. It also helps avoid the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regret</strong> of realising we never properly considered something important."},{"text":"First, be clear about your goal. Then gather information from different sources and consider what could go wrong. For an important choice, it helps to wait a night and ask someone who has no personal interest in the result. These steps do not guarantee success, but they make the decision better informed.","phrases":[{"en":"better informed","meaning":"Based on more useful or reliable knowledge."}],"question":"How can we make good decisions?","html":"First, be clear about your goal. Then gather information from different sources and consider what could go wrong. For an important choice, it helps to wait a night and ask someone who has no personal interest in the result. These steps do not guarantee success, but they make the decision <strong class=\"key-phrase\">better informed</strong>."},{"text":"Yes, with more freedom as they grow. A small child can choose clothes or spend pocket money, where mistakes are not very costly. Teenagers can make larger choices about study and time. Parents should neither control everything nor ignore serious risks. The level of responsibility needs to match the child's age and ability.","phrases":[{"en":"pocket money","meaning":"A small amount of money regularly given to a child to spend."}],"question":"Do you think parents should allow their children to make their own decisions?","html":"Yes, with more freedom as they grow. A small child can choose clothes or spend <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pocket money</strong>, where mistakes are not very costly. Teenagers can make larger choices about study and time. Parents should neither control everything nor ignore serious risks. The level of responsibility needs to match the child&#x27;s age and ability."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-033-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-033-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-033-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"What dreams tell us and whether to interpret them","prompt":"What can dreams tell us about ourselves?\nWhy are dreams considered important in many cultures?\nDo you think it is helpful to interpret someone's dreams for them?","answer":"Q: What can dreams tell us about ourselves?\nA: Something, but less than people would like. Dreams clearly draw on recent experience — you dream about whatever you&#x27;ve been worrying about, so they can point you towards a concern you haven&#x27;t consciously admitted to yourself. I once had the same anxiety dream about missing a flight every night for a week before a big exam, which wasn&#x27;t exactly subtle. What I&#x27;m sceptical about is the idea of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fixed symbols</strong>, where a particular object always means one thing — there&#x27;s no real evidence for that. That said, I wouldn&#x27;t dismiss dreams completely; one that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeps coming back</strong> is at least worth paying attention to.\n\nQ: Why are dreams considered important in many cultures?\nA: Because they feel significant. A dream is vivid, emotionally intense and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely outside your control</strong>, so it&#x27;s natural to assume it came from somewhere else rather than from your own mind. Historically, many cultures treated dreams as messages from ancestors or warnings about the future, and important decisions, going to war, choosing a leader, were sometimes taken on that basis. They also served a social function, giving communities a shared language for fears that were difficult to state directly. Modern psychology takes a far more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">modest view</strong> of what dreams can actually tell us, but the emotional pull hasn&#x27;t gone away just because the science caught up.\n\nQ: Do you think it is helpful to interpret someone&#x27;s dreams for them?\nA: It can be, but it depends entirely on how it&#x27;s done. If it opens a conversation about what someone&#x27;s anxious about, that&#x27;s useful, and the dream&#x27;s simply the way in. What I&#x27;d avoid is presenting an interpretation as fact, because telling somebody confidently what their dream means can create <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a false certainty</strong> they then act on — deciding to leave a job because a dream book said water means change, say. A therapist explores it together with the person; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fixed reading from a book</strong> does the opposite.","topic":"Dreams","phrases":[{"en":"fixed symbols","context":"Q: What can dreams tell us about ourselves?\nA: Something, but less than people would like. Dreams clearly draw on recent experience — you dream about whatever you've been worrying about, so they can point you towards a concern you haven't consciously admitted to yourself. I once had the same anxiety dream about missing a flight every night for a week before a big exam, which wasn't exactly subtle. What I'm sceptical about is the idea of fixed symbols, where a particular object always means one thing — there's no real evidence for that. That said, I wouldn't dismiss dreams completely; one that keeps coming back is at least worth paying attention to."},{"en":"keeps coming back","context":"Q: What can dreams tell us about ourselves?\nA: Something, but less than people would like. Dreams clearly draw on recent experience — you dream about whatever you've been worrying about, so they can point you towards a concern you haven't consciously admitted to yourself. I once had the same anxiety dream about missing a flight every night for a week before a big exam, which wasn't exactly subtle. What I'm sceptical about is the idea of fixed symbols, where a particular object always means one thing — there's no real evidence for that. That said, I wouldn't dismiss dreams completely; one that keeps coming back is at least worth paying attention to."},{"en":"completely outside your control","context":"Q: Why are dreams considered important in many cultures?\nA: Because they feel significant. A dream is vivid, emotionally intense and completely outside your control, so it's natural to assume it came from somewhere else rather than from your own mind. Historically, many cultures treated dreams as messages from ancestors or warnings about the future, and important decisions, going to war, choosing a leader, were sometimes taken on that basis. They also served a social function, giving communities a shared language for fears that were difficult to state directly. Modern psychology takes a far more modest view of what dreams can actually tell us, but the emotional pull hasn't gone away just because the science caught up."},{"en":"modest view","context":"Q: Why are dreams considered important in many cultures?\nA: Because they feel significant. A dream is vivid, emotionally intense and completely outside your control, so it's natural to assume it came from somewhere else rather than from your own mind. Historically, many cultures treated dreams as messages from ancestors or warnings about the future, and important decisions, going to war, choosing a leader, were sometimes taken on that basis. They also served a social function, giving communities a shared language for fears that were difficult to state directly. Modern psychology takes a far more modest view of what dreams can actually tell us, but the emotional pull hasn't gone away just because the science caught up."},{"en":"a false certainty","context":"Q: Do you think it is helpful to interpret someone's dreams for them?\nA: It can be, but it depends entirely on how it's done. If it opens a conversation about what someone's anxious about, that's useful, and the dream's simply the way in. What I'd avoid is presenting an interpretation as fact, because telling somebody confidently what their dream means can create a false certainty they then act on — deciding to leave a job because a dream book said water means change, say. A therapist explores it together with the person; a fixed reading from a book does the opposite."},{"en":"a fixed reading from a book","context":"Q: Do you think it is helpful to interpret someone's dreams for them?\nA: It can be, but it depends entirely on how it's done. If it opens a conversation about what someone's anxious about, that's useful, and the dream's simply the way in. What I'd avoid is presenting an interpretation as fact, because telling somebody confidently what their dream means can create a false certainty they then act on — deciding to leave a job because a dream book said water means change, say. A therapist explores it together with the person; a fixed reading from a book does the opposite."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Dreams","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-034","_displayNo":"#034","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-034-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-034-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-034-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Dreams may show what we have recently experienced or worried about. Before an exam, for example, I repeatedly dreamed about missing a flight. That suggested anxiety rather than a secret prediction. I don't think every object in a dream has one fixed meaning, but a repeated dream may still encourage us to consider our feelings.","phrases":[{"en":"anxiety","meaning":"A feeling of worry or nervousness."}],"question":"What can dreams tell us about ourselves?","html":"Dreams may show what we have recently experienced or worried about. Before an exam, for example, I repeatedly dreamed about missing a flight. That suggested <strong class=\"key-phrase\">anxiety</strong> rather than a secret prediction. I don&#x27;t think every object in a dream has one fixed meaning, but a repeated dream may still encourage us to consider our feelings."},{"text":"Dreams can feel very real and emotional, so people may think they have a special source or message. Many cultures have treated them as warnings or messages from ancestors. They can also give people a way to discuss fears. Modern explanations are usually more cautious, but the strong feelings that dreams create still matter to people.","phrases":[{"en":"ancestors","meaning":"Family members or people from earlier generations."}],"question":"Why are dreams considered important in many cultures?","html":"Dreams can feel very real and emotional, so people may think they have a special source or message. Many cultures have treated them as warnings or messages from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ancestors</strong>. They can also give people a way to discuss fears. Modern explanations are usually more cautious, but the strong feelings that dreams create still matter to people."},{"text":"It can help if it starts a conversation about the person's feelings or worries. However, I would not claim that one interpretation is certainly true. Someone might make an important decision based on a meaning that is only a guess. Exploring possibilities together is safer than telling them a dream has a single fixed message.","phrases":[{"en":"interpretation","meaning":"One possible explanation of what something means."}],"question":"Do you think it is helpful to interpret someone's dreams for them?","html":"It can help if it starts a conversation about the person&#x27;s feelings or worries. However, I would not claim that one <strong class=\"key-phrase\">interpretation</strong> is certainly true. Someone might make an important decision based on a meaning that is only a guess. Exploring possibilities together is safer than telling them a dream has a single fixed message."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-034-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-034-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-034-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"What makes a good lesson, and teaching yourself","prompt":"What do you think makes a good lesson?\nDo you think it is better to have a teacher or to teach yourself?\nDo you think the traditional classroom will disappear in the future?","answer":"Q: What do you think makes a good lesson?\nA: I think a good lesson is one that&#x27;s interesting and engaging. By &#x27;engaging&#x27; I mean the students should feel actually involved, not just present — they should feel they&#x27;re learning something <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relevant to them</strong>. A lot depends on how the teacher <strong class=\"key-phrase\">delivers the content</strong>, too, in a way students actually respond to. My favourite teacher at university used to get us to teach parts of the lessons ourselves, which worked brilliantly for confident students, though it left the quieter students visibly uncomfortable.\n\nQ: Do you think it is better to have a teacher or to teach yourself?\nA: Well, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">there&#x27;s no substitute for</strong> a good teacher, in most cases. You can teach yourself, but you&#x27;ll usually learn a lot faster <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with the guidance of a teacher</strong> who can see your mistakes as you make them. When you&#x27;re learning a language, for instance, you really need someone to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">correct your mistakes</strong> — books can give you grammar and vocabulary, but they can&#x27;t tell you where you&#x27;re actually going wrong as you speak. A teacher also keeps you working when your motivation drops, which is usually what defeats people studying entirely alone. The exception&#x27;s a highly motivated adult learner with a specific, narrow goal — someone cramming for a work exam can often manage perfectly well with self-study materials alone.\n\nQ: Do you think the traditional classroom will disappear in the future?\nA: The <strong class=\"key-phrase\">traditional classroom</strong> won&#x27;t disappear, but it may become less dominant. Students can learn factual content online now and use classroom time for discussion, feedback and practical work instead, so the teacher&#x27;s role may shift from delivering information to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">guiding and evaluating learning</strong>. Teaching moved online during the pandemic and that made the limits fairly clear: students could reach the material perfectly well, but attainment still fell, because most people need the structure of a fixed time, a room, and somebody who notices when they&#x27;ve stopped trying. The exception&#x27;s a small minority of highly self-directed adult learners, for whom online-only study genuinely does work.","topic":"Education","phrases":[{"en":"relevant to them","context":"Q: What do you think makes a good lesson?\nA: I think a good lesson is one that's interesting and engaging. By 'engaging' I mean the students should feel actually involved, not just present — they should feel they're learning something relevant to them. A lot depends on how the teacher delivers the content, too, in a way students actually respond to. My favourite teacher at university used to get us to teach parts of the lessons ourselves, which worked brilliantly for confident students, though it left the quieter students visibly uncomfortable."},{"en":"delivers the content","context":"Q: What do you think makes a good lesson?\nA: I think a good lesson is one that's interesting and engaging. By 'engaging' I mean the students should feel actually involved, not just present — they should feel they're learning something relevant to them. A lot depends on how the teacher delivers the content, too, in a way students actually respond to. My favourite teacher at university used to get us to teach parts of the lessons ourselves, which worked brilliantly for confident students, though it left the quieter students visibly uncomfortable."},{"en":"there's no substitute for","context":"Q: Do you think it is better to have a teacher or to teach yourself?\nA: Well, there's no substitute for a good teacher, in most cases. You can teach yourself, but you'll usually learn a lot faster with the guidance of a teacher who can see your mistakes as you make them. When you're learning a language, for instance, you really need someone to correct your mistakes — books can give you grammar and vocabulary, but they can't tell you where you're actually going wrong as you speak. A teacher also keeps you working when your motivation drops, which is usually what defeats people studying entirely alone. The exception's a highly motivated adult learner with a specific, narrow goal — someone cramming for a work exam can often manage perfectly well with self-study materials alone."},{"en":"with the guidance of a teacher","context":"Q: Do you think it is better to have a teacher or to teach yourself?\nA: Well, there's no substitute for a good teacher, in most cases. You can teach yourself, but you'll usually learn a lot faster with the guidance of a teacher who can see your mistakes as you make them. When you're learning a language, for instance, you really need someone to correct your mistakes — books can give you grammar and vocabulary, but they can't tell you where you're actually going wrong as you speak. A teacher also keeps you working when your motivation drops, which is usually what defeats people studying entirely alone. The exception's a highly motivated adult learner with a specific, narrow goal — someone cramming for a work exam can often manage perfectly well with self-study materials alone."},{"en":"correct your mistakes","context":"Q: Do you think it is better to have a teacher or to teach yourself?\nA: Well, there's no substitute for a good teacher, in most cases. You can teach yourself, but you'll usually learn a lot faster with the guidance of a teacher who can see your mistakes as you make them. When you're learning a language, for instance, you really need someone to correct your mistakes — books can give you grammar and vocabulary, but they can't tell you where you're actually going wrong as you speak. A teacher also keeps you working when your motivation drops, which is usually what defeats people studying entirely alone. The exception's a highly motivated adult learner with a specific, narrow goal — someone cramming for a work exam can often manage perfectly well with self-study materials alone."},{"en":"traditional classroom","context":"Q: Do you think the traditional classroom will disappear in the future?\nA: The traditional classroom won't disappear, but it may become less dominant. Students can learn factual content online now and use classroom time for discussion, feedback and practical work instead, so the teacher's role may shift from delivering information to guiding and evaluating learning. Teaching moved online during the pandemic and that made the limits fairly clear: students could reach the material perfectly well, but attainment still fell, because most people need the structure of a fixed time, a room, and somebody who notices when they've stopped trying. The exception's a small minority of highly self-directed adult learners, for whom online-only study genuinely does work."},{"en":"guiding and evaluating learning","context":"Q: Do you think the traditional classroom will disappear in the future?\nA: The traditional classroom won't disappear, but it may become less dominant. Students can learn factual content online now and use classroom time for discussion, feedback and practical work instead, so the teacher's role may shift from delivering information to guiding and evaluating learning. Teaching moved online during the pandemic and that made the limits fairly clear: students could reach the material perfectly well, but attainment still fell, because most people need the structure of a fixed time, a room, and somebody who notices when they've stopped trying. The exception's a small minority of highly self-directed adult learners, for whom online-only study genuinely does work."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Education","id":"speaking-part-3-035","_displayNo":"#035","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-035-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-035-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-035-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"A good lesson makes students interested and actively involved. They should understand why the topic is useful, and the teacher needs to explain it in a way they can follow. My university teacher sometimes asked us to teach parts ourselves. That worked well for confident students, but quieter students needed a different way to participate.","phrases":[{"en":"actively involved","meaning":"Taking part rather than only watching or listening."}],"question":"What do you think makes a good lesson?","html":"A good lesson makes students interested and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">actively involved</strong>. They should understand why the topic is useful, and the teacher needs to explain it in a way they can follow. My university teacher sometimes asked us to teach parts ourselves. That worked well for confident students, but quieter students needed a different way to participate."},{"text":"Usually a good teacher helps people learn faster by noticing and correcting mistakes. Language learners especially benefit from feedback on their speaking. Teachers also provide structure when motivation falls. However, an adult with a clear goal and strong motivation may study successfully alone, particularly for a specific exam. So it depends partly on the learner.","phrases":[{"en":"motivation","meaning":"The desire or reason that makes you want to do something."}],"question":"Do you think it is better to have a teacher or to teach yourself?","html":"Usually a good teacher helps people learn faster by noticing and correcting mistakes. Language learners especially benefit from feedback on their speaking. Teachers also provide structure when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">motivation</strong> falls. However, an adult with a clear goal and strong motivation may study successfully alone, particularly for a specific exam. So it depends partly on the learner."},{"text":"I don't think it will disappear. Students may learn facts online and use class time for discussion, practice and feedback. However, many still need a set time, a teacher and a shared space to keep working steadily. Online-only study can suit independent adults, but a mixture is likely to work better for many learners.","phrases":[{"en":"independent adults","meaning":"Adults who can organise and manage their learning without much help."}],"question":"Do you think the traditional classroom will disappear in the future?","html":"I don&#x27;t think it will disappear. Students may learn facts online and use class time for discussion, practice and feedback. However, many still need a set time, a teacher and a shared space to keep working steadily. Online-only study can suit <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independent adults</strong>, but a mixture is likely to work better for many learners."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-035-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-035-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-035-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How many subjects schools teach, and changes to the system","prompt":"Do you think too many subjects are taught at school?\nHave there been any major changes in the education system in your country?\nShould everyone be entitled to go to university?","answer":"Q: Do you think too many subjects are taught at school?\nA: In some systems, yes. There&#x27;s a genuine tension here: breadth lets children discover what they&#x27;re actually good at, but an overcrowded timetable means nothing gets studied in real depth, and students end up memorising for exams and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forgetting immediately afterwards</strong>. I&#x27;d rather see fewer subjects taught properly — the core ones, language, maths and science, need enough time for real understanding, and I&#x27;d protect those before adding anything new. I wouldn&#x27;t cut breadth completely, though; a child who never tries art or music simply never finds out whether <strong class=\"key-phrase\">that&#x27;s where their talent lies</strong>.\n\nQ: Have there been any major changes in the education system in your country?\nA: Several. The most obvious is technology, since classrooms use digital resources routinely now and students have access to information their teachers never had. Assessment&#x27;s also become far more central, with much more frequent testing, which has advantages but does <strong class=\"key-phrase\">narrow what teachers cover</strong>, since anything not tested tends to get squeezed out. There&#x27;s far more international influence too: English is taught earlier, international curricula have spread, and studying abroad&#x27;s now a realistic ambition for many more families than it was a generation ago. Not every family benefits equally, though — that international option still depends heavily on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">household income</strong>.\n\nQ: Should everyone be entitled to go to university?\nA: Everyone should have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fair opportunity</strong>, in the sense that money should never be the barrier. But entitlement&#x27;s a different thing from everyone actually going. A degree isn&#x27;t the right path for every person, and countries that pushed participation very high often ended up with graduates stuck in jobs that never needed a degree in the first place, carrying <strong class=\"key-phrase\">student debt</strong> for it. Strong vocational training deserves equal status — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a skilled electrician is not a failed academic</strong>.","topic":"Education","phrases":[{"en":"forgetting immediately afterwards","context":"Q: Do you think too many subjects are taught at school?\nA: In some systems, yes. There's a genuine tension here: breadth lets children discover what they're actually good at, but an overcrowded timetable means nothing gets studied in real depth, and students end up memorising for exams and forgetting immediately afterwards. I'd rather see fewer subjects taught properly — the core ones, language, maths and science, need enough time for real understanding, and I'd protect those before adding anything new. I wouldn't cut breadth completely, though; a child who never tries art or music simply never finds out whether that's where their talent lies."},{"en":"that's where their talent lies","context":"Q: Do you think too many subjects are taught at school?\nA: In some systems, yes. There's a genuine tension here: breadth lets children discover what they're actually good at, but an overcrowded timetable means nothing gets studied in real depth, and students end up memorising for exams and forgetting immediately afterwards. I'd rather see fewer subjects taught properly — the core ones, language, maths and science, need enough time for real understanding, and I'd protect those before adding anything new. I wouldn't cut breadth completely, though; a child who never tries art or music simply never finds out whether that's where their talent lies."},{"en":"narrow what teachers cover","context":"Q: Have there been any major changes in the education system in your country?\nA: Several. The most obvious is technology, since classrooms use digital resources routinely now and students have access to information their teachers never had. Assessment's also become far more central, with much more frequent testing, which has advantages but does narrow what teachers cover, since anything not tested tends to get squeezed out. There's far more international influence too: English is taught earlier, international curricula have spread, and studying abroad's now a realistic ambition for many more families than it was a generation ago. Not every family benefits equally, though — that international option still depends heavily on household income."},{"en":"household income","context":"Q: Have there been any major changes in the education system in your country?\nA: Several. The most obvious is technology, since classrooms use digital resources routinely now and students have access to information their teachers never had. Assessment's also become far more central, with much more frequent testing, which has advantages but does narrow what teachers cover, since anything not tested tends to get squeezed out. There's far more international influence too: English is taught earlier, international curricula have spread, and studying abroad's now a realistic ambition for many more families than it was a generation ago. Not every family benefits equally, though — that international option still depends heavily on household income."},{"en":"a fair opportunity","context":"Q: Should everyone be entitled to go to university?\nA: Everyone should have a fair opportunity, in the sense that money should never be the barrier. But entitlement's a different thing from everyone actually going. A degree isn't the right path for every person, and countries that pushed participation very high often ended up with graduates stuck in jobs that never needed a degree in the first place, carrying student debt for it. Strong vocational training deserves equal status — a skilled electrician is not a failed academic."},{"en":"student debt","context":"Q: Should everyone be entitled to go to university?\nA: Everyone should have a fair opportunity, in the sense that money should never be the barrier. But entitlement's a different thing from everyone actually going. A degree isn't the right path for every person, and countries that pushed participation very high often ended up with graduates stuck in jobs that never needed a degree in the first place, carrying student debt for it. Strong vocational training deserves equal status — a skilled electrician is not a failed academic."},{"en":"a skilled electrician is not a failed academic","context":"Q: Should everyone be entitled to go to university?\nA: Everyone should have a fair opportunity, in the sense that money should never be the barrier. But entitlement's a different thing from everyone actually going. A degree isn't the right path for every person, and countries that pushed participation very high often ended up with graduates stuck in jobs that never needed a degree in the first place, carrying student debt for it. Strong vocational training deserves equal status — a skilled electrician is not a failed academic."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Education","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-036","_displayNo":"#036","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-036-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-036-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-036-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Sometimes there are so many subjects that students only memorise enough to pass tests. I would give key subjects such as languages, maths and science enough time for real understanding. However, schools should still offer some variety. Without trying art or music, children may never discover that they have a talent for them.","phrases":[{"en":"talent","meaning":"A natural or developed ability to do something well."}],"question":"Do you think too many subjects are taught at school?","html":"Sometimes there are so many subjects that students only memorise enough to pass tests. I would give key subjects such as languages, maths and science enough time for real understanding. However, schools should still offer some variety. Without trying art or music, children may never discover that they have a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">talent</strong> for them."},{"text":"Technology is a major change: students now use digital resources and can find information easily. There is also more testing, which can push teachers towards exam content. English and international education have become more important too. However, these opportunities are unequal, since studying abroad or following certain programmes still depends strongly on family income.","phrases":[{"en":"digital resources","meaning":"Learning materials available through computers or the internet."}],"question":"Have there been any major changes in the education system in your country?","html":"Technology is a major change: students now use <strong class=\"key-phrase\">digital resources</strong> and can find information easily. There is also more testing, which can push teachers towards exam content. English and international education have become more important too. However, these opportunities are unequal, since studying abroad or following certain programmes still depends strongly on family income."},{"text":"Everyone should have a fair chance if university suits them, and lack of money should not block that. However, a degree is not the best route for everyone. Some graduates take jobs that do not need one while still owing money. Strong practical training deserves equal respect because skilled work is valuable too.","phrases":[{"en":"practical training","meaning":"Education that teaches the skills needed for a particular kind of work."}],"question":"Should everyone be entitled to go to university?","html":"Everyone should have a fair chance if university suits them, and lack of money should not block that. However, a degree is not the best route for everyone. Some graduates take jobs that do not need one while still owing money. Strong <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical training</strong> deserves equal respect because skilled work is valuable too."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-036-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-036-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-036-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"The classroom of the future and what makes a good teacher","prompt":"How will classrooms change in the future?\nWhat qualities make a good teacher?","answer":"Q: How will classrooms change in the future?\nA: I expect a mixture rather than a revolution. Digital tools will handle the routine parts — drilling vocabulary, marking exercises, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adjusting difficulty to each student</strong> — because software can do that better and faster than one teacher with thirty pupils. But the classroom itself won&#x27;t disappear: remote teaching during the pandemic showed fairly clearly that most children don&#x27;t learn well without a teacher physically present, and that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the weakest students suffer most</strong> when that structure disappears. It&#x27;s not universal, though — a small number of highly motivated older students genuinely thrive with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">self-paced online courses</strong>, so the mix will probably look different by age group.\n\nQ: What qualities make a good teacher?\nA: Three things stand out for me. First, they know the subject properly, because you can only explain something simply if you actually understand it deeply yourself. Second, they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">notice when a student is lost</strong>, which sounds obvious but is surprisingly rare in practice. And third, they set high expectations while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">making it safe to be wrong</strong> — if pupils are afraid of looking foolish, they stop asking questions, and the learning stops right along with it. None of that requires charisma particularly; it requires sustained attention to the individual student, which is exactly why <strong class=\"key-phrase\">class size matters</strong> so much — even the best teacher can&#x27;t give thirty-five children that kind of attention.","topic":"Education","phrases":[{"en":"adjusting difficulty to each student","context":"Q: How will classrooms change in the future?\nA: I expect a mixture rather than a revolution. Digital tools will handle the routine parts — drilling vocabulary, marking exercises, adjusting difficulty to each student — because software can do that better and faster than one teacher with thirty pupils. But the classroom itself won't disappear: remote teaching during the pandemic showed fairly clearly that most children don't learn well without a teacher physically present, and that the weakest students suffer most when that structure disappears. It's not universal, though — a small number of highly motivated older students genuinely thrive with self-paced online courses, so the mix will probably look different by age group."},{"en":"the weakest students suffer most","context":"Q: How will classrooms change in the future?\nA: I expect a mixture rather than a revolution. Digital tools will handle the routine parts — drilling vocabulary, marking exercises, adjusting difficulty to each student — because software can do that better and faster than one teacher with thirty pupils. But the classroom itself won't disappear: remote teaching during the pandemic showed fairly clearly that most children don't learn well without a teacher physically present, and that the weakest students suffer most when that structure disappears. It's not universal, though — a small number of highly motivated older students genuinely thrive with self-paced online courses, so the mix will probably look different by age group."},{"en":"self-paced online courses","context":"Q: How will classrooms change in the future?\nA: I expect a mixture rather than a revolution. Digital tools will handle the routine parts — drilling vocabulary, marking exercises, adjusting difficulty to each student — because software can do that better and faster than one teacher with thirty pupils. But the classroom itself won't disappear: remote teaching during the pandemic showed fairly clearly that most children don't learn well without a teacher physically present, and that the weakest students suffer most when that structure disappears. It's not universal, though — a small number of highly motivated older students genuinely thrive with self-paced online courses, so the mix will probably look different by age group."},{"en":"notice when a student is lost","context":"Q: What qualities make a good teacher?\nA: Three things stand out for me. First, they know the subject properly, because you can only explain something simply if you actually understand it deeply yourself. Second, they notice when a student is lost, which sounds obvious but is surprisingly rare in practice. And third, they set high expectations while making it safe to be wrong — if pupils are afraid of looking foolish, they stop asking questions, and the learning stops right along with it. None of that requires charisma particularly; it requires sustained attention to the individual student, which is exactly why class size matters so much — even the best teacher can't give thirty-five children that kind of attention."},{"en":"making it safe to be wrong","context":"Q: What qualities make a good teacher?\nA: Three things stand out for me. First, they know the subject properly, because you can only explain something simply if you actually understand it deeply yourself. Second, they notice when a student is lost, which sounds obvious but is surprisingly rare in practice. And third, they set high expectations while making it safe to be wrong — if pupils are afraid of looking foolish, they stop asking questions, and the learning stops right along with it. None of that requires charisma particularly; it requires sustained attention to the individual student, which is exactly why class size matters so much — even the best teacher can't give thirty-five children that kind of attention."},{"en":"class size matters","context":"Q: What qualities make a good teacher?\nA: Three things stand out for me. First, they know the subject properly, because you can only explain something simply if you actually understand it deeply yourself. Second, they notice when a student is lost, which sounds obvious but is surprisingly rare in practice. And third, they set high expectations while making it safe to be wrong — if pupils are afraid of looking foolish, they stop asking questions, and the learning stops right along with it. None of that requires charisma particularly; it requires sustained attention to the individual student, which is exactly why class size matters so much — even the best teacher can't give thirty-five children that kind of attention."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Education","id":"speaking-part-3-037","_displayNo":"#037","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-037-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-037-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I expect more digital tools for routine practice, marking and activities matched to each student's level. Teachers will still matter because many children need guidance and a clear structure. Older, highly motivated students may prefer more independent online study. So classrooms will probably combine technology and personal teaching, with the balance depending on students' ages and needs.","phrases":[{"en":"guidance","meaning":"Help and advice about how to do something."}],"question":"How will classrooms change in the future?","html":"I expect more digital tools for routine practice, marking and activities matched to each student&#x27;s level. Teachers will still matter because many children need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">guidance</strong> and a clear structure. Older, highly motivated students may prefer more independent online study. So classrooms will probably combine technology and personal teaching, with the balance depending on students&#x27; ages and needs."},{"text":"A good teacher understands the subject, notices when students are confused and expects effort without making mistakes feel shameful. Students need to feel safe asking questions. These qualities require attention to each person, not just an entertaining personality. That is why class size matters: even an excellent teacher struggles to support too many learners individually.","phrases":[{"en":"individually","meaning":"Separately, with attention to each person."}],"question":"What qualities make a good teacher?","html":"A good teacher understands the subject, notices when students are confused and expects effort without making mistakes feel shameful. Students need to feel safe asking questions. These qualities require attention to each person, not just an entertaining personality. That is why class size matters: even an excellent teacher struggles to support too many learners <strong class=\"key-phrase\">individually</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-037-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-037-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Showing anger, and whether men and women differ","prompt":"Do you think it is good to show your emotions when you are angry?\nIn your opinion, do women show their emotions more than men?\nWhy do you think men tend to show their emotions less than women?","answer":"Q: Do you think it is good to show your emotions when you are angry?\nA: It&#x27;s better than concealing it, but how you show it decides everything. Suppressing anger doesn&#x27;t remove it — it usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resurfaces later as resentment</strong>, or comes out in some completely unrelated argument a week later over something trivial like the washing-up. Saying clearly what caused the problem, while you&#x27;re still able to speak calmly, generally resolves it. What doesn&#x27;t work is shouting or insulting somebody, because the other person stops listening to the complaint and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">starts defending themselves against the attack</strong> instead — at which point nothing productive is actually being said.\n\nQ: In your opinion, do women show their emotions more than men?\nA: Outwardly, often yes, but I&#x27;d question whether they actually feel more. The difference looks largely learned to me — in most cultures girls are permitted <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a wider range of emotional expression</strong>, while boys are steered towards a narrower one from a young age. So the same feeling gets displayed differently rather than experienced differently. The variation between individuals is far larger than the variation between the sexes, though; I know men who are highly expressive and women who reveal almost nothing, my own brother and sister being a perfect example of exactly that.\n\nQ: Why do you think men tend to show their emotions less than women?\nA: Mostly upbringing, I&#x27;d say. Boys are told very early not to cry, often by well-meaning adults, and they quickly learn that visible distress <strong class=\"key-phrase\">invites mockery from other boys</strong>. That becomes self-reinforcing: if nobody in the group admits to struggling, admitting it feels like being the only weak one. It has real consequences too, since men are noticeably less likely to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ask for help when they need it</strong>. That&#x27;s shifting a bit in younger generations, though, where <strong class=\"key-phrase\">talking openly</strong> about mental health has become far more normal among male friend groups than it was even ten years ago.","topic":"Emotions","phrases":[{"en":"resurfaces later as resentment","context":"Q: Do you think it is good to show your emotions when you are angry?\nA: It's better than concealing it, but how you show it decides everything. Suppressing anger doesn't remove it — it usually resurfaces later as resentment, or comes out in some completely unrelated argument a week later over something trivial like the washing-up. Saying clearly what caused the problem, while you're still able to speak calmly, generally resolves it. What doesn't work is shouting or insulting somebody, because the other person stops listening to the complaint and starts defending themselves against the attack instead — at which point nothing productive is actually being said."},{"en":"starts defending themselves against the attack","context":"Q: Do you think it is good to show your emotions when you are angry?\nA: It's better than concealing it, but how you show it decides everything. Suppressing anger doesn't remove it — it usually resurfaces later as resentment, or comes out in some completely unrelated argument a week later over something trivial like the washing-up. Saying clearly what caused the problem, while you're still able to speak calmly, generally resolves it. What doesn't work is shouting or insulting somebody, because the other person stops listening to the complaint and starts defending themselves against the attack instead — at which point nothing productive is actually being said."},{"en":"a wider range of emotional expression","context":"Q: In your opinion, do women show their emotions more than men?\nA: Outwardly, often yes, but I'd question whether they actually feel more. The difference looks largely learned to me — in most cultures girls are permitted a wider range of emotional expression, while boys are steered towards a narrower one from a young age. So the same feeling gets displayed differently rather than experienced differently. The variation between individuals is far larger than the variation between the sexes, though; I know men who are highly expressive and women who reveal almost nothing, my own brother and sister being a perfect example of exactly that."},{"en":"invites mockery from other boys","context":"Q: Why do you think men tend to show their emotions less than women?\nA: Mostly upbringing, I'd say. Boys are told very early not to cry, often by well-meaning adults, and they quickly learn that visible distress invites mockery from other boys. That becomes self-reinforcing: if nobody in the group admits to struggling, admitting it feels like being the only weak one. It has real consequences too, since men are noticeably less likely to ask for help when they need it. That's shifting a bit in younger generations, though, where talking openly about mental health has become far more normal among male friend groups than it was even ten years ago."},{"en":"ask for help when they need it","context":"Q: Why do you think men tend to show their emotions less than women?\nA: Mostly upbringing, I'd say. Boys are told very early not to cry, often by well-meaning adults, and they quickly learn that visible distress invites mockery from other boys. That becomes self-reinforcing: if nobody in the group admits to struggling, admitting it feels like being the only weak one. It has real consequences too, since men are noticeably less likely to ask for help when they need it. That's shifting a bit in younger generations, though, where talking openly about mental health has become far more normal among male friend groups than it was even ten years ago."},{"en":"talking openly","context":"Q: Why do you think men tend to show their emotions less than women?\nA: Mostly upbringing, I'd say. Boys are told very early not to cry, often by well-meaning adults, and they quickly learn that visible distress invites mockery from other boys. That becomes self-reinforcing: if nobody in the group admits to struggling, admitting it feels like being the only weak one. It has real consequences too, since men are noticeably less likely to ask for help when they need it. That's shifting a bit in younger generations, though, where talking openly about mental health has become far more normal among male friend groups than it was even ten years ago."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Emotions","id":"speaking-part-3-038","_displayNo":"#038","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-038-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-038-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-038-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, if you explain the anger calmly. Hiding it does not necessarily solve the problem and may lead to another argument later. Saying what upset you can help. Shouting or insulting someone usually makes them defend themselves instead of listen, though. The important thing is expressing the feeling clearly without attacking the other person.","phrases":[{"en":"defend themselves","meaning":"Try to protect themselves from criticism or blame."}],"question":"Do you think it is good to show your emotions when you are angry?","html":"Yes, if you explain the anger calmly. Hiding it does not necessarily solve the problem and may lead to another argument later. Saying what upset you can help. Shouting or insulting someone usually makes them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">defend themselves</strong> instead of listen, though. The important thing is expressing the feeling clearly without attacking the other person."},{"text":"Women may sometimes show emotion more openly, but that does not prove they feel more. Children often learn different rules about expressing feelings: girls may be allowed more freedom, while boys are discouraged. Individual differences are large, though. I know expressive men and quiet women, so I would avoid judging a person mainly by gender.","phrases":[{"en":"expressive","meaning":"Showing thoughts and feelings openly."}],"question":"In your opinion, do women show their emotions more than men?","html":"Women may sometimes show emotion more openly, but that does not prove they feel more. Children often learn different rules about expressing feelings: girls may be allowed more freedom, while boys are discouraged. Individual differences are large, though. I know <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expressive</strong> men and quiet women, so I would avoid judging a person mainly by gender."},{"text":"Boys are often taught not to cry and may fear being laughed at if they admit difficulties. When no one in a group talks openly, asking for help can feel like weakness. However, this is changing among some younger men as discussing mental health becomes more normal. I think upbringing and social expectations matter more than a simple natural difference.","phrases":[{"en":"upbringing","meaning":"The way a person is taught and cared for while growing up."}],"question":"Why do you think men tend to show their emotions less than women?","html":"Boys are often taught not to cry and may fear being laughed at if they admit difficulties. When no one in a group talks openly, asking for help can feel like weakness. However, this is changing among some younger men as discussing mental health becomes more normal. I think <strong class=\"key-phrase\">upbringing</strong> and social expectations matter more than a simple natural difference."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-038-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-038-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-038-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"What excites people, and staying calm","prompt":"What generally makes people in your country excited?\nAre people in your country generally calm, or do they get excited easily?\nHow do you think people show their excitement?","answer":"Q: What generally makes people in your country excited?\nA: Sport&#x27;s probably the biggest one — a national football victory genuinely brings people out onto the streets in a way almost nothing else does. Beyond that, family celebrations like weddings and New Year, and personal milestones such as exam results or a first job. Announcements about things people have been waiting for, a concert finally being confirmed, say, also generate a surprising amount of excitement. The common thread is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">anticipation combined with emotional investment</strong>; excitement seems to need both. It&#x27;s not universal, though — plenty of people find big national events <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely underwhelming</strong>, and get far more excited by something purely personal instead.\n\nQ: Are people in your country generally calm, or do they get excited easily?\nA: It depends enormously on the setting, which I suspect is true of most cultures. In formal situations, at work, with people they don&#x27;t know well, dealing with officials, people are quite restrained, and showing strong emotion would seem <strong class=\"key-phrase\">out of place</strong>. But at a festival, a football match or a family gathering, exactly the same people can be extremely expressive. So it&#x27;s less about temperament than about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">where the culture permits emotion</strong>. That&#x27;s not the whole story, though; some individuals stay reserved wherever they are, festival or office, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personality still matters</strong> on top of context.\n\nQ: How do you think people show their excitement?\nA: The physical signs are fairly universal — speaking faster and louder, smiling, gesturing more, and an obvious need to tell somebody immediately. That last part&#x27;s interesting, because excitement seems to need an audience in a way other emotions don&#x27;t. Online it looks different, expressed through messages and emojis, but I&#x27;d say it reads as more deliberate, more composed than the real thing. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Face-to-face reactions are harder to fake</strong> because they happen before you&#x27;ve had time to think about how you want to look.","topic":"Emotions","phrases":[{"en":"anticipation combined with emotional investment","context":"Q: What generally makes people in your country excited?\nA: Sport's probably the biggest one — a national football victory genuinely brings people out onto the streets in a way almost nothing else does. Beyond that, family celebrations like weddings and New Year, and personal milestones such as exam results or a first job. Announcements about things people have been waiting for, a concert finally being confirmed, say, also generate a surprising amount of excitement. The common thread is anticipation combined with emotional investment; excitement seems to need both. It's not universal, though — plenty of people find big national events completely underwhelming, and get far more excited by something purely personal instead."},{"en":"completely underwhelming","context":"Q: What generally makes people in your country excited?\nA: Sport's probably the biggest one — a national football victory genuinely brings people out onto the streets in a way almost nothing else does. Beyond that, family celebrations like weddings and New Year, and personal milestones such as exam results or a first job. Announcements about things people have been waiting for, a concert finally being confirmed, say, also generate a surprising amount of excitement. The common thread is anticipation combined with emotional investment; excitement seems to need both. It's not universal, though — plenty of people find big national events completely underwhelming, and get far more excited by something purely personal instead."},{"en":"out of place","context":"Q: Are people in your country generally calm, or do they get excited easily?\nA: It depends enormously on the setting, which I suspect is true of most cultures. In formal situations, at work, with people they don't know well, dealing with officials, people are quite restrained, and showing strong emotion would seem out of place. But at a festival, a football match or a family gathering, exactly the same people can be extremely expressive. So it's less about temperament than about where the culture permits emotion. That's not the whole story, though; some individuals stay reserved wherever they are, festival or office, so personality still matters on top of context."},{"en":"where the culture permits emotion","context":"Q: Are people in your country generally calm, or do they get excited easily?\nA: It depends enormously on the setting, which I suspect is true of most cultures. In formal situations, at work, with people they don't know well, dealing with officials, people are quite restrained, and showing strong emotion would seem out of place. But at a festival, a football match or a family gathering, exactly the same people can be extremely expressive. So it's less about temperament than about where the culture permits emotion. That's not the whole story, though; some individuals stay reserved wherever they are, festival or office, so personality still matters on top of context."},{"en":"personality still matters","context":"Q: Are people in your country generally calm, or do they get excited easily?\nA: It depends enormously on the setting, which I suspect is true of most cultures. In formal situations, at work, with people they don't know well, dealing with officials, people are quite restrained, and showing strong emotion would seem out of place. But at a festival, a football match or a family gathering, exactly the same people can be extremely expressive. So it's less about temperament than about where the culture permits emotion. That's not the whole story, though; some individuals stay reserved wherever they are, festival or office, so personality still matters on top of context."},{"en":"Face-to-face reactions are harder to fake","context":"Q: How do you think people show their excitement?\nA: The physical signs are fairly universal — speaking faster and louder, smiling, gesturing more, and an obvious need to tell somebody immediately. That last part's interesting, because excitement seems to need an audience in a way other emotions don't. Online it looks different, expressed through messages and emojis, but I'd say it reads as more deliberate, more composed than the real thing. Face-to-face reactions are harder to fake because they happen before you've had time to think about how you want to look."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Emotions","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-039","_displayNo":"#039","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-039-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-039-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-039-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"National football victories, weddings, New Year celebrations and personal achievements often create excitement. People also get excited about events they have waited for, such as a concert. Usually they need both expectation and a personal reason to care. However, interests vary: someone may ignore a huge national event but feel thrilled about getting their first job.","phrases":[{"en":"personal achievements","meaning":"Goals or successes that matter in your own life."}],"question":"What generally makes people in your country excited?","html":"National football victories, weddings, New Year celebrations and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personal achievements</strong> often create excitement. People also get excited about events they have waited for, such as a concert. Usually they need both expectation and a personal reason to care. However, interests vary: someone may ignore a huge national event but feel thrilled about getting their first job."},{"text":"It depends on the setting. People may control their feelings at work or around strangers but show much more excitement at a match or family celebration. Culture affects when strong emotion seems acceptable. Personality matters too, though, because some people remain quiet in almost every situation. I would not describe a whole country in only one way.","phrases":[{"en":"setting","meaning":"The place or situation in which something happens."}],"question":"Are people in your country generally calm, or do they get excited easily?","html":"It depends on the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">setting</strong>. People may control their feelings at work or around strangers but show much more excitement at a match or family celebration. Culture affects when strong emotion seems acceptable. Personality matters too, though, because some people remain quiet in almost every situation. I would not describe a whole country in only one way."},{"text":"People often smile, move their hands more and speak faster or louder. They also want to share their news immediately, as if excitement needs someone to hear it. Online, people use messages and emojis instead. Those reactions may be more carefully chosen, while a face-to-face response often happens before someone has time to plan it.","phrases":[{"en":"response","meaning":"What someone says or does after something happens."}],"question":"How do you think people show their excitement?","html":"People often smile, move their hands more and speak faster or louder. They also want to share their news immediately, as if excitement needs someone to hear it. Online, people use messages and emojis instead. Those reactions may be more carefully chosen, while a face-to-face <strong class=\"key-phrase\">response</strong> often happens before someone has time to plan it."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-039-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-039-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-039-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Human effects on wild animals and protecting wildlife","prompt":"What effects do you think humans have on wild animals?\nWhat measures could we take to protect wildlife?\nIs it the responsibility of schools to teach children about protecting wildlife?","answer":"Q: What effects do you think humans have on wild animals?\nA: Habitat destruction&#x27;s the largest by a long way — clearing forests and draining wetlands for agriculture removes the places animals actually live, and no amount of protection helps a species with nowhere left to go. Beyond that, pollution, overfishing and climate change all take a heavy toll. There&#x27;s a subtler effect too: roads and cities <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fragment habitats into isolated patches</strong>, so populations can no longer migrate or interbreed, and small isolated groups tend to die out gradually without anyone really noticing. It&#x27;s not universally bad news, though — a handful of adaptable species, foxes and certain birds, have actually thrived alongside humans by exploiting <strong class=\"key-phrase\">urban environments</strong> most others can&#x27;t use.\n\nQ: What measures could we take to protect wildlife?\nA: Protecting habitat&#x27;s the foundation, since everything else is secondary to having somewhere to live. That means properly funded national parks with actual rangers, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not simply lines drawn on a map</strong>. Enforcing anti-poaching law matters too, along with reducing demand in the countries that buy wildlife products. And local communities have to benefit financially from it, because conservation imposed on people who lose crops or land to it will always be resisted, whatever the law says on paper — a farmer who&#x27;s lost his harvest to elephants isn&#x27;t going to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel any goodwill</strong> towards the park next door.\n\nQ: Is it the responsibility of schools to teach children about protecting wildlife?\nA: Schools have a clear role, though I wouldn&#x27;t say the responsibility&#x27;s theirs alone. Children who learn about ecosystems and endangered species early tend to keep that concern into adulthood, and they often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">influence their parents&#x27; behaviour at home</strong>, which is a genuinely useful side effect. What works better than classroom lessons, though, is direct contact: a single trip to a forest or a nature reserve tends to do more than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a term of textbook material</strong>, simply because it&#x27;s harder to care about a species you&#x27;ve never actually seen.","topic":"Environment & Nature","phrases":[{"en":"fragment habitats into isolated patches","context":"Q: What effects do you think humans have on wild animals?\nA: Habitat destruction's the largest by a long way — clearing forests and draining wetlands for agriculture removes the places animals actually live, and no amount of protection helps a species with nowhere left to go. Beyond that, pollution, overfishing and climate change all take a heavy toll. There's a subtler effect too: roads and cities fragment habitats into isolated patches, so populations can no longer migrate or interbreed, and small isolated groups tend to die out gradually without anyone really noticing. It's not universally bad news, though — a handful of adaptable species, foxes and certain birds, have actually thrived alongside humans by exploiting urban environments most others can't use."},{"en":"urban environments","context":"Q: What effects do you think humans have on wild animals?\nA: Habitat destruction's the largest by a long way — clearing forests and draining wetlands for agriculture removes the places animals actually live, and no amount of protection helps a species with nowhere left to go. Beyond that, pollution, overfishing and climate change all take a heavy toll. There's a subtler effect too: roads and cities fragment habitats into isolated patches, so populations can no longer migrate or interbreed, and small isolated groups tend to die out gradually without anyone really noticing. It's not universally bad news, though — a handful of adaptable species, foxes and certain birds, have actually thrived alongside humans by exploiting urban environments most others can't use."},{"en":"not simply lines drawn on a map","context":"Q: What measures could we take to protect wildlife?\nA: Protecting habitat's the foundation, since everything else is secondary to having somewhere to live. That means properly funded national parks with actual rangers, not simply lines drawn on a map. Enforcing anti-poaching law matters too, along with reducing demand in the countries that buy wildlife products. And local communities have to benefit financially from it, because conservation imposed on people who lose crops or land to it will always be resisted, whatever the law says on paper — a farmer who's lost his harvest to elephants isn't going to feel any goodwill towards the park next door."},{"en":"feel any goodwill","context":"Q: What measures could we take to protect wildlife?\nA: Protecting habitat's the foundation, since everything else is secondary to having somewhere to live. That means properly funded national parks with actual rangers, not simply lines drawn on a map. Enforcing anti-poaching law matters too, along with reducing demand in the countries that buy wildlife products. And local communities have to benefit financially from it, because conservation imposed on people who lose crops or land to it will always be resisted, whatever the law says on paper — a farmer who's lost his harvest to elephants isn't going to feel any goodwill towards the park next door."},{"en":"influence their parents' behaviour at home","context":"Q: Is it the responsibility of schools to teach children about protecting wildlife?\nA: Schools have a clear role, though I wouldn't say the responsibility's theirs alone. Children who learn about ecosystems and endangered species early tend to keep that concern into adulthood, and they often influence their parents' behaviour at home, which is a genuinely useful side effect. What works better than classroom lessons, though, is direct contact: a single trip to a forest or a nature reserve tends to do more than a term of textbook material, simply because it's harder to care about a species you've never actually seen."},{"en":"a term of textbook material","context":"Q: Is it the responsibility of schools to teach children about protecting wildlife?\nA: Schools have a clear role, though I wouldn't say the responsibility's theirs alone. Children who learn about ecosystems and endangered species early tend to keep that concern into adulthood, and they often influence their parents' behaviour at home, which is a genuinely useful side effect. What works better than classroom lessons, though, is direct contact: a single trip to a forest or a nature reserve tends to do more than a term of textbook material, simply because it's harder to care about a species you've never actually seen."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Environment & Nature","id":"speaking-part-3-040","_displayNo":"#040","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-040-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-040-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-040-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Humans destroy habitats by clearing forests or changing land for farming and building. Pollution, fishing and climate change cause further harm. Roads also separate animal groups, making movement and breeding harder. Some adaptable animals do well in cities, but many cannot use those environments. Protecting a species is difficult if it no longer has a suitable place to live.","phrases":[{"en":"habitats","meaning":"The natural places where particular animals or plants live."}],"question":"What effects do you think humans have on wild animals?","html":"Humans destroy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">habitats</strong> by clearing forests or changing land for farming and building. Pollution, fishing and climate change cause further harm. Roads also separate animal groups, making movement and breeding harder. Some adaptable animals do well in cities, but many cannot use those environments. Protecting a species is difficult if it no longer has a suitable place to live."},{"text":"We should protect habitats, support parks properly and enforce laws against illegal hunting. Reducing demand for wildlife products is also important. Local people need benefits from conservation, especially if animals damage their crops or land use is restricted. Protection is more likely to succeed when communities have a reason to support it, rather than only being told what not to do.","phrases":[{"en":"conservation","meaning":"Protecting nature and using its resources carefully."}],"question":"What measures could we take to protect wildlife?","html":"We should protect habitats, support parks properly and enforce laws against illegal hunting. Reducing demand for wildlife products is also important. Local people need benefits from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">conservation</strong>, especially if animals damage their crops or land use is restricted. Protection is more likely to succeed when communities have a reason to support it, rather than only being told what not to do."},{"text":"Schools should help, but they are not the only ones responsible. Learning about nature can build concern that lasts into adulthood, and children may influence family habits too. Visits to forests or nature reserves can make the subject more meaningful than textbooks alone. It is easier to care about something after seeing and experiencing it.","phrases":[{"en":"nature reserves","meaning":"Areas protected so that wildlife and natural habitats can survive."}],"question":"Is it the responsibility of schools to teach children about protecting wildlife?","html":"Schools should help, but they are not the only ones responsible. Learning about nature can build concern that lasts into adulthood, and children may influence family habits too. Visits to forests or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nature reserves</strong> can make the subject more meaningful than textbooks alone. It is easier to care about something after seeing and experiencing it."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-040-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-040-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-040-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Water transport compared with other forms","prompt":"How does water transport compare with other forms of transport?\nWhat are the advantages and disadvantages of water transport?\nHow important is it for a town or city to be located near a river or the sea?","answer":"Q: How does water transport compare with other forms of transport?\nA: It occupies a specific niche. It&#x27;s far slower than air freight — weeks rather than hours — but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cost per tonne is a fraction</strong>, which is why almost all international trade in bulk goods goes by sea. Compared with road transport it&#x27;s much less flexible, because you&#x27;re tied to ports and navigable rivers, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">something else has to cover the final leg</strong> of the journey to the actual destination. So it suits bulk, low-value cargo far better than anything urgent or door-to-door.\n\nQ: What are the advantages and disadvantages of water transport?\nA: The advantages are capacity and fuel efficiency: a single container ship carries what would otherwise need thousands of lorries, using far less fuel per tonne, and it keeps freight traffic off the roads. The disadvantages are speed, inflexible routes and environmental risk, since an oil spill causes damage lasting decades and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">building ports destroys coastal habitat</strong>. Shipping also burns <strong class=\"key-phrase\">some of the dirtiest fuel available</strong>, which is only now being regulated properly — it&#x27;s not that the industry can&#x27;t clean itself up, it simply hasn&#x27;t had much incentive to until now.\n\nQ: How important is it for a town or city to be located near a river or the sea?\nA: Historically it was decisive, since almost every major old city sits on water because that was the only practical way to trade and to obtain fresh water. Today it matters far less for transport, as roads and air freight have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduced the dependence</strong>, but it hasn&#x27;t disappeared — river and coastal cities still do well out of tourism and industry. The complication is climate: coastal cities now face flooding, so the location that created them has become a liability, and today&#x27;s advantage can quietly turn into tomorrow&#x27;s expensive problem.","topic":"Environment & Nature","phrases":[{"en":"the cost per tonne is a fraction","context":"Q: How does water transport compare with other forms of transport?\nA: It occupies a specific niche. It's far slower than air freight — weeks rather than hours — but the cost per tonne is a fraction, which is why almost all international trade in bulk goods goes by sea. Compared with road transport it's much less flexible, because you're tied to ports and navigable rivers, and something else has to cover the final leg of the journey to the actual destination. So it suits bulk, low-value cargo far better than anything urgent or door-to-door."},{"en":"something else has to cover the final leg","context":"Q: How does water transport compare with other forms of transport?\nA: It occupies a specific niche. It's far slower than air freight — weeks rather than hours — but the cost per tonne is a fraction, which is why almost all international trade in bulk goods goes by sea. Compared with road transport it's much less flexible, because you're tied to ports and navigable rivers, and something else has to cover the final leg of the journey to the actual destination. So it suits bulk, low-value cargo far better than anything urgent or door-to-door."},{"en":"building ports destroys coastal habitat","context":"Q: What are the advantages and disadvantages of water transport?\nA: The advantages are capacity and fuel efficiency: a single container ship carries what would otherwise need thousands of lorries, using far less fuel per tonne, and it keeps freight traffic off the roads. The disadvantages are speed, inflexible routes and environmental risk, since an oil spill causes damage lasting decades and building ports destroys coastal habitat. Shipping also burns some of the dirtiest fuel available, which is only now being regulated properly — it's not that the industry can't clean itself up, it simply hasn't had much incentive to until now."},{"en":"some of the dirtiest fuel available","context":"Q: What are the advantages and disadvantages of water transport?\nA: The advantages are capacity and fuel efficiency: a single container ship carries what would otherwise need thousands of lorries, using far less fuel per tonne, and it keeps freight traffic off the roads. The disadvantages are speed, inflexible routes and environmental risk, since an oil spill causes damage lasting decades and building ports destroys coastal habitat. Shipping also burns some of the dirtiest fuel available, which is only now being regulated properly — it's not that the industry can't clean itself up, it simply hasn't had much incentive to until now."},{"en":"reduced the dependence","context":"Q: How important is it for a town or city to be located near a river or the sea?\nA: Historically it was decisive, since almost every major old city sits on water because that was the only practical way to trade and to obtain fresh water. Today it matters far less for transport, as roads and air freight have reduced the dependence, but it hasn't disappeared — river and coastal cities still do well out of tourism and industry. The complication is climate: coastal cities now face flooding, so the location that created them has become a liability, and today's advantage can quietly turn into tomorrow's expensive problem."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Environment & Nature","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-041","_displayNo":"#041","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-041-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-041-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-041-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Water transport is much slower than flying but usually cheaper for heavy goods. That makes it useful for large shipments that are not urgent. It is less flexible than road transport because boats depend on ports and rivers. Goods often need a truck for the final journey, so shipping is not ideal for direct, fast deliveries.","phrases":[{"en":"shipments","meaning":"Large amounts of goods sent from one place to another."}],"question":"How does water transport compare with other forms of transport?","html":"Water transport is much slower than flying but usually cheaper for heavy goods. That makes it useful for large <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shipments</strong> that are not urgent. It is less flexible than road transport because boats depend on ports and rivers. Goods often need a truck for the final journey, so shipping is not ideal for direct, fast deliveries."},{"text":"Ships carry huge loads using less fuel per tonne than many smaller road vehicles, and they reduce lorry traffic. However, they are slow and follow limited routes. They can also cause serious pollution, especially through fuel emissions or oil spills, while ports may damage coastal habitats. So being efficient does not mean water transport has no environmental costs.","phrases":[{"en":"fuel emissions","meaning":"Polluting gases released when fuel is burned."}],"question":"What are the advantages and disadvantages of water transport?","html":"Ships carry huge loads using less fuel per tonne than many smaller road vehicles, and they reduce lorry traffic. However, they are slow and follow limited routes. They can also cause serious pollution, especially through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fuel emissions</strong> or oil spills, while ports may damage coastal habitats. So being efficient does not mean water transport has no environmental costs."},{"text":"It used to be extremely important because water provided trade routes and a supply of fresh water. Roads and air transport have reduced that dependence, although rivers and coasts still support tourism and industry. However, flooding is a growing risk for some coastal cities. A location that once created wealth may now require expensive protection.","phrases":[{"en":"dependence","meaning":"The need to rely on someone or something."}],"question":"How important is it for a town or city to be located near a river or the sea?","html":"It used to be extremely important because water provided trade routes and a supply of fresh water. Roads and air transport have reduced that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dependence</strong>, although rivers and coasts still support tourism and industry. However, flooding is a growing risk for some coastal cities. A location that once created wealth may now require expensive protection."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-041-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-041-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-041-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How aware people are of environmental problems","prompt":"How aware do you think people are nowadays about environmental issues?","answer":"Q: How aware do you think people are nowadays about environmental issues?\nA: Awareness is far higher than it was twenty years ago. Climate change, plastic waste and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">air quality</strong> are covered constantly in the news, and most schools now teach the subject, so even young children can explain why recycling matters. What I&#x27;d question is how much that awareness actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changes behaviour</strong>. People know that flying frequently and eating a lot of meat are damaging, but they carry on doing both, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the sustainable option</strong> is usually more expensive or less convenient. So I&#x27;d say we&#x27;ve largely solved <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the information problem</strong>, but not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the incentive problem</strong>. It isn&#x27;t that people need convincing any more; it&#x27;s that doing the right thing still costs more than doing the easy thing.","topic":"Environment & Nature","phrases":[{"en":"air quality","context":"Q: How aware do you think people are nowadays about environmental issues?\nA: Awareness is far higher than it was twenty years ago. Climate change, plastic waste and air quality are covered constantly in the news, and most schools now teach the subject, so even young children can explain why recycling matters. What I'd question is how much that awareness actually changes behaviour. People know that flying frequently and eating a lot of meat are damaging, but they carry on doing both, because the sustainable option is usually more expensive or less convenient. So I'd say we've largely solved the information problem, but not the incentive problem. It isn't that people need convincing any more; it's that doing the right thing still costs more than doing the easy thing."},{"en":"changes behaviour","context":"Q: How aware do you think people are nowadays about environmental issues?\nA: Awareness is far higher than it was twenty years ago. Climate change, plastic waste and air quality are covered constantly in the news, and most schools now teach the subject, so even young children can explain why recycling matters. What I'd question is how much that awareness actually changes behaviour. People know that flying frequently and eating a lot of meat are damaging, but they carry on doing both, because the sustainable option is usually more expensive or less convenient. So I'd say we've largely solved the information problem, but not the incentive problem. It isn't that people need convincing any more; it's that doing the right thing still costs more than doing the easy thing."},{"en":"the sustainable option","context":"Q: How aware do you think people are nowadays about environmental issues?\nA: Awareness is far higher than it was twenty years ago. Climate change, plastic waste and air quality are covered constantly in the news, and most schools now teach the subject, so even young children can explain why recycling matters. What I'd question is how much that awareness actually changes behaviour. People know that flying frequently and eating a lot of meat are damaging, but they carry on doing both, because the sustainable option is usually more expensive or less convenient. So I'd say we've largely solved the information problem, but not the incentive problem. It isn't that people need convincing any more; it's that doing the right thing still costs more than doing the easy thing."},{"en":"the information problem","context":"Q: How aware do you think people are nowadays about environmental issues?\nA: Awareness is far higher than it was twenty years ago. Climate change, plastic waste and air quality are covered constantly in the news, and most schools now teach the subject, so even young children can explain why recycling matters. What I'd question is how much that awareness actually changes behaviour. People know that flying frequently and eating a lot of meat are damaging, but they carry on doing both, because the sustainable option is usually more expensive or less convenient. So I'd say we've largely solved the information problem, but not the incentive problem. It isn't that people need convincing any more; it's that doing the right thing still costs more than doing the easy thing."},{"en":"the incentive problem","context":"Q: How aware do you think people are nowadays about environmental issues?\nA: Awareness is far higher than it was twenty years ago. Climate change, plastic waste and air quality are covered constantly in the news, and most schools now teach the subject, so even young children can explain why recycling matters. What I'd question is how much that awareness actually changes behaviour. People know that flying frequently and eating a lot of meat are damaging, but they carry on doing both, because the sustainable option is usually more expensive or less convenient. So I'd say we've largely solved the information problem, but not the incentive problem. It isn't that people need convincing any more; it's that doing the right thing still costs more than doing the easy thing."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Environment & Nature","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-042","_displayNo":"#042","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-042-q01.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"People know much more about climate change, plastic waste and dirty air because schools and news discuss them regularly. However, knowing is different from changing habits. People may continue flying often or making other harmful choices because greener options cost more or take extra effort. I think making better choices practical and affordable is now as important as providing information.","phrases":[{"en":"affordable","meaning":"Not too expensive for people to pay for."}],"question":"How aware do you think people are nowadays about environmental issues?","html":"People know much more about climate change, plastic waste and dirty air because schools and news discuss them regularly. However, knowing is different from changing habits. People may continue flying often or making other harmful choices because greener options cost more or take extra effort. I think making better choices practical and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">affordable</strong> is now as important as providing information."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-042-q01.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Human effects on the environment and what can be done","prompt":"What are the effects of human activities on the environment?\nWhat can be done to protect the environment?\nWhat would be the impact of global warming on the environment?","answer":"Q: What are the effects of human activities on the environment?\nA: The largest are deforestation, burning fossil fuels, intensive agriculture and waste. Between them they&#x27;ve altered habitats and the climate itself, and the consequences are visible: species disappearing at <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an unprecedented rate</strong>, air quality in major cities that measurably shortens lives, and rivers carrying plastic and agricultural runoff. What concerns me most is the change in weather patterns, because that affects food production, and food security affects almost everything else in a society. Not all of this is equally permanent, though — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">air quality can bounce back within years</strong> once emissions drop, whereas a lost species or a collapsed fishery doesn&#x27;t come back at all.\n\nQ: What can be done to protect the environment?\nA: The decisive action has to come from governments, because individuals can&#x27;t buy clean options that don&#x27;t exist. That means enforceable emissions standards, investment in public transport and renewable energy, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">making polluters pay for the damage they cause</strong> rather than passing it to the public. Individual behaviour matters and shouldn&#x27;t be dismissed, but I&#x27;m impatient with campaigns that place the whole responsibility on consumers while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the underlying system stays identical</strong>.\n\nQ: What would be the impact of global warming on the environment?\nA: More intense heatwaves, rising sea levels, longer droughts in some regions and heavier flooding in others. Ecosystems will be disrupted because species <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot relocate as fast as their habitats change</strong>. The part that receives less attention is the human distribution of it: the countries that will suffer worst contributed least to the emissions and have the fewest resources to adapt. That&#x27;s what makes it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a question of fairness</strong>, not only of science. It isn&#x27;t uniformly bad news, either — a handful of colder regions may see longer growing seasons, though that&#x27;s a small and temporary upside next to the disruption everywhere else.","topic":"Environment & Nature","phrases":[{"en":"an unprecedented rate","context":"Q: What are the effects of human activities on the environment?\nA: The largest are deforestation, burning fossil fuels, intensive agriculture and waste. Between them they've altered habitats and the climate itself, and the consequences are visible: species disappearing at an unprecedented rate, air quality in major cities that measurably shortens lives, and rivers carrying plastic and agricultural runoff. What concerns me most is the change in weather patterns, because that affects food production, and food security affects almost everything else in a society. Not all of this is equally permanent, though — air quality can bounce back within years once emissions drop, whereas a lost species or a collapsed fishery doesn't come back at all."},{"en":"air quality can bounce back within years","context":"Q: What are the effects of human activities on the environment?\nA: The largest are deforestation, burning fossil fuels, intensive agriculture and waste. Between them they've altered habitats and the climate itself, and the consequences are visible: species disappearing at an unprecedented rate, air quality in major cities that measurably shortens lives, and rivers carrying plastic and agricultural runoff. What concerns me most is the change in weather patterns, because that affects food production, and food security affects almost everything else in a society. Not all of this is equally permanent, though — air quality can bounce back within years once emissions drop, whereas a lost species or a collapsed fishery doesn't come back at all."},{"en":"making polluters pay for the damage they cause","context":"Q: What can be done to protect the environment?\nA: The decisive action has to come from governments, because individuals can't buy clean options that don't exist. That means enforceable emissions standards, investment in public transport and renewable energy, and making polluters pay for the damage they cause rather than passing it to the public. Individual behaviour matters and shouldn't be dismissed, but I'm impatient with campaigns that place the whole responsibility on consumers while the underlying system stays identical."},{"en":"the underlying system stays identical","context":"Q: What can be done to protect the environment?\nA: The decisive action has to come from governments, because individuals can't buy clean options that don't exist. That means enforceable emissions standards, investment in public transport and renewable energy, and making polluters pay for the damage they cause rather than passing it to the public. Individual behaviour matters and shouldn't be dismissed, but I'm impatient with campaigns that place the whole responsibility on consumers while the underlying system stays identical."},{"en":"cannot relocate as fast as their habitats change","context":"Q: What would be the impact of global warming on the environment?\nA: More intense heatwaves, rising sea levels, longer droughts in some regions and heavier flooding in others. Ecosystems will be disrupted because species cannot relocate as fast as their habitats change. The part that receives less attention is the human distribution of it: the countries that will suffer worst contributed least to the emissions and have the fewest resources to adapt. That's what makes it a question of fairness, not only of science. It isn't uniformly bad news, either — a handful of colder regions may see longer growing seasons, though that's a small and temporary upside next to the disruption everywhere else."},{"en":"a question of fairness","context":"Q: What would be the impact of global warming on the environment?\nA: More intense heatwaves, rising sea levels, longer droughts in some regions and heavier flooding in others. Ecosystems will be disrupted because species cannot relocate as fast as their habitats change. The part that receives less attention is the human distribution of it: the countries that will suffer worst contributed least to the emissions and have the fewest resources to adapt. That's what makes it a question of fairness, not only of science. It isn't uniformly bad news, either — a handful of colder regions may see longer growing seasons, though that's a small and temporary upside next to the disruption everywhere else."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Environment & Nature","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-043","_displayNo":"#043","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-043-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-043-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-043-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Cutting down forests, burning fossil fuels, intensive farming and waste damage both nature and the climate. They can destroy habitats, pollute rivers and make the air unhealthy. Changing weather is especially worrying because it affects food production. Some damage can improve when pollution falls, but losing a species may be permanent, so prevention matters.","phrases":[{"en":"prevention","meaning":"Taking action to stop a problem before it happens."}],"question":"What are the effects of human activities on the environment?","html":"Cutting down forests, burning fossil fuels, intensive farming and waste damage both nature and the climate. They can destroy habitats, pollute rivers and make the air unhealthy. Changing weather is especially worrying because it affects food production. Some damage can improve when pollution falls, but losing a species may be permanent, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prevention</strong> matters."},{"text":"Governments need to lead by setting pollution limits and investing in clean energy and public transport. People cannot choose a greener option if none is available. Companies should also pay for the damage they cause. Individual habits matter, but asking consumers to solve everything while the wider system stays the same is not enough.","phrases":[{"en":"consumers","meaning":"People who buy or use goods and services."}],"question":"What can be done to protect the environment?","html":"Governments need to lead by setting pollution limits and investing in clean energy and public transport. People cannot choose a greener option if none is available. Companies should also pay for the damage they cause. Individual habits matter, but asking <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consumers</strong> to solve everything while the wider system stays the same is not enough."},{"text":"It can cause stronger heatwaves, rising seas, droughts and floods. Animals and plants may struggle because their habitats change faster than they can move. Poorer countries may suffer most despite contributing less to the problem, so fairness matters too. Some cold areas might briefly benefit from warmer growing conditions, but that does not balance the wider damage.","phrases":[{"en":"droughts","meaning":"Long periods with too little rain."}],"question":"What would be the impact of global warming on the environment?","html":"It can cause stronger heatwaves, rising seas, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">droughts</strong> and floods. Animals and plants may struggle because their habitats change faster than they can move. Poorer countries may suffer most despite contributing less to the problem, so fairness matters too. Some cold areas might briefly benefit from warmer growing conditions, but that does not balance the wider damage."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-043-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-043-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-043-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Whether life is becoming safer or more dangerous","prompt":"In what ways is life becoming safer, and in what ways is it becoming more dangerous?\nShould people always avoid dangers, or is it a good idea to take a risk?\nWhat risk should people try to avoid?","answer":"Q: In what ways is life becoming safer, and in what ways is it becoming more dangerous?\nA: It&#x27;s become dramatically safer in the most fundamental respects. Medicine has eliminated diseases that once routinely killed children, building and fire regulations have reduced accidents enormously, and workplace deaths are now a small fraction of what they were. The new dangers are different in character — they&#x27;re <strong class=\"key-phrase\">systemic rather than personal</strong>. Cybercrime, climate instability and highly connected supply chains mean a failure in one place can spread globally within days. The catch is that this kind of danger is much harder to protect yourself from individually; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no amount of personal caution</strong> stops a bank&#x27;s systems going down or a supply chain breaking.\n\nQ: Should people always avoid dangers, or is it a good idea to take a risk?\nA: Avoiding all risk is neither possible nor desirable. Nothing worth doing — starting a business, moving abroad, learning something in public — comes without the possibility of failure, and a person who avoids all of it ends up with a very narrow life. The useful distinction is between <strong class=\"key-phrase\">informed and reckless risk</strong>. Informed means you understand the downside and you&#x27;ve decided it&#x27;s worth taking; reckless means you simply never looked at it in the first place. That said, the line <strong class=\"key-phrase\">isn&#x27;t always obvious in the moment</strong> — plenty of reckless decisions felt informed right up until they went wrong.\n\nQ: What risk should people try to avoid?\nA: The ones where the potential harm is permanent and the benefit is small — that&#x27;s the test I&#x27;d apply. Dangerous driving is the obvious example, since you save a few minutes and risk your life. High-interest debt belongs in the same category, as does ignoring a serious medical symptom. If <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the worst realistic outcome cannot be undone</strong>, the reward has to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely substantial</strong> to justify it, whereas a risk you can recover from is worth taking more freely.","topic":"Experiences","phrases":[{"en":"systemic rather than personal","context":"Q: In what ways is life becoming safer, and in what ways is it becoming more dangerous?\nA: It's become dramatically safer in the most fundamental respects. Medicine has eliminated diseases that once routinely killed children, building and fire regulations have reduced accidents enormously, and workplace deaths are now a small fraction of what they were. The new dangers are different in character — they're systemic rather than personal. Cybercrime, climate instability and highly connected supply chains mean a failure in one place can spread globally within days. The catch is that this kind of danger is much harder to protect yourself from individually; no amount of personal caution stops a bank's systems going down or a supply chain breaking."},{"en":"no amount of personal caution","context":"Q: In what ways is life becoming safer, and in what ways is it becoming more dangerous?\nA: It's become dramatically safer in the most fundamental respects. Medicine has eliminated diseases that once routinely killed children, building and fire regulations have reduced accidents enormously, and workplace deaths are now a small fraction of what they were. The new dangers are different in character — they're systemic rather than personal. Cybercrime, climate instability and highly connected supply chains mean a failure in one place can spread globally within days. The catch is that this kind of danger is much harder to protect yourself from individually; no amount of personal caution stops a bank's systems going down or a supply chain breaking."},{"en":"informed and reckless risk","context":"Q: Should people always avoid dangers, or is it a good idea to take a risk?\nA: Avoiding all risk is neither possible nor desirable. Nothing worth doing — starting a business, moving abroad, learning something in public — comes without the possibility of failure, and a person who avoids all of it ends up with a very narrow life. The useful distinction is between informed and reckless risk. Informed means you understand the downside and you've decided it's worth taking; reckless means you simply never looked at it in the first place. That said, the line isn't always obvious in the moment — plenty of reckless decisions felt informed right up until they went wrong."},{"en":"isn't always obvious in the moment","context":"Q: Should people always avoid dangers, or is it a good idea to take a risk?\nA: Avoiding all risk is neither possible nor desirable. Nothing worth doing — starting a business, moving abroad, learning something in public — comes without the possibility of failure, and a person who avoids all of it ends up with a very narrow life. The useful distinction is between informed and reckless risk. Informed means you understand the downside and you've decided it's worth taking; reckless means you simply never looked at it in the first place. That said, the line isn't always obvious in the moment — plenty of reckless decisions felt informed right up until they went wrong."},{"en":"the worst realistic outcome cannot be undone","context":"Q: What risk should people try to avoid?\nA: The ones where the potential harm is permanent and the benefit is small — that's the test I'd apply. Dangerous driving is the obvious example, since you save a few minutes and risk your life. High-interest debt belongs in the same category, as does ignoring a serious medical symptom. If the worst realistic outcome cannot be undone, the reward has to be genuinely substantial to justify it, whereas a risk you can recover from is worth taking more freely."},{"en":"genuinely substantial","context":"Q: What risk should people try to avoid?\nA: The ones where the potential harm is permanent and the benefit is small — that's the test I'd apply. Dangerous driving is the obvious example, since you save a few minutes and risk your life. High-interest debt belongs in the same category, as does ignoring a serious medical symptom. If the worst realistic outcome cannot be undone, the reward has to be genuinely substantial to justify it, whereas a risk you can recover from is worth taking more freely."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Experiences","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-044","_displayNo":"#044","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-044-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-044-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-044-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Medicine, safer buildings and workplace rules have reduced many traditional dangers. However, new risks such as cybercrime and climate problems can affect whole systems. A failure in one country may disrupt supplies elsewhere very quickly. These dangers are harder to avoid personally because being careful cannot protect you from every failure in a bank or transport network.","phrases":[{"en":"disrupt","meaning":"Interrupt something and stop it working normally."}],"question":"In what ways is life becoming safer, and in what ways is it becoming more dangerous?","html":"Medicine, safer buildings and workplace rules have reduced many traditional dangers. However, new risks such as cybercrime and climate problems can affect whole systems. A failure in one country may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disrupt</strong> supplies elsewhere very quickly. These dangers are harder to avoid personally because being careful cannot protect you from every failure in a bank or transport network."},{"text":"People should not avoid every risk. Starting a business, moving abroad or learning a new skill all involve possible failure. What matters is understanding the downside and deciding whether the benefit is worth it. Acting without considering consequences is different. Even careful decisions can go wrong, but that is not a reason to avoid all opportunities.","phrases":[{"en":"downside","meaning":"The negative part or possible disadvantage of something."}],"question":"Should people always avoid dangers, or is it a good idea to take a risk?","html":"People should not avoid every risk. Starting a business, moving abroad or learning a new skill all involve possible failure. What matters is understanding the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">downside</strong> and deciding whether the benefit is worth it. Acting without considering consequences is different. Even careful decisions can go wrong, but that is not a reason to avoid all opportunities."},{"text":"I would avoid risks with lasting harm and very little benefit. Dangerous driving is a clear example: saving a few minutes is not worth risking a life. Taking on very expensive debt or ignoring a serious health problem can also have major consequences. Risks are easier to accept when a mistake can be repaired or recovered from.","phrases":[{"en":"consequences","meaning":"The results or effects of an action."}],"question":"What risk should people try to avoid?","html":"I would avoid risks with lasting harm and very little benefit. Dangerous driving is a clear example: saving a few minutes is not worth risking a life. Taking on very expensive debt or ignoring a serious health problem can also have major <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consequences</strong>. Risks are easier to accept when a mistake can be repaired or recovered from."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-044-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-044-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-044-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Family likeness, and family against friends","prompt":"In what ways can a family be similar to each other?\nIn terms of her personality, are people more influenced by their family or by their friends?\nDo you think daughters are always more similar to their mothers than to male relatives?","answer":"Q: In what ways can a family be similar to each other?\nA: The obvious similarities are physical — height, features, the way people walk. But what strikes me more is the behavioural side: families share a sense of humour, particular expressions, how loudly they talk and how they handle disagreement. Some of that&#x27;s genetic, but a great deal is simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">absorbed from years of proximity</strong>. Values around money, work and hospitality also pass down remarkably reliably, usually without anyone deliberately teaching them and often without the family noticing it&#x27;s happening. It doesn&#x27;t hold for every trait, though — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a strong-willed child in a quiet family</strong> can turn out nothing like the people who raised them.\n\nQ: In terms of her personality, are people more influenced by their family or by their friends?\nA: It shifts with age, which I think is the key point. Family dominates early childhood, setting the basic values and habits, and those tend to persist. During adolescence friends become far more influential — teenagers adjust their language, clothing and often their ambitions <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to match their group</strong>. In adulthood people usually settle somewhere between the two, keeping the family&#x27;s underlying values while having acquired much of their manner and outlook from their peers. It&#x27;s not a clean split, though; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a strong personality can resist both influences</strong> and stay fairly constant from childhood onward.\n\nQ: Do you think daughters are always more similar to their mothers than to male relatives?\nA: No, and I think that assumption comes from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expectation rather than observation</strong>. Genetics doesn&#x27;t work that way — a daughter can easily inherit her father&#x27;s temperament or her grandfather&#x27;s build. What may create an appearance of similarity is that mothers and daughters often spend more time together, so mannerisms get copied. But plenty of families would tell you that the daughter is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unmistakably like her father</strong>, and nobody in those families finds it surprising. The one place the stereotype has some basis is early childcare, since whoever spends the most hours with a baby shapes it a little more than the other parent does.","topic":"Family","phrases":[{"en":"absorbed from years of proximity","context":"Q: In what ways can a family be similar to each other?\nA: The obvious similarities are physical — height, features, the way people walk. But what strikes me more is the behavioural side: families share a sense of humour, particular expressions, how loudly they talk and how they handle disagreement. Some of that's genetic, but a great deal is simply absorbed from years of proximity. Values around money, work and hospitality also pass down remarkably reliably, usually without anyone deliberately teaching them and often without the family noticing it's happening. It doesn't hold for every trait, though — a strong-willed child in a quiet family can turn out nothing like the people who raised them."},{"en":"a strong-willed child in a quiet family","context":"Q: In what ways can a family be similar to each other?\nA: The obvious similarities are physical — height, features, the way people walk. But what strikes me more is the behavioural side: families share a sense of humour, particular expressions, how loudly they talk and how they handle disagreement. Some of that's genetic, but a great deal is simply absorbed from years of proximity. Values around money, work and hospitality also pass down remarkably reliably, usually without anyone deliberately teaching them and often without the family noticing it's happening. It doesn't hold for every trait, though — a strong-willed child in a quiet family can turn out nothing like the people who raised them."},{"en":"to match their group","context":"Q: In terms of her personality, are people more influenced by their family or by their friends?\nA: It shifts with age, which I think is the key point. Family dominates early childhood, setting the basic values and habits, and those tend to persist. During adolescence friends become far more influential — teenagers adjust their language, clothing and often their ambitions to match their group. In adulthood people usually settle somewhere between the two, keeping the family's underlying values while having acquired much of their manner and outlook from their peers. It's not a clean split, though; a strong personality can resist both influences and stay fairly constant from childhood onward."},{"en":"a strong personality can resist both influences","context":"Q: In terms of her personality, are people more influenced by their family or by their friends?\nA: It shifts with age, which I think is the key point. Family dominates early childhood, setting the basic values and habits, and those tend to persist. During adolescence friends become far more influential — teenagers adjust their language, clothing and often their ambitions to match their group. In adulthood people usually settle somewhere between the two, keeping the family's underlying values while having acquired much of their manner and outlook from their peers. It's not a clean split, though; a strong personality can resist both influences and stay fairly constant from childhood onward."},{"en":"expectation rather than observation","context":"Q: Do you think daughters are always more similar to their mothers than to male relatives?\nA: No, and I think that assumption comes from expectation rather than observation. Genetics doesn't work that way — a daughter can easily inherit her father's temperament or her grandfather's build. What may create an appearance of similarity is that mothers and daughters often spend more time together, so mannerisms get copied. But plenty of families would tell you that the daughter is unmistakably like her father, and nobody in those families finds it surprising. The one place the stereotype has some basis is early childcare, since whoever spends the most hours with a baby shapes it a little more than the other parent does."},{"en":"unmistakably like her father","context":"Q: Do you think daughters are always more similar to their mothers than to male relatives?\nA: No, and I think that assumption comes from expectation rather than observation. Genetics doesn't work that way — a daughter can easily inherit her father's temperament or her grandfather's build. What may create an appearance of similarity is that mothers and daughters often spend more time together, so mannerisms get copied. But plenty of families would tell you that the daughter is unmistakably like her father, and nobody in those families finds it surprising. The one place the stereotype has some basis is early childcare, since whoever spends the most hours with a baby shapes it a little more than the other parent does."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Family","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-045","_displayNo":"#045","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-045-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-045-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-045-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Families can share physical features, such as height or facial appearance. They also copy habits, expressions, humour and ways of handling arguments after years together. Ideas about money and work can pass between generations without formal teaching. However, family influence is not complete; a child may develop a very different personality from the people who raised them.","phrases":[{"en":"physical features","meaning":"Visible parts of a person's appearance, such as face shape or height."}],"question":"In what ways can a family be similar to each other?","html":"Families can share <strong class=\"key-phrase\">physical features</strong>, such as height or facial appearance. They also copy habits, expressions, humour and ways of handling arguments after years together. Ideas about money and work can pass between generations without formal teaching. However, family influence is not complete; a child may develop a very different personality from the people who raised them."},{"text":"It changes with age. Family shapes early habits and values, while friends become especially influential during the teenage years. Adults often keep family values but adopt ways of speaking or thinking from friends. Some people resist both influences strongly, though. I think personality develops through a mixture rather than coming entirely from one group.","phrases":[{"en":"influential","meaning":"Having a strong effect on what people think or do."}],"question":"In terms of her personality, are people more influenced by their family or by their friends?","html":"It changes with age. Family shapes early habits and values, while friends become especially <strong class=\"key-phrase\">influential</strong> during the teenage years. Adults often keep family values but adopt ways of speaking or thinking from friends. Some people resist both influences strongly, though. I think personality develops through a mixture rather than coming entirely from one group."},{"text":"No. A daughter can resemble her father or another male relative in appearance or personality. Mothers and daughters may copy each other's habits if they spend a lot of time together, but that is not a rule. The person providing most early care may have a stronger influence, regardless of whether they are male or female.","phrases":[{"en":"resemble","meaning":"Look or behave like someone else."}],"question":"Do you think daughters are always more similar to their mothers than to male relatives?","html":"No. A daughter can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resemble</strong> her father or another male relative in appearance or personality. Mothers and daughters may copy each other&#x27;s habits if they spend a lot of time together, but that is not a rule. The person providing most early care may have a stronger influence, regardless of whether they are male or female."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-045-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-045-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-045-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Celebrities now and celebrities in the past","prompt":"Are there any differences between the types of people who were seen as celebrities in the past and those who are celebrities nowadays?","answer":"Q: Are there any differences between the types of people who were seen as celebrities in the past and those who are celebrities nowadays?\nA: Yes, I think <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the route to fame</strong> has changed completely. In the past, people became well known because of their position or their ability — monarchs, generals, politicians, or exceptional writers and performers. Then film, radio and television created a new category of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">international star</strong>, but those people still had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a recognised craft</strong> behind them. What&#x27;s different now is that social media allows anyone to become famous without any particular skill. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Visibility itself has become the achievement</strong>, and in some cases controversy is what generates the audience, which would&#x27;ve been unthinkable a generation ago. That&#x27;s not true across the board, though — plenty of today&#x27;s biggest names, in music or sport especially, still built their following on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">years of genuine skill</strong> before social media ever came into it.","topic":"Famous People","phrases":[{"en":"the route to fame","context":"Q: Are there any differences between the types of people who were seen as celebrities in the past and those who are celebrities nowadays?\nA: Yes, I think the route to fame has changed completely. In the past, people became well known because of their position or their ability — monarchs, generals, politicians, or exceptional writers and performers. Then film, radio and television created a new category of international star, but those people still had a recognised craft behind them. What's different now is that social media allows anyone to become famous without any particular skill. Visibility itself has become the achievement, and in some cases controversy is what generates the audience, which would've been unthinkable a generation ago. That's not true across the board, though — plenty of today's biggest names, in music or sport especially, still built their following on years of genuine skill before social media ever came into it."},{"en":"international star","context":"Q: Are there any differences between the types of people who were seen as celebrities in the past and those who are celebrities nowadays?\nA: Yes, I think the route to fame has changed completely. In the past, people became well known because of their position or their ability — monarchs, generals, politicians, or exceptional writers and performers. Then film, radio and television created a new category of international star, but those people still had a recognised craft behind them. What's different now is that social media allows anyone to become famous without any particular skill. Visibility itself has become the achievement, and in some cases controversy is what generates the audience, which would've been unthinkable a generation ago. That's not true across the board, though — plenty of today's biggest names, in music or sport especially, still built their following on years of genuine skill before social media ever came into it."},{"en":"a recognised craft","context":"Q: Are there any differences between the types of people who were seen as celebrities in the past and those who are celebrities nowadays?\nA: Yes, I think the route to fame has changed completely. In the past, people became well known because of their position or their ability — monarchs, generals, politicians, or exceptional writers and performers. Then film, radio and television created a new category of international star, but those people still had a recognised craft behind them. What's different now is that social media allows anyone to become famous without any particular skill. Visibility itself has become the achievement, and in some cases controversy is what generates the audience, which would've been unthinkable a generation ago. That's not true across the board, though — plenty of today's biggest names, in music or sport especially, still built their following on years of genuine skill before social media ever came into it."},{"en":"Visibility itself has become the achievement","context":"Q: Are there any differences between the types of people who were seen as celebrities in the past and those who are celebrities nowadays?\nA: Yes, I think the route to fame has changed completely. In the past, people became well known because of their position or their ability — monarchs, generals, politicians, or exceptional writers and performers. Then film, radio and television created a new category of international star, but those people still had a recognised craft behind them. What's different now is that social media allows anyone to become famous without any particular skill. Visibility itself has become the achievement, and in some cases controversy is what generates the audience, which would've been unthinkable a generation ago. That's not true across the board, though — plenty of today's biggest names, in music or sport especially, still built their following on years of genuine skill before social media ever came into it."},{"en":"years of genuine skill","context":"Q: Are there any differences between the types of people who were seen as celebrities in the past and those who are celebrities nowadays?\nA: Yes, I think the route to fame has changed completely. In the past, people became well known because of their position or their ability — monarchs, generals, politicians, or exceptional writers and performers. Then film, radio and television created a new category of international star, but those people still had a recognised craft behind them. What's different now is that social media allows anyone to become famous without any particular skill. Visibility itself has become the achievement, and in some cases controversy is what generates the audience, which would've been unthinkable a generation ago. That's not true across the board, though — plenty of today's biggest names, in music or sport especially, still built their following on years of genuine skill before social media ever came into it."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Famous People","id":"speaking-part-3-046","_displayNo":"#046","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-046-q01.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, the way people become famous has changed. In the past, position or recognised skills often mattered most. Film and television then created international stars. Social media now lets people gain attention through visibility or controversy, sometimes without an obvious skill. However, many modern athletes and musicians still become famous through years of serious practice and achievement.","phrases":[{"en":"controversy","meaning":"Strong public disagreement about a person or issue."}],"question":"Are there any differences between the types of people who were seen as celebrities in the past and those who are celebrities nowadays?","html":"Yes, the way people become famous has changed. In the past, position or recognised skills often mattered most. Film and television then created international stars. Social media now lets people gain attention through visibility or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">controversy</strong>, sometimes without an obvious skill. However, many modern athletes and musicians still become famous through years of serious practice and achievement."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-046-q01.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Role models for children, and being a child","prompt":"Is it important for young children to have a role model?\nWhat are the advantages and disadvantages of being a child?\nHow do you imagine traditional relationships changing in the future?","answer":"Q: Is it important for young children to have a role model?\nA: Yes, because abstract values mean very little to a child until they see them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">embodied in a person</strong>. Watching someone they admire work hard or behave decently teaches far more than simply being told to. What I&#x27;d argue against is having a single role model, particularly a celebrity, because every public figure eventually disappoints. Several is healthier, and the most useful ones are usually people children actually know, such as a teacher or a relative — someone whose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">flaws are visible too</strong>, not just the polished version a celebrity presents.\n\nQ: What are the advantages and disadvantages of being a child?\nA: The advantages are freedom from responsibility and an enormous amount of time to learn — a child can spend a year becoming good at something with no financial pressure at all. The disadvantage is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">powerlessness</strong>. Children don&#x27;t choose where they live, which school they attend or how they&#x27;re treated, and their well-being depends almost entirely on the adults around them. That&#x27;s why a stable home matters so much — though it&#x27;s worth saying that within a stable home, plenty of children would happily trade some of that freedom for a bit more say in decisions that affect them.\n\nQ: How do you imagine traditional relationships changing in the future?\nA: I&#x27;d expect them to become more equal and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less defined by fixed roles</strong> — who earns, who cares for the children, who makes decisions. That&#x27;s already well advanced in many places. Families are also more geographically scattered, so relationships increasingly have to be maintained across distance, which technology makes possible without quite replicating the real thing. What families will need to protect is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unstructured time together</strong>, since messaging conspicuously fails to provide it.","topic":"Famous People","phrases":[{"en":"embodied in a person","context":"Q: Is it important for young children to have a role model?\nA: Yes, because abstract values mean very little to a child until they see them embodied in a person. Watching someone they admire work hard or behave decently teaches far more than simply being told to. What I'd argue against is having a single role model, particularly a celebrity, because every public figure eventually disappoints. Several is healthier, and the most useful ones are usually people children actually know, such as a teacher or a relative — someone whose flaws are visible too, not just the polished version a celebrity presents."},{"en":"flaws are visible too","context":"Q: Is it important for young children to have a role model?\nA: Yes, because abstract values mean very little to a child until they see them embodied in a person. Watching someone they admire work hard or behave decently teaches far more than simply being told to. What I'd argue against is having a single role model, particularly a celebrity, because every public figure eventually disappoints. Several is healthier, and the most useful ones are usually people children actually know, such as a teacher or a relative — someone whose flaws are visible too, not just the polished version a celebrity presents."},{"en":"powerlessness","context":"Q: What are the advantages and disadvantages of being a child?\nA: The advantages are freedom from responsibility and an enormous amount of time to learn — a child can spend a year becoming good at something with no financial pressure at all. The disadvantage is powerlessness. Children don't choose where they live, which school they attend or how they're treated, and their well-being depends almost entirely on the adults around them. That's why a stable home matters so much — though it's worth saying that within a stable home, plenty of children would happily trade some of that freedom for a bit more say in decisions that affect them."},{"en":"less defined by fixed roles","context":"Q: How do you imagine traditional relationships changing in the future?\nA: I'd expect them to become more equal and less defined by fixed roles — who earns, who cares for the children, who makes decisions. That's already well advanced in many places. Families are also more geographically scattered, so relationships increasingly have to be maintained across distance, which technology makes possible without quite replicating the real thing. What families will need to protect is unstructured time together, since messaging conspicuously fails to provide it."},{"en":"unstructured time together","context":"Q: How do you imagine traditional relationships changing in the future?\nA: I'd expect them to become more equal and less defined by fixed roles — who earns, who cares for the children, who makes decisions. That's already well advanced in many places. Families are also more geographically scattered, so relationships increasingly have to be maintained across distance, which technology makes possible without quite replicating the real thing. What families will need to protect is unstructured time together, since messaging conspicuously fails to provide it."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Famous People","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-047","_displayNo":"#047","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-047-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-047-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-047-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, because children understand values better when they see someone practising them. A teacher or relative who works hard and treats people well can be a useful example. I would encourage several role models rather than one perfect hero. Seeing ordinary people's weaknesses also gives children a more realistic picture than a carefully managed celebrity image.","phrases":[{"en":"role models","meaning":"People whose behaviour others admire and may try to copy."}],"question":"Is it important for young children to have a role model?","html":"Yes, because children understand values better when they see someone practising them. A teacher or relative who works hard and treats people well can be a useful example. I would encourage several <strong class=\"key-phrase\">role models</strong> rather than one perfect hero. Seeing ordinary people&#x27;s weaknesses also gives children a more realistic picture than a carefully managed celebrity image."},{"text":"Children usually have fewer financial responsibilities and more time to learn. However, they have little control over where they live, their school or how adults treat them. Their happiness depends heavily on the home around them. A stable family can make childhood enjoyable, but children may still want more say in decisions affecting their lives.","phrases":[{"en":"more say","meaning":"Have a greater opportunity to influence a decision."}],"question":"What are the advantages and disadvantages of being a child?","html":"Children usually have fewer financial responsibilities and more time to learn. However, they have little control over where they live, their school or how adults treat them. Their happiness depends heavily on the home around them. A stable family can make childhood enjoyable, but children may still want <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more say</strong> in decisions affecting their lives."},{"text":"I expect family roles to become more equal, with less fixed ideas about who earns money or cares for children. Families may also live further apart and rely more on technology to stay connected. However, messages cannot fully replace relaxed time together. Protecting that time will remain important even as relationships become more flexible.","phrases":[{"en":"flexible","meaning":"Able to change to suit different needs or situations."}],"question":"How do you imagine traditional relationships changing in the future?","html":"I expect family roles to become more equal, with less fixed ideas about who earns money or cares for children. Families may also live further apart and rely more on technology to stay connected. However, messages cannot fully replace relaxed time together. Protecting that time will remain important even as relationships become more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">flexible</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-047-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-047-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-047-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How films have changed and the future of cinemas","prompt":"Do you think films have changed since you were a child?\nAs the technology for home viewing improves, do you think people will stop going to the cinema in future?","answer":"Q: Do you think films have changed since you were a child?\nA: No, I don&#x27;t think films have changed all that much since I was a child. When I was younger I enjoyed watching action films, and the Hollywood formula for this type of film seems to be the same today. For example, I liked the original &#x27;Superman&#x27; films, and superheroes are still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a popular subject</strong> for film-makers. What&#x27;s changed is the technology rather than the stories: the effects are far more convincing now, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a franchise runs for a decade</strong> instead of producing one sequel and moving on.\n\nQ: As the technology for home viewing improves, do you think people will stop going to the cinema in future?\nA: No, I don&#x27;t think that people will stop going to the cinema altogether. People can already buy fantastic <strong class=\"key-phrase\">home viewing equipment</strong>, but it still feels more special to share the experience of watching a new film with a theatre full of people. I don&#x27;t think that technology will ever fully <strong class=\"key-phrase\">replicate that cinema atmosphere</strong>. There&#x27;s also the fact that going out is an occasion in a way that staying in simply isn&#x27;t — you leave the house, you can&#x27;t pause it, and you aren&#x27;t checking your phone every ten minutes. Cinemas will have to work harder for that loyalty, though; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ticket prices and dull scheduling</strong> are exactly the kind of thing that pushes people back towards their sofas.","topic":"Film & Television","phrases":[{"en":"a popular subject","context":"Q: Do you think films have changed since you were a child?\nA: No, I don't think films have changed all that much since I was a child. When I was younger I enjoyed watching action films, and the Hollywood formula for this type of film seems to be the same today. For example, I liked the original 'Superman' films, and superheroes are still a popular subject for film-makers. What's changed is the technology rather than the stories: the effects are far more convincing now, and a franchise runs for a decade instead of producing one sequel and moving on."},{"en":"a franchise runs for a decade","context":"Q: Do you think films have changed since you were a child?\nA: No, I don't think films have changed all that much since I was a child. When I was younger I enjoyed watching action films, and the Hollywood formula for this type of film seems to be the same today. For example, I liked the original 'Superman' films, and superheroes are still a popular subject for film-makers. What's changed is the technology rather than the stories: the effects are far more convincing now, and a franchise runs for a decade instead of producing one sequel and moving on."},{"en":"home viewing equipment","context":"Q: As the technology for home viewing improves, do you think people will stop going to the cinema in future?\nA: No, I don't think that people will stop going to the cinema altogether. People can already buy fantastic home viewing equipment, but it still feels more special to share the experience of watching a new film with a theatre full of people. I don't think that technology will ever fully replicate that cinema atmosphere. There's also the fact that going out is an occasion in a way that staying in simply isn't — you leave the house, you can't pause it, and you aren't checking your phone every ten minutes. Cinemas will have to work harder for that loyalty, though; ticket prices and dull scheduling are exactly the kind of thing that pushes people back towards their sofas."},{"en":"replicate that cinema atmosphere","context":"Q: As the technology for home viewing improves, do you think people will stop going to the cinema in future?\nA: No, I don't think that people will stop going to the cinema altogether. People can already buy fantastic home viewing equipment, but it still feels more special to share the experience of watching a new film with a theatre full of people. I don't think that technology will ever fully replicate that cinema atmosphere. There's also the fact that going out is an occasion in a way that staying in simply isn't — you leave the house, you can't pause it, and you aren't checking your phone every ten minutes. Cinemas will have to work harder for that loyalty, though; ticket prices and dull scheduling are exactly the kind of thing that pushes people back towards their sofas."},{"en":"ticket prices and dull scheduling","context":"Q: As the technology for home viewing improves, do you think people will stop going to the cinema in future?\nA: No, I don't think that people will stop going to the cinema altogether. People can already buy fantastic home viewing equipment, but it still feels more special to share the experience of watching a new film with a theatre full of people. I don't think that technology will ever fully replicate that cinema atmosphere. There's also the fact that going out is an occasion in a way that staying in simply isn't — you leave the house, you can't pause it, and you aren't checking your phone every ten minutes. Cinemas will have to work harder for that loyalty, though; ticket prices and dull scheduling are exactly the kind of thing that pushes people back towards their sofas."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Film & Television","id":"speaking-part-3-048","_displayNo":"#048","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-048-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-048-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"The technology has changed more than the basic stories, I think. I enjoyed superhero action films such as Superman as a child, and similar films are still popular. Effects now look more realistic, and successful stories may continue for many films. However, the familiar heroes and basic story patterns often remain much the same.","phrases":[{"en":"realistic","meaning":"Looking or feeling close to real life."}],"question":"Do you think films have changed since you were a child?","html":"The technology has changed more than the basic stories, I think. I enjoyed superhero action films such as Superman as a child, and similar films are still popular. Effects now look more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">realistic</strong>, and successful stories may continue for many films. However, the familiar heroes and basic story patterns often remain much the same."},{"text":"No, cinemas offer a shared atmosphere that home equipment cannot fully replace. Going out for a new film feels special, and people may concentrate more without phones or pauses. However, cinemas still need attractive films and reasonable prices. If visiting becomes too expensive or disappointing, people will naturally choose the comfort of home more often.","phrases":[{"en":"shared atmosphere","meaning":"The feeling created by experiencing something with other people."}],"question":"As the technology for home viewing improves, do you think people will stop going to the cinema in future?","html":"No, cinemas offer a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shared atmosphere</strong> that home equipment cannot fully replace. Going out for a new film feels special, and people may concentrate more without phones or pauses. However, cinemas still need attractive films and reasonable prices. If visiting becomes too expensive or disappointing, people will naturally choose the comfort of home more often."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-048-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-048-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Television for education or entertainment","prompt":"Do you think most people watch TV for education or for entertainment?\nShould TV play a role in educating children; how?\nHow do you think TV viewing habits change as people get older?","answer":"Q: Do you think most people watch TV for education or for entertainment?\nA: I think people watch TV primarily for entertainment. There are far more entertainment programmes than educational ones, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in my experience</strong> most people treat television as a form of relaxation in the evening. If I think about the most popular TV programmes in the UK, such as talent shows like &#x27;X Factor&#x27; or soap operas like &#x27;Eastenders&#x27;, the focus is definitely on entertainment rather than education. That&#x27;s not the whole picture, though — people do pick up a great deal by accident from documentaries and news programmes, so the education is often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">incidental rather than intended</strong>.\n\nQ: Should TV play a role in educating children; how?\nA: In my opinion, yes — TV should definitely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">play a role in</strong> educating children. Good children&#x27;s TV programmes should tell stories that contain some kind of lesson about how to behave or what&#x27;s morally right and wrong. Many of the traditional fairy tales, such as &#x27;Cinderella&#x27;, have been made into TV programmes, and there&#x27;s always a positive message in those stories. Programmes about science and the natural world do much the same job for older children, and they often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spark an interest that lasts</strong>. It can&#x27;t replace an adult explaining things, though — a five-year-old watching alone will pick up the story but often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">miss the lesson underneath it</strong>.\n\nQ: How do you think TV viewing habits change as people get older?\nA: TV viewing habits obviously change a lot as we get older. While toddlers might watch programmes about talking animals, teenagers prefer action and adventure or sports, and as adults we start taking more of an interest in news and politics. My own preferences, for example, have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changed over the years</strong> — I&#x27;d never have watched news programmes when I was younger. That&#x27;s not true for everyone, though; plenty of adults keep the same taste in cartoons or action films they had as teenagers, and I don&#x27;t think that says anything bad about them.","topic":"Film & Television","phrases":[{"en":"in my experience","context":"Q: Do you think most people watch TV for education or for entertainment?\nA: I think people watch TV primarily for entertainment. There are far more entertainment programmes than educational ones, and in my experience most people treat television as a form of relaxation in the evening. If I think about the most popular TV programmes in the UK, such as talent shows like 'X Factor' or soap operas like 'Eastenders', the focus is definitely on entertainment rather than education. That's not the whole picture, though — people do pick up a great deal by accident from documentaries and news programmes, so the education is often incidental rather than intended."},{"en":"incidental rather than intended","context":"Q: Do you think most people watch TV for education or for entertainment?\nA: I think people watch TV primarily for entertainment. There are far more entertainment programmes than educational ones, and in my experience most people treat television as a form of relaxation in the evening. If I think about the most popular TV programmes in the UK, such as talent shows like 'X Factor' or soap operas like 'Eastenders', the focus is definitely on entertainment rather than education. That's not the whole picture, though — people do pick up a great deal by accident from documentaries and news programmes, so the education is often incidental rather than intended."},{"en":"play a role in","context":"Q: Should TV play a role in educating children; how?\nA: In my opinion, yes — TV should definitely play a role in educating children. Good children's TV programmes should tell stories that contain some kind of lesson about how to behave or what's morally right and wrong. Many of the traditional fairy tales, such as 'Cinderella', have been made into TV programmes, and there's always a positive message in those stories. Programmes about science and the natural world do much the same job for older children, and they often spark an interest that lasts. It can't replace an adult explaining things, though — a five-year-old watching alone will pick up the story but often miss the lesson underneath it."},{"en":"spark an interest that lasts","context":"Q: Should TV play a role in educating children; how?\nA: In my opinion, yes — TV should definitely play a role in educating children. Good children's TV programmes should tell stories that contain some kind of lesson about how to behave or what's morally right and wrong. Many of the traditional fairy tales, such as 'Cinderella', have been made into TV programmes, and there's always a positive message in those stories. Programmes about science and the natural world do much the same job for older children, and they often spark an interest that lasts. It can't replace an adult explaining things, though — a five-year-old watching alone will pick up the story but often miss the lesson underneath it."},{"en":"miss the lesson underneath it","context":"Q: Should TV play a role in educating children; how?\nA: In my opinion, yes — TV should definitely play a role in educating children. Good children's TV programmes should tell stories that contain some kind of lesson about how to behave or what's morally right and wrong. Many of the traditional fairy tales, such as 'Cinderella', have been made into TV programmes, and there's always a positive message in those stories. Programmes about science and the natural world do much the same job for older children, and they often spark an interest that lasts. It can't replace an adult explaining things, though — a five-year-old watching alone will pick up the story but often miss the lesson underneath it."},{"en":"changed over the years","context":"Q: How do you think TV viewing habits change as people get older?\nA: TV viewing habits obviously change a lot as we get older. While toddlers might watch programmes about talking animals, teenagers prefer action and adventure or sports, and as adults we start taking more of an interest in news and politics. My own preferences, for example, have changed over the years — I'd never have watched news programmes when I was younger. That's not true for everyone, though; plenty of adults keep the same taste in cartoons or action films they had as teenagers, and I don't think that says anything bad about them."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Film & Television","id":"speaking-part-3-049","_displayNo":"#049","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-049-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-049-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-049-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Mostly for entertainment. After work, people often want to relax with talent shows, dramas or other enjoyable programmes. There are many more of these than programmes mainly designed to teach. However, viewers can still learn from documentaries and news without making education their main goal, so entertainment and learning are not always completely separate.","phrases":[{"en":"entertainment","meaning":"Activities or programmes people enjoy in their free time."}],"question":"Do you think most people watch TV for education or for entertainment?","html":"Mostly for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">entertainment</strong>. After work, people often want to relax with talent shows, dramas or other enjoyable programmes. There are many more of these than programmes mainly designed to teach. However, viewers can still learn from documentaries and news without making education their main goal, so entertainment and learning are not always completely separate."},{"text":"Yes. Stories can help children think about behaviour and right and wrong, while science programmes can build interest in the world. Fairy tales such as Cinderella can carry useful messages. However, young children may follow the story without understanding the lesson, so an adult discussing it with them can make the experience more valuable.","phrases":[{"en":"carry useful messages","meaning":"Communicate ideas that people can learn from."}],"question":"Should TV play a role in educating children; how?","html":"Yes. Stories can help children think about behaviour and right and wrong, while science programmes can build interest in the world. Fairy tales such as Cinderella can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carry useful messages</strong>. However, young children may follow the story without understanding the lesson, so an adult discussing it with them can make the experience more valuable."},{"text":"Young children may watch talking animals, while teenagers often prefer action, sport or adventure. Adults may become more interested in news and politics; that happened to me. However, these are general patterns, not fixed rules. Some adults still enjoy cartoons or action films, and keeping those interests does not mean there is anything wrong with them.","phrases":[{"en":"general patterns","meaning":"Common tendencies that do not apply to every individual."}],"question":"How do you think TV viewing habits change as people get older?","html":"Young children may watch talking animals, while teenagers often prefer action, sport or adventure. Adults may become more interested in news and politics; that happened to me. However, these are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">general patterns</strong>, not fixed rules. Some adults still enjoy cartoons or action films, and keeping those interests does not mean there is anything wrong with them."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-049-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-049-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-049-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Whether cinema is gaining or losing ground","prompt":"Do you think the cinema has increased or decreased in popularity in recent years?\nIn your opinion, will this trend continue into the future?\nWhat are the advantages and disadvantages of making films of real-life events?","answer":"Q: Do you think the cinema has increased or decreased in popularity in recent years?\nA: Overall it&#x27;s declined, and streaming is the obvious reason — for the price of one ticket you get a month of viewing at home. But it hasn&#x27;t collapsed either. Big spectacle films still fill cinemas, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">premium formats</strong> with large screens and proper sound have actually grown. What&#x27;s changed is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the occasion</strong>: going to the cinema used to be the default way to see a film, and now it&#x27;s a deliberate outing that people plan.\n\nQ: In your opinion, will this trend continue into the future?\nA: I think so. Streaming will remain dominant for everyday viewing, because convenience nearly always wins. Cinemas will survive by offering what a living room genuinely can&#x27;t — scale, sound, and the experience of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">watching with an audience</strong>, which does change how a comedy or a horror film feels. I&#x27;d expect fewer cinemas overall but better ones, used increasingly for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">live broadcasts and re-releases</strong> rather than only for new films on their opening weekend. Smaller independent cinemas are the ones I&#x27;d worry about, since they can&#x27;t easily compete on either price or spectacle.\n\nQ: What are the advantages and disadvantages of making films of real-life events?\nA: The advantage is reach. A well-made film about a historical event will be seen by far more people than any book on the subject, and it can make something abstract feel immediate. The problem is that drama <strong class=\"key-phrase\">requires simplification</strong> — you need clear heroes, a narrative arc and often invented dialogue. Audiences then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remember the film version as fact</strong>, and that&#x27;s considerably more serious when the events are recent and the people involved are still alive to be misrepresented. It&#x27;s not all bad, though — a film that gets a few details wrong can still send people off to read the real history afterwards, which a textbook alone rarely manages.","topic":"Film & Television","phrases":[{"en":"premium formats","context":"Q: Do you think the cinema has increased or decreased in popularity in recent years?\nA: Overall it's declined, and streaming is the obvious reason — for the price of one ticket you get a month of viewing at home. But it hasn't collapsed either. Big spectacle films still fill cinemas, and premium formats with large screens and proper sound have actually grown. What's changed is the occasion: going to the cinema used to be the default way to see a film, and now it's a deliberate outing that people plan."},{"en":"the occasion","context":"Q: Do you think the cinema has increased or decreased in popularity in recent years?\nA: Overall it's declined, and streaming is the obvious reason — for the price of one ticket you get a month of viewing at home. But it hasn't collapsed either. Big spectacle films still fill cinemas, and premium formats with large screens and proper sound have actually grown. What's changed is the occasion: going to the cinema used to be the default way to see a film, and now it's a deliberate outing that people plan."},{"en":"watching with an audience","context":"Q: In your opinion, will this trend continue into the future?\nA: I think so. Streaming will remain dominant for everyday viewing, because convenience nearly always wins. Cinemas will survive by offering what a living room genuinely can't — scale, sound, and the experience of watching with an audience, which does change how a comedy or a horror film feels. I'd expect fewer cinemas overall but better ones, used increasingly for live broadcasts and re-releases rather than only for new films on their opening weekend. Smaller independent cinemas are the ones I'd worry about, since they can't easily compete on either price or spectacle."},{"en":"live broadcasts and re-releases","context":"Q: In your opinion, will this trend continue into the future?\nA: I think so. Streaming will remain dominant for everyday viewing, because convenience nearly always wins. Cinemas will survive by offering what a living room genuinely can't — scale, sound, and the experience of watching with an audience, which does change how a comedy or a horror film feels. I'd expect fewer cinemas overall but better ones, used increasingly for live broadcasts and re-releases rather than only for new films on their opening weekend. Smaller independent cinemas are the ones I'd worry about, since they can't easily compete on either price or spectacle."},{"en":"requires simplification","context":"Q: What are the advantages and disadvantages of making films of real-life events?\nA: The advantage is reach. A well-made film about a historical event will be seen by far more people than any book on the subject, and it can make something abstract feel immediate. The problem is that drama requires simplification — you need clear heroes, a narrative arc and often invented dialogue. Audiences then remember the film version as fact, and that's considerably more serious when the events are recent and the people involved are still alive to be misrepresented. It's not all bad, though — a film that gets a few details wrong can still send people off to read the real history afterwards, which a textbook alone rarely manages."},{"en":"remember the film version as fact","context":"Q: What are the advantages and disadvantages of making films of real-life events?\nA: The advantage is reach. A well-made film about a historical event will be seen by far more people than any book on the subject, and it can make something abstract feel immediate. The problem is that drama requires simplification — you need clear heroes, a narrative arc and often invented dialogue. Audiences then remember the film version as fact, and that's considerably more serious when the events are recent and the people involved are still alive to be misrepresented. It's not all bad, though — a film that gets a few details wrong can still send people off to read the real history afterwards, which a textbook alone rarely manages."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Film & Television","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-050","_displayNo":"#050","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-050-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-050-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-050-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I think cinema has become less common for everyday viewing because streaming offers many films for a low monthly cost. However, big films and large screens still attract audiences. The cinema has changed from the usual way to watch any film into a special outing that people choose and plan more carefully.","phrases":[{"en":"outing","meaning":"A trip away from home for enjoyment."}],"question":"Do you think the cinema has increased or decreased in popularity in recent years?","html":"I think cinema has become less common for everyday viewing because streaming offers many films for a low monthly cost. However, big films and large screens still attract audiences. The cinema has changed from the usual way to watch any film into a special <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outing</strong> that people choose and plan more carefully."},{"text":"Probably. Streaming is convenient, while cinemas will need to offer excellent sound, large screens and a shared experience. There may be fewer cinemas, but more special screenings and live broadcasts. Smaller independent cinemas could struggle because they cannot easily compete on either price or expensive technology, even if some audiences value their different choices.","phrases":[{"en":"independent cinemas","meaning":"Cinemas run separately from large commercial chains."}],"question":"In your opinion, will this trend continue into the future?","html":"Probably. Streaming is convenient, while cinemas will need to offer excellent sound, large screens and a shared experience. There may be fewer cinemas, but more special screenings and live broadcasts. Smaller <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independent cinemas</strong> could struggle because they cannot easily compete on either price or expensive technology, even if some audiences value their different choices."},{"text":"Films can introduce a historical event to many people and make it feel real. However, a clear dramatic story often needs shortened events or invented conversations. Viewers may then remember fiction as fact, which can unfairly affect real people. Still, an interesting film may encourage viewers to read the actual history afterwards, so it can start useful learning.","phrases":[{"en":"invented conversations","meaning":"Dialogue created by a writer rather than taken from a real record."}],"question":"What are the advantages and disadvantages of making films of real-life events?","html":"Films can introduce a historical event to many people and make it feel real. However, a clear dramatic story often needs shortened events or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">invented conversations</strong>. Viewers may then remember fiction as fact, which can unfairly affect real people. Still, an interesting film may encourage viewers to read the actual history afterwards, so it can start useful learning."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-050-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-050-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-050-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Feeding a guest, and popular food in your country","prompt":"If you invite a friend, what meal are you likely to offer them?\nWhat kinds of food are popular in your country?\nWhat are people's eating habits like in your country?","answer":"Q: If you invite a friend, what meal are you likely to offer them?\nA: Something shared, usually — grilled meat or tofu, rice, a couple of vegetable dishes and a soup, all placed in the middle for people to help themselves. That style works well because everyone simply takes what they want, so it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accommodates different tastes</strong> without my having to ask anybody in advance. It also means I&#x27;m not stuck in the kitchen all evening, which rather defeats the purpose of inviting somebody. The one time it doesn&#x27;t work is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a serious allergy or dietary restriction</strong>, where I do still have to ask ahead rather than leave it to chance.\n\nQ: What kinds of food are popular in your country?\nA: Rice and noodle dishes are the foundation of almost every meal, and street food is enormously popular because it&#x27;s cheap, fast and often better than what restaurants serve, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regional specialities</strong> matter a great deal too; people are quite proud about which province prepares a particular dish properly. Convenience food and international chains have grown steadily, especially among younger people, but traditional dishes still dominate because they&#x27;re affordable and tied closely to cultural identity.\n\nQ: What are people&#x27;s eating habits like in your country?\nA: They&#x27;re changing in two opposite directions at once. On one side, people in cities eat far more processed food and order delivery constantly, mainly because working hours <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leave no time to cook</strong>. On the other, a growing group pays close attention to ingredients, buying organic produce and avoiding sugar. So you find health food shops opening on the same street as fast food outlets, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">both doing well</strong> at the same time. Older generations sit slightly outside both trends, mind, since plenty of grandparents cook the same simple, unprocessed meals they always have, largely untouched by either shift.","topic":"Food","phrases":[{"en":"accommodates different tastes","context":"Q: If you invite a friend, what meal are you likely to offer them?\nA: Something shared, usually — grilled meat or tofu, rice, a couple of vegetable dishes and a soup, all placed in the middle for people to help themselves. That style works well because everyone simply takes what they want, so it accommodates different tastes without my having to ask anybody in advance. It also means I'm not stuck in the kitchen all evening, which rather defeats the purpose of inviting somebody. The one time it doesn't work is a serious allergy or dietary restriction, where I do still have to ask ahead rather than leave it to chance."},{"en":"a serious allergy or dietary restriction","context":"Q: If you invite a friend, what meal are you likely to offer them?\nA: Something shared, usually — grilled meat or tofu, rice, a couple of vegetable dishes and a soup, all placed in the middle for people to help themselves. That style works well because everyone simply takes what they want, so it accommodates different tastes without my having to ask anybody in advance. It also means I'm not stuck in the kitchen all evening, which rather defeats the purpose of inviting somebody. The one time it doesn't work is a serious allergy or dietary restriction, where I do still have to ask ahead rather than leave it to chance."},{"en":"regional specialities","context":"Q: What kinds of food are popular in your country?\nA: Rice and noodle dishes are the foundation of almost every meal, and street food is enormously popular because it's cheap, fast and often better than what restaurants serve, and regional specialities matter a great deal too; people are quite proud about which province prepares a particular dish properly. Convenience food and international chains have grown steadily, especially among younger people, but traditional dishes still dominate because they're affordable and tied closely to cultural identity."},{"en":"leave no time to cook","context":"Q: What are people's eating habits like in your country?\nA: They're changing in two opposite directions at once. On one side, people in cities eat far more processed food and order delivery constantly, mainly because working hours leave no time to cook. On the other, a growing group pays close attention to ingredients, buying organic produce and avoiding sugar. So you find health food shops opening on the same street as fast food outlets, and both doing well at the same time. Older generations sit slightly outside both trends, mind, since plenty of grandparents cook the same simple, unprocessed meals they always have, largely untouched by either shift."},{"en":"both doing well","context":"Q: What are people's eating habits like in your country?\nA: They're changing in two opposite directions at once. On one side, people in cities eat far more processed food and order delivery constantly, mainly because working hours leave no time to cook. On the other, a growing group pays close attention to ingredients, buying organic produce and avoiding sugar. So you find health food shops opening on the same street as fast food outlets, and both doing well at the same time. Older generations sit slightly outside both trends, mind, since plenty of grandparents cook the same simple, unprocessed meals they always have, largely untouched by either shift."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Food","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-051","_displayNo":"#051","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-051-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-051-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-051-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I'd offer shared dishes such as grilled meat or tofu, rice, vegetables and soup. Guests can choose what they enjoy, and I can spend time with them instead of cooking all evening. However, I would ask about serious allergies or dietary needs beforehand. A flexible meal is useful, but it does not replace checking important restrictions.","phrases":[{"en":"dietary needs","meaning":"Particular requirements about what a person can or should eat."}],"question":"If you invite a friend, what meal are you likely to offer them?","html":"I&#x27;d offer shared dishes such as grilled meat or tofu, rice, vegetables and soup. Guests can choose what they enjoy, and I can spend time with them instead of cooking all evening. However, I would ask about serious allergies or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dietary needs</strong> beforehand. A flexible meal is useful, but it does not replace checking important restrictions."},{"text":"Rice and noodles are central, and street food is popular because it is quick, affordable and often very good. Different regions also take pride in their special dishes. International chains and convenience foods are growing, especially among younger people. Even so, traditional meals remain common because they are affordable and closely connected with local culture.","phrases":[{"en":"take pride in","meaning":"Feel pleased and proud about something connected with you."}],"question":"What kinds of food are popular in your country?","html":"Rice and noodles are central, and street food is popular because it is quick, affordable and often very good. Different regions also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take pride in</strong> their special dishes. International chains and convenience foods are growing, especially among younger people. Even so, traditional meals remain common because they are affordable and closely connected with local culture."},{"text":"Some city residents eat more processed meals and order food because work leaves little time to cook. Others are becoming more careful about ingredients and avoiding too much sugar. Both trends can happen in the same area. Many older people, meanwhile, continue preparing the same simple meals they have always eaten, outside either new trend.","phrases":[{"en":"processed meals","meaning":"Food changed or prepared in a factory before being sold."}],"question":"What are people's eating habits like in your country?","html":"Some city residents eat more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">processed meals</strong> and order food because work leaves little time to cook. Others are becoming more careful about ingredients and avoiding too much sugar. Both trends can happen in the same area. Many older people, meanwhile, continue preparing the same simple meals they have always eaten, outside either new trend."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-051-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-051-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-051-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"The qualities that matter in a friend","prompt":"What do you think are the most important qualities for friends to have?\nHow important do you think it is for a person to spend some time alone?","answer":"Q: What do you think are the most important qualities for friends to have?\nA: The most important qualities are honesty, reliability and mutual respect. Shared interests make a friendship enjoyable, but trust is what makes it durable. A close friend should be able to listen, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tell the truth tactfully</strong> and remain dependable when circumstances become difficult. That last part is really the test: plenty of people are good company while everything&#x27;s going well, but a genuine friend is the one who still answers the phone when you&#x27;re the one with the problem. That doesn&#x27;t mean every friendship needs to survive a crisis to count, though — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a lighter, purely social friendship</strong> is still perfectly valid, just a different kind of relationship.\n\nQ: How important do you think it is for a person to spend some time alone?\nA: Spending some time alone is essential because it allows people to process experiences and recover from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">constant social demands</strong>. Even a short walk or quiet coffee can create space for reflection. Solitude becomes unhealthy only when it turns into <strong class=\"key-phrase\">persistent isolation</strong>. I&#x27;d add that people differ enormously in how much they need: some <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recover their energy in company</strong> and find being alone draining, whereas others can&#x27;t think clearly until the room&#x27;s quiet. Neither one is a flaw; the mistake is assuming everyone works the same way.","topic":"Friendship","phrases":[{"en":"tell the truth tactfully","context":"Q: What do you think are the most important qualities for friends to have?\nA: The most important qualities are honesty, reliability and mutual respect. Shared interests make a friendship enjoyable, but trust is what makes it durable. A close friend should be able to listen, tell the truth tactfully and remain dependable when circumstances become difficult. That last part is really the test: plenty of people are good company while everything's going well, but a genuine friend is the one who still answers the phone when you're the one with the problem. That doesn't mean every friendship needs to survive a crisis to count, though — a lighter, purely social friendship is still perfectly valid, just a different kind of relationship."},{"en":"a lighter, purely social friendship","context":"Q: What do you think are the most important qualities for friends to have?\nA: The most important qualities are honesty, reliability and mutual respect. Shared interests make a friendship enjoyable, but trust is what makes it durable. A close friend should be able to listen, tell the truth tactfully and remain dependable when circumstances become difficult. That last part is really the test: plenty of people are good company while everything's going well, but a genuine friend is the one who still answers the phone when you're the one with the problem. That doesn't mean every friendship needs to survive a crisis to count, though — a lighter, purely social friendship is still perfectly valid, just a different kind of relationship."},{"en":"constant social demands","context":"Q: How important do you think it is for a person to spend some time alone?\nA: Spending some time alone is essential because it allows people to process experiences and recover from constant social demands. Even a short walk or quiet coffee can create space for reflection. Solitude becomes unhealthy only when it turns into persistent isolation. I'd add that people differ enormously in how much they need: some recover their energy in company and find being alone draining, whereas others can't think clearly until the room's quiet. Neither one is a flaw; the mistake is assuming everyone works the same way."},{"en":"persistent isolation","context":"Q: How important do you think it is for a person to spend some time alone?\nA: Spending some time alone is essential because it allows people to process experiences and recover from constant social demands. Even a short walk or quiet coffee can create space for reflection. Solitude becomes unhealthy only when it turns into persistent isolation. I'd add that people differ enormously in how much they need: some recover their energy in company and find being alone draining, whereas others can't think clearly until the room's quiet. Neither one is a flaw; the mistake is assuming everyone works the same way."},{"en":"recover their energy in company","context":"Q: How important do you think it is for a person to spend some time alone?\nA: Spending some time alone is essential because it allows people to process experiences and recover from constant social demands. Even a short walk or quiet coffee can create space for reflection. Solitude becomes unhealthy only when it turns into persistent isolation. I'd add that people differ enormously in how much they need: some recover their energy in company and find being alone draining, whereas others can't think clearly until the room's quiet. Neither one is a flaw; the mistake is assuming everyone works the same way."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Friendship","id":"speaking-part-3-052","_displayNo":"#052","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-052-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-052-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Honesty, reliability and respect matter most. Similar interests make a friendship enjoyable, but trust helps it last. A close friend listens and supports you when things are difficult, not just when life is fun. However, lighter friendships still have value. Not every person we enjoy spending time with needs to become someone we rely on in a crisis.","phrases":[{"en":"reliability","meaning":"The quality of being trustworthy and dependable."}],"question":"What do you think are the most important qualities for friends to have?","html":"Honesty, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reliability</strong> and respect matter most. Similar interests make a friendship enjoyable, but trust helps it last. A close friend listens and supports you when things are difficult, not just when life is fun. However, lighter friendships still have value. Not every person we enjoy spending time with needs to become someone we rely on in a crisis."},{"text":"Some time alone helps people rest, understand their experiences and think clearly. A quiet walk or coffee may be enough. However, being alone too much can become unhealthy isolation. People also need different amounts: some recover energy with friends, while others need silence. I think the right balance matters more than expecting everyone to prefer the same thing.","phrases":[{"en":"isolation","meaning":"Being separated from other people or having very little contact."}],"question":"How important do you think it is for a person to spend some time alone?","html":"Some time alone helps people rest, understand their experiences and think clearly. A quiet walk or coffee may be enough. However, being alone too much can become unhealthy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">isolation</strong>. People also need different amounts: some recover energy with friends, while others need silence. I think the right balance matters more than expecting everyone to prefer the same thing."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-052-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-052-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How people meet friends, and who finds it easier","prompt":"How do people usually meet new friends where you live?\nIs it easier for adults or children to make new friends and why?\nHow are friendships different from relationships at work or college?","answer":"Q: How do people usually meet new friends where you live?\nA: School and university are where most lasting friendships start, simply because you spend years in the same place with the same people. After that it&#x27;s work, mutual friends and shared activities such as sports clubs or classes, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shared activity</strong> works particularly well because you&#x27;re not obliged to make conversation from nothing; there&#x27;s an obvious thing to talk about. Online communities matter too, though those friendships usually need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">move offline to last</strong> — a group chat on its own rarely turns into a close friendship.\n\nQ: Is it easier for adults or children to make new friends and why?\nA: Children, clearly. They spend <strong class=\"key-phrase\">repeated unstructured time</strong> with the same group, which is exactly the condition friendship needs, and they haven&#x27;t yet learned to be cautious — a child can meet someone in a park and be friends within an hour. Adults have the opposite situation: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fixed schedules and established social circles</strong>, plus a self-consciousness about appearing to need people. So adult friendship generally requires deliberate, repeated effort, and a great many people simply never get round to making it.\n\nQ: How are friendships different from relationships at work or college?\nA: The essential difference is that friendship is voluntary and has no other purpose. At work you&#x27;re placed with people you didn&#x27;t choose, and there&#x27;s a structure around the relationship — someone evaluates someone else, and there are things you can&#x27;t say honestly. Colleagues can absolutely become genuine friends, but usually only once <strong class=\"key-phrase\">that structure is removed</strong>, or after one of you leaves the organisation. Until then there&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a natural ceiling on the trust</strong>.","topic":"Friendship","phrases":[{"en":"shared activity","context":"Q: How do people usually meet new friends where you live?\nA: School and university are where most lasting friendships start, simply because you spend years in the same place with the same people. After that it's work, mutual friends and shared activities such as sports clubs or classes, and shared activity works particularly well because you're not obliged to make conversation from nothing; there's an obvious thing to talk about. Online communities matter too, though those friendships usually need to move offline to last — a group chat on its own rarely turns into a close friendship."},{"en":"move offline to last","context":"Q: How do people usually meet new friends where you live?\nA: School and university are where most lasting friendships start, simply because you spend years in the same place with the same people. After that it's work, mutual friends and shared activities such as sports clubs or classes, and shared activity works particularly well because you're not obliged to make conversation from nothing; there's an obvious thing to talk about. Online communities matter too, though those friendships usually need to move offline to last — a group chat on its own rarely turns into a close friendship."},{"en":"repeated unstructured time","context":"Q: Is it easier for adults or children to make new friends and why?\nA: Children, clearly. They spend repeated unstructured time with the same group, which is exactly the condition friendship needs, and they haven't yet learned to be cautious — a child can meet someone in a park and be friends within an hour. Adults have the opposite situation: fixed schedules and established social circles, plus a self-consciousness about appearing to need people. So adult friendship generally requires deliberate, repeated effort, and a great many people simply never get round to making it."},{"en":"fixed schedules and established social circles","context":"Q: Is it easier for adults or children to make new friends and why?\nA: Children, clearly. They spend repeated unstructured time with the same group, which is exactly the condition friendship needs, and they haven't yet learned to be cautious — a child can meet someone in a park and be friends within an hour. Adults have the opposite situation: fixed schedules and established social circles, plus a self-consciousness about appearing to need people. So adult friendship generally requires deliberate, repeated effort, and a great many people simply never get round to making it."},{"en":"that structure is removed","context":"Q: How are friendships different from relationships at work or college?\nA: The essential difference is that friendship is voluntary and has no other purpose. At work you're placed with people you didn't choose, and there's a structure around the relationship — someone evaluates someone else, and there are things you can't say honestly. Colleagues can absolutely become genuine friends, but usually only once that structure is removed, or after one of you leaves the organisation. Until then there's a natural ceiling on the trust."},{"en":"a natural ceiling on the trust","context":"Q: How are friendships different from relationships at work or college?\nA: The essential difference is that friendship is voluntary and has no other purpose. At work you're placed with people you didn't choose, and there's a structure around the relationship — someone evaluates someone else, and there are things you can't say honestly. Colleagues can absolutely become genuine friends, but usually only once that structure is removed, or after one of you leaves the organisation. Until then there's a natural ceiling on the trust."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Friendship","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-053","_displayNo":"#053","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-053-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-053-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-053-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Many friendships begin at school or university because people spend a lot of time together. Adults also meet through work, mutual friends and shared activities such as classes. An activity gives them an easy subject to discuss. Online groups can help too, although regular personal contact often helps those connections become closer and last longer.","phrases":[{"en":"mutual friends","meaning":"People who are friends with both of you."}],"question":"How do people usually meet new friends where you live?","html":"Many friendships begin at school or university because people spend a lot of time together. Adults also meet through work, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mutual friends</strong> and shared activities such as classes. An activity gives them an easy subject to discuss. Online groups can help too, although regular personal contact often helps those connections become closer and last longer."},{"text":"Usually children, because they have repeated free time together and worry less about making an impression. Adults have work schedules, existing friendship groups and sometimes feel embarrassed about needing company. Making new friends as an adult therefore takes deliberate effort and repeated meetings. It is possible, but the right conditions do not happen as automatically.","phrases":[{"en":"making an impression","meaning":"Trying to affect how other people see or judge you."}],"question":"Is it easier for adults or children to make new friends and why?","html":"Usually children, because they have repeated free time together and worry less about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">making an impression</strong>. Adults have work schedules, existing friendship groups and sometimes feel embarrassed about needing company. Making new friends as an adult therefore takes deliberate effort and repeated meetings. It is possible, but the right conditions do not happen as automatically."},{"text":"Friendship is chosen freely, while work or college relationships often begin because people are placed together. At work, responsibilities and power differences may limit what people can say honestly. Colleagues can become close friends, but it may be easier after changing jobs. Until then, professional roles can create a limit on openness and trust.","phrases":[{"en":"openness","meaning":"Willingness to share thoughts and feelings honestly."}],"question":"How are friendships different from relationships at work or college?","html":"Friendship is chosen freely, while work or college relationships often begin because people are placed together. At work, responsibilities and power differences may limit what people can say honestly. Colleagues can become close friends, but it may be easier after changing jobs. Until then, professional roles can create a limit on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">openness</strong> and trust."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-053-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-053-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-053-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Presents children ask for, and advertising to children","prompt":"What kinds of presents do children ask for today?\nWhat effect has advertising had on children's preferences?\nIs there a case for banning advertising directed at children?","answer":"Q: What kinds of presents do children ask for today?\nA: Mostly electronics and things connected to them — phones, tablets, games and in-game purchases, which parents often find harder to understand than a physical toy. Branded clothing and trainers matter a great deal to older children. What&#x27;s changed is where the requests come from: children now ask for specific items they&#x27;ve seen an influencer using, so the demand arrives <strong class=\"key-phrase\">already attached to a brand</strong> rather than to a general idea of what they&#x27;d enjoy. It&#x27;s not universal, though — plenty of younger children still just want <strong class=\"key-phrase\">whatever toy a friend happens to have</strong>, brand aside.\n\nQ: What effect has advertising had on children&#x27;s preferences?\nA: It&#x27;s made them extraordinarily brand-aware — young children can identify logos before they can read. The more serious effect concerns younger children specifically, because below about eight they don&#x27;t reliably understand that an advertisement is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trying to sell them something</strong>. They take the claims as straightforward fact. Repeated exposure then turns something they&#x27;d never heard of into something they feel they need, and that pressure lands on the parents. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Older children are more resistant</strong>, since by their teens most can recognise persuasion for what it is, even if they don&#x27;t always act on that awareness.\n\nQ: Is there a case for banning advertising directed at children?\nA: There&#x27;s a strong case for restricting it, particularly for unhealthy food and for games using <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gambling-like mechanisms</strong>. The argument is straightforward: advertising assumes an audience capable of evaluating a claim, and small children aren&#x27;t. A total ban would be difficult to enforce given how much content crosses borders online, so I&#x27;d focus regulation on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the youngest age groups</strong> and the most harmful categories rather than attempting to prohibit everything.","topic":"Gifts","phrases":[{"en":"already attached to a brand","context":"Q: What kinds of presents do children ask for today?\nA: Mostly electronics and things connected to them — phones, tablets, games and in-game purchases, which parents often find harder to understand than a physical toy. Branded clothing and trainers matter a great deal to older children. What's changed is where the requests come from: children now ask for specific items they've seen an influencer using, so the demand arrives already attached to a brand rather than to a general idea of what they'd enjoy. It's not universal, though — plenty of younger children still just want whatever toy a friend happens to have, brand aside."},{"en":"whatever toy a friend happens to have","context":"Q: What kinds of presents do children ask for today?\nA: Mostly electronics and things connected to them — phones, tablets, games and in-game purchases, which parents often find harder to understand than a physical toy. Branded clothing and trainers matter a great deal to older children. What's changed is where the requests come from: children now ask for specific items they've seen an influencer using, so the demand arrives already attached to a brand rather than to a general idea of what they'd enjoy. It's not universal, though — plenty of younger children still just want whatever toy a friend happens to have, brand aside."},{"en":"trying to sell them something","context":"Q: What effect has advertising had on children's preferences?\nA: It's made them extraordinarily brand-aware — young children can identify logos before they can read. The more serious effect concerns younger children specifically, because below about eight they don't reliably understand that an advertisement is trying to sell them something. They take the claims as straightforward fact. Repeated exposure then turns something they'd never heard of into something they feel they need, and that pressure lands on the parents. Older children are more resistant, since by their teens most can recognise persuasion for what it is, even if they don't always act on that awareness."},{"en":"Older children are more resistant","context":"Q: What effect has advertising had on children's preferences?\nA: It's made them extraordinarily brand-aware — young children can identify logos before they can read. The more serious effect concerns younger children specifically, because below about eight they don't reliably understand that an advertisement is trying to sell them something. They take the claims as straightforward fact. Repeated exposure then turns something they'd never heard of into something they feel they need, and that pressure lands on the parents. Older children are more resistant, since by their teens most can recognise persuasion for what it is, even if they don't always act on that awareness."},{"en":"gambling-like mechanisms","context":"Q: Is there a case for banning advertising directed at children?\nA: There's a strong case for restricting it, particularly for unhealthy food and for games using gambling-like mechanisms. The argument is straightforward: advertising assumes an audience capable of evaluating a claim, and small children aren't. A total ban would be difficult to enforce given how much content crosses borders online, so I'd focus regulation on the youngest age groups and the most harmful categories rather than attempting to prohibit everything."},{"en":"the youngest age groups","context":"Q: Is there a case for banning advertising directed at children?\nA: There's a strong case for restricting it, particularly for unhealthy food and for games using gambling-like mechanisms. The argument is straightforward: advertising assumes an audience capable of evaluating a claim, and small children aren't. A total ban would be difficult to enforce given how much content crosses borders online, so I'd focus regulation on the youngest age groups and the most harmful categories rather than attempting to prohibit everything."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Gifts","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-054","_displayNo":"#054","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-054-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-054-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-054-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Many ask for phones, tablets, games and online game items. Older children may want branded clothes or trainers. Influencers can create demand for a particular brand instead of just a type of toy. However, younger children often still want something simply because a friend has it, so technology and branding do not explain every request.","phrases":[{"en":"demand","meaning":"The desire to buy or obtain a product."}],"question":"What kinds of presents do children ask for today?","html":"Many ask for phones, tablets, games and online game items. Older children may want branded clothes or trainers. Influencers can create <strong class=\"key-phrase\">demand</strong> for a particular brand instead of just a type of toy. However, younger children often still want something simply because a friend has it, so technology and branding do not explain every request."},{"text":"Adverts make children recognise brands early and can turn an unfamiliar product into something they feel they need. Younger children may believe claims without understanding that someone is trying to sell to them. This creates pressure on parents. Older children usually recognise advertising more easily, though knowing its purpose does not always stop it influencing them.","phrases":[{"en":"claims","meaning":"Statements saying that something is true or has a particular benefit."}],"question":"What effect has advertising had on children's preferences?","html":"Adverts make children recognise brands early and can turn an unfamiliar product into something they feel they need. Younger children may believe <strong class=\"key-phrase\">claims</strong> without understanding that someone is trying to sell to them. This creates pressure on parents. Older children usually recognise advertising more easily, though knowing its purpose does not always stop it influencing them."},{"text":"I support strong limits, especially for unhealthy food and games that encourage gambling-like spending. Young children cannot judge advertising claims as well as adults. A complete ban would be difficult online because content comes from different countries. I would focus on the youngest audiences and the most harmful products, where protection is most clearly needed.","phrases":[{"en":"limits","meaning":"Rules controlling how much something can happen or what is allowed."}],"question":"Is there a case for banning advertising directed at children?","html":"I support strong <strong class=\"key-phrase\">limits</strong>, especially for unhealthy food and games that encourage gambling-like spending. Young children cannot judge advertising claims as well as adults. A complete ban would be difficult online because content comes from different countries. I would focus on the youngest audiences and the most harmful products, where protection is most clearly needed."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-054-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-054-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-054-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"What the past teaches, and what may come next","prompt":"What do you think we can learn by studying events of the past?\nWhat important events do you think might take place in the future?","answer":"Q: What do you think we can learn by studying events of the past?\nA: I think we can learn a lot by studying history. Just as individual people learn from their mistakes, societies can learn from the mistakes made by previous governments or leaders. For example, from what I&#x27;ve read in the newspapers, many economists are looking back to the time of the Great Depression, around 80 years ago, in order to understand the financial crisis that is currently affecting many countries around the world. Even if we don&#x27;t always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learn from mistakes</strong>, I think it&#x27;s fascinating to study history because it gives us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an insight into who we are</strong> and where we come from.\n\nQ: What important events do you think might take place in the future?\nA: It&#x27;s really difficult to predict what will happen in the future; most of the big, historic events of the past would have been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">impossible to foresee</strong>. For example, I don&#x27;t think that anyone living 100 years ago could have imagined that people would one day walk on the moon. That&#x27;s not true of every kind of change, though — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">slow trends like an ageing population</strong> are fairly predictable decades in advance; it&#x27;s the sudden breakthroughs and shocks that catch everyone out. If I had to guess, I&#x27;d like to think that scientists will <strong class=\"key-phrase\">invent cures for diseases</strong> like cancer, and we&#x27;ll all live longer.","topic":"History","phrases":[{"en":"learn from mistakes","context":"Q: What do you think we can learn by studying events of the past?\nA: I think we can learn a lot by studying history. Just as individual people learn from their mistakes, societies can learn from the mistakes made by previous governments or leaders. For example, from what I've read in the newspapers, many economists are looking back to the time of the Great Depression, around 80 years ago, in order to understand the financial crisis that is currently affecting many countries around the world. Even if we don't always learn from mistakes, I think it's fascinating to study history because it gives us an insight into who we are and where we come from."},{"en":"an insight into who we are","context":"Q: What do you think we can learn by studying events of the past?\nA: I think we can learn a lot by studying history. Just as individual people learn from their mistakes, societies can learn from the mistakes made by previous governments or leaders. For example, from what I've read in the newspapers, many economists are looking back to the time of the Great Depression, around 80 years ago, in order to understand the financial crisis that is currently affecting many countries around the world. Even if we don't always learn from mistakes, I think it's fascinating to study history because it gives us an insight into who we are and where we come from."},{"en":"impossible to foresee","context":"Q: What important events do you think might take place in the future?\nA: It's really difficult to predict what will happen in the future; most of the big, historic events of the past would have been impossible to foresee. For example, I don't think that anyone living 100 years ago could have imagined that people would one day walk on the moon. That's not true of every kind of change, though — slow trends like an ageing population are fairly predictable decades in advance; it's the sudden breakthroughs and shocks that catch everyone out. If I had to guess, I'd like to think that scientists will invent cures for diseases like cancer, and we'll all live longer."},{"en":"slow trends like an ageing population","context":"Q: What important events do you think might take place in the future?\nA: It's really difficult to predict what will happen in the future; most of the big, historic events of the past would have been impossible to foresee. For example, I don't think that anyone living 100 years ago could have imagined that people would one day walk on the moon. That's not true of every kind of change, though — slow trends like an ageing population are fairly predictable decades in advance; it's the sudden breakthroughs and shocks that catch everyone out. If I had to guess, I'd like to think that scientists will invent cures for diseases like cancer, and we'll all live longer."},{"en":"invent cures for diseases","context":"Q: What important events do you think might take place in the future?\nA: It's really difficult to predict what will happen in the future; most of the big, historic events of the past would have been impossible to foresee. For example, I don't think that anyone living 100 years ago could have imagined that people would one day walk on the moon. That's not true of every kind of change, though — slow trends like an ageing population are fairly predictable decades in advance; it's the sudden breakthroughs and shocks that catch everyone out. If I had to guess, I'd like to think that scientists will invent cures for diseases like cancer, and we'll all live longer."}],"subtype":"","summary":"History","id":"speaking-part-3-055","_displayNo":"#055","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-055-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-055-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"History helps us understand earlier mistakes and how societies developed. Governments can compare past financial crises or conflicts with present problems, rather than assume everything is new. We do not always use the lessons well, but studying them still gives useful context. History also helps explain who we are and how our communities came to be.","phrases":[{"en":"context","meaning":"Background information that helps explain a situation."}],"question":"What do you think we can learn by studying events of the past?","html":"History helps us understand earlier mistakes and how societies developed. Governments can compare past financial crises or conflicts with present problems, rather than assume everything is new. We do not always use the lessons well, but studying them still gives useful <strong class=\"key-phrase\">context</strong>. History also helps explain who we are and how our communities came to be."},{"text":"It is very hard to predict major events. People in the distant past could not easily imagine later achievements such as walking on the moon. Slow changes, like an ageing population, are easier to expect than sudden discoveries or crises. I hope future science will find better treatments for serious diseases and help people live longer.","phrases":[{"en":"an ageing population","meaning":"A population in which older people form a growing share."}],"question":"What important events do you think might take place in the future?","html":"It is very hard to predict major events. People in the distant past could not easily imagine later achievements such as walking on the moon. Slow changes, like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an ageing population</strong>, are easier to expect than sudden discoveries or crises. I hope future science will find better treatments for serious diseases and help people live longer."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-055-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-055-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Whether studying history prevents repeated mistakes","prompt":"Studying history can help countries to avoid repeating past mistakes. Do you agree or not?\nHow can studying your own country's history help you understand present-day situations?\nHow important do you consider it to study the history of other countries?","answer":"Q: Studying history can help countries to avoid repeating past mistakes. Do you agree or not?\nA: I agree in principle, though the record suggests it works less often than it should. History does reveal patterns — how financial crises build, how conflicts escalate, what happens when a country isolates itself economically. The difficulty is recognising which past situation actually resembles the present one, because leaders tend to select the analogy that supports what they&#x27;d already decided to do anyway. So the knowledge only helps where there&#x27;s a genuine willingness to be corrected.\n\nQ: How can studying your own country&#x27;s history help you understand present-day situations?\nA: It explains things that otherwise look arbitrary — why institutions are organised as they are, why particular regions are wealthier, why certain political arguments recur every generation. Without that background, current events seem to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">come from nowhere</strong>. The important condition is that it&#x27;s taught critically, including the uncomfortable episodes. A national history presented purely as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a patriotic story</strong> doesn&#x27;t explain the present; it simply produces people who are easy to mislead about it.\n\nQ: How important do you consider it to study the history of other countries?\nA: Very important, because no country developed on its own. Trade, migration and war connected societies long before anyone used the word globalisation, and you can&#x27;t understand your own country&#x27;s position without that. There&#x27;s a second benefit as well: reading how another society understands the same events is the fastest cure for assuming that your own version is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">simply the truth</strong> rather than one account among several. Nobody can study every country in depth, of course, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the practical priority</strong> should be the histories most entangled with your own — former colonisers, close neighbours, major trading partners — rather than an impossible attempt at everything.","topic":"History","phrases":[{"en":"come from nowhere","context":"Q: How can studying your own country's history help you understand present-day situations?\nA: It explains things that otherwise look arbitrary — why institutions are organised as they are, why particular regions are wealthier, why certain political arguments recur every generation. Without that background, current events seem to come from nowhere. The important condition is that it's taught critically, including the uncomfortable episodes. A national history presented purely as a patriotic story doesn't explain the present; it simply produces people who are easy to mislead about it."},{"en":"a patriotic story","context":"Q: How can studying your own country's history help you understand present-day situations?\nA: It explains things that otherwise look arbitrary — why institutions are organised as they are, why particular regions are wealthier, why certain political arguments recur every generation. Without that background, current events seem to come from nowhere. The important condition is that it's taught critically, including the uncomfortable episodes. A national history presented purely as a patriotic story doesn't explain the present; it simply produces people who are easy to mislead about it."},{"en":"simply the truth","context":"Q: How important do you consider it to study the history of other countries?\nA: Very important, because no country developed on its own. Trade, migration and war connected societies long before anyone used the word globalisation, and you can't understand your own country's position without that. There's a second benefit as well: reading how another society understands the same events is the fastest cure for assuming that your own version is simply the truth rather than one account among several. Nobody can study every country in depth, of course, so the practical priority should be the histories most entangled with your own — former colonisers, close neighbours, major trading partners — rather than an impossible attempt at everything."},{"en":"the practical priority","context":"Q: How important do you consider it to study the history of other countries?\nA: Very important, because no country developed on its own. Trade, migration and war connected societies long before anyone used the word globalisation, and you can't understand your own country's position without that. There's a second benefit as well: reading how another society understands the same events is the fastest cure for assuming that your own version is simply the truth rather than one account among several. Nobody can study every country in depth, of course, so the practical priority should be the histories most entangled with your own — former colonisers, close neighbours, major trading partners — rather than an impossible attempt at everything."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"History","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-056","_displayNo":"#056","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-056-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-056-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-056-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I agree that it can help, but only if people use it honestly. History shows how crises and conflicts develop. The difficulty is deciding whether a past situation really matches the present. Leaders may choose only examples supporting their existing plans. Lessons are useful when people are willing to question their own views, not just defend them.","phrases":[{"en":"existing plans","meaning":"Plans that have already been made."}],"question":"Studying history can help countries to avoid repeating past mistakes. Do you agree or not?","html":"I agree that it can help, but only if people use it honestly. History shows how crises and conflicts develop. The difficulty is deciding whether a past situation really matches the present. Leaders may choose only examples supporting their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">existing plans</strong>. Lessons are useful when people are willing to question their own views, not just defend them."},{"text":"It explains why institutions, regions and political arguments are the way they are. Without that background, current events can seem to appear from nowhere. However, history needs to include mistakes and uncomfortable events, not only achievements. A story designed just to make people proud may hide information they need to understand their country today.","phrases":[{"en":"institutions","meaning":"Established organisations or systems that shape public life."}],"question":"How can studying your own country's history help you understand present-day situations?","html":"It explains why <strong class=\"key-phrase\">institutions</strong>, regions and political arguments are the way they are. Without that background, current events can seem to appear from nowhere. However, history needs to include mistakes and uncomfortable events, not only achievements. A story designed just to make people proud may hide information they need to understand their country today."},{"text":"It is very important because trade, migration and war have connected countries for centuries. Another country's account can also challenge our own assumptions about the same event. Nobody can study every place deeply, so I would start with neighbours, major trading partners and countries closely linked to our history. That makes learning both useful and manageable.","phrases":[{"en":"assumptions","meaning":"Ideas accepted as true without being fully checked."}],"question":"How important do you consider it to study the history of other countries?","html":"It is very important because trade, migration and war have connected countries for centuries. Another country&#x27;s account can also challenge our own <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assumptions</strong> about the same event. Nobody can study every place deeply, so I would start with neighbours, major trading partners and countries closely linked to our history. That makes learning both useful and manageable."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-056-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-056-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-056-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How the design of homes will change","prompt":"How do you think the design of homes will change in the future?","answer":"Q: How do you think the design of homes will change in the future?\nA: I think the exterior will stay fairly conventional, because people are quite conservative about how a house should look, but the inside will change considerably. The main driver will be energy costs, so better insulation, solar panels and heat pumps will become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">standard rather than optional</strong>. Space will also be used more flexibly, with rooms that serve as an office during the day and something else in the evening, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the shift to home working</strong> has made necessary. And with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ageing populations</strong>, I&#x27;d expect far more single-storey layouts and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">step-free access</strong>. New builds will lead the way; the millions of older houses already standing won&#x27;t get any of this unless someone pays to retrofit them, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retrofitting is far more expensive</strong> than designing it in from the start.","topic":"Housing & Architecture","phrases":[{"en":"standard rather than optional","context":"Q: How do you think the design of homes will change in the future?\nA: I think the exterior will stay fairly conventional, because people are quite conservative about how a house should look, but the inside will change considerably. The main driver will be energy costs, so better insulation, solar panels and heat pumps will become standard rather than optional. Space will also be used more flexibly, with rooms that serve as an office during the day and something else in the evening, which the shift to home working has made necessary. And with ageing populations, I'd expect far more single-storey layouts and step-free access. New builds will lead the way; the millions of older houses already standing won't get any of this unless someone pays to retrofit them, and retrofitting is far more expensive than designing it in from the start."},{"en":"the shift to home working","context":"Q: How do you think the design of homes will change in the future?\nA: I think the exterior will stay fairly conventional, because people are quite conservative about how a house should look, but the inside will change considerably. The main driver will be energy costs, so better insulation, solar panels and heat pumps will become standard rather than optional. Space will also be used more flexibly, with rooms that serve as an office during the day and something else in the evening, which the shift to home working has made necessary. And with ageing populations, I'd expect far more single-storey layouts and step-free access. New builds will lead the way; the millions of older houses already standing won't get any of this unless someone pays to retrofit them, and retrofitting is far more expensive than designing it in from the start."},{"en":"ageing populations","context":"Q: How do you think the design of homes will change in the future?\nA: I think the exterior will stay fairly conventional, because people are quite conservative about how a house should look, but the inside will change considerably. The main driver will be energy costs, so better insulation, solar panels and heat pumps will become standard rather than optional. Space will also be used more flexibly, with rooms that serve as an office during the day and something else in the evening, which the shift to home working has made necessary. And with ageing populations, I'd expect far more single-storey layouts and step-free access. New builds will lead the way; the millions of older houses already standing won't get any of this unless someone pays to retrofit them, and retrofitting is far more expensive than designing it in from the start."},{"en":"step-free access","context":"Q: How do you think the design of homes will change in the future?\nA: I think the exterior will stay fairly conventional, because people are quite conservative about how a house should look, but the inside will change considerably. The main driver will be energy costs, so better insulation, solar panels and heat pumps will become standard rather than optional. Space will also be used more flexibly, with rooms that serve as an office during the day and something else in the evening, which the shift to home working has made necessary. And with ageing populations, I'd expect far more single-storey layouts and step-free access. New builds will lead the way; the millions of older houses already standing won't get any of this unless someone pays to retrofit them, and retrofitting is far more expensive than designing it in from the start."},{"en":"retrofitting is far more expensive","context":"Q: How do you think the design of homes will change in the future?\nA: I think the exterior will stay fairly conventional, because people are quite conservative about how a house should look, but the inside will change considerably. The main driver will be energy costs, so better insulation, solar panels and heat pumps will become standard rather than optional. Space will also be used more flexibly, with rooms that serve as an office during the day and something else in the evening, which the shift to home working has made necessary. And with ageing populations, I'd expect far more single-storey layouts and step-free access. New builds will lead the way; the millions of older houses already standing won't get any of this unless someone pays to retrofit them, and retrofitting is far more expensive than designing it in from the start."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Housing & Architecture","id":"speaking-part-3-057","_displayNo":"#057","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-057-q01.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Homes may look familiar outside but change more inside. Higher energy costs could encourage better insulation and clean heating, while rooms may serve several purposes, such as work and relaxation. Older populations may need fewer stairs and easier entrances. New homes can include these features from the start; improving existing houses may be slower because it costs more.","phrases":[{"en":"insulation","meaning":"Material that helps stop heat escaping or entering a building."}],"question":"How do you think the design of homes will change in the future?","html":"Homes may look familiar outside but change more inside. Higher energy costs could encourage better <strong class=\"key-phrase\">insulation</strong> and clean heating, while rooms may serve several purposes, such as work and relaxation. Older populations may need fewer stairs and easier entrances. New homes can include these features from the start; improving existing houses may be slower because it costs more."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-057-q01.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Renting or buying, and leaving the family home","prompt":"Do you think it is better to rent or to buy a place to live in?\nDo you agree that there is a right age for young adults to stop living with their parents?","answer":"Q: Do you think it is better to rent or to buy a place to live in?\nA: I think it&#x27;s better to buy your own home if you can afford to do that. Buying your own home is a better investment <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the long term</strong>, because you can sell the home and perhaps even <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a profit on</strong> it. The alternative is to pay rent to a landlord who can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ask you to leave at any moment</strong>. The difficulty is that deposits have become enormous relative to wages, so a great many people can&#x27;t realistically buy however sensible it might be.\n\nQ: Do you agree that there is a right age for young adults to stop living with their parents?\nA: There&#x27;s no universal age at which young adults should leave home. Financial independence, housing costs, education and family culture all matter. The important point is that adults gradually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take responsibility for</strong> money and daily decisions, whether they live alone at eighteen or remain with their parents longer. It&#x27;s also worth saying that staying at home much longer is completely normal in many cultures, and nobody there regards it as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a failure to grow up</strong>. That judgement is quite specific to certain Western countries.","topic":"Housing & Architecture","phrases":[{"en":"in the long term","context":"Q: Do you think it is better to rent or to buy a place to live in?\nA: I think it's better to buy your own home if you can afford to do that. Buying your own home is a better investment in the long term, because you can sell the home and perhaps even make a profit on it. The alternative is to pay rent to a landlord who can ask you to leave at any moment. The difficulty is that deposits have become enormous relative to wages, so a great many people can't realistically buy however sensible it might be."},{"en":"make a profit on","context":"Q: Do you think it is better to rent or to buy a place to live in?\nA: I think it's better to buy your own home if you can afford to do that. Buying your own home is a better investment in the long term, because you can sell the home and perhaps even make a profit on it. The alternative is to pay rent to a landlord who can ask you to leave at any moment. The difficulty is that deposits have become enormous relative to wages, so a great many people can't realistically buy however sensible it might be."},{"en":"ask you to leave at any moment","context":"Q: Do you think it is better to rent or to buy a place to live in?\nA: I think it's better to buy your own home if you can afford to do that. Buying your own home is a better investment in the long term, because you can sell the home and perhaps even make a profit on it. The alternative is to pay rent to a landlord who can ask you to leave at any moment. The difficulty is that deposits have become enormous relative to wages, so a great many people can't realistically buy however sensible it might be."},{"en":"take responsibility for","context":"Q: Do you agree that there is a right age for young adults to stop living with their parents?\nA: There's no universal age at which young adults should leave home. Financial independence, housing costs, education and family culture all matter. The important point is that adults gradually take responsibility for money and daily decisions, whether they live alone at eighteen or remain with their parents longer. It's also worth saying that staying at home much longer is completely normal in many cultures, and nobody there regards it as a failure to grow up. That judgement is quite specific to certain Western countries."},{"en":"a failure to grow up","context":"Q: Do you agree that there is a right age for young adults to stop living with their parents?\nA: There's no universal age at which young adults should leave home. Financial independence, housing costs, education and family culture all matter. The important point is that adults gradually take responsibility for money and daily decisions, whether they live alone at eighteen or remain with their parents longer. It's also worth saying that staying at home much longer is completely normal in many cultures, and nobody there regards it as a failure to grow up. That judgement is quite specific to certain Western countries."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Housing & Architecture","id":"speaking-part-3-058","_displayNo":"#058","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-058-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-058-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I prefer buying if the cost is manageable. It gives more long-term control, and the home may keep or increase its value. Renting means paying an owner and having less certainty about staying. However, buying requires a large deposit, which many wages cannot support. So although ownership appeals to me, it is not a realistic choice for everyone.","phrases":[{"en":"deposit","meaning":"An initial payment made before paying the rest of a purchase price."}],"question":"Do you think it is better to rent or to buy a place to live in?","html":"I prefer buying if the cost is manageable. It gives more long-term control, and the home may keep or increase its value. Renting means paying an owner and having less certainty about staying. However, buying requires a large <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deposit</strong>, which many wages cannot support. So although ownership appeals to me, it is not a realistic choice for everyone."},{"text":"No, there is no right age for everyone. Money, education, housing costs and culture all affect the decision. More importantly, young adults should gradually learn to manage responsibilities and make choices. They can do that while living with parents too. In many cultures, staying home longer is normal rather than a sign that someone has failed to mature.","phrases":[{"en":"mature","meaning":"Become more responsible and able to manage adult life."}],"question":"Do you agree that there is a right age for young adults to stop living with their parents?","html":"No, there is no right age for everyone. Money, education, housing costs and culture all affect the decision. More importantly, young adults should gradually learn to manage responsibilities and make choices. They can do that while living with parents too. In many cultures, staying home longer is normal rather than a sign that someone has failed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mature</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-058-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-058-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Protecting historic buildings and what they bring an area","prompt":"How do people in your country feel about protecting historic buildings?\nDo you think an area can benefit from having an interesting historic place locally, and in what way?\nWhat do you think will happen to historic places or buildings in the future?","answer":"Q: How do people in your country feel about protecting historic buildings?\nA: Most people support it in principle, since nobody argues that heritage should simply be demolished. The disagreement starts when there&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a cost attached</strong>. Restoration is expensive, and when public money is involved, or when protection blocks housing that people badly need, opinion divides quickly. What I&#x27;ve noticed is that support is much stronger where old buildings remain in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">everyday use</strong> as cafes, offices or homes, rather than being preserved as untouchable monuments nobody enters.\n\nQ: Do you think an area can benefit from having an interesting historic place locally, and in what way?\nA: Yes, in several ways. The economic benefit is obvious: visitors spend money on food, accommodation and transport, and that supports jobs which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot easily be moved elsewhere</strong>. There&#x27;s a less measurable benefit too — a distinctive local landmark gives residents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">something to identify with</strong>, particularly in places that have lost their traditional industry. The important condition is that some of the tourist revenue goes back into maintaining the site and the surrounding streets, which doesn&#x27;t always happen.\n\nQ: What do you think will happen to historic places or buildings in the future?\nA: I&#x27;d expect the picture to divide. Famous sites will be better protected than ever, because they generate income and attract political attention, though over-tourism will force them to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">limit visitor numbers</strong>. Less celebrated buildings are the vulnerable ones, since ordinary old housing gets demolished quietly because nobody campaigns for it. Digital recording will improve enormously, but a detailed scan <strong class=\"key-phrase\">documents a building rather than preserving it</strong>, and no virtual model replaces standing inside the real thing.","topic":"Housing & Architecture","phrases":[{"en":"a cost attached","context":"Q: How do people in your country feel about protecting historic buildings?\nA: Most people support it in principle, since nobody argues that heritage should simply be demolished. The disagreement starts when there's a cost attached. Restoration is expensive, and when public money is involved, or when protection blocks housing that people badly need, opinion divides quickly. What I've noticed is that support is much stronger where old buildings remain in everyday use as cafes, offices or homes, rather than being preserved as untouchable monuments nobody enters."},{"en":"everyday use","context":"Q: How do people in your country feel about protecting historic buildings?\nA: Most people support it in principle, since nobody argues that heritage should simply be demolished. The disagreement starts when there's a cost attached. Restoration is expensive, and when public money is involved, or when protection blocks housing that people badly need, opinion divides quickly. What I've noticed is that support is much stronger where old buildings remain in everyday use as cafes, offices or homes, rather than being preserved as untouchable monuments nobody enters."},{"en":"cannot easily be moved elsewhere","context":"Q: Do you think an area can benefit from having an interesting historic place locally, and in what way?\nA: Yes, in several ways. The economic benefit is obvious: visitors spend money on food, accommodation and transport, and that supports jobs which cannot easily be moved elsewhere. There's a less measurable benefit too — a distinctive local landmark gives residents something to identify with, particularly in places that have lost their traditional industry. The important condition is that some of the tourist revenue goes back into maintaining the site and the surrounding streets, which doesn't always happen."},{"en":"something to identify with","context":"Q: Do you think an area can benefit from having an interesting historic place locally, and in what way?\nA: Yes, in several ways. The economic benefit is obvious: visitors spend money on food, accommodation and transport, and that supports jobs which cannot easily be moved elsewhere. There's a less measurable benefit too — a distinctive local landmark gives residents something to identify with, particularly in places that have lost their traditional industry. The important condition is that some of the tourist revenue goes back into maintaining the site and the surrounding streets, which doesn't always happen."},{"en":"limit visitor numbers","context":"Q: What do you think will happen to historic places or buildings in the future?\nA: I'd expect the picture to divide. Famous sites will be better protected than ever, because they generate income and attract political attention, though over-tourism will force them to limit visitor numbers. Less celebrated buildings are the vulnerable ones, since ordinary old housing gets demolished quietly because nobody campaigns for it. Digital recording will improve enormously, but a detailed scan documents a building rather than preserving it, and no virtual model replaces standing inside the real thing."},{"en":"documents a building rather than preserving it","context":"Q: What do you think will happen to historic places or buildings in the future?\nA: I'd expect the picture to divide. Famous sites will be better protected than ever, because they generate income and attract political attention, though over-tourism will force them to limit visitor numbers. Less celebrated buildings are the vulnerable ones, since ordinary old housing gets demolished quietly because nobody campaigns for it. Digital recording will improve enormously, but a detailed scan documents a building rather than preserving it, and no virtual model replaces standing inside the real thing."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Housing & Architecture","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-059","_displayNo":"#059","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-059-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-059-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-059-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Most support protecting them until the cost becomes clear. Repairing old buildings can be expensive, and protection may compete with the need for new homes. Support often grows when historic buildings remain useful as cafes, offices or houses. I think people find it easier to value heritage when it is still part of everyday life.","phrases":[{"en":"heritage","meaning":"Buildings, traditions or objects inherited from the past."}],"question":"How do people in your country feel about protecting historic buildings?","html":"Most support protecting them until the cost becomes clear. Repairing old buildings can be expensive, and protection may compete with the need for new homes. Support often grows when historic buildings remain useful as cafes, offices or houses. I think people find it easier to value <strong class=\"key-phrase\">heritage</strong> when it is still part of everyday life."},{"text":"Yes, visitors spend money locally, supporting jobs in food, accommodation and transport. A historic place can also give residents a shared identity and something to feel proud of. However, some of the income needs to return to the site and surrounding area. Otherwise, the place attracts visitors without receiving enough care to remain enjoyable or safe.","phrases":[{"en":"accommodation","meaning":"A place where people stay or live."}],"question":"Do you think an area can benefit from having an interesting historic place locally, and in what way?","html":"Yes, visitors spend money locally, supporting jobs in food, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accommodation</strong> and transport. A historic place can also give residents a shared identity and something to feel proud of. However, some of the income needs to return to the site and surrounding area. Otherwise, the place attracts visitors without receiving enough care to remain enjoyable or safe."},{"text":"Famous places will probably receive more protection because they attract money and attention, although visitor numbers may need limits. Ordinary old buildings are at greater risk because fewer people campaign for them. Digital records can preserve information, but a scan cannot replace a real building. I expect better recording alongside an uneven picture of actual protection.","phrases":[{"en":"uneven picture","meaning":"A situation in which some things improve or receive support more than others."}],"question":"What do you think will happen to historic places or buildings in the future?","html":"Famous places will probably receive more protection because they attract money and attention, although visitor numbers may need limits. Ordinary old buildings are at greater risk because fewer people campaign for them. Digital records can preserve information, but a scan cannot replace a real building. I expect better recording alongside an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">uneven picture</strong> of actual protection."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-059-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-059-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-059-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Why holidays matter, and why some people stay at home","prompt":"Do you think that it is important for people to go on holiday?\nWhy do you think some people prefer not to go abroad on holiday?","answer":"Q: Do you think that it is important for people to go on holiday?\nA: Yes, I think we all need to go on holiday at least once or twice a year. It isn&#x27;t healthy to work all year round without some time off to relax; we all need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take a break</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recharge our batteries</strong> from time to time. Last summer, for example, I went on holiday to France for a couple of weeks, and it was great to leave all of my usual responsibilities behind me — I came home feeling properly refreshed. It doesn&#x27;t have to mean going abroad, though; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a quiet week at home</strong> away from work emails can do almost the same job for a fraction of the cost.\n\nQ: Why do you think some people prefer not to go abroad on holiday?\nA: Cost is usually the biggest factor — it&#x27;s simply more expensive to fly somewhere and stay in a hotel than to holiday at home, especially for a family. There&#x27;s also the stress of a foreign country where you don&#x27;t speak the language and can&#x27;t easily <strong class=\"key-phrase\">integrate with the locals</strong>, which some people would rather avoid altogether. And plenty genuinely just love where they live and don&#x27;t feel any pull to leave it. That&#x27;s not the full story for everyone, though — some people avoid flying specifically because of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the environmental impact</strong>, which has very little to do with cost or comfort at all.","topic":"Leisure & Holidays","phrases":[{"en":"take a break","context":"Q: Do you think that it is important for people to go on holiday?\nA: Yes, I think we all need to go on holiday at least once or twice a year. It isn't healthy to work all year round without some time off to relax; we all need to take a break and recharge our batteries from time to time. Last summer, for example, I went on holiday to France for a couple of weeks, and it was great to leave all of my usual responsibilities behind me — I came home feeling properly refreshed. It doesn't have to mean going abroad, though; a quiet week at home away from work emails can do almost the same job for a fraction of the cost."},{"en":"recharge our batteries","context":"Q: Do you think that it is important for people to go on holiday?\nA: Yes, I think we all need to go on holiday at least once or twice a year. It isn't healthy to work all year round without some time off to relax; we all need to take a break and recharge our batteries from time to time. Last summer, for example, I went on holiday to France for a couple of weeks, and it was great to leave all of my usual responsibilities behind me — I came home feeling properly refreshed. It doesn't have to mean going abroad, though; a quiet week at home away from work emails can do almost the same job for a fraction of the cost."},{"en":"a quiet week at home","context":"Q: Do you think that it is important for people to go on holiday?\nA: Yes, I think we all need to go on holiday at least once or twice a year. It isn't healthy to work all year round without some time off to relax; we all need to take a break and recharge our batteries from time to time. Last summer, for example, I went on holiday to France for a couple of weeks, and it was great to leave all of my usual responsibilities behind me — I came home feeling properly refreshed. It doesn't have to mean going abroad, though; a quiet week at home away from work emails can do almost the same job for a fraction of the cost."},{"en":"integrate with the locals","context":"Q: Why do you think some people prefer not to go abroad on holiday?\nA: Cost is usually the biggest factor — it's simply more expensive to fly somewhere and stay in a hotel than to holiday at home, especially for a family. There's also the stress of a foreign country where you don't speak the language and can't easily integrate with the locals, which some people would rather avoid altogether. And plenty genuinely just love where they live and don't feel any pull to leave it. That's not the full story for everyone, though — some people avoid flying specifically because of the environmental impact, which has very little to do with cost or comfort at all."},{"en":"the environmental impact","context":"Q: Why do you think some people prefer not to go abroad on holiday?\nA: Cost is usually the biggest factor — it's simply more expensive to fly somewhere and stay in a hotel than to holiday at home, especially for a family. There's also the stress of a foreign country where you don't speak the language and can't easily integrate with the locals, which some people would rather avoid altogether. And plenty genuinely just love where they live and don't feel any pull to leave it. That's not the full story for everyone, though — some people avoid flying specifically because of the environmental impact, which has very little to do with cost or comfort at all."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Leisure & Holidays","id":"speaking-part-3-060","_displayNo":"#060","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-060-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-060-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, people need breaks to rest rather than work all year. A holiday can remove normal responsibilities and help us return with more energy; a trip to France did that for me. However, it does not have to mean travelling abroad. A quiet week at home without work messages can provide many of the same benefits for less money.","phrases":[{"en":"responsibilities","meaning":"Duties or tasks you are expected to manage."}],"question":"Do you think that it is important for people to go on holiday?","html":"Yes, people need breaks to rest rather than work all year. A holiday can remove normal <strong class=\"key-phrase\">responsibilities</strong> and help us return with more energy; a trip to France did that for me. However, it does not have to mean travelling abroad. A quiet week at home without work messages can provide many of the same benefits for less money."},{"text":"Cost is one reason, especially for families paying for travel and hotels. Some people also dislike unfamiliar languages or the stress of a foreign place. Others genuinely enjoy their local area and feel no need to leave. Environmental concerns can matter too, so staying home is not always only about money or comfort.","phrases":[{"en":"Environmental concerns","meaning":"Worries about damage to nature or the climate."}],"question":"Why do you think some people prefer not to go abroad on holiday?","html":"Cost is one reason, especially for families paying for travel and hotels. Some people also dislike unfamiliar languages or the stress of a foreign place. Others genuinely enjoy their local area and feel no need to leave. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Environmental concerns</strong> can matter too, so staying home is not always only about money or comfort."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-060-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-060-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Leisure and the economy","prompt":"What is the relationship between leisure and the economy?\nHow does the economy benefit from people's leisure activities?\nDo men and women enjoy the same type of leisure activities?","answer":"Q: What is the relationship between leisure and the economy?\nA: Well, people spend a huge amount of money on all sorts of leisure activities, so I&#x27;d say leisure is a genuinely important part of the economy in most countries. Leisure could be anything people do in their free time — eating out, going to the cinema, watching a football match, or staying in a hotel — and millions of people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a living from</strong> these industries. What I&#x27;d add, though, is that leisure spending is one of the first things households cut when money&#x27;s tight, so it&#x27;s also one of the more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exposed parts of the economy</strong>.\n\nQ: How does the economy benefit from people&#x27;s leisure activities?\nA: The leisure industry makes a huge contribution to the economy. It keeps millions of people in employment, and those employees pay tax and spend their wages on other goods and services, so the money keeps moving round the economy rather than sitting still. Tourism works the same way but on a bigger scale, because it pulls in money from outside the country altogether. The one qualification I&#x27;d make is that a lot of leisure jobs are seasonal or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">poorly paid</strong>, so the economy benefits even when the people doing the work don&#x27;t benefit all that much.\n\nQ: Do men and women enjoy the same type of leisure activities?\nA: Yes and no. I think both men and women enjoy things like eating in restaurants or going to the cinema, but I&#x27;m sure there are other activities that are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more popular with one gender</strong>. For example, I think more men than women go to football matches. I&#x27;d be careful about generalising, though, because those differences seem to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">narrowing</strong>. Women&#x27;s football has grown enormously in the last few years, and a lot of the differences people assume are natural turn out to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mostly habit</strong>.","topic":"Leisure & Holidays","phrases":[{"en":"make a living from","context":"Q: What is the relationship between leisure and the economy?\nA: Well, people spend a huge amount of money on all sorts of leisure activities, so I'd say leisure is a genuinely important part of the economy in most countries. Leisure could be anything people do in their free time — eating out, going to the cinema, watching a football match, or staying in a hotel — and millions of people make a living from these industries. What I'd add, though, is that leisure spending is one of the first things households cut when money's tight, so it's also one of the more exposed parts of the economy."},{"en":"exposed parts of the economy","context":"Q: What is the relationship between leisure and the economy?\nA: Well, people spend a huge amount of money on all sorts of leisure activities, so I'd say leisure is a genuinely important part of the economy in most countries. Leisure could be anything people do in their free time — eating out, going to the cinema, watching a football match, or staying in a hotel — and millions of people make a living from these industries. What I'd add, though, is that leisure spending is one of the first things households cut when money's tight, so it's also one of the more exposed parts of the economy."},{"en":"poorly paid","context":"Q: How does the economy benefit from people's leisure activities?\nA: The leisure industry makes a huge contribution to the economy. It keeps millions of people in employment, and those employees pay tax and spend their wages on other goods and services, so the money keeps moving round the economy rather than sitting still. Tourism works the same way but on a bigger scale, because it pulls in money from outside the country altogether. The one qualification I'd make is that a lot of leisure jobs are seasonal or poorly paid, so the economy benefits even when the people doing the work don't benefit all that much."},{"en":"more popular with one gender","context":"Q: Do men and women enjoy the same type of leisure activities?\nA: Yes and no. I think both men and women enjoy things like eating in restaurants or going to the cinema, but I'm sure there are other activities that are more popular with one gender. For example, I think more men than women go to football matches. I'd be careful about generalising, though, because those differences seem to be narrowing. Women's football has grown enormously in the last few years, and a lot of the differences people assume are natural turn out to be mostly habit."},{"en":"narrowing","context":"Q: Do men and women enjoy the same type of leisure activities?\nA: Yes and no. I think both men and women enjoy things like eating in restaurants or going to the cinema, but I'm sure there are other activities that are more popular with one gender. For example, I think more men than women go to football matches. I'd be careful about generalising, though, because those differences seem to be narrowing. Women's football has grown enormously in the last few years, and a lot of the differences people assume are natural turn out to be mostly habit."},{"en":"mostly habit","context":"Q: Do men and women enjoy the same type of leisure activities?\nA: Yes and no. I think both men and women enjoy things like eating in restaurants or going to the cinema, but I'm sure there are other activities that are more popular with one gender. For example, I think more men than women go to football matches. I'd be careful about generalising, though, because those differences seem to be narrowing. Women's football has grown enormously in the last few years, and a lot of the differences people assume are natural turn out to be mostly habit."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Leisure & Holidays","id":"speaking-part-3-061","_displayNo":"#061","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-061-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-061-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-061-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Leisure is an important part of the economy because people spend money on restaurants, films, sport and holidays. Those activities provide many jobs. However, this spending can fall quickly when families have less money, since entertainment is easier to cut than food or housing. So leisure supports the economy but can also be especially vulnerable during difficult periods.","phrases":[{"en":"vulnerable","meaning":"Easily affected or harmed by a problem."}],"question":"What is the relationship between leisure and the economy?","html":"Leisure is an important part of the economy because people spend money on restaurants, films, sport and holidays. Those activities provide many jobs. However, this spending can fall quickly when families have less money, since entertainment is easier to cut than food or housing. So leisure supports the economy but can also be especially <strong class=\"key-phrase\">vulnerable</strong> during difficult periods."},{"text":"Leisure businesses employ people who then spend their wages and pay taxes, so the money supports other parts of the economy too. Tourism also brings income from visitors outside the country. However, some jobs are seasonal or poorly paid. The industry may create wealth without giving every worker a secure or comfortable living.","phrases":[{"en":"seasonal","meaning":"Available or happening only during certain parts of the year."}],"question":"How does the economy benefit from people's leisure activities?","html":"Leisure businesses employ people who then spend their wages and pay taxes, so the money supports other parts of the economy too. Tourism also brings income from visitors outside the country. However, some jobs are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">seasonal</strong> or poorly paid. The industry may create wealth without giving every worker a secure or comfortable living."},{"text":"They share many interests, such as restaurants and cinema, although some activities attract more of one gender. Football matches have traditionally been an example. However, those differences are changing, and habits do not prove a natural preference. I would consider the individual rather than assume men and women must enjoy separate kinds of leisure.","phrases":[{"en":"gender","meaning":"Being male, female or another gender identity."}],"question":"Do men and women enjoy the same type of leisure activities?","html":"They share many interests, such as restaurants and cinema, although some activities attract more of one <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gender</strong>. Football matches have traditionally been an example. However, those differences are changing, and habits do not prove a natural preference. I would consider the individual rather than assume men and women must enjoy separate kinds of leisure."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-061-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-061-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-061-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Leisure time in the past and hobbies in the future","prompt":"Did people have more or less leisure time in the past?\nDo you think hobbies will change much in the future?","answer":"Q: Did people have more or less leisure time in the past?\nA: When my grandparents were young, I think they generally had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less leisure time</strong> than we do now. They didn&#x27;t have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the gadgets we take for granted</strong>, so ordinary tasks simply took longer — cooking a meal from scratch instead of using a microwave, washing clothes by hand, that sort of thing. Most people also worked six days a week, so whatever free time they had was mostly spent resting rather than doing anything organised. I&#x27;d say that&#x27;s a bit of a generalisation, though, because it depended enormously on money — a wealthier household with servants would have had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">plenty of leisure</strong> even back then.\n\nQ: Do you think hobbies will change much in the future?\nA: No, I don&#x27;t think hobbies will change all that much in the future — I&#x27;m sure people will still play games and sports much as they always have. Where I do think things are shifting is the Internet, which is changing the way we spend our free time. What I suspect will happen is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the old hobbies survive</strong> but move partly online: people still cook, garden and play music, but now they learn it from videos and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">share the results with strangers</strong>, which is genuinely new. The hobbies themselves probably won&#x27;t change; how we learn and show them off will.","topic":"Leisure & Holidays","phrases":[{"en":"less leisure time","context":"Q: Did people have more or less leisure time in the past?\nA: When my grandparents were young, I think they generally had less leisure time than we do now. They didn't have the gadgets we take for granted, so ordinary tasks simply took longer — cooking a meal from scratch instead of using a microwave, washing clothes by hand, that sort of thing. Most people also worked six days a week, so whatever free time they had was mostly spent resting rather than doing anything organised. I'd say that's a bit of a generalisation, though, because it depended enormously on money — a wealthier household with servants would have had plenty of leisure even back then."},{"en":"the gadgets we take for granted","context":"Q: Did people have more or less leisure time in the past?\nA: When my grandparents were young, I think they generally had less leisure time than we do now. They didn't have the gadgets we take for granted, so ordinary tasks simply took longer — cooking a meal from scratch instead of using a microwave, washing clothes by hand, that sort of thing. Most people also worked six days a week, so whatever free time they had was mostly spent resting rather than doing anything organised. I'd say that's a bit of a generalisation, though, because it depended enormously on money — a wealthier household with servants would have had plenty of leisure even back then."},{"en":"plenty of leisure","context":"Q: Did people have more or less leisure time in the past?\nA: When my grandparents were young, I think they generally had less leisure time than we do now. They didn't have the gadgets we take for granted, so ordinary tasks simply took longer — cooking a meal from scratch instead of using a microwave, washing clothes by hand, that sort of thing. Most people also worked six days a week, so whatever free time they had was mostly spent resting rather than doing anything organised. I'd say that's a bit of a generalisation, though, because it depended enormously on money — a wealthier household with servants would have had plenty of leisure even back then."},{"en":"the old hobbies survive","context":"Q: Do you think hobbies will change much in the future?\nA: No, I don't think hobbies will change all that much in the future — I'm sure people will still play games and sports much as they always have. Where I do think things are shifting is the Internet, which is changing the way we spend our free time. What I suspect will happen is that the old hobbies survive but move partly online: people still cook, garden and play music, but now they learn it from videos and share the results with strangers, which is genuinely new. The hobbies themselves probably won't change; how we learn and show them off will."},{"en":"share the results with strangers","context":"Q: Do you think hobbies will change much in the future?\nA: No, I don't think hobbies will change all that much in the future — I'm sure people will still play games and sports much as they always have. Where I do think things are shifting is the Internet, which is changing the way we spend our free time. What I suspect will happen is that the old hobbies survive but move partly online: people still cook, garden and play music, but now they learn it from videos and share the results with strangers, which is genuinely new. The hobbies themselves probably won't change; how we learn and show them off will."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Leisure & Holidays","id":"speaking-part-3-062","_displayNo":"#062","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-062-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-062-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Many people in my grandparents' generation had less free time. Household jobs took longer without modern appliances, and working six days a week was common. They often used the remaining time simply to rest. However, money made a large difference: wealthy families with paid help could enjoy much more leisure, so the past was not the same for everyone.","phrases":[{"en":"appliances","meaning":"Machines used for household tasks, such as washing or cooking."}],"question":"Did people have more or less leisure time in the past?","html":"Many people in my grandparents&#x27; generation had less free time. Household jobs took longer without modern <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appliances</strong>, and working six days a week was common. They often used the remaining time simply to rest. However, money made a large difference: wealthy families with paid help could enjoy much more leisure, so the past was not the same for everyone."},{"text":"The basic hobbies may stay similar: people will still cook, garden, play music and enjoy sport. The internet is changing how they learn and share those activities. Someone can now follow a video lesson or show a result to people worldwide. So I expect familiar hobbies to continue, but with different tools and social connections.","phrases":[{"en":"social connections","meaning":"Relationships or links with other people."}],"question":"Do you think hobbies will change much in the future?","html":"The basic hobbies may stay similar: people will still cook, garden, play music and enjoy sport. The internet is changing how they learn and share those activities. Someone can now follow a video lesson or show a result to people worldwide. So I expect familiar hobbies to continue, but with different tools and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">social connections</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-062-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-062-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Morning or evening people, and what evenings are for","prompt":"Are you a morning or evening type of person?\nWhat activities do people often do in the evening in your country?\nIs evening a good time for doing exercises?","answer":"Q: Are you a morning or evening type of person?\nA: Definitely a morning person. My concentration&#x27;s sharpest in the first few hours after waking, before messages start arriving and the day fills up, so that&#x27;s when I try to do anything demanding. By the evening I&#x27;m fine for routine tasks but not for anything requiring real thought — I&#x27;ve tried working late plenty of times and it never lasts more than a few days, because I get the hours in but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the quality drops noticeably</strong>. I know plenty of genuine night owls whose brains work the opposite way, though, so I wouldn&#x27;t say it&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a universal rule</strong>.\n\nQ: What activities do people often do in the evening in your country?\nA: Eating together is probably the main one — dinner&#x27;s still a family occasion in most households, far more so than lunch. After that it varies quite a bit. Loads of people exercise, either at a gym or walking and jogging in parks, which stay busy surprisingly late. Younger people meet friends at cafes, and shopping streets are often livelier in the evening than during the day. For working adults in big cities, evening is realistically <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the only personal time they get</strong>, though that&#x27;s starting to change now that more people work from home and can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">grab an hour during the day</strong> instead.\n\nQ: Is evening a good time for doing exercises?\nA: For most people, yes — often it&#x27;s the only time available, and exercise you actually do beats exercise you theoretically should do at six in the morning before work. The one caution is intensity. Hard training raises your body temperature and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaves you too alert to sleep</strong>, so I&#x27;d finish anything intense at least an hour or two before bed. Anything moderate, like a walk or gentle swim, is fine right up until bedtime, but leave a proper gap to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wind down</strong> afterwards.","topic":"Leisure & Holidays","phrases":[{"en":"the quality drops noticeably","context":"Q: Are you a morning or evening type of person?\nA: Definitely a morning person. My concentration's sharpest in the first few hours after waking, before messages start arriving and the day fills up, so that's when I try to do anything demanding. By the evening I'm fine for routine tasks but not for anything requiring real thought — I've tried working late plenty of times and it never lasts more than a few days, because I get the hours in but the quality drops noticeably. I know plenty of genuine night owls whose brains work the opposite way, though, so I wouldn't say it's a universal rule."},{"en":"a universal rule","context":"Q: Are you a morning or evening type of person?\nA: Definitely a morning person. My concentration's sharpest in the first few hours after waking, before messages start arriving and the day fills up, so that's when I try to do anything demanding. By the evening I'm fine for routine tasks but not for anything requiring real thought — I've tried working late plenty of times and it never lasts more than a few days, because I get the hours in but the quality drops noticeably. I know plenty of genuine night owls whose brains work the opposite way, though, so I wouldn't say it's a universal rule."},{"en":"the only personal time they get","context":"Q: What activities do people often do in the evening in your country?\nA: Eating together is probably the main one — dinner's still a family occasion in most households, far more so than lunch. After that it varies quite a bit. Loads of people exercise, either at a gym or walking and jogging in parks, which stay busy surprisingly late. Younger people meet friends at cafes, and shopping streets are often livelier in the evening than during the day. For working adults in big cities, evening is realistically the only personal time they get, though that's starting to change now that more people work from home and can grab an hour during the day instead."},{"en":"grab an hour during the day","context":"Q: What activities do people often do in the evening in your country?\nA: Eating together is probably the main one — dinner's still a family occasion in most households, far more so than lunch. After that it varies quite a bit. Loads of people exercise, either at a gym or walking and jogging in parks, which stay busy surprisingly late. Younger people meet friends at cafes, and shopping streets are often livelier in the evening than during the day. For working adults in big cities, evening is realistically the only personal time they get, though that's starting to change now that more people work from home and can grab an hour during the day instead."},{"en":"leaves you too alert to sleep","context":"Q: Is evening a good time for doing exercises?\nA: For most people, yes — often it's the only time available, and exercise you actually do beats exercise you theoretically should do at six in the morning before work. The one caution is intensity. Hard training raises your body temperature and leaves you too alert to sleep, so I'd finish anything intense at least an hour or two before bed. Anything moderate, like a walk or gentle swim, is fine right up until bedtime, but leave a proper gap to wind down afterwards."},{"en":"wind down","context":"Q: Is evening a good time for doing exercises?\nA: For most people, yes — often it's the only time available, and exercise you actually do beats exercise you theoretically should do at six in the morning before work. The one caution is intensity. Hard training raises your body temperature and leaves you too alert to sleep, so I'd finish anything intense at least an hour or two before bed. Anything moderate, like a walk or gentle swim, is fine right up until bedtime, but leave a proper gap to wind down afterwards."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Leisure & Holidays","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-063","_displayNo":"#063","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-063-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-063-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-063-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I'm a morning person because I concentrate best soon after waking, before messages and other demands build up. I try to do difficult work then and leave simpler tasks for the evening. Working late gives me more hours but poorer results. However, some people genuinely work better at night, so my routine would not suit everyone.","phrases":[{"en":"demands","meaning":"Things requiring your time, effort or attention."}],"question":"Are you a morning or evening type of person?","html":"I&#x27;m a morning person because I concentrate best soon after waking, before messages and other <strong class=\"key-phrase\">demands</strong> build up. I try to do difficult work then and leave simpler tasks for the evening. Working late gives me more hours but poorer results. However, some people genuinely work better at night, so my routine would not suit everyone."},{"text":"Many families eat dinner together, then people exercise, walk in parks or meet friends at cafes. Shopping areas can also become busy. For many workers, evening is their main personal time after a full day. However, people working from home may have more freedom to take breaks earlier, so patterns depend on work and household arrangements.","phrases":[{"en":"household arrangements","meaning":"The way people organise living and responsibilities at home."}],"question":"What activities do people often do in the evening in your country?","html":"Many families eat dinner together, then people exercise, walk in parks or meet friends at cafes. Shopping areas can also become busy. For many workers, evening is their main personal time after a full day. However, people working from home may have more freedom to take breaks earlier, so patterns depend on work and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">household arrangements</strong>."},{"text":"Yes, if it is the time people can manage regularly. Doing exercise in the evening is more useful than planning a morning session that never happens. However, very hard training close to bedtime may make it difficult to relax. I would leave time to calm down afterwards and choose gentler activity when it is already late.","phrases":[{"en":"calm down","meaning":"Become more relaxed and less physically or mentally excited."}],"question":"Is evening a good time for doing exercises?","html":"Yes, if it is the time people can manage regularly. Doing exercise in the evening is more useful than planning a morning session that never happens. However, very hard training close to bedtime may make it difficult to relax. I would leave time to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">calm down</strong> afterwards and choose gentler activity when it is already late."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-063-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-063-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-063-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Television as relaxation, and leisure against stress","prompt":"Is watching television a good way of relaxing?\nWhat leisure activities would you recommend as a way of combating stress?\nDo young people get enough physical exercise these days?","answer":"Q: Is watching television a good way of relaxing?\nA: It can be, particularly after a mentally demanding day when you want something that requires no effort. But I&#x27;d distinguish between choosing a programme and just leaving the television on. Deliberate viewing for an hour is genuinely restful, whereas <strong class=\"key-phrase\">automatic viewing</strong> for four hours tends to leave people feeling worse — you&#x27;ve lost the evening and you&#x27;re not actually rested. The same applies to scrolling on a phone, which has largely replaced it for younger people, so the habit hasn&#x27;t disappeared, it&#x27;s just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">moved to a smaller screen</strong>.\n\nQ: What leisure activities would you recommend as a way of combating stress?\nA: Anything physical first of all — walking, swimming, cycling — because exercise <strong class=\"key-phrase\">works on the body&#x27;s stress response directly</strong> rather than merely distracting you from it. Beyond that, I&#x27;d add anything that needs enough concentration to occupy your mind, like cooking, gardening or playing an instrument, plus anything social, since isolation makes stress considerably worse. The most important thing, though, is that it&#x27;s something the person will actually keep doing every week rather than in theory, because an activity that feels like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a chore</strong> rarely survives a busy week.\n\nQ: Do young people get enough physical exercise these days?\nA: Most don&#x27;t, and I don&#x27;t think it&#x27;s mainly laziness. Study takes longer than it used to, cities aren&#x27;t really built for walking or cycling safely, and screens are simply more available. The activity that used to happen automatically — walking to school, playing outside — has been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">designed out of daily life</strong>. So it now has to be provided deliberately, and schools that only offer competitive team sports <strong class=\"key-phrase\">miss most of the pupils who need it</strong>. The exception is schools that also offer non-competitive options like dance or climbing — they tend to reach far more of the class.","topic":"Leisure & Holidays","phrases":[{"en":"automatic viewing","context":"Q: Is watching television a good way of relaxing?\nA: It can be, particularly after a mentally demanding day when you want something that requires no effort. But I'd distinguish between choosing a programme and just leaving the television on. Deliberate viewing for an hour is genuinely restful, whereas automatic viewing for four hours tends to leave people feeling worse — you've lost the evening and you're not actually rested. The same applies to scrolling on a phone, which has largely replaced it for younger people, so the habit hasn't disappeared, it's just moved to a smaller screen."},{"en":"moved to a smaller screen","context":"Q: Is watching television a good way of relaxing?\nA: It can be, particularly after a mentally demanding day when you want something that requires no effort. But I'd distinguish between choosing a programme and just leaving the television on. Deliberate viewing for an hour is genuinely restful, whereas automatic viewing for four hours tends to leave people feeling worse — you've lost the evening and you're not actually rested. The same applies to scrolling on a phone, which has largely replaced it for younger people, so the habit hasn't disappeared, it's just moved to a smaller screen."},{"en":"works on the body's stress response directly","context":"Q: What leisure activities would you recommend as a way of combating stress?\nA: Anything physical first of all — walking, swimming, cycling — because exercise works on the body's stress response directly rather than merely distracting you from it. Beyond that, I'd add anything that needs enough concentration to occupy your mind, like cooking, gardening or playing an instrument, plus anything social, since isolation makes stress considerably worse. The most important thing, though, is that it's something the person will actually keep doing every week rather than in theory, because an activity that feels like a chore rarely survives a busy week."},{"en":"a chore","context":"Q: What leisure activities would you recommend as a way of combating stress?\nA: Anything physical first of all — walking, swimming, cycling — because exercise works on the body's stress response directly rather than merely distracting you from it. Beyond that, I'd add anything that needs enough concentration to occupy your mind, like cooking, gardening or playing an instrument, plus anything social, since isolation makes stress considerably worse. The most important thing, though, is that it's something the person will actually keep doing every week rather than in theory, because an activity that feels like a chore rarely survives a busy week."},{"en":"designed out of daily life","context":"Q: Do young people get enough physical exercise these days?\nA: Most don't, and I don't think it's mainly laziness. Study takes longer than it used to, cities aren't really built for walking or cycling safely, and screens are simply more available. The activity that used to happen automatically — walking to school, playing outside — has been designed out of daily life. So it now has to be provided deliberately, and schools that only offer competitive team sports miss most of the pupils who need it. The exception is schools that also offer non-competitive options like dance or climbing — they tend to reach far more of the class."},{"en":"miss most of the pupils who need it","context":"Q: Do young people get enough physical exercise these days?\nA: Most don't, and I don't think it's mainly laziness. Study takes longer than it used to, cities aren't really built for walking or cycling safely, and screens are simply more available. The activity that used to happen automatically — walking to school, playing outside — has been designed out of daily life. So it now has to be provided deliberately, and schools that only offer competitive team sports miss most of the pupils who need it. The exception is schools that also offer non-competitive options like dance or climbing — they tend to reach far more of the class."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Leisure & Holidays","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-064","_displayNo":"#064","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-064-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-064-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-064-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"It can be helpful after a tiring day, especially if you choose a programme you enjoy. However, watching deliberately for an hour is different from leaving the TV on all evening. Long, automatic viewing may waste time without leaving you rested. The same problem can happen with phones, so the way we use screens matters more than the device.","phrases":[{"en":"deliberately","meaning":"With a clear purpose or conscious choice."}],"question":"Is watching television a good way of relaxing?","html":"It can be helpful after a tiring day, especially if you choose a programme you enjoy. However, watching <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deliberately</strong> for an hour is different from leaving the TV on all evening. Long, automatic viewing may waste time without leaving you rested. The same problem can happen with phones, so the way we use screens matters more than the device."},{"text":"Physical activities such as walking, swimming and cycling can help. Cooking, gardening or music may also work because they hold your attention, while social activities reduce loneliness. The best choice is something you actually enjoy and will repeat. If it feels like another duty, it is less likely to continue when life becomes busy.","phrases":[{"en":"loneliness","meaning":"The unhappy feeling of lacking the company or connection you want."}],"question":"What leisure activities would you recommend as a way of combating stress?","html":"Physical activities such as walking, swimming and cycling can help. Cooking, gardening or music may also work because they hold your attention, while social activities reduce <strong class=\"key-phrase\">loneliness</strong>. The best choice is something you actually enjoy and will repeat. If it feels like another duty, it is less likely to continue when life becomes busy."},{"text":"Many do not, but it is not simply because they are lazy. Study, unsafe streets and easy access to screens reduce natural opportunities to move. Schools need to provide activity deliberately. Offering only competitive team games leaves some pupils out, so choices such as dance or climbing may help more young people find something they enjoy.","phrases":[{"en":"competitive","meaning":"Involving an effort to win or perform better than others."}],"question":"Do young people get enough physical exercise these days?","html":"Many do not, but it is not simply because they are lazy. Study, unsafe streets and easy access to screens reduce natural opportunities to move. Schools need to provide activity deliberately. Offering only <strong class=\"key-phrase\">competitive</strong> team games leaves some pupils out, so choices such as dance or climbing may help more young people find something they enjoy."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-064-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-064-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-064-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Superstition and what people believe","prompt":"Do you think people in your country are superstitious?\nCan you describe a superstition that they believe in?\nDo you think people are less superstitious than they used to be?","answer":"Q: Do you think people in your country are superstitious?\nA: To a degree, yes, though it varies a lot by generation. Older people tend to take certain beliefs seriously, particularly around dates, numbers and important decisions, whereas younger people tend to treat the same customs as tradition rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">literal belief</strong> — they&#x27;ll follow them at a wedding without genuinely thinking that ignoring them would cause misfortune. It&#x27;s not strictly an age thing, though; what I&#x27;ve noticed is that superstition strengthens whenever the stakes are high and the outcome&#x27;s outside your control, whatever generation you&#x27;re from.\n\nQ: Can you describe a superstition that they believe in?\nA: The most widespread one&#x27;s probably to do with numbers. Certain numbers are considered lucky and others unlucky, largely because of how they sound in the language, and it has real practical consequences: people will pay significantly more for a phone number or a house number with the right digits, and buildings sometimes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">skip unlucky floor numbers</strong> entirely. What&#x27;s interesting is that plenty of the people who do this will happily admit there&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no rational basis</strong> for it — they just don&#x27;t want to take the risk.\n\nQ: Do you think people are less superstitious than they used to be?\nA: Less so in the literal sense, yes — education and access to scientific explanation mean fewer people genuinely believe a ritual will change an outcome. But it hasn&#x27;t disappeared so much as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changed function</strong>; it survives as cultural habit and emotional comfort more than actual belief. You see it clearly with students before exams, or athletes with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pre-match routines</strong>. They don&#x27;t really believe it works; it just reduces anxiety when they can&#x27;t control what happens next.","topic":"Luck & Superstition","phrases":[{"en":"literal belief","context":"Q: Do you think people in your country are superstitious?\nA: To a degree, yes, though it varies a lot by generation. Older people tend to take certain beliefs seriously, particularly around dates, numbers and important decisions, whereas younger people tend to treat the same customs as tradition rather than literal belief — they'll follow them at a wedding without genuinely thinking that ignoring them would cause misfortune. It's not strictly an age thing, though; what I've noticed is that superstition strengthens whenever the stakes are high and the outcome's outside your control, whatever generation you're from."},{"en":"skip unlucky floor numbers","context":"Q: Can you describe a superstition that they believe in?\nA: The most widespread one's probably to do with numbers. Certain numbers are considered lucky and others unlucky, largely because of how they sound in the language, and it has real practical consequences: people will pay significantly more for a phone number or a house number with the right digits, and buildings sometimes skip unlucky floor numbers entirely. What's interesting is that plenty of the people who do this will happily admit there's no rational basis for it — they just don't want to take the risk."},{"en":"no rational basis","context":"Q: Can you describe a superstition that they believe in?\nA: The most widespread one's probably to do with numbers. Certain numbers are considered lucky and others unlucky, largely because of how they sound in the language, and it has real practical consequences: people will pay significantly more for a phone number or a house number with the right digits, and buildings sometimes skip unlucky floor numbers entirely. What's interesting is that plenty of the people who do this will happily admit there's no rational basis for it — they just don't want to take the risk."},{"en":"changed function","context":"Q: Do you think people are less superstitious than they used to be?\nA: Less so in the literal sense, yes — education and access to scientific explanation mean fewer people genuinely believe a ritual will change an outcome. But it hasn't disappeared so much as changed function; it survives as cultural habit and emotional comfort more than actual belief. You see it clearly with students before exams, or athletes with pre-match routines. They don't really believe it works; it just reduces anxiety when they can't control what happens next."},{"en":"pre-match routines","context":"Q: Do you think people are less superstitious than they used to be?\nA: Less so in the literal sense, yes — education and access to scientific explanation mean fewer people genuinely believe a ritual will change an outcome. But it hasn't disappeared so much as changed function; it survives as cultural habit and emotional comfort more than actual belief. You see it clearly with students before exams, or athletes with pre-match routines. They don't really believe it works; it just reduces anxiety when they can't control what happens next."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Luck & Superstition","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-065","_displayNo":"#065","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-065-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-065-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-065-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Some are, especially around dates, numbers and important decisions. Older people may take these beliefs more literally, while younger people often follow them as traditions. However, superstition can affect any age when the result matters and feels outside our control. In those moments, a familiar custom may provide comfort even when someone doubts that it really works.","phrases":[{"en":"superstition","meaning":"A belief that certain actions or objects bring luck without a clear logical basis."}],"question":"Do you think people in your country are superstitious?","html":"Some are, especially around dates, numbers and important decisions. Older people may take these beliefs more literally, while younger people often follow them as traditions. However, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">superstition</strong> can affect any age when the result matters and feels outside our control. In those moments, a familiar custom may provide comfort even when someone doubts that it really works."},{"text":"Beliefs about lucky and unlucky numbers are common. Their sounds can suggest positive or negative meanings in the language. People may pay extra for a particular phone number or avoid certain floor numbers. Some admit there is no logical evidence, but still prefer following the custom rather than feeling they have taken an unnecessary chance.","phrases":[{"en":"logical evidence","meaning":"Facts or reasoning that clearly support an idea."}],"question":"Can you describe a superstition that they believe in?","html":"Beliefs about lucky and unlucky numbers are common. Their sounds can suggest positive or negative meanings in the language. People may pay extra for a particular phone number or avoid certain floor numbers. Some admit there is no <strong class=\"key-phrase\">logical evidence</strong>, but still prefer following the custom rather than feeling they have taken an unnecessary chance."},{"text":"Probably fewer people believe a ritual directly changes an outcome, because education offers more scientific explanations. However, the customs have not disappeared. They may now provide comfort or a sense of tradition. Students and athletes can follow routines before important events without fully believing in them, simply because doing so makes them feel less anxious.","phrases":[{"en":"ritual","meaning":"A repeated action performed in a particular way for a special reason."}],"question":"Do you think people are less superstitious than they used to be?","html":"Probably fewer people believe a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ritual</strong> directly changes an outcome, because education offers more scientific explanations. However, the customs have not disappeared. They may now provide comfort or a sense of tradition. Students and athletes can follow routines before important events without fully believing in them, simply because doing so makes them feel less anxious."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-065-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-065-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-065-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Why saving matters and teaching children about money","prompt":"Why is it important for people to save some of their money?\nIn your view, should children be taught how to manage money?\nDo you think that girls are better at saving money than boys?","answer":"Q: Why is it important for people to save some of their money?\nA: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">In the long term</strong> I think it&#x27;s important to save for retirement, but it&#x27;s also a good idea to have money set aside so you can deal with unexpected problems sooner than that. For example, I&#x27;ve learned to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put some money aside</strong> for car repairs, and that came in useful recently when I needed my car&#x27;s brake pads changed — if I hadn&#x27;t had savings, the bill could have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sent me into the red</strong>. The exception, I think, is people on very low incomes, where telling them to save doesn&#x27;t help much if there&#x27;s nothing left over at the end of the month.\n\nQ: In your view, should children be taught how to manage money?\nA: Not at school, no — I think it&#x27;s mainly the job of parents to teach children that gradually, and a lot of it they pick up naturally just by growing up around money. I remember being given money for sweets or an ice cream as a kid, and I soon learned <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what I could afford</strong> and what I couldn&#x27;t. Where I&#x27;d make an exception is the more technical stuff — interest, credit, pensions — because that genuinely is easier to learn properly in a classroom than to pick up by trial and error.\n\nQ: Do you think that girls are better at saving money than boys?\nA: I&#x27;ve never really thought about that before, and I don&#x27;t see any obvious reason why girls would be better savers than boys, or the other way round. I suppose it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depends more on</strong> the individual&#x27;s personality than their gender — a child who likes cheap toys might not think about saving at all, whereas one who wants something expensive, like a bike or a games console, might <strong class=\"key-phrase\">summon the willpower</strong> to save up! If there is a pattern, I&#x27;d guess it&#x27;s more about how parents treat sons and daughters differently around money than anything to do with the children themselves.","topic":"Money","phrases":[{"en":"In the long term","context":"Q: Why is it important for people to save some of their money?\nA: In the long term I think it's important to save for retirement, but it's also a good idea to have money set aside so you can deal with unexpected problems sooner than that. For example, I've learned to put some money aside for car repairs, and that came in useful recently when I needed my car's brake pads changed — if I hadn't had savings, the bill could have sent me into the red. The exception, I think, is people on very low incomes, where telling them to save doesn't help much if there's nothing left over at the end of the month."},{"en":"put some money aside","context":"Q: Why is it important for people to save some of their money?\nA: In the long term I think it's important to save for retirement, but it's also a good idea to have money set aside so you can deal with unexpected problems sooner than that. For example, I've learned to put some money aside for car repairs, and that came in useful recently when I needed my car's brake pads changed — if I hadn't had savings, the bill could have sent me into the red. The exception, I think, is people on very low incomes, where telling them to save doesn't help much if there's nothing left over at the end of the month."},{"en":"sent me into the red","context":"Q: Why is it important for people to save some of their money?\nA: In the long term I think it's important to save for retirement, but it's also a good idea to have money set aside so you can deal with unexpected problems sooner than that. For example, I've learned to put some money aside for car repairs, and that came in useful recently when I needed my car's brake pads changed — if I hadn't had savings, the bill could have sent me into the red. The exception, I think, is people on very low incomes, where telling them to save doesn't help much if there's nothing left over at the end of the month."},{"en":"what I could afford","context":"Q: In your view, should children be taught how to manage money?\nA: Not at school, no — I think it's mainly the job of parents to teach children that gradually, and a lot of it they pick up naturally just by growing up around money. I remember being given money for sweets or an ice cream as a kid, and I soon learned what I could afford and what I couldn't. Where I'd make an exception is the more technical stuff — interest, credit, pensions — because that genuinely is easier to learn properly in a classroom than to pick up by trial and error."},{"en":"depends more on","context":"Q: Do you think that girls are better at saving money than boys?\nA: I've never really thought about that before, and I don't see any obvious reason why girls would be better savers than boys, or the other way round. I suppose it depends more on the individual's personality than their gender — a child who likes cheap toys might not think about saving at all, whereas one who wants something expensive, like a bike or a games console, might summon the willpower to save up! If there is a pattern, I'd guess it's more about how parents treat sons and daughters differently around money than anything to do with the children themselves."},{"en":"summon the willpower","context":"Q: Do you think that girls are better at saving money than boys?\nA: I've never really thought about that before, and I don't see any obvious reason why girls would be better savers than boys, or the other way round. I suppose it depends more on the individual's personality than their gender — a child who likes cheap toys might not think about saving at all, whereas one who wants something expensive, like a bike or a games console, might summon the willpower to save up! If there is a pattern, I'd guess it's more about how parents treat sons and daughters differently around money than anything to do with the children themselves."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Money","id":"speaking-part-3-066","_displayNo":"#066","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-066-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-066-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-066-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Savings help with retirement and unexpected expenses, such as car repairs. Having money ready can prevent a sudden bill from becoming debt; that helped me when my brakes needed work. However, saving requires some money left after essentials. People on very low incomes may not have that choice, so telling them to save is not always useful.","phrases":[{"en":"unexpected expenses","meaning":"Costs you did not know or plan would arise."}],"question":"Why is it important for people to save some of their money?","html":"Savings help with retirement and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unexpected expenses</strong>, such as car repairs. Having money ready can prevent a sudden bill from becoming debt; that helped me when my brakes needed work. However, saving requires some money left after essentials. People on very low incomes may not have that choice, so telling them to save is not always useful."},{"text":"Yes, but I think parents should teach most everyday money habits gradually. Pocket money can show children what they can afford and what they need to save for. Schools are more useful for technical subjects such as interest, borrowing and pensions. Those are better explained clearly than learned only by making expensive mistakes later.","phrases":[{"en":"interest","meaning":"Extra money paid for borrowing money or earned on some savings."}],"question":"In your view, should children be taught how to manage money?","html":"Yes, but I think parents should teach most everyday money habits gradually. Pocket money can show children what they can afford and what they need to save for. Schools are more useful for technical subjects such as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">interest</strong>, borrowing and pensions. Those are better explained clearly than learned only by making expensive mistakes later."},{"text":"I don't see a clear reason why either group would naturally save better. Personality and goals seem more important. A child wanting an expensive bicycle may save carefully, while another has no reason to wait. If boys and girls behave differently, that may also reflect how adults teach or treat them around money.","phrases":[{"en":"reflect","meaning":"Show or be influenced by something."}],"question":"Do you think that girls are better at saving money than boys?","html":"I don&#x27;t see a clear reason why either group would naturally save better. Personality and goals seem more important. A child wanting an expensive bicycle may save carefully, while another has no reason to wait. If boys and girls behave differently, that may also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reflect</strong> how adults teach or treat them around money."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-066-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-066-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-066-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Reacting to a situation and accounting for it","prompt":"Do you think other people would feel the same in that situation?\nHave you had a similar experience since then?\nHow would you account for the popularity of credit cards?","answer":"Q: Do you think other people would feel the same in that situation?\nA: Most people would, I think — the embarrassment comes from it happening in front of others rather than from the money itself. Being unable to pay in a queue makes you feel judged, even though the cause is usually something trivial, like a technical fault, rather than someone actually being broke. What varies is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">how long the feeling lasts</strong>, though: if it&#x27;s a one-off, people forget it within the day, but if it reflects <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine financial difficulty</strong>, the anxiety doesn&#x27;t go away nearly as quickly.\n\nQ: Have you had a similar experience since then?\nA: Minor versions, yes — a card that wouldn&#x27;t read, or a payment app failing when I had no cash on me as a backup. Nothing serious, but enough to change my habits a little. I now carry a small amount of cash and keep <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a second payment method</strong> on me, which probably sounds excessive in a country where almost everyone pays by phone these days. It&#x27;s less about the money, really, than about not wanting to be caught out in front of a queue of people.\n\nQ: How would you account for the popularity of credit cards?\nA: Convenience, mainly — they&#x27;re accepted almost everywhere, work internationally, and mean you don&#x27;t have to carry cash around. The protections matter too: if a purchase goes wrong, or a card&#x27;s used fraudulently, you can usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get the money back</strong>, which you can&#x27;t with cash, and rewards or cashback add a further incentive on top. The danger is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">separating the purchase from the payment</strong> makes spending feel less real, and interest on unpaid balances is genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">punishing</strong>.","topic":"Money","phrases":[{"en":"how long the feeling lasts","context":"Q: Do you think other people would feel the same in that situation?\nA: Most people would, I think — the embarrassment comes from it happening in front of others rather than from the money itself. Being unable to pay in a queue makes you feel judged, even though the cause is usually something trivial, like a technical fault, rather than someone actually being broke. What varies is how long the feeling lasts, though: if it's a one-off, people forget it within the day, but if it reflects a genuine financial difficulty, the anxiety doesn't go away nearly as quickly."},{"en":"a genuine financial difficulty","context":"Q: Do you think other people would feel the same in that situation?\nA: Most people would, I think — the embarrassment comes from it happening in front of others rather than from the money itself. Being unable to pay in a queue makes you feel judged, even though the cause is usually something trivial, like a technical fault, rather than someone actually being broke. What varies is how long the feeling lasts, though: if it's a one-off, people forget it within the day, but if it reflects a genuine financial difficulty, the anxiety doesn't go away nearly as quickly."},{"en":"a second payment method","context":"Q: Have you had a similar experience since then?\nA: Minor versions, yes — a card that wouldn't read, or a payment app failing when I had no cash on me as a backup. Nothing serious, but enough to change my habits a little. I now carry a small amount of cash and keep a second payment method on me, which probably sounds excessive in a country where almost everyone pays by phone these days. It's less about the money, really, than about not wanting to be caught out in front of a queue of people."},{"en":"get the money back","context":"Q: How would you account for the popularity of credit cards?\nA: Convenience, mainly — they're accepted almost everywhere, work internationally, and mean you don't have to carry cash around. The protections matter too: if a purchase goes wrong, or a card's used fraudulently, you can usually get the money back, which you can't with cash, and rewards or cashback add a further incentive on top. The danger is that separating the purchase from the payment makes spending feel less real, and interest on unpaid balances is genuinely punishing."},{"en":"separating the purchase from the payment","context":"Q: How would you account for the popularity of credit cards?\nA: Convenience, mainly — they're accepted almost everywhere, work internationally, and mean you don't have to carry cash around. The protections matter too: if a purchase goes wrong, or a card's used fraudulently, you can usually get the money back, which you can't with cash, and rewards or cashback add a further incentive on top. The danger is that separating the purchase from the payment makes spending feel less real, and interest on unpaid balances is genuinely punishing."},{"en":"punishing","context":"Q: How would you account for the popularity of credit cards?\nA: Convenience, mainly — they're accepted almost everywhere, work internationally, and mean you don't have to carry cash around. The protections matter too: if a purchase goes wrong, or a card's used fraudulently, you can usually get the money back, which you can't with cash, and rewards or cashback add a further incentive on top. The danger is that separating the purchase from the payment makes spending feel less real, and interest on unpaid balances is genuinely punishing."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Money","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-067","_displayNo":"#067","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-067-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-067-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-067-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I think most people would feel embarrassed if they could not pay while others waited. The problem may be a technical fault rather than lack of money, but it can still feel as if people are judging you. A one-off incident is soon forgotten; if it connects with real money worries, the anxiety may last much longer.","phrases":[{"en":"one-off incident","meaning":"Something that happens once rather than repeatedly."}],"question":"Do you think other people would feel the same in that situation?","html":"I think most people would feel embarrassed if they could not pay while others waited. The problem may be a technical fault rather than lack of money, but it can still feel as if people are judging you. A <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one-off incident</strong> is soon forgotten; if it connects with real money worries, the anxiety may last much longer."},{"text":"Yes, small payment problems have happened again, such as a card or app not working when I had no cash. They were not serious, but they changed my habits. I now carry some cash and another way to pay. It is mainly to avoid feeling stuck in front of a queue, rather than a concern about the amount.","phrases":[{"en":"another way to pay","meaning":"A different payment method available if the first fails."}],"question":"Have you had a similar experience since then?","html":"Yes, small payment problems have happened again, such as a card or app not working when I had no cash. They were not serious, but they changed my habits. I now carry some cash and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">another way to pay</strong>. It is mainly to avoid feeling stuck in front of a queue, rather than a concern about the amount."},{"text":"Credit cards are convenient, widely accepted and useful abroad. Some also offer protection if a purchase goes wrong, along with rewards or cashback. However, paying later can make spending feel less real. If users leave balances unpaid, interest can become expensive. Their popularity is understandable, but the convenience works best when spending remains under control.","phrases":[{"en":"under control","meaning":"Managed so it does not become excessive or harmful."}],"question":"How would you account for the popularity of credit cards?","html":"Credit cards are convenient, widely accepted and useful abroad. Some also offer protection if a purchase goes wrong, along with rewards or cashback. However, paying later can make spending feel less real. If users leave balances unpaid, interest can become expensive. Their popularity is understandable, but the convenience works best when spending remains <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under control</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-067-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-067-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-067-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Museums and art galleries, and recommending one","prompt":"Do you like museums/ art galleries?\nWould you recommend this museum to other people?","answer":"Q: Do you like museums/ art galleries?\nA: I do, particularly ones that explain the context rather than just displaying objects behind glass. A case of pottery doesn&#x27;t mean much to me on its own, but if the exhibition explains who made it, why, and what daily life was like at the time, an abstract historical period suddenly becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a tangible human story</strong>. I find I remember those visits years afterwards. The ones I enjoy least, though, are the sort that assume you already know the period and don&#x27;t bother explaining the basics.\n\nQ: Would you recommend this museum to other people?\nA: Yes, I would. The collection&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">well organised</strong> and the labelling&#x27;s written for non-specialists, so you don&#x27;t need any <strong class=\"key-phrase\">background knowledge</strong> to follow it. It also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strikes a good balance</strong> — there&#x27;s enough depth for someone genuinely interested, but it&#x27;s not so dense that an ordinary visitor leaves feeling <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overwhelmed</strong>. I&#x27;d suggest going on a weekday morning, though, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">it gets extremely crowded</strong> at weekends, and two hours is about right for a first visit if you go when it&#x27;s quiet.","topic":"Museums","phrases":[{"en":"a tangible human story","context":"Q: Do you like museums/ art galleries?\nA: I do, particularly ones that explain the context rather than just displaying objects behind glass. A case of pottery doesn't mean much to me on its own, but if the exhibition explains who made it, why, and what daily life was like at the time, an abstract historical period suddenly becomes a tangible human story. I find I remember those visits years afterwards. The ones I enjoy least, though, are the sort that assume you already know the period and don't bother explaining the basics."},{"en":"well organised","context":"Q: Would you recommend this museum to other people?\nA: Yes, I would. The collection's well organised and the labelling's written for non-specialists, so you don't need any background knowledge to follow it. It also strikes a good balance — there's enough depth for someone genuinely interested, but it's not so dense that an ordinary visitor leaves feeling overwhelmed. I'd suggest going on a weekday morning, though, because it gets extremely crowded at weekends, and two hours is about right for a first visit if you go when it's quiet."},{"en":"background knowledge","context":"Q: Would you recommend this museum to other people?\nA: Yes, I would. The collection's well organised and the labelling's written for non-specialists, so you don't need any background knowledge to follow it. It also strikes a good balance — there's enough depth for someone genuinely interested, but it's not so dense that an ordinary visitor leaves feeling overwhelmed. I'd suggest going on a weekday morning, though, because it gets extremely crowded at weekends, and two hours is about right for a first visit if you go when it's quiet."},{"en":"strikes a good balance","context":"Q: Would you recommend this museum to other people?\nA: Yes, I would. The collection's well organised and the labelling's written for non-specialists, so you don't need any background knowledge to follow it. It also strikes a good balance — there's enough depth for someone genuinely interested, but it's not so dense that an ordinary visitor leaves feeling overwhelmed. I'd suggest going on a weekday morning, though, because it gets extremely crowded at weekends, and two hours is about right for a first visit if you go when it's quiet."},{"en":"overwhelmed","context":"Q: Would you recommend this museum to other people?\nA: Yes, I would. The collection's well organised and the labelling's written for non-specialists, so you don't need any background knowledge to follow it. It also strikes a good balance — there's enough depth for someone genuinely interested, but it's not so dense that an ordinary visitor leaves feeling overwhelmed. I'd suggest going on a weekday morning, though, because it gets extremely crowded at weekends, and two hours is about right for a first visit if you go when it's quiet."},{"en":"it gets extremely crowded","context":"Q: Would you recommend this museum to other people?\nA: Yes, I would. The collection's well organised and the labelling's written for non-specialists, so you don't need any background knowledge to follow it. It also strikes a good balance — there's enough depth for someone genuinely interested, but it's not so dense that an ordinary visitor leaves feeling overwhelmed. I'd suggest going on a weekday morning, though, because it gets extremely crowded at weekends, and two hours is about right for a first visit if you go when it's quiet."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Museums","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-068","_displayNo":"#068","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-068-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-068-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, especially when exhibitions explain the people and daily life behind objects. A pot becomes much more interesting if I understand who made it and why. That turns a distant historical period into a human story. I enjoy museums less when labels assume visitors already know the background and leave basic questions unanswered.","phrases":[{"en":"background","meaning":"The information needed to understand a subject or event."}],"question":"Do you like museums/ art galleries?","html":"Yes, especially when exhibitions explain the people and daily life behind objects. A pot becomes much more interesting if I understand who made it and why. That turns a distant historical period into a human story. I enjoy museums less when labels assume visitors already know the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">background</strong> and leave basic questions unanswered."},{"text":"Yes. The displays are organised clearly and explained for ordinary visitors, so you do not need specialist knowledge. There is enough detail to learn something without feeling overwhelmed. I would suggest a weekday morning because weekends can be crowded. About two hours would give a first-time visitor a useful introduction without becoming too tiring.","phrases":[{"en":"overwhelmed","meaning":"Feeling that there is too much to deal with or understand."}],"question":"Would you recommend this museum to other people?","html":"Yes. The displays are organised clearly and explained for ordinary visitors, so you do not need specialist knowledge. There is enough detail to learn something without feeling <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overwhelmed</strong>. I would suggest a weekday morning because weekends can be crowded. About two hours would give a first-time visitor a useful introduction without becoming too tiring."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-068-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-068-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Music young people like, and what technology changed","prompt":"What kinds of music are popular with young people in your country?\nHow has technology affected the music that is popular with young people?\nWhat do you think influences a young person's taste in music?","answer":"Q: What kinds of music are popular with young people in your country?\nA: Pop and hip-hop dominate, along with electronic dance music and Korean pop, which has got <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an enormous following</strong> here. What strikes me is how international the taste&#x27;s become. My parents&#x27; generation listened almost entirely to domestic music because that&#x27;s what was available, whereas young people now discover things through streaming and short videos, so a song from anywhere can become popular here within days. Local music&#x27;s still strong, but it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">competes globally now</strong> in a way it never used to.\n\nQ: How has technology affected the music that is popular with young people?\nA: In two significant ways, really. Production first — you can record something decent on a laptop these days, so artists don&#x27;t need a record label to be heard, and that&#x27;s opened things up to people who&#x27;d never have got through the old system. The second is discovery: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recommendation algorithms</strong> decide a huge amount of what people actually hear, and because they keep suggesting what resembles what you already like, taste can narrow even while choice technically expands.\n\nQ: What do you think influences a young person&#x27;s taste in music?\nA: Friends most of all, especially in the teenage years, because music&#x27;s partly a way of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">signalling which group you belong to</strong>. Family matters earlier on, since people often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep an attachment to</strong> whatever was played at home. Beyond that, films, games and online platforms introduce a lot too. But there&#x27;s something genuinely personal in it as well: people attach songs to particular periods of their life, and that connection has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nothing to do</strong> with what anybody else thinks.","topic":"Music","phrases":[{"en":"an enormous following","context":"Q: What kinds of music are popular with young people in your country?\nA: Pop and hip-hop dominate, along with electronic dance music and Korean pop, which has got an enormous following here. What strikes me is how international the taste's become. My parents' generation listened almost entirely to domestic music because that's what was available, whereas young people now discover things through streaming and short videos, so a song from anywhere can become popular here within days. Local music's still strong, but it competes globally now in a way it never used to."},{"en":"competes globally now","context":"Q: What kinds of music are popular with young people in your country?\nA: Pop and hip-hop dominate, along with electronic dance music and Korean pop, which has got an enormous following here. What strikes me is how international the taste's become. My parents' generation listened almost entirely to domestic music because that's what was available, whereas young people now discover things through streaming and short videos, so a song from anywhere can become popular here within days. Local music's still strong, but it competes globally now in a way it never used to."},{"en":"recommendation algorithms","context":"Q: How has technology affected the music that is popular with young people?\nA: In two significant ways, really. Production first — you can record something decent on a laptop these days, so artists don't need a record label to be heard, and that's opened things up to people who'd never have got through the old system. The second is discovery: recommendation algorithms decide a huge amount of what people actually hear, and because they keep suggesting what resembles what you already like, taste can narrow even while choice technically expands."},{"en":"signalling which group you belong to","context":"Q: What do you think influences a young person's taste in music?\nA: Friends most of all, especially in the teenage years, because music's partly a way of signalling which group you belong to. Family matters earlier on, since people often keep an attachment to whatever was played at home. Beyond that, films, games and online platforms introduce a lot too. But there's something genuinely personal in it as well: people attach songs to particular periods of their life, and that connection has nothing to do with what anybody else thinks."},{"en":"keep an attachment to","context":"Q: What do you think influences a young person's taste in music?\nA: Friends most of all, especially in the teenage years, because music's partly a way of signalling which group you belong to. Family matters earlier on, since people often keep an attachment to whatever was played at home. Beyond that, films, games and online platforms introduce a lot too. But there's something genuinely personal in it as well: people attach songs to particular periods of their life, and that connection has nothing to do with what anybody else thinks."},{"en":"nothing to do","context":"Q: What do you think influences a young person's taste in music?\nA: Friends most of all, especially in the teenage years, because music's partly a way of signalling which group you belong to. Family matters earlier on, since people often keep an attachment to whatever was played at home. Beyond that, films, games and online platforms introduce a lot too. But there's something genuinely personal in it as well: people attach songs to particular periods of their life, and that connection has nothing to do with what anybody else thinks."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Music","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-069","_displayNo":"#069","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-069-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-069-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-069-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Pop, hip-hop, electronic dance music and K-pop are very popular. Young people discover songs through streaming and short videos, so their choices are much more international than my parents' were. A song from another country can become familiar quickly. Local music still matters, but it now competes with a much wider range of music.","phrases":[{"en":"streaming","meaning":"Listening to or watching online content without downloading the whole file first."}],"question":"What kinds of music are popular with young people in your country?","html":"Pop, hip-hop, electronic dance music and K-pop are very popular. Young people discover songs through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">streaming</strong> and short videos, so their choices are much more international than my parents&#x27; were. A song from another country can become familiar quickly. Local music still matters, but it now competes with a much wider range of music."},{"text":"Technology makes it easier to create music on a laptop, so artists do not always need a record company. It also changes discovery through recommendations made by apps. These can help listeners find songs, but often suggest similar music repeatedly. As a result, people may have more choice available while actually exploring fewer different styles.","phrases":[{"en":"recommendations","meaning":"Suggestions about things you might like or find useful."}],"question":"How has technology affected the music that is popular with young people?","html":"Technology makes it easier to create music on a laptop, so artists do not always need a record company. It also changes discovery through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recommendations</strong> made by apps. These can help listeners find songs, but often suggest similar music repeatedly. As a result, people may have more choice available while actually exploring fewer different styles."},{"text":"Friends strongly influence teenagers because music helps show which group they belong to. Family introduces music earlier, while films, games and online platforms add new choices. Personal memories matter too. A song linked to an important period can remain special even when friends dislike it, so musical taste is not only a result of outside influence.","phrases":[{"en":"musical taste","meaning":"The types of music a person enjoys."}],"question":"What do you think influences a young person's taste in music?","html":"Friends strongly influence teenagers because music helps show which group they belong to. Family introduces music earlier, while films, games and online platforms add new choices. Personal memories matter too. A song linked to an important period can remain special even when friends dislike it, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">musical taste</strong> is not only a result of outside influence."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-069-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-069-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-069-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Whether newspapers still matter","prompt":"In your opinion, are newspapers important?","answer":"Q: In your opinion, are newspapers important?\nA: Yes, though I&#x27;d say it&#x27;s professional journalism that matters rather than the printed paper itself. Newspapers employ reporters, editors and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fact-checkers</strong>, and they fund investigations that can take months and might ultimately produce nothing usable. An individual posting on social media simply can&#x27;t do that. The other thing they provide is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accountability</strong>: if a newspaper prints something false, it can be sued and forced to correct it, whereas an anonymous account <strong class=\"key-phrase\">faces no consequences at all</strong>. I wouldn&#x27;t pretend it&#x27;s a perfect system, though — plenty of papers are owned by people with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an obvious agenda</strong>, and that clearly shapes what gets covered. Even so, the format may well disappear, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the process behind it</strong> is something we&#x27;d genuinely struggle to replace.","topic":"News & Media","phrases":[{"en":"fact-checkers","context":"Q: In your opinion, are newspapers important?\nA: Yes, though I'd say it's professional journalism that matters rather than the printed paper itself. Newspapers employ reporters, editors and fact-checkers, and they fund investigations that can take months and might ultimately produce nothing usable. An individual posting on social media simply can't do that. The other thing they provide is accountability: if a newspaper prints something false, it can be sued and forced to correct it, whereas an anonymous account faces no consequences at all. I wouldn't pretend it's a perfect system, though — plenty of papers are owned by people with an obvious agenda, and that clearly shapes what gets covered. Even so, the format may well disappear, but the process behind it is something we'd genuinely struggle to replace."},{"en":"accountability","context":"Q: In your opinion, are newspapers important?\nA: Yes, though I'd say it's professional journalism that matters rather than the printed paper itself. Newspapers employ reporters, editors and fact-checkers, and they fund investigations that can take months and might ultimately produce nothing usable. An individual posting on social media simply can't do that. The other thing they provide is accountability: if a newspaper prints something false, it can be sued and forced to correct it, whereas an anonymous account faces no consequences at all. I wouldn't pretend it's a perfect system, though — plenty of papers are owned by people with an obvious agenda, and that clearly shapes what gets covered. Even so, the format may well disappear, but the process behind it is something we'd genuinely struggle to replace."},{"en":"faces no consequences at all","context":"Q: In your opinion, are newspapers important?\nA: Yes, though I'd say it's professional journalism that matters rather than the printed paper itself. Newspapers employ reporters, editors and fact-checkers, and they fund investigations that can take months and might ultimately produce nothing usable. An individual posting on social media simply can't do that. The other thing they provide is accountability: if a newspaper prints something false, it can be sued and forced to correct it, whereas an anonymous account faces no consequences at all. I wouldn't pretend it's a perfect system, though — plenty of papers are owned by people with an obvious agenda, and that clearly shapes what gets covered. Even so, the format may well disappear, but the process behind it is something we'd genuinely struggle to replace."},{"en":"an obvious agenda","context":"Q: In your opinion, are newspapers important?\nA: Yes, though I'd say it's professional journalism that matters rather than the printed paper itself. Newspapers employ reporters, editors and fact-checkers, and they fund investigations that can take months and might ultimately produce nothing usable. An individual posting on social media simply can't do that. The other thing they provide is accountability: if a newspaper prints something false, it can be sued and forced to correct it, whereas an anonymous account faces no consequences at all. I wouldn't pretend it's a perfect system, though — plenty of papers are owned by people with an obvious agenda, and that clearly shapes what gets covered. Even so, the format may well disappear, but the process behind it is something we'd genuinely struggle to replace."},{"en":"the process behind it","context":"Q: In your opinion, are newspapers important?\nA: Yes, though I'd say it's professional journalism that matters rather than the printed paper itself. Newspapers employ reporters, editors and fact-checkers, and they fund investigations that can take months and might ultimately produce nothing usable. An individual posting on social media simply can't do that. The other thing they provide is accountability: if a newspaper prints something false, it can be sued and forced to correct it, whereas an anonymous account faces no consequences at all. I wouldn't pretend it's a perfect system, though — plenty of papers are owned by people with an obvious agenda, and that clearly shapes what gets covered. Even so, the format may well disappear, but the process behind it is something we'd genuinely struggle to replace."}],"subtype":"","summary":"News & Media","id":"speaking-part-3-070","_displayNo":"#070","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-070-q01.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, but the reporting process matters more than printed paper. Newspapers support reporters, editors and investigations that take time and money. They can also be held responsible for false reporting, unlike many anonymous online accounts. Some owners influence coverage unfairly, so they are not perfect. However, losing professional journalism would mean losing something difficult to replace.","phrases":[{"en":"professional journalism","meaning":"News reporting carried out through trained staff and editorial checks."}],"question":"In your opinion, are newspapers important?","html":"Yes, but the reporting process matters more than printed paper. Newspapers support reporters, editors and investigations that take time and money. They can also be held responsible for false reporting, unlike many anonymous online accounts. Some owners influence coverage unfairly, so they are not perfect. However, losing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">professional journalism</strong> would mean losing something difficult to replace."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-070-q01.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Newspapers against radio and television for news","prompt":"In what ways are newspapers better for learning about the news than radio or television?\nDo you think newspapers should be completely free to say whatever they want?\nWhat do people enjoy reading in your country?","answer":"Q: In what ways are newspapers better for learning about the news than radio or television?\nA: The main advantage is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">control over the pace</strong>. With a broadcast you move at the presenter&#x27;s speed, whereas reading lets you slow down on something complicated, go back over a paragraph and check where a claim actually came from. Print also has more room, so a newspaper can give you the background to a story rather than just the headline, and it&#x27;s easier to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tell reporting from opinion</strong>, because the two are physically separated on the page. The trade-off is immediacy, though — if something&#x27;s breaking right now, a newspaper that&#x27;s printed once a day can&#x27;t compete with television or a news app.\n\nQ: Do you think newspapers should be completely free to say whatever they want?\nA: No, and I don&#x27;t think any country actually allows that in practice. A free press is essential, since without it governments aren&#x27;t <strong class=\"key-phrase\">held to account</strong>. But that freedom has to sit alongside laws on defamation, privacy and incitement, because publishing a false accusation can destroy someone who has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no comparable platform to respond</strong>. The distinction I&#x27;d draw is between restricting what the press may investigate, which is dangerous, and holding it responsible for what it actually prints, which isn&#x27;t the same thing at all.\n\nQ: What do people enjoy reading in your country?\nA: News and sport are probably the most widely followed, along with entertainment and celebrity coverage. Business and property attract a lot of attention too, especially among people my age. What&#x27;s changed is that people no longer read a whole publication — they follow individual topics through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">social media feeds</strong> instead, so someone might read enormously about football and almost nothing else. That&#x27;s convenient, but it means most people never encounter <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a story they did not already care about</strong>.","topic":"News & Media","phrases":[{"en":"control over the pace","context":"Q: In what ways are newspapers better for learning about the news than radio or television?\nA: The main advantage is control over the pace. With a broadcast you move at the presenter's speed, whereas reading lets you slow down on something complicated, go back over a paragraph and check where a claim actually came from. Print also has more room, so a newspaper can give you the background to a story rather than just the headline, and it's easier to tell reporting from opinion, because the two are physically separated on the page. The trade-off is immediacy, though — if something's breaking right now, a newspaper that's printed once a day can't compete with television or a news app."},{"en":"tell reporting from opinion","context":"Q: In what ways are newspapers better for learning about the news than radio or television?\nA: The main advantage is control over the pace. With a broadcast you move at the presenter's speed, whereas reading lets you slow down on something complicated, go back over a paragraph and check where a claim actually came from. Print also has more room, so a newspaper can give you the background to a story rather than just the headline, and it's easier to tell reporting from opinion, because the two are physically separated on the page. The trade-off is immediacy, though — if something's breaking right now, a newspaper that's printed once a day can't compete with television or a news app."},{"en":"held to account","context":"Q: Do you think newspapers should be completely free to say whatever they want?\nA: No, and I don't think any country actually allows that in practice. A free press is essential, since without it governments aren't held to account. But that freedom has to sit alongside laws on defamation, privacy and incitement, because publishing a false accusation can destroy someone who has no comparable platform to respond. The distinction I'd draw is between restricting what the press may investigate, which is dangerous, and holding it responsible for what it actually prints, which isn't the same thing at all."},{"en":"no comparable platform to respond","context":"Q: Do you think newspapers should be completely free to say whatever they want?\nA: No, and I don't think any country actually allows that in practice. A free press is essential, since without it governments aren't held to account. But that freedom has to sit alongside laws on defamation, privacy and incitement, because publishing a false accusation can destroy someone who has no comparable platform to respond. The distinction I'd draw is between restricting what the press may investigate, which is dangerous, and holding it responsible for what it actually prints, which isn't the same thing at all."},{"en":"social media feeds","context":"Q: What do people enjoy reading in your country?\nA: News and sport are probably the most widely followed, along with entertainment and celebrity coverage. Business and property attract a lot of attention too, especially among people my age. What's changed is that people no longer read a whole publication — they follow individual topics through social media feeds instead, so someone might read enormously about football and almost nothing else. That's convenient, but it means most people never encounter a story they did not already care about."},{"en":"a story they did not already care about","context":"Q: What do people enjoy reading in your country?\nA: News and sport are probably the most widely followed, along with entertainment and celebrity coverage. Business and property attract a lot of attention too, especially among people my age. What's changed is that people no longer read a whole publication — they follow individual topics through social media feeds instead, so someone might read enormously about football and almost nothing else. That's convenient, but it means most people never encounter a story they did not already care about."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"News & Media","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-071","_displayNo":"#071","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-071-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-071-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-071-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Reading lets you choose your own pace, return to difficult passages and check the source of a claim. Newspapers also have room for background and usually separate opinion from reporting clearly. Their weakness is speed: a daily printed paper cannot update as quickly as television or an app when an event is happening right now.","phrases":[{"en":"choose your own pace","meaning":"Decide how quickly or slowly to do something."}],"question":"In what ways are newspapers better for learning about the news than radio or television?","html":"Reading lets you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">choose your own pace</strong>, return to difficult passages and check the source of a claim. Newspapers also have room for background and usually separate opinion from reporting clearly. Their weakness is speed: a daily printed paper cannot update as quickly as television or an app when an event is happening right now."},{"text":"No. A free press is important for questioning governments, but it should still respect privacy and rules against harmful false accusations. Publishing something untrue can seriously damage someone who cannot respond equally. I would distinguish preventing a newspaper from investigating from making it responsible for what it publishes. Freedom and responsibility should work together.","phrases":[{"en":"false accusations","meaning":"Untrue statements saying someone has done something wrong."}],"question":"Do you think newspapers should be completely free to say whatever they want?","html":"No. A free press is important for questioning governments, but it should still respect privacy and rules against harmful <strong class=\"key-phrase\">false accusations</strong>. Publishing something untrue can seriously damage someone who cannot respond equally. I would distinguish preventing a newspaper from investigating from making it responsible for what it publishes. Freedom and responsibility should work together."},{"text":"News, sport, business and celebrity stories attract many readers. People also follow property and other personal interests. Increasingly, they find individual stories through social media rather than read a whole newspaper. That is convenient, but it can narrow their knowledge: someone following only football may rarely encounter important stories about anything else.","phrases":[{"en":"encounter","meaning":"Come across or experience something."}],"question":"What do people enjoy reading in your country?","html":"News, sport, business and celebrity stories attract many readers. People also follow property and other personal interests. Increasingly, they find individual stories through social media rather than read a whole newspaper. That is convenient, but it can narrow their knowledge: someone following only football may rarely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">encounter</strong> important stories about anything else."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-071-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-071-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-071-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Where people get news, and the future of newspapers","prompt":"Which do you prefer for news: newspapers, television or radio? Why?\nWhat will happen to newspapers as the internet becomes more dominant?","answer":"Q: Which do you prefer for news: newspapers, television or radio? Why?\nA: I prefer reading, mainly because I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">control the pace</strong>. I can slow down on something complicated, check where a claim came from and tell reporting from opinion, which is much harder when a broadcast&#x27;s moving at its own speed. That said, television&#x27;s better for live events where you genuinely need to see what&#x27;s happening, and radio suits me when I&#x27;m driving or cooking — but for actually understanding something properly, I&#x27;d still choose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the written version</strong> every time.\n\nQ: What will happen to newspapers as the internet becomes more dominant?\nA: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Printed circulation</strong> will keep falling, and I don&#x27;t think anything reverses that now. But journalism itself won&#x27;t disappear, because the demand for reliable information&#x27;s arguably higher than ever. The stronger organisations have already moved into <strong class=\"key-phrase\">digital subscriptions</strong>, newsletters and podcasts, and the ones that survive will be the ones that keep their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">editorial standards</strong> rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">chasing clicks</strong>. What worries me is local news, though, because those papers don&#x27;t have a realistic subscription model, and plenty of towns now have nobody reporting on the council at all.","topic":"News & Media","phrases":[{"en":"control the pace","context":"Q: Which do you prefer for news: newspapers, television or radio? Why?\nA: I prefer reading, mainly because I can control the pace. I can slow down on something complicated, check where a claim came from and tell reporting from opinion, which is much harder when a broadcast's moving at its own speed. That said, television's better for live events where you genuinely need to see what's happening, and radio suits me when I'm driving or cooking — but for actually understanding something properly, I'd still choose the written version every time."},{"en":"the written version","context":"Q: Which do you prefer for news: newspapers, television or radio? Why?\nA: I prefer reading, mainly because I can control the pace. I can slow down on something complicated, check where a claim came from and tell reporting from opinion, which is much harder when a broadcast's moving at its own speed. That said, television's better for live events where you genuinely need to see what's happening, and radio suits me when I'm driving or cooking — but for actually understanding something properly, I'd still choose the written version every time."},{"en":"Printed circulation","context":"Q: What will happen to newspapers as the internet becomes more dominant?\nA: Printed circulation will keep falling, and I don't think anything reverses that now. But journalism itself won't disappear, because the demand for reliable information's arguably higher than ever. The stronger organisations have already moved into digital subscriptions, newsletters and podcasts, and the ones that survive will be the ones that keep their editorial standards rather than chasing clicks. What worries me is local news, though, because those papers don't have a realistic subscription model, and plenty of towns now have nobody reporting on the council at all."},{"en":"digital subscriptions","context":"Q: What will happen to newspapers as the internet becomes more dominant?\nA: Printed circulation will keep falling, and I don't think anything reverses that now. But journalism itself won't disappear, because the demand for reliable information's arguably higher than ever. The stronger organisations have already moved into digital subscriptions, newsletters and podcasts, and the ones that survive will be the ones that keep their editorial standards rather than chasing clicks. What worries me is local news, though, because those papers don't have a realistic subscription model, and plenty of towns now have nobody reporting on the council at all."},{"en":"editorial standards","context":"Q: What will happen to newspapers as the internet becomes more dominant?\nA: Printed circulation will keep falling, and I don't think anything reverses that now. But journalism itself won't disappear, because the demand for reliable information's arguably higher than ever. The stronger organisations have already moved into digital subscriptions, newsletters and podcasts, and the ones that survive will be the ones that keep their editorial standards rather than chasing clicks. What worries me is local news, though, because those papers don't have a realistic subscription model, and plenty of towns now have nobody reporting on the council at all."},{"en":"chasing clicks","context":"Q: What will happen to newspapers as the internet becomes more dominant?\nA: Printed circulation will keep falling, and I don't think anything reverses that now. But journalism itself won't disappear, because the demand for reliable information's arguably higher than ever. The stronger organisations have already moved into digital subscriptions, newsletters and podcasts, and the ones that survive will be the ones that keep their editorial standards rather than chasing clicks. What worries me is local news, though, because those papers don't have a realistic subscription model, and plenty of towns now have nobody reporting on the council at all."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"News & Media","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-072","_displayNo":"#072","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-072-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-072-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I prefer written news because I can slow down, reread and check where information comes from. It helps me understand complicated issues. Television is better for seeing a live event, and radio works while driving or cooking. Each has a use, but for careful understanding I would usually choose to read.","phrases":[{"en":"reread","meaning":"Read something again."}],"question":"Which do you prefer for news: newspapers, television or radio? Why?","html":"I prefer written news because I can slow down, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reread</strong> and check where information comes from. It helps me understand complicated issues. Television is better for seeing a live event, and radio works while driving or cooking. Each has a use, but for careful understanding I would usually choose to read."},{"text":"Printed newspapers will probably keep losing readers, but reliable journalism will still be needed. Strong organisations can use subscriptions, newsletters and podcasts while keeping careful reporting standards. I worry more about local newspapers, which may struggle to earn enough online. If they disappear, some communities lose the people checking what local officials are doing.","phrases":[{"en":"subscriptions","meaning":"Regular payments for continued access to a service or publication."}],"question":"What will happen to newspapers as the internet becomes more dominant?","html":"Printed newspapers will probably keep losing readers, but reliable journalism will still be needed. Strong organisations can use <strong class=\"key-phrase\">subscriptions</strong>, newsletters and podcasts while keeping careful reporting standards. I worry more about local newspapers, which may struggle to earn enough online. If they disappear, some communities lose the people checking what local officials are doing."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-072-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-072-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Taking photographs and looking at other people's","prompt":"Are you a keen photographer?\nDo you like looking at other people's photographs?\nDo you prefer to take a photo or film and why?","answer":"Q: Are you a keen photographer?\nA: I take photographs fairly regularly, but I wouldn&#x27;t call myself a photographer in any serious sense — it&#x27;s mostly travel and family occasions. What&#x27;s changed is that I&#x27;ve become far more selective. When phones made it free to take a thousand pictures, I did exactly that, and then never looked at any of them again. Now I take far fewer and actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">choose between them afterwards</strong>, though I&#x27;ll admit that discipline slips the moment something exciting happens and I end up back at fifty shots of the same view.\n\nQ: Do you like looking at other people&#x27;s photographs?\nA: It depends entirely on how they&#x27;re presented. If someone&#x27;s chosen twenty images <strong class=\"key-phrase\">that tell a story</strong>, I&#x27;m genuinely interested, especially photographs from an earlier period, because you can see how people dressed and what places looked like before they changed. What&#x27;s difficult is being shown four hundred <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unedited pictures</strong> from someone&#x27;s holiday, because the problem isn&#x27;t the photographs themselves, it&#x27;s that no selection&#x27;s been made, so nothing stands out. The one exception is close family — I&#x27;ll happily sit through all four hundred of those, because even a handful of good ones makes it worthwhile.\n\nQ: Do you prefer to take a photo or film and why?\nA: Photographs, generally. A still image&#x27;s quick to revisit — you take it in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at a glance</strong> — and it leaves something to the imagination, because you don&#x27;t know what happened just before or after. Video <strong class=\"key-phrase\">captures movement and voices</strong>, though, which is irreplaceable for recordings of relatives in particular — that&#x27;s the one area where I&#x27;d choose it every time. But it demands time to watch and a lot of storage, and I&#x27;ve noticed that filming an event can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stop you experiencing it</strong> properly.","topic":"Photography","phrases":[{"en":"choose between them afterwards","context":"Q: Are you a keen photographer?\nA: I take photographs fairly regularly, but I wouldn't call myself a photographer in any serious sense — it's mostly travel and family occasions. What's changed is that I've become far more selective. When phones made it free to take a thousand pictures, I did exactly that, and then never looked at any of them again. Now I take far fewer and actually choose between them afterwards, though I'll admit that discipline slips the moment something exciting happens and I end up back at fifty shots of the same view."},{"en":"that tell a story","context":"Q: Do you like looking at other people's photographs?\nA: It depends entirely on how they're presented. If someone's chosen twenty images that tell a story, I'm genuinely interested, especially photographs from an earlier period, because you can see how people dressed and what places looked like before they changed. What's difficult is being shown four hundred unedited pictures from someone's holiday, because the problem isn't the photographs themselves, it's that no selection's been made, so nothing stands out. The one exception is close family — I'll happily sit through all four hundred of those, because even a handful of good ones makes it worthwhile."},{"en":"unedited pictures","context":"Q: Do you like looking at other people's photographs?\nA: It depends entirely on how they're presented. If someone's chosen twenty images that tell a story, I'm genuinely interested, especially photographs from an earlier period, because you can see how people dressed and what places looked like before they changed. What's difficult is being shown four hundred unedited pictures from someone's holiday, because the problem isn't the photographs themselves, it's that no selection's been made, so nothing stands out. The one exception is close family — I'll happily sit through all four hundred of those, because even a handful of good ones makes it worthwhile."},{"en":"at a glance","context":"Q: Do you prefer to take a photo or film and why?\nA: Photographs, generally. A still image's quick to revisit — you take it in at a glance — and it leaves something to the imagination, because you don't know what happened just before or after. Video captures movement and voices, though, which is irreplaceable for recordings of relatives in particular — that's the one area where I'd choose it every time. But it demands time to watch and a lot of storage, and I've noticed that filming an event can stop you experiencing it properly."},{"en":"captures movement and voices","context":"Q: Do you prefer to take a photo or film and why?\nA: Photographs, generally. A still image's quick to revisit — you take it in at a glance — and it leaves something to the imagination, because you don't know what happened just before or after. Video captures movement and voices, though, which is irreplaceable for recordings of relatives in particular — that's the one area where I'd choose it every time. But it demands time to watch and a lot of storage, and I've noticed that filming an event can stop you experiencing it properly."},{"en":"stop you experiencing it","context":"Q: Do you prefer to take a photo or film and why?\nA: Photographs, generally. A still image's quick to revisit — you take it in at a glance — and it leaves something to the imagination, because you don't know what happened just before or after. Video captures movement and voices, though, which is irreplaceable for recordings of relatives in particular — that's the one area where I'd choose it every time. But it demands time to watch and a lot of storage, and I've noticed that filming an event can stop you experiencing it properly."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Photography","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-073","_displayNo":"#073","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-073-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-073-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-073-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I take photos regularly, mainly of trips and family events, but I am not a serious photographer. I used to take huge numbers and never look at them. Now I try to take fewer and choose the best afterwards. That habit still disappears sometimes when I get excited and take many shots of the same thing.","phrases":[{"en":"shots","meaning":"Individual photographs taken with a camera."}],"question":"Are you a keen photographer?","html":"I take photos regularly, mainly of trips and family events, but I am not a serious photographer. I used to take huge numbers and never look at them. Now I try to take fewer and choose the best afterwards. That habit still disappears sometimes when I get excited and take many <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shots</strong> of the same thing."},{"text":"Yes, when they have been chosen carefully and tell a story. Older photos are especially interesting because they show how people and places have changed. Hundreds of similar holiday pictures are harder to enjoy because nothing stands out. I make an exception for close family, though, since even ordinary pictures of them can mean something to me.","phrases":[{"en":"stands out","meaning":"Is more noticeable or memorable than the things around it."}],"question":"Do you like looking at other people's photographs?","html":"Yes, when they have been chosen carefully and tell a story. Older photos are especially interesting because they show how people and places have changed. Hundreds of similar holiday pictures are harder to enjoy because nothing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stands out</strong>. I make an exception for close family, though, since even ordinary pictures of them can mean something to me."},{"text":"Usually photos, because they are quick to look at again and leave room for imagination. Video is better for saving voices and movement, especially of relatives. However, it takes more storage and time to watch. Filming can also distract me from enjoying an event, so I choose it more carefully than taking a few pictures.","phrases":[{"en":"storage","meaning":"Space available for keeping digital files."}],"question":"Do you prefer to take a photo or film and why?","html":"Usually photos, because they are quick to look at again and leave room for imagination. Video is better for saving voices and movement, especially of relatives. However, it takes more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">storage</strong> and time to watch. Filming can also distract me from enjoying an event, so I choose it more carefully than taking a few pictures."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-073-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-073-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-073-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Showing politeness, at home and outside it","prompt":"In your country's culture, how do you show that you are being polite?\nAre we less polite with members of our families than with people we do not know?","answer":"Q: In your country&#x27;s culture, how do you show that you are being polite?\nA: Politeness in the UK&#x27;s often shown through indirect language, personal space and frequent use of &#x27;please&#x27;, &#x27;thank you&#x27; and &#x27;sorry&#x27;. People normally say something like, &#x27;Could we have the bill, please?&#x27; rather than giving a direct command — the wording <strong class=\"key-phrase\">softens the request</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">signals respect</strong>. Queuing&#x27;s another one; people take it far more seriously than visitors often expect, and pushing in front of somebody is one of the few things that&#x27;ll genuinely annoy an otherwise reserved crowd. The downside is that visitors from more direct-speaking cultures sometimes read all that indirectness as insincere, when it&#x27;s really just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the local convention</strong>.\n\nQ: Are we less polite with members of our families than with people we do not know?\nA: I suppose it&#x27;s normal to be a bit <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more relaxed about politeness</strong> with family members. Most people tend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">speak in a more informal way</strong> at home — in the UK we still say &#x27;please&#x27; and &#x27;thanks&#x27;, but it&#x27;s fine to use colloquial language and nicknames you&#x27;d never use with someone you didn&#x27;t know. I&#x27;d say the politeness doesn&#x27;t actually disappear, though; it just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changes form</strong> — within a family it shows up as remembering things and doing small favours rather than as careful wording.","topic":"Politeness","phrases":[{"en":"softens the request","context":"Q: In your country's culture, how do you show that you are being polite?\nA: Politeness in the UK's often shown through indirect language, personal space and frequent use of 'please', 'thank you' and 'sorry'. People normally say something like, 'Could we have the bill, please?' rather than giving a direct command — the wording softens the request and signals respect. Queuing's another one; people take it far more seriously than visitors often expect, and pushing in front of somebody is one of the few things that'll genuinely annoy an otherwise reserved crowd. The downside is that visitors from more direct-speaking cultures sometimes read all that indirectness as insincere, when it's really just the local convention."},{"en":"signals respect","context":"Q: In your country's culture, how do you show that you are being polite?\nA: Politeness in the UK's often shown through indirect language, personal space and frequent use of 'please', 'thank you' and 'sorry'. People normally say something like, 'Could we have the bill, please?' rather than giving a direct command — the wording softens the request and signals respect. Queuing's another one; people take it far more seriously than visitors often expect, and pushing in front of somebody is one of the few things that'll genuinely annoy an otherwise reserved crowd. The downside is that visitors from more direct-speaking cultures sometimes read all that indirectness as insincere, when it's really just the local convention."},{"en":"the local convention","context":"Q: In your country's culture, how do you show that you are being polite?\nA: Politeness in the UK's often shown through indirect language, personal space and frequent use of 'please', 'thank you' and 'sorry'. People normally say something like, 'Could we have the bill, please?' rather than giving a direct command — the wording softens the request and signals respect. Queuing's another one; people take it far more seriously than visitors often expect, and pushing in front of somebody is one of the few things that'll genuinely annoy an otherwise reserved crowd. The downside is that visitors from more direct-speaking cultures sometimes read all that indirectness as insincere, when it's really just the local convention."},{"en":"more relaxed about politeness","context":"Q: Are we less polite with members of our families than with people we do not know?\nA: I suppose it's normal to be a bit more relaxed about politeness with family members. Most people tend to speak in a more informal way at home — in the UK we still say 'please' and 'thanks', but it's fine to use colloquial language and nicknames you'd never use with someone you didn't know. I'd say the politeness doesn't actually disappear, though; it just changes form — within a family it shows up as remembering things and doing small favours rather than as careful wording."},{"en":"speak in a more informal way","context":"Q: Are we less polite with members of our families than with people we do not know?\nA: I suppose it's normal to be a bit more relaxed about politeness with family members. Most people tend to speak in a more informal way at home — in the UK we still say 'please' and 'thanks', but it's fine to use colloquial language and nicknames you'd never use with someone you didn't know. I'd say the politeness doesn't actually disappear, though; it just changes form — within a family it shows up as remembering things and doing small favours rather than as careful wording."},{"en":"changes form","context":"Q: Are we less polite with members of our families than with people we do not know?\nA: I suppose it's normal to be a bit more relaxed about politeness with family members. Most people tend to speak in a more informal way at home — in the UK we still say 'please' and 'thanks', but it's fine to use colloquial language and nicknames you'd never use with someone you didn't know. I'd say the politeness doesn't actually disappear, though; it just changes form — within a family it shows up as remembering things and doing small favours rather than as careful wording."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Politeness","id":"speaking-part-3-074","_displayNo":"#074","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-074-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-074-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"In the UK, people often use polite questions, say please and thank you, and respect personal space. Waiting your turn in a queue is important too. These habits show consideration rather than direct demands. However, people from more direct-speaking cultures may misunderstand the careful wording as dishonest, when it is simply the usual local style.","phrases":[{"en":"consideration","meaning":"Care for other people's feelings, needs or comfort."}],"question":"In your country's culture, how do you show that you are being polite?","html":"In the UK, people often use polite questions, say please and thank you, and respect personal space. Waiting your turn in a queue is important too. These habits show <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consideration</strong> rather than direct demands. However, people from more direct-speaking cultures may misunderstand the careful wording as dishonest, when it is simply the usual local style."},{"text":"We usually speak more casually at home, using nicknames and language we might avoid with strangers. That does not mean respect disappears. In a family, politeness may show through remembering someone's needs or doing small favours rather than choosing formal words. I think the form changes because people know each other better, while the care can remain.","phrases":[{"en":"favours","meaning":"Helpful actions done for someone without expecting payment."}],"question":"Are we less polite with members of our families than with people we do not know?","html":"We usually speak more casually at home, using nicknames and language we might avoid with strangers. That does not mean respect disappears. In a family, politeness may show through remembering someone&#x27;s needs or doing small <strong class=\"key-phrase\">favours</strong> rather than choosing formal words. I think the form changes because people know each other better, while the care can remain."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-074-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-074-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Managing risk and the impact of natural hazards","prompt":"How important is risk management?\nWhat can be done to prevent natural disasters?\nWhat are the impacts of natural hazards on the surrounding areas?","answer":"Q: How important is risk management?\nA: It&#x27;s essential in any situation where failure would be expensive or dangerous. The basic idea&#x27;s simple enough — you identify what could go wrong, rank each possibility by how likely it is and how serious it&#x27;d be, and then put safeguards in place <strong class=\"key-phrase\">proportionate to that</strong>. What people underestimate is the cost of getting it wrong: most major industrial accidents were preceded by warnings that somebody had already flagged and nobody had acted on. The danger is doing it badly, though — a box-ticking risk assessment that lists hazards nobody then fixes is arguably worse than having none at all, because it creates <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a false sense</strong> that the problem&#x27;s been handled.\n\nQ: What can be done to prevent natural disasters?\nA: Strictly speaking you can&#x27;t prevent the natural event itself, since an earthquake or a typhoon&#x27;s going to happen regardless. What you can prevent is the disaster, which is really a question of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">how exposed people are</strong>. That means not building on floodplains, enforcing construction standards so buildings don&#x27;t collapse, installing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">early-warning systems</strong> and, crucially, teaching people what to do. Japan&#x27;s the obvious example, where the same earthquake causes far fewer deaths than it would elsewhere — though even Japan doesn&#x27;t get it right every time, since a big enough event can still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overwhelm defences</strong> built for anything smaller.\n\nQ: What are the impacts of natural hazards on the surrounding areas?\nA: The immediate damage to homes and infrastructure is what gets reported, but the longer-term effects are often worse. Livelihoods disappear when farmland&#x27;s contaminated or boats destroyed, children lose a year or more of schooling, and health services collapse exactly when they&#x27;re needed most. There&#x27;s also displacement, since people move away and frequently never return. That&#x27;s why recovery has to mean far more than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rebuilding the physical structures</strong>. The one qualification is that reconstruction aid sometimes leaves a place with better infrastructure than it had before, though that&#x27;s small comfort to the people who lost everything to get there.","topic":"Risk & Safety","phrases":[{"en":"proportionate to that","context":"Q: How important is risk management?\nA: It's essential in any situation where failure would be expensive or dangerous. The basic idea's simple enough — you identify what could go wrong, rank each possibility by how likely it is and how serious it'd be, and then put safeguards in place proportionate to that. What people underestimate is the cost of getting it wrong: most major industrial accidents were preceded by warnings that somebody had already flagged and nobody had acted on. The danger is doing it badly, though — a box-ticking risk assessment that lists hazards nobody then fixes is arguably worse than having none at all, because it creates a false sense that the problem's been handled."},{"en":"a false sense","context":"Q: How important is risk management?\nA: It's essential in any situation where failure would be expensive or dangerous. The basic idea's simple enough — you identify what could go wrong, rank each possibility by how likely it is and how serious it'd be, and then put safeguards in place proportionate to that. What people underestimate is the cost of getting it wrong: most major industrial accidents were preceded by warnings that somebody had already flagged and nobody had acted on. The danger is doing it badly, though — a box-ticking risk assessment that lists hazards nobody then fixes is arguably worse than having none at all, because it creates a false sense that the problem's been handled."},{"en":"how exposed people are","context":"Q: What can be done to prevent natural disasters?\nA: Strictly speaking you can't prevent the natural event itself, since an earthquake or a typhoon's going to happen regardless. What you can prevent is the disaster, which is really a question of how exposed people are. That means not building on floodplains, enforcing construction standards so buildings don't collapse, installing early-warning systems and, crucially, teaching people what to do. Japan's the obvious example, where the same earthquake causes far fewer deaths than it would elsewhere — though even Japan doesn't get it right every time, since a big enough event can still overwhelm defences built for anything smaller."},{"en":"early-warning systems","context":"Q: What can be done to prevent natural disasters?\nA: Strictly speaking you can't prevent the natural event itself, since an earthquake or a typhoon's going to happen regardless. What you can prevent is the disaster, which is really a question of how exposed people are. That means not building on floodplains, enforcing construction standards so buildings don't collapse, installing early-warning systems and, crucially, teaching people what to do. Japan's the obvious example, where the same earthquake causes far fewer deaths than it would elsewhere — though even Japan doesn't get it right every time, since a big enough event can still overwhelm defences built for anything smaller."},{"en":"overwhelm defences","context":"Q: What can be done to prevent natural disasters?\nA: Strictly speaking you can't prevent the natural event itself, since an earthquake or a typhoon's going to happen regardless. What you can prevent is the disaster, which is really a question of how exposed people are. That means not building on floodplains, enforcing construction standards so buildings don't collapse, installing early-warning systems and, crucially, teaching people what to do. Japan's the obvious example, where the same earthquake causes far fewer deaths than it would elsewhere — though even Japan doesn't get it right every time, since a big enough event can still overwhelm defences built for anything smaller."},{"en":"rebuilding the physical structures","context":"Q: What are the impacts of natural hazards on the surrounding areas?\nA: The immediate damage to homes and infrastructure is what gets reported, but the longer-term effects are often worse. Livelihoods disappear when farmland's contaminated or boats destroyed, children lose a year or more of schooling, and health services collapse exactly when they're needed most. There's also displacement, since people move away and frequently never return. That's why recovery has to mean far more than rebuilding the physical structures. The one qualification is that reconstruction aid sometimes leaves a place with better infrastructure than it had before, though that's small comfort to the people who lost everything to get there."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Risk & Safety","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-075","_displayNo":"#075","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-075-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-075-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-075-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"It is essential when mistakes could cause serious harm or expense. We need to identify possible problems, judge their likelihood and seriousness, then take suitable precautions. Simply writing a list is not enough. If warnings are recorded but ignored, a risk assessment may create false confidence instead of improving safety. The actions following the assessment matter most.","phrases":[{"en":"precautions","meaning":"Steps taken in advance to reduce danger or prevent problems."}],"question":"How important is risk management?","html":"It is essential when mistakes could cause serious harm or expense. We need to identify possible problems, judge their likelihood and seriousness, then take suitable <strong class=\"key-phrase\">precautions</strong>. Simply writing a list is not enough. If warnings are recorded but ignored, a risk assessment may create false confidence instead of improving safety. The actions following the assessment matter most."},{"text":"We cannot usually stop an earthquake or storm, but we can reduce the harm. Avoiding risky building locations, using strong construction, providing warnings and teaching people what to do all help. Countries with good preparation can lose fewer lives. However, a very large event may still exceed the protection planned, so preparation reduces risk rather than removing it completely.","phrases":[{"en":"exceed","meaning":"Be greater than an expected amount or limit."}],"question":"What can be done to prevent natural disasters?","html":"We cannot usually stop an earthquake or storm, but we can reduce the harm. Avoiding risky building locations, using strong construction, providing warnings and teaching people what to do all help. Countries with good preparation can lose fewer lives. However, a very large event may still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exceed</strong> the protection planned, so preparation reduces risk rather than removing it completely."},{"text":"They damage homes and roads, but the effects continue much longer. People may lose farms or businesses, children miss school and health services struggle. Some residents move away permanently. Recovery therefore needs jobs, services and community support, not just new buildings. Rebuilding may sometimes improve facilities, but that does not cancel the suffering people have experienced.","phrases":[{"en":"Recovery","meaning":"The process of returning to a safer or more normal situation after harm."}],"question":"What are the impacts of natural hazards on the surrounding areas?","html":"They damage homes and roads, but the effects continue much longer. People may lose farms or businesses, children miss school and health services struggle. Some residents move away permanently. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Recovery</strong> therefore needs jobs, services and community support, not just new buildings. Rebuilding may sometimes improve facilities, but that does not cancel the suffering people have experienced."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-075-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-075-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-075-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Whether the seasons still shape behaviour","prompt":"Do you think the seasons still influence people's behaviour?","answer":"Q: Do you think the seasons still influence people&#x27;s behaviour?\nA: Yes, though much less than they used to. People still adjust their clothing, routines and free time according to daylight and temperature — in summer we eat outdoors and travel more, whereas in winter people stay in and socialise at home. Where the influence&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weakened considerably</strong> is food and work: supermarkets sell strawberries in December, and most people now work indoors under <strong class=\"key-phrase\">artificial light</strong>, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the farming calendar</strong> that once governed everything has almost no effect on daily life. I&#x27;d say the seasons now shape <strong class=\"key-phrase\">our mood and habits</strong> far more than our economy, though that&#x27;s arguably a bigger effect than people realise — plenty of low mood in winter genuinely does trace back to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a lack of daylight</strong>.","topic":"Seasons","phrases":[{"en":"weakened considerably","context":"Q: Do you think the seasons still influence people's behaviour?\nA: Yes, though much less than they used to. People still adjust their clothing, routines and free time according to daylight and temperature — in summer we eat outdoors and travel more, whereas in winter people stay in and socialise at home. Where the influence's weakened considerably is food and work: supermarkets sell strawberries in December, and most people now work indoors under artificial light, so the farming calendar that once governed everything has almost no effect on daily life. I'd say the seasons now shape our mood and habits far more than our economy, though that's arguably a bigger effect than people realise — plenty of low mood in winter genuinely does trace back to a lack of daylight."},{"en":"artificial light","context":"Q: Do you think the seasons still influence people's behaviour?\nA: Yes, though much less than they used to. People still adjust their clothing, routines and free time according to daylight and temperature — in summer we eat outdoors and travel more, whereas in winter people stay in and socialise at home. Where the influence's weakened considerably is food and work: supermarkets sell strawberries in December, and most people now work indoors under artificial light, so the farming calendar that once governed everything has almost no effect on daily life. I'd say the seasons now shape our mood and habits far more than our economy, though that's arguably a bigger effect than people realise — plenty of low mood in winter genuinely does trace back to a lack of daylight."},{"en":"the farming calendar","context":"Q: Do you think the seasons still influence people's behaviour?\nA: Yes, though much less than they used to. People still adjust their clothing, routines and free time according to daylight and temperature — in summer we eat outdoors and travel more, whereas in winter people stay in and socialise at home. Where the influence's weakened considerably is food and work: supermarkets sell strawberries in December, and most people now work indoors under artificial light, so the farming calendar that once governed everything has almost no effect on daily life. I'd say the seasons now shape our mood and habits far more than our economy, though that's arguably a bigger effect than people realise — plenty of low mood in winter genuinely does trace back to a lack of daylight."},{"en":"our mood and habits","context":"Q: Do you think the seasons still influence people's behaviour?\nA: Yes, though much less than they used to. People still adjust their clothing, routines and free time according to daylight and temperature — in summer we eat outdoors and travel more, whereas in winter people stay in and socialise at home. Where the influence's weakened considerably is food and work: supermarkets sell strawberries in December, and most people now work indoors under artificial light, so the farming calendar that once governed everything has almost no effect on daily life. I'd say the seasons now shape our mood and habits far more than our economy, though that's arguably a bigger effect than people realise — plenty of low mood in winter genuinely does trace back to a lack of daylight."},{"en":"a lack of daylight","context":"Q: Do you think the seasons still influence people's behaviour?\nA: Yes, though much less than they used to. People still adjust their clothing, routines and free time according to daylight and temperature — in summer we eat outdoors and travel more, whereas in winter people stay in and socialise at home. Where the influence's weakened considerably is food and work: supermarkets sell strawberries in December, and most people now work indoors under artificial light, so the farming calendar that once governed everything has almost no effect on daily life. I'd say the seasons now shape our mood and habits far more than our economy, though that's arguably a bigger effect than people realise — plenty of low mood in winter genuinely does trace back to a lack of daylight."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Seasons","id":"speaking-part-3-076","_displayNo":"#076","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-076-q01.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, seasons still change clothes, mood and leisure habits. People spend more time outside in summer and socialise indoors in winter. However, food and work depend less on seasons now because of supermarkets and indoor jobs. The influence has become less directly economic, but limited winter daylight can still have a real effect on how people feel and behave.","phrases":[{"en":"daylight","meaning":"Natural light during the daytime."}],"question":"Do you think the seasons still influence people's behaviour?","html":"Yes, seasons still change clothes, mood and leisure habits. People spend more time outside in summer and socialise indoors in winter. However, food and work depend less on seasons now because of supermarkets and indoor jobs. The influence has become less directly economic, but limited winter <strong class=\"key-phrase\">daylight</strong> can still have a real effect on how people feel and behave."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-076-q01.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Shopping for clothes and judging people by them","prompt":"Do you enjoy shopping for clothes?\nWhat kind of clothes do you feel least comfortable in?\nHow much do you judge a person by their appearance?","answer":"Q: Do you enjoy shopping for clothes?\nA: Only when I know what I&#x27;m looking for. If I need a particular thing, I quite enjoy the process of finding something well made that&#x27;ll last. What I don&#x27;t enjoy is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">browsing with no purpose</strong> — I find it exhausting, and that&#x27;s exactly when I end up buying things I never wear. I also prefer buying clothes in person rather than online, because sizing&#x27;s so inconsistent between brands that ordering blind&#x27;s usually a waste of time. That said, for basics like socks or plain T-shirts, where fit barely matters, I&#x27;ll happily buy online instead.\n\nQ: What kind of clothes do you feel least comfortable in?\nA: Anything too tight or too formal, really. I dislike stiff, heavy fabrics, and I&#x27;m uncomfortable in clothes that are wrong for the weather — being too hot in a suit is genuinely distracting. There&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a psychological element</strong> too: if I&#x27;m dressed very differently from everyone around me, I spend the whole time aware of it. For me, comfortable means being able to move naturally and then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forget about what I am wearing</strong>. The exception&#x27;s somewhere like a wedding or an interview, where I&#x27;ll happily put up with a few uncomfortable hours because looking right matters more that day than feeling right.\n\nQ: How much do you judge a person by their appearance?\nA: I&#x27;d be dishonest if I said not at all, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">first impressions</strong> form automatically and appearance&#x27;s a large part of that. What I try to do is treat it as information rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">evidence of character</strong>. Someone&#x27;s clothing usually tells you about their job, their culture or simply what they could afford that month — it doesn&#x27;t tell you who they actually are. I&#x27;ve been wrong about people often enough to have learned that judging quickly mostly just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reinforces stereotypes</strong>.","topic":"Shopping","phrases":[{"en":"browsing with no purpose","context":"Q: Do you enjoy shopping for clothes?\nA: Only when I know what I'm looking for. If I need a particular thing, I quite enjoy the process of finding something well made that'll last. What I don't enjoy is browsing with no purpose — I find it exhausting, and that's exactly when I end up buying things I never wear. I also prefer buying clothes in person rather than online, because sizing's so inconsistent between brands that ordering blind's usually a waste of time. That said, for basics like socks or plain T-shirts, where fit barely matters, I'll happily buy online instead."},{"en":"a psychological element","context":"Q: What kind of clothes do you feel least comfortable in?\nA: Anything too tight or too formal, really. I dislike stiff, heavy fabrics, and I'm uncomfortable in clothes that are wrong for the weather — being too hot in a suit is genuinely distracting. There's a psychological element too: if I'm dressed very differently from everyone around me, I spend the whole time aware of it. For me, comfortable means being able to move naturally and then forget about what I am wearing. The exception's somewhere like a wedding or an interview, where I'll happily put up with a few uncomfortable hours because looking right matters more that day than feeling right."},{"en":"forget about what I am wearing","context":"Q: What kind of clothes do you feel least comfortable in?\nA: Anything too tight or too formal, really. I dislike stiff, heavy fabrics, and I'm uncomfortable in clothes that are wrong for the weather — being too hot in a suit is genuinely distracting. There's a psychological element too: if I'm dressed very differently from everyone around me, I spend the whole time aware of it. For me, comfortable means being able to move naturally and then forget about what I am wearing. The exception's somewhere like a wedding or an interview, where I'll happily put up with a few uncomfortable hours because looking right matters more that day than feeling right."},{"en":"first impressions","context":"Q: How much do you judge a person by their appearance?\nA: I'd be dishonest if I said not at all, because first impressions form automatically and appearance's a large part of that. What I try to do is treat it as information rather than evidence of character. Someone's clothing usually tells you about their job, their culture or simply what they could afford that month — it doesn't tell you who they actually are. I've been wrong about people often enough to have learned that judging quickly mostly just reinforces stereotypes."},{"en":"evidence of character","context":"Q: How much do you judge a person by their appearance?\nA: I'd be dishonest if I said not at all, because first impressions form automatically and appearance's a large part of that. What I try to do is treat it as information rather than evidence of character. Someone's clothing usually tells you about their job, their culture or simply what they could afford that month — it doesn't tell you who they actually are. I've been wrong about people often enough to have learned that judging quickly mostly just reinforces stereotypes."},{"en":"reinforces stereotypes","context":"Q: How much do you judge a person by their appearance?\nA: I'd be dishonest if I said not at all, because first impressions form automatically and appearance's a large part of that. What I try to do is treat it as information rather than evidence of character. Someone's clothing usually tells you about their job, their culture or simply what they could afford that month — it doesn't tell you who they actually are. I've been wrong about people often enough to have learned that judging quickly mostly just reinforces stereotypes."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Shopping","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-077","_displayNo":"#077","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-077-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-077-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-077-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Only when I have a clear purpose. I enjoy finding a well-made item that will last, but browsing without a plan is tiring and can lead to unnecessary purchases. I prefer trying most clothes in person because sizes vary. For basic things such as socks or plain T-shirts, though, ordering online is convenient enough.","phrases":[{"en":"browsing","meaning":"Looking around without necessarily deciding to buy something."}],"question":"Do you enjoy shopping for clothes?","html":"Only when I have a clear purpose. I enjoy finding a well-made item that will last, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">browsing</strong> without a plan is tiring and can lead to unnecessary purchases. I prefer trying most clothes in person because sizes vary. For basic things such as socks or plain T-shirts, though, ordering online is convenient enough."},{"text":"I dislike tight, very formal clothes and heavy fabrics, especially in hot weather. I also feel uncomfortable if I am dressed very differently from everyone else. Ideally, I want to move freely and forget about my clothes. For a wedding or interview, however, I accept some discomfort because dressing appropriately matters more on that occasion.","phrases":[{"en":"appropriately","meaning":"In a way that suits the situation or occasion."}],"question":"What kind of clothes do you feel least comfortable in?","html":"I dislike tight, very formal clothes and heavy fabrics, especially in hot weather. I also feel uncomfortable if I am dressed very differently from everyone else. Ideally, I want to move freely and forget about my clothes. For a wedding or interview, however, I accept some discomfort because dressing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appropriately</strong> matters more on that occasion."},{"text":"Appearance affects first impressions, so I cannot honestly say it has no influence. However, clothing may reflect someone's work, culture or budget rather than character. I try to treat it as limited information and get to know the person. Judging quickly can simply repeat stereotypes, and I have been wrong about people often enough to remember that.","phrases":[{"en":"stereotypes","meaning":"Fixed general ideas about groups that may not fit individuals."}],"question":"How much do you judge a person by their appearance?","html":"Appearance affects first impressions, so I cannot honestly say it has no influence. However, clothing may reflect someone&#x27;s work, culture or budget rather than character. I try to treat it as limited information and get to know the person. Judging quickly can simply repeat <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stereotypes</strong>, and I have been wrong about people often enough to remember that."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-077-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-077-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-077-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Natural ability against developing a skill","prompt":"Could you comment on the idea that children shouldn't waste their time trying to develop a skill if they lack natural ability?\nIs it important to learn a skill?\nWhat do you think are the most important skills for children to learn in the modern world?","answer":"Q: Could you comment on the idea that children shouldn&#x27;t waste their time trying to develop a skill if they lack natural ability?\nA: I disagree fairly strongly. Natural ability affects how quickly someone improves, not whether they can improve at all, and most people vastly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overestimate how much talent explains</strong>. A child who practises consistently will overtake a naturally gifted one who doesn&#x27;t, in almost any field. There&#x27;s a wider point too: the value of learning an instrument isn&#x27;t only the music, it&#x27;s discovering that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sustained effort</strong> produces results, and that lesson transfers to everything else a child attempts. I&#x27;d make one exception, though — right at the elite level, in top-tier sport or music, natural ability probably does start to matter more, but that&#x27;s a tiny fraction of children and no reason to hold the rest back.\n\nQ: Is it important to learn a skill?\nA: Yes, and the process matters more than the specific skill itself. Learning something difficult teaches you what your own progress actually looks like — that it&#x27;s slow, uneven, and that plateaus are normal rather than a signal to stop. People who&#x27;ve been through that once are usually much harder to discourage later on, in completely unrelated areas. The particular skill may well become obsolete — nobody needs my childhood recorder-playing now — but the habit of deliberate practice stays useful for a lifetime.\n\nQ: What do you think are the most important skills for children to learn in the modern world?\nA: The foundations haven&#x27;t changed — reading, writing and maths still determine what everything else is built on. Beyond that I&#x27;d prioritise <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the ability to evaluate information</strong>, because children now encounter enormous amounts of it with no filter. Communication and working with others matter too, since almost nothing significant gets done alone, and increasingly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learning how to learn</strong>, since most of them will change occupation several times over a career. The one thing I&#x27;d push back on is treating these as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one-off modules</strong> — they only really stick when they&#x27;re built into ordinary lessons.","topic":"Skills","phrases":[{"en":"overestimate how much talent explains","context":"Q: Could you comment on the idea that children shouldn't waste their time trying to develop a skill if they lack natural ability?\nA: I disagree fairly strongly. Natural ability affects how quickly someone improves, not whether they can improve at all, and most people vastly overestimate how much talent explains. A child who practises consistently will overtake a naturally gifted one who doesn't, in almost any field. There's a wider point too: the value of learning an instrument isn't only the music, it's discovering that sustained effort produces results, and that lesson transfers to everything else a child attempts. I'd make one exception, though — right at the elite level, in top-tier sport or music, natural ability probably does start to matter more, but that's a tiny fraction of children and no reason to hold the rest back."},{"en":"sustained effort","context":"Q: Could you comment on the idea that children shouldn't waste their time trying to develop a skill if they lack natural ability?\nA: I disagree fairly strongly. Natural ability affects how quickly someone improves, not whether they can improve at all, and most people vastly overestimate how much talent explains. A child who practises consistently will overtake a naturally gifted one who doesn't, in almost any field. There's a wider point too: the value of learning an instrument isn't only the music, it's discovering that sustained effort produces results, and that lesson transfers to everything else a child attempts. I'd make one exception, though — right at the elite level, in top-tier sport or music, natural ability probably does start to matter more, but that's a tiny fraction of children and no reason to hold the rest back."},{"en":"the ability to evaluate information","context":"Q: What do you think are the most important skills for children to learn in the modern world?\nA: The foundations haven't changed — reading, writing and maths still determine what everything else is built on. Beyond that I'd prioritise the ability to evaluate information, because children now encounter enormous amounts of it with no filter. Communication and working with others matter too, since almost nothing significant gets done alone, and increasingly learning how to learn, since most of them will change occupation several times over a career. The one thing I'd push back on is treating these as one-off modules — they only really stick when they're built into ordinary lessons."},{"en":"learning how to learn","context":"Q: What do you think are the most important skills for children to learn in the modern world?\nA: The foundations haven't changed — reading, writing and maths still determine what everything else is built on. Beyond that I'd prioritise the ability to evaluate information, because children now encounter enormous amounts of it with no filter. Communication and working with others matter too, since almost nothing significant gets done alone, and increasingly learning how to learn, since most of them will change occupation several times over a career. The one thing I'd push back on is treating these as one-off modules — they only really stick when they're built into ordinary lessons."},{"en":"one-off modules","context":"Q: What do you think are the most important skills for children to learn in the modern world?\nA: The foundations haven't changed — reading, writing and maths still determine what everything else is built on. Beyond that I'd prioritise the ability to evaluate information, because children now encounter enormous amounts of it with no filter. Communication and working with others matter too, since almost nothing significant gets done alone, and increasingly learning how to learn, since most of them will change occupation several times over a career. The one thing I'd push back on is treating these as one-off modules — they only really stick when they're built into ordinary lessons."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Skills","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-078","_displayNo":"#078","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-078-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-078-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-078-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I disagree. Natural ability can affect how quickly a child improves, but practice still makes a difference. A talented child who never works may progress less than someone who keeps trying. Learning also teaches that effort produces results, which helps elsewhere. Talent may matter more at the very highest level, but that is no reason to discourage most children.","phrases":[{"en":"Natural ability","meaning":"A skill or strength someone appears to have without much training."}],"question":"Could you comment on the idea that children shouldn't waste their time trying to develop a skill if they lack natural ability?","html":"I disagree. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Natural ability</strong> can affect how quickly a child improves, but practice still makes a difference. A talented child who never works may progress less than someone who keeps trying. Learning also teaches that effort produces results, which helps elsewhere. Talent may matter more at the very highest level, but that is no reason to discourage most children."},{"text":"Yes, because learning a skill teaches us how improvement works. Progress can be slow or uneven, and a difficult period does not mean we should stop. Once people understand that, they may handle challenges better in other areas. The original skill might become less useful, but the habit of careful, repeated practice can stay valuable throughout life.","phrases":[{"en":"repeated practice","meaning":"Doing an activity many times to improve at it."}],"question":"Is it important to learn a skill?","html":"Yes, because learning a skill teaches us how improvement works. Progress can be slow or uneven, and a difficult period does not mean we should stop. Once people understand that, they may handle challenges better in other areas. The original skill might become less useful, but the habit of careful, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">repeated practice</strong> can stay valuable throughout life."},{"text":"Reading, writing and maths remain basic. Children also need to judge information carefully, communicate and work with others. Learning how to learn matters because their jobs may change several times. These skills should be practised throughout ordinary lessons rather than taught once in a separate activity, so children can use them in different real situations.","phrases":[{"en":"judge information","meaning":"Decide how trustworthy, useful or accurate information is."}],"question":"What do you think are the most important skills for children to learn in the modern world?","html":"Reading, writing and maths remain basic. Children also need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">judge information</strong> carefully, communicate and work with others. Learning how to learn matters because their jobs may change several times. These skills should be practised throughout ordinary lessons rather than taught once in a separate activity, so children can use them in different real situations."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-078-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-078-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-078-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Why sport matters and sportspeople as role models","prompt":"Why do you think sport is important?\nDo you think famous sportspeople are good role models for children?\nDo you agree that sports stars earn too much money?","answer":"Q: Why do you think sport is important?\nA: I think sport&#x27;s important for a few different reasons. For me, doing a sport&#x27;s mainly about having fun — when I play football, for example, I forget about everything else and just enjoy myself. It also helps you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep fit</strong> and healthy, and it&#x27;s a good way to socialise and make friends. There&#x27;s a discipline side too: training regularly teaches you that progress is slow and uneven, and that lesson transfers to almost everything else you do. That&#x27;s assuming it stays enjoyable, though — plenty of people are put off sport for life by being forced into competitive games at school that they never actually liked.\n\nQ: Do you think famous sportspeople are good role models for children?\nA: I think that sportspeople should be good role models. Children <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look up to</strong> their favourite football players, like David Beckham for example, so I think that these people have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an enormous responsibility</strong>. They should try to be a good influence, and behave in the right way. That said, I think it&#x27;s unfair to expect them to be perfect. They&#x27;re usually young people under enormous public pressure, and we ask far more of them than we would of anyone else.\n\nQ: Do you agree that sports stars earn too much money?\nA: In my opinion, it&#x27;s fair that the best sportspeople earn a lot of money. Being a top sportsperson <strong class=\"key-phrase\">requires hours of practice</strong>, and there are millions of sports fans who are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">willing to pay</strong> to see them play. If we don&#x27;t want sportspeople to earn so much money, we shouldn&#x27;t go to watch them. What does seem unfair is the gap within the sport itself. A handful of players earn extraordinary sums while most professionals earn an ordinary wage and retire in their thirties.","topic":"Sports & Exercise","phrases":[{"en":"keep fit","context":"Q: Why do you think sport is important?\nA: I think sport's important for a few different reasons. For me, doing a sport's mainly about having fun — when I play football, for example, I forget about everything else and just enjoy myself. It also helps you keep fit and healthy, and it's a good way to socialise and make friends. There's a discipline side too: training regularly teaches you that progress is slow and uneven, and that lesson transfers to almost everything else you do. That's assuming it stays enjoyable, though — plenty of people are put off sport for life by being forced into competitive games at school that they never actually liked."},{"en":"look up to","context":"Q: Do you think famous sportspeople are good role models for children?\nA: I think that sportspeople should be good role models. Children look up to their favourite football players, like David Beckham for example, so I think that these people have an enormous responsibility. They should try to be a good influence, and behave in the right way. That said, I think it's unfair to expect them to be perfect. They're usually young people under enormous public pressure, and we ask far more of them than we would of anyone else."},{"en":"an enormous responsibility","context":"Q: Do you think famous sportspeople are good role models for children?\nA: I think that sportspeople should be good role models. Children look up to their favourite football players, like David Beckham for example, so I think that these people have an enormous responsibility. They should try to be a good influence, and behave in the right way. That said, I think it's unfair to expect them to be perfect. They're usually young people under enormous public pressure, and we ask far more of them than we would of anyone else."},{"en":"requires hours of practice","context":"Q: Do you agree that sports stars earn too much money?\nA: In my opinion, it's fair that the best sportspeople earn a lot of money. Being a top sportsperson requires hours of practice, and there are millions of sports fans who are willing to pay to see them play. If we don't want sportspeople to earn so much money, we shouldn't go to watch them. What does seem unfair is the gap within the sport itself. A handful of players earn extraordinary sums while most professionals earn an ordinary wage and retire in their thirties."},{"en":"willing to pay","context":"Q: Do you agree that sports stars earn too much money?\nA: In my opinion, it's fair that the best sportspeople earn a lot of money. Being a top sportsperson requires hours of practice, and there are millions of sports fans who are willing to pay to see them play. If we don't want sportspeople to earn so much money, we shouldn't go to watch them. What does seem unfair is the gap within the sport itself. A handful of players earn extraordinary sums while most professionals earn an ordinary wage and retire in their thirties."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Sports & Exercise","id":"speaking-part-3-079","_displayNo":"#079","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-079-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-079-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-079-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Sport is enjoyable, helps fitness and creates chances to make friends. Training also teaches patience because improvement is not always quick. When I play football, I can forget other worries and enjoy the moment. However, these benefits depend partly on enjoying the activity. Forcing someone into a game they dislike may put them off sport for years.","phrases":[{"en":"put them off","meaning":"Make them dislike or avoid something."}],"question":"Why do you think sport is important?","html":"Sport is enjoyable, helps fitness and creates chances to make friends. Training also teaches patience because improvement is not always quick. When I play football, I can forget other worries and enjoy the moment. However, these benefits depend partly on enjoying the activity. Forcing someone into a game they dislike may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put them off</strong> sport for years."},{"text":"They should try to be because children admire them and copy their behaviour. Famous players therefore have a responsibility to act fairly and set a useful example. However, expecting perfection is unfair. Many are young and under constant pressure, so we should recognise their mistakes without assuming they must be ideal people in every part of life.","phrases":[{"en":"admire","meaning":"Respect someone and think highly of them."}],"question":"Do you think famous sportspeople are good role models for children?","html":"They should try to be because children <strong class=\"key-phrase\">admire</strong> them and copy their behaviour. Famous players therefore have a responsibility to act fairly and set a useful example. However, expecting perfection is unfair. Many are young and under constant pressure, so we should recognise their mistakes without assuming they must be ideal people in every part of life."},{"text":"I think high earnings can be fair because top athletes train hard and many fans pay to watch them. The money reflects that demand. What concerns me more is the difference within sport: a few stars earn enormous sums while many professionals have ordinary pay and short careers. That gap seems harder to justify than success itself.","phrases":[{"en":"justify","meaning":"Give a convincing reason why something is reasonable or fair."}],"question":"Do you agree that sports stars earn too much money?","html":"I think high earnings can be fair because top athletes train hard and many fans pay to watch them. The money reflects that demand. What concerns me more is the difference within sport: a few stars earn enormous sums while many professionals have ordinary pay and short careers. That gap seems harder to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">justify</strong> than success itself."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-079-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-079-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-079-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Attending sporting events and funding sport","prompt":"Do you often go to sporting events?\nAre you very competitive?\nDo you agree or disagree that governments should spend large sums on major sporting events?","answer":"Q: Do you often go to sporting events?\nA: Not often, but I go when I can for the bigger ones. What television genuinely can&#x27;t reproduce is the atmosphere — thousands of people reacting simultaneously, and the sense that you&#x27;re part of something rather than just watching it. You actually see less of the detail than you would at home, but you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel far more</strong>. The obstacle&#x27;s cost, though: between tickets, travel and food, a single match&#x27;s become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an expensive day out</strong>.\n\nQ: Are you very competitive?\nA: I&#x27;d say I&#x27;m competitive with myself rather than with other people. I like measuring progress — running slightly faster than last month, doing something better than I did before — and that keeps me motivated without much downside. Competing directly with others I find less useful, because it turns everything into <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a question of status</strong>, and there&#x27;s always somebody better than you. Comparing yourself with your previous self is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the version you can actually win</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you agree or disagree that governments should spend large sums on major sporting events?\nA: I partly agree, though I&#x27;m sceptical. The arguments for it are real — infrastructure gets built, the country gets attention, and there&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuine national pride</strong>. But the costs regularly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">run far over budget</strong>, and cities are left with stadiums nobody uses. It&#x27;s very hard to defend when hospitals are short of staff. If a government does bid, I&#x27;d say the spending should be transparent and every venue should have a clear use afterwards, not just for the two weeks of the event itself.","topic":"Sports & Exercise","phrases":[{"en":"feel far more","context":"Q: Do you often go to sporting events?\nA: Not often, but I go when I can for the bigger ones. What television genuinely can't reproduce is the atmosphere — thousands of people reacting simultaneously, and the sense that you're part of something rather than just watching it. You actually see less of the detail than you would at home, but you feel far more. The obstacle's cost, though: between tickets, travel and food, a single match's become an expensive day out."},{"en":"an expensive day out","context":"Q: Do you often go to sporting events?\nA: Not often, but I go when I can for the bigger ones. What television genuinely can't reproduce is the atmosphere — thousands of people reacting simultaneously, and the sense that you're part of something rather than just watching it. You actually see less of the detail than you would at home, but you feel far more. The obstacle's cost, though: between tickets, travel and food, a single match's become an expensive day out."},{"en":"a question of status","context":"Q: Are you very competitive?\nA: I'd say I'm competitive with myself rather than with other people. I like measuring progress — running slightly faster than last month, doing something better than I did before — and that keeps me motivated without much downside. Competing directly with others I find less useful, because it turns everything into a question of status, and there's always somebody better than you. Comparing yourself with your previous self is the version you can actually win."},{"en":"the version you can actually win","context":"Q: Are you very competitive?\nA: I'd say I'm competitive with myself rather than with other people. I like measuring progress — running slightly faster than last month, doing something better than I did before — and that keeps me motivated without much downside. Competing directly with others I find less useful, because it turns everything into a question of status, and there's always somebody better than you. Comparing yourself with your previous self is the version you can actually win."},{"en":"genuine national pride","context":"Q: Do you agree or disagree that governments should spend large sums on major sporting events?\nA: I partly agree, though I'm sceptical. The arguments for it are real — infrastructure gets built, the country gets attention, and there's genuine national pride. But the costs regularly run far over budget, and cities are left with stadiums nobody uses. It's very hard to defend when hospitals are short of staff. If a government does bid, I'd say the spending should be transparent and every venue should have a clear use afterwards, not just for the two weeks of the event itself."},{"en":"run far over budget","context":"Q: Do you agree or disagree that governments should spend large sums on major sporting events?\nA: I partly agree, though I'm sceptical. The arguments for it are real — infrastructure gets built, the country gets attention, and there's genuine national pride. But the costs regularly run far over budget, and cities are left with stadiums nobody uses. It's very hard to defend when hospitals are short of staff. If a government does bid, I'd say the spending should be transparent and every venue should have a clear use afterwards, not just for the two weeks of the event itself."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Sports & Exercise","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-080","_displayNo":"#080","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-080-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-080-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-080-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Not regularly, but I sometimes attend major events. The shared reaction of thousands of fans creates an atmosphere television cannot copy, even if I see less detail than at home. However, tickets, travel and food can make one match expensive. So I enjoy the experience, but cost limits how often I can go.","phrases":[{"en":"limits","meaning":"Restricts the amount or frequency of something."}],"question":"Do you often go to sporting events?","html":"Not regularly, but I sometimes attend major events. The shared reaction of thousands of fans creates an atmosphere television cannot copy, even if I see less detail than at home. However, tickets, travel and food can make one match expensive. So I enjoy the experience, but cost <strong class=\"key-phrase\">limits</strong> how often I can go."},{"text":"I mainly compete with my previous performance. Running faster or doing a task better than last month motivates me without making everything about beating others. Direct comparison is less useful because someone will always be better. Personal progress gives me a clearer and more realistic target, although I still enjoy challenging myself.","phrases":[{"en":"challenging myself","meaning":"Trying something difficult to test or improve my ability."}],"question":"Are you very competitive?","html":"I mainly compete with my previous performance. Running faster or doing a task better than last month motivates me without making everything about beating others. Direct comparison is less useful because someone will always be better. Personal progress gives me a clearer and more realistic target, although I still enjoy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">challenging myself</strong>."},{"text":"I partly agree because major events can improve facilities and create national pride. However, costs often rise beyond the plan, and stadiums may be unused afterwards. That is difficult to accept when essential services need money. Governments should explain spending openly and show how every venue will benefit the public long after the event.","phrases":[{"en":"venue","meaning":"Places where events or activities are held."}],"question":"Do you agree or disagree that governments should spend large sums on major sporting events?","html":"I partly agree because major events can improve facilities and create national pride. However, costs often rise beyond the plan, and stadiums may be unused afterwards. That is difficult to accept when essential services need money. Governments should explain spending openly and show how every <strong class=\"key-phrase\">venue</strong> will benefit the public long after the event."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-080-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-080-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-080-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Exercise and health, and compulsory sport at school","prompt":"How can regular exercise improve your health?\nShould schools make sport compulsory?\nHow have the types of exercise people do changed in recent years?","answer":"Q: How can regular exercise improve your health?\nA: Exercise improves health in two ways, and people usually only think about one of them. Physically, it&#x27;s well documented — it strengthens the heart and circulation, preserves muscle and bone density, and cuts the risk of diabetes and several cancers quite substantially. What people underestimate is the mental side: it&#x27;s one of the most effective treatments there is for mild anxiety, and it improves sleep, which then affects everything else you do that day. The catch is that it&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consistency rather than intensity</strong> that actually delivers these benefits — someone who walks briskly most days for years ends up healthier than someone who trains hard for a month and then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">burns out and gives up</strong>.\n\nQ: Should schools make sport compulsory?\nA: I think so, up to a point. Left to choose, the children who most need physical activity are exactly the ones who&#x27;ll <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opt out</strong>, so making it compulsory reaches them. What I&#x27;d change is the range on offer, though. Traditional school sport means competitive team games, which suits maybe half the pupils and actively puts off the rest. If schools offered swimming, cycling or dance as alternatives too, compulsion would be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far easier to justify</strong>.\n\nQ: How have the types of exercise people do changed in recent years?\nA: Exercise has become much more individual and much more measured. Gym training, running and yoga have grown at the expense of organised team sports, largely because they fit around unpredictable working hours in a way a Tuesday-night five-a-side match doesn&#x27;t. Apps and watches have added <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a tracking element</strong> that didn&#x27;t exist before, which motivates some people and makes others slightly obsessive about the numbers. The underlying reason is that work has become sedentary, so movement now has to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deliberately scheduled</strong> rather than happening naturally.","topic":"Sports & Exercise","phrases":[{"en":"consistency rather than intensity","context":"Q: How can regular exercise improve your health?\nA: Exercise improves health in two ways, and people usually only think about one of them. Physically, it's well documented — it strengthens the heart and circulation, preserves muscle and bone density, and cuts the risk of diabetes and several cancers quite substantially. What people underestimate is the mental side: it's one of the most effective treatments there is for mild anxiety, and it improves sleep, which then affects everything else you do that day. The catch is that it's consistency rather than intensity that actually delivers these benefits — someone who walks briskly most days for years ends up healthier than someone who trains hard for a month and then burns out and gives up."},{"en":"burns out and gives up","context":"Q: How can regular exercise improve your health?\nA: Exercise improves health in two ways, and people usually only think about one of them. Physically, it's well documented — it strengthens the heart and circulation, preserves muscle and bone density, and cuts the risk of diabetes and several cancers quite substantially. What people underestimate is the mental side: it's one of the most effective treatments there is for mild anxiety, and it improves sleep, which then affects everything else you do that day. The catch is that it's consistency rather than intensity that actually delivers these benefits — someone who walks briskly most days for years ends up healthier than someone who trains hard for a month and then burns out and gives up."},{"en":"opt out","context":"Q: Should schools make sport compulsory?\nA: I think so, up to a point. Left to choose, the children who most need physical activity are exactly the ones who'll opt out, so making it compulsory reaches them. What I'd change is the range on offer, though. Traditional school sport means competitive team games, which suits maybe half the pupils and actively puts off the rest. If schools offered swimming, cycling or dance as alternatives too, compulsion would be far easier to justify."},{"en":"far easier to justify","context":"Q: Should schools make sport compulsory?\nA: I think so, up to a point. Left to choose, the children who most need physical activity are exactly the ones who'll opt out, so making it compulsory reaches them. What I'd change is the range on offer, though. Traditional school sport means competitive team games, which suits maybe half the pupils and actively puts off the rest. If schools offered swimming, cycling or dance as alternatives too, compulsion would be far easier to justify."},{"en":"a tracking element","context":"Q: How have the types of exercise people do changed in recent years?\nA: Exercise has become much more individual and much more measured. Gym training, running and yoga have grown at the expense of organised team sports, largely because they fit around unpredictable working hours in a way a Tuesday-night five-a-side match doesn't. Apps and watches have added a tracking element that didn't exist before, which motivates some people and makes others slightly obsessive about the numbers. The underlying reason is that work has become sedentary, so movement now has to be deliberately scheduled rather than happening naturally."},{"en":"deliberately scheduled","context":"Q: How have the types of exercise people do changed in recent years?\nA: Exercise has become much more individual and much more measured. Gym training, running and yoga have grown at the expense of organised team sports, largely because they fit around unpredictable working hours in a way a Tuesday-night five-a-side match doesn't. Apps and watches have added a tracking element that didn't exist before, which motivates some people and makes others slightly obsessive about the numbers. The underlying reason is that work has become sedentary, so movement now has to be deliberately scheduled rather than happening naturally."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Sports & Exercise","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-081","_displayNo":"#081","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-081-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-081-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-081-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Regular exercise supports the heart, muscles and bones, and can help sleep and mood. These mental benefits matter as well as physical fitness. The key is keeping it up over time, rather than training extremely hard for a short period and stopping. A manageable activity done regularly is more useful than an ambitious plan someone cannot maintain.","phrases":[{"en":"keeping it up","meaning":"Continuing an activity or habit over time."}],"question":"How can regular exercise improve your health?","html":"Regular exercise supports the heart, muscles and bones, and can help sleep and mood. These mental benefits matter as well as physical fitness. The key is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeping it up</strong> over time, rather than training extremely hard for a short period and stopping. A manageable activity done regularly is more useful than an ambitious plan someone cannot maintain."},{"text":"Yes, to some extent, because children who most need activity might otherwise avoid it. However, schools should offer more than competitive team games. Swimming, cycling or dance may appeal to different pupils. Requiring movement is easier to support when children have a reasonable choice and do not feel forced into a sport they hate.","phrases":[{"en":"appeal to","meaning":"Seem interesting or attractive to someone."}],"question":"Should schools make sport compulsory?","html":"Yes, to some extent, because children who most need activity might otherwise avoid it. However, schools should offer more than competitive team games. Swimming, cycling or dance may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appeal to</strong> different pupils. Requiring movement is easier to support when children have a reasonable choice and do not feel forced into a sport they hate."},{"text":"More people choose individual activities such as gym training, running or yoga because these fit changing work schedules. Apps and watches also make progress easy to measure. That motivates some people but makes others worry too much about numbers. Since work now involves more sitting, exercise often needs to be planned rather than happening naturally during the day.","phrases":[{"en":"work schedules","meaning":"The times and arrangements for when people work."}],"question":"How have the types of exercise people do changed in recent years?","html":"More people choose individual activities such as gym training, running or yoga because these fit changing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work schedules</strong>. Apps and watches also make progress easy to measure. That motivates some people but makes others worry too much about numbers. Since work now involves more sitting, exercise often needs to be planned rather than happening naturally during the day."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-081-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-081-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-081-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How games have changed since childhood","prompt":"How have games changed since you were a child?\nDo you think this has been a positive change and why?\nWhy do you think children like playing games?","answer":"Q: How have games changed since you were a child?\nA: The shift is from unstructured outdoor play to structured digital games. When I was young, children largely invented their own games outside, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">negotiating the rules as they went</strong>. Now the rules come built in, the environment&#x27;s designed by somebody else, and most play happens on a screen. The games themselves are far more sophisticated and often genuinely creative, so it&#x27;s not simply a step down. What&#x27;s been lost, though, is the physical movement and the face-to-face negotiation that used to come with it — a screen doesn&#x27;t teach a child how to talk another kid out of changing the rules halfway through.\n\nQ: Do you think this has been a positive change and why?\nA: It&#x27;s genuinely mixed. Digital games develop real skills — strategy, quick decision-making, cooperating with people in other countries — and for children who aren&#x27;t athletic, they offer somewhere to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be good at something</strong>. The problems are about displacement rather than the games themselves: hours spent playing are hours not spent moving or sleeping. So I&#x27;d <strong class=\"key-phrase\">judge it by proportion rather than by principle</strong> — a child who plays for an hour and also goes outside is perfectly fine, whereas one who does nothing else clearly isn&#x27;t.\n\nQ: Why do you think children like playing games?\nA: Several things at once. Games provide a clear challenge with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immediate feedback</strong>, which school often doesn&#x27;t, since you know instantly whether you succeeded. There&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">steady, visible progress</strong>, which is satisfying in a way that long-term effort at school usually isn&#x27;t. And they&#x27;re safe: you can lose, fail and try again <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without any real consequence</strong>, which is quite rare in a child&#x27;s life. Add playing with friends, and it&#x27;s a powerful combination — though that safety net is also exactly why games can&#x27;t teach a child how to handle a failure that actually matters.","topic":"Sports & Exercise","phrases":[{"en":"negotiating the rules as they went","context":"Q: How have games changed since you were a child?\nA: The shift is from unstructured outdoor play to structured digital games. When I was young, children largely invented their own games outside, negotiating the rules as they went. Now the rules come built in, the environment's designed by somebody else, and most play happens on a screen. The games themselves are far more sophisticated and often genuinely creative, so it's not simply a step down. What's been lost, though, is the physical movement and the face-to-face negotiation that used to come with it — a screen doesn't teach a child how to talk another kid out of changing the rules halfway through."},{"en":"be good at something","context":"Q: Do you think this has been a positive change and why?\nA: It's genuinely mixed. Digital games develop real skills — strategy, quick decision-making, cooperating with people in other countries — and for children who aren't athletic, they offer somewhere to be good at something. The problems are about displacement rather than the games themselves: hours spent playing are hours not spent moving or sleeping. So I'd judge it by proportion rather than by principle — a child who plays for an hour and also goes outside is perfectly fine, whereas one who does nothing else clearly isn't."},{"en":"judge it by proportion rather than by principle","context":"Q: Do you think this has been a positive change and why?\nA: It's genuinely mixed. Digital games develop real skills — strategy, quick decision-making, cooperating with people in other countries — and for children who aren't athletic, they offer somewhere to be good at something. The problems are about displacement rather than the games themselves: hours spent playing are hours not spent moving or sleeping. So I'd judge it by proportion rather than by principle — a child who plays for an hour and also goes outside is perfectly fine, whereas one who does nothing else clearly isn't."},{"en":"immediate feedback","context":"Q: Why do you think children like playing games?\nA: Several things at once. Games provide a clear challenge with immediate feedback, which school often doesn't, since you know instantly whether you succeeded. There's steady, visible progress, which is satisfying in a way that long-term effort at school usually isn't. And they're safe: you can lose, fail and try again without any real consequence, which is quite rare in a child's life. Add playing with friends, and it's a powerful combination — though that safety net is also exactly why games can't teach a child how to handle a failure that actually matters."},{"en":"steady, visible progress","context":"Q: Why do you think children like playing games?\nA: Several things at once. Games provide a clear challenge with immediate feedback, which school often doesn't, since you know instantly whether you succeeded. There's steady, visible progress, which is satisfying in a way that long-term effort at school usually isn't. And they're safe: you can lose, fail and try again without any real consequence, which is quite rare in a child's life. Add playing with friends, and it's a powerful combination — though that safety net is also exactly why games can't teach a child how to handle a failure that actually matters."},{"en":"without any real consequence","context":"Q: Why do you think children like playing games?\nA: Several things at once. Games provide a clear challenge with immediate feedback, which school often doesn't, since you know instantly whether you succeeded. There's steady, visible progress, which is satisfying in a way that long-term effort at school usually isn't. And they're safe: you can lose, fail and try again without any real consequence, which is quite rare in a child's life. Add playing with friends, and it's a powerful combination — though that safety net is also exactly why games can't teach a child how to handle a failure that actually matters."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Sports & Exercise","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-082","_displayNo":"#082","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-082-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-082-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-082-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"More play now happens in digital games with rules already designed for the player. In my childhood, children often played outside and invented rules together. Modern games can be creative and complex, so the change is not entirely negative. However, some physical movement and face-to-face discussion have been lost when play moves mainly to screens.","phrases":[{"en":"face-to-face discussion","meaning":"Talking together while physically in the same place."}],"question":"How have games changed since you were a child?","html":"More play now happens in digital games with rules already designed for the player. In my childhood, children often played outside and invented rules together. Modern games can be creative and complex, so the change is not entirely negative. However, some physical movement and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">face-to-face discussion</strong> have been lost when play moves mainly to screens."},{"text":"It has both good and bad sides. Digital games can develop planning, quick decisions and teamwork, including with people abroad. They also give less sporty children a chance to succeed. Problems arise when gaming replaces sleep, exercise or other activities. I would judge the balance in a child's life rather than treat all games as either good or bad.","phrases":[{"en":"teamwork","meaning":"Working together with others to achieve something."}],"question":"Do you think this has been a positive change and why?","html":"It has both good and bad sides. Digital games can develop planning, quick decisions and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">teamwork</strong>, including with people abroad. They also give less sporty children a chance to succeed. Problems arise when gaming replaces sleep, exercise or other activities. I would judge the balance in a child&#x27;s life rather than treat all games as either good or bad."},{"text":"Games offer clear goals, quick feedback and visible progress. Children can fail and try again without serious consequences, which makes experimenting enjoyable. Playing with friends adds to that appeal. However, this safe setting has limits: learning to recover from a lost game is not exactly the same as facing a real-world failure with lasting effects.","phrases":[{"en":"feedback","meaning":"Information showing how well you did and what could improve."}],"question":"Why do you think children like playing games?","html":"Games offer clear goals, quick <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feedback</strong> and visible progress. Children can fail and try again without serious consequences, which makes experimenting enjoyable. Playing with friends adds to that appeal. However, this safe setting has limits: learning to recover from a lost game is not exactly the same as facing a real-world failure with lasting effects."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-082-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-082-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-082-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How people keep fit and why exercise matters","prompt":"What do most people in your country do to keep fit?\nHow important is it for people to do some regular physical exercise?\nWhy do you think that modern lifestyles are not healthy?","answer":"Q: What do most people in your country do to keep fit?\nA: Walking is the most common by a distance — parks and lakesides are busy from early morning, particularly with older people. Gym membership has grown enormously among younger adults in cities, and badminton and football are the popular team sports. There&#x27;s also been a big rise in following workout videos at home, which took off during the pandemic and never really went away. What the popular activities share is that they&#x27;re cheap and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fit around a working day</strong> — though that&#x27;s much truer in the cities than in rural areas, where gyms and classes simply aren&#x27;t as available.\n\nQ: How important is it for people to do some regular physical exercise?\nA: Extremely important, and more so than it used to be. Work and travel once involved a great deal of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">incidental movement</strong>, and modern life has removed almost all of it — we sit to work, sit to travel and sit to relax. So exercise now has to be deliberately scheduled or it simply doesn&#x27;t happen. Beyond the cardiovascular benefits, it largely determines whether people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay independent in old age</strong>. It isn&#x27;t <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a cure-all</strong>, though — it won&#x27;t undo a genuinely poor diet or make up for years of bad sleep, so it has to work alongside the other things, not instead of them.\n\nQ: Why do you think that modern lifestyles are not healthy?\nA: It&#x27;s an accumulation of small things rather than one large cause. People sit for eight or nine hours, sleep irregularly because screens keep them up, eat processed food because it&#x27;s quick and cheap, and live under constant low-level stress from being permanently contactable. Any one of these alone would be manageable. The problem is that they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reinforce one another</strong> — poor sleep makes you eat badly, and eating badly makes you inactive, so it becomes a cycle that&#x27;s hard to break from just one direction.","topic":"Sports & Exercise","phrases":[{"en":"fit around a working day","context":"Q: What do most people in your country do to keep fit?\nA: Walking is the most common by a distance — parks and lakesides are busy from early morning, particularly with older people. Gym membership has grown enormously among younger adults in cities, and badminton and football are the popular team sports. There's also been a big rise in following workout videos at home, which took off during the pandemic and never really went away. What the popular activities share is that they're cheap and fit around a working day — though that's much truer in the cities than in rural areas, where gyms and classes simply aren't as available."},{"en":"incidental movement","context":"Q: How important is it for people to do some regular physical exercise?\nA: Extremely important, and more so than it used to be. Work and travel once involved a great deal of incidental movement, and modern life has removed almost all of it — we sit to work, sit to travel and sit to relax. So exercise now has to be deliberately scheduled or it simply doesn't happen. Beyond the cardiovascular benefits, it largely determines whether people stay independent in old age. It isn't a cure-all, though — it won't undo a genuinely poor diet or make up for years of bad sleep, so it has to work alongside the other things, not instead of them."},{"en":"stay independent in old age","context":"Q: How important is it for people to do some regular physical exercise?\nA: Extremely important, and more so than it used to be. Work and travel once involved a great deal of incidental movement, and modern life has removed almost all of it — we sit to work, sit to travel and sit to relax. So exercise now has to be deliberately scheduled or it simply doesn't happen. Beyond the cardiovascular benefits, it largely determines whether people stay independent in old age. It isn't a cure-all, though — it won't undo a genuinely poor diet or make up for years of bad sleep, so it has to work alongside the other things, not instead of them."},{"en":"a cure-all","context":"Q: How important is it for people to do some regular physical exercise?\nA: Extremely important, and more so than it used to be. Work and travel once involved a great deal of incidental movement, and modern life has removed almost all of it — we sit to work, sit to travel and sit to relax. So exercise now has to be deliberately scheduled or it simply doesn't happen. Beyond the cardiovascular benefits, it largely determines whether people stay independent in old age. It isn't a cure-all, though — it won't undo a genuinely poor diet or make up for years of bad sleep, so it has to work alongside the other things, not instead of them."},{"en":"reinforce one another","context":"Q: Why do you think that modern lifestyles are not healthy?\nA: It's an accumulation of small things rather than one large cause. People sit for eight or nine hours, sleep irregularly because screens keep them up, eat processed food because it's quick and cheap, and live under constant low-level stress from being permanently contactable. Any one of these alone would be manageable. The problem is that they reinforce one another — poor sleep makes you eat badly, and eating badly makes you inactive, so it becomes a cycle that's hard to break from just one direction."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Sports & Exercise","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-083","_displayNo":"#083","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-083-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-083-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-083-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Walking is very common, especially around parks and lakes early in the day. Younger city residents also use gyms, play badminton or football, and follow workout videos at home. Popular activities tend to be affordable and easy to fit around work. Rural areas may have fewer gyms and classes, though, so available choices differ by location.","phrases":[{"en":"fit around work","meaning":"Be arranged at times that do not conflict with work."}],"question":"What do most people in your country do to keep fit?","html":"Walking is very common, especially around parks and lakes early in the day. Younger city residents also use gyms, play badminton or football, and follow workout videos at home. Popular activities tend to be affordable and easy to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fit around work</strong>. Rural areas may have fewer gyms and classes, though, so available choices differ by location."},{"text":"It is extremely important because normal life now involves so much sitting. People often need to plan movement or it never happens. Exercise supports health and helps maintain independence later in life. However, it does not solve everything: poor food habits or lack of sleep still matter. It should work alongside other healthy habits, not replace them.","phrases":[{"en":"independence","meaning":"The ability to manage your life without needing constant help."}],"question":"How important is it for people to do some regular physical exercise?","html":"It is extremely important because normal life now involves so much sitting. People often need to plan movement or it never happens. Exercise supports health and helps maintain <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independence</strong> later in life. However, it does not solve everything: poor food habits or lack of sleep still matter. It should work alongside other healthy habits, not replace them."},{"text":"Several habits build on one another. People sit for long hours, stay up with screens, choose quick processed food and feel constantly available for work. Poor sleep can then make eating and activity habits worse. It becomes a cycle rather than one isolated problem. Improving health may therefore require several manageable changes instead of one dramatic solution.","phrases":[{"en":"a cycle","meaning":"A repeated pattern in which effects lead back to similar causes."}],"question":"Why do you think that modern lifestyles are not healthy?","html":"Several habits build on one another. People sit for long hours, stay up with screens, choose quick processed food and feel constantly available for work. Poor sleep can then make eating and activity habits worse. It becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a cycle</strong> rather than one isolated problem. Improving health may therefore require several manageable changes instead of one dramatic solution."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-083-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-083-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-083-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Exercise among young people and changing attitudes","prompt":"What forms of exercise are popular among young people?\nHave people's attitudes towards exercise changed in your country in recent years?","answer":"Q: What forms of exercise are popular among young people?\nA: Gym training is probably the most popular now, along with running, football, badminton and yoga. What&#x27;s changed is how people find these activities — social media has driven a lot of interest towards anything that produces <strong class=\"key-phrase\">visible results</strong> and can be tracked with an app, so people share their progress in a way they never used to. Team sports remain popular, but individual training that fits <strong class=\"key-phrase\">your own schedule</strong> has grown fastest — the one thing it can&#x27;t really replace, though, is the social side that comes automatically with playing on a team.\n\nQ: Have people&#x27;s attitudes towards exercise changed in your country in recent years?\nA: Yes, considerably. Exercise used to be associated mainly with competitive sport, whereas people now connect it with mental health, appearance and general lifestyle, which has brought in a lot of people who were never sporty as children. That&#x27;s a positive shift overall. The one drawback is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">heavily edited images</strong> online set standards nobody can realistically meet, and that puts <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unnecessary pressure</strong> on younger people in particular. Gyms have become a good deal more welcoming to beginners, though, which helps <strong class=\"key-phrase\">balance that out</strong>.","topic":"Sports & Exercise","phrases":[{"en":"visible results","context":"Q: What forms of exercise are popular among young people?\nA: Gym training is probably the most popular now, along with running, football, badminton and yoga. What's changed is how people find these activities — social media has driven a lot of interest towards anything that produces visible results and can be tracked with an app, so people share their progress in a way they never used to. Team sports remain popular, but individual training that fits your own schedule has grown fastest — the one thing it can't really replace, though, is the social side that comes automatically with playing on a team."},{"en":"your own schedule","context":"Q: What forms of exercise are popular among young people?\nA: Gym training is probably the most popular now, along with running, football, badminton and yoga. What's changed is how people find these activities — social media has driven a lot of interest towards anything that produces visible results and can be tracked with an app, so people share their progress in a way they never used to. Team sports remain popular, but individual training that fits your own schedule has grown fastest — the one thing it can't really replace, though, is the social side that comes automatically with playing on a team."},{"en":"heavily edited images","context":"Q: Have people's attitudes towards exercise changed in your country in recent years?\nA: Yes, considerably. Exercise used to be associated mainly with competitive sport, whereas people now connect it with mental health, appearance and general lifestyle, which has brought in a lot of people who were never sporty as children. That's a positive shift overall. The one drawback is that heavily edited images online set standards nobody can realistically meet, and that puts unnecessary pressure on younger people in particular. Gyms have become a good deal more welcoming to beginners, though, which helps balance that out."},{"en":"unnecessary pressure","context":"Q: Have people's attitudes towards exercise changed in your country in recent years?\nA: Yes, considerably. Exercise used to be associated mainly with competitive sport, whereas people now connect it with mental health, appearance and general lifestyle, which has brought in a lot of people who were never sporty as children. That's a positive shift overall. The one drawback is that heavily edited images online set standards nobody can realistically meet, and that puts unnecessary pressure on younger people in particular. Gyms have become a good deal more welcoming to beginners, though, which helps balance that out."},{"en":"balance that out","context":"Q: Have people's attitudes towards exercise changed in your country in recent years?\nA: Yes, considerably. Exercise used to be associated mainly with competitive sport, whereas people now connect it with mental health, appearance and general lifestyle, which has brought in a lot of people who were never sporty as children. That's a positive shift overall. The one drawback is that heavily edited images online set standards nobody can realistically meet, and that puts unnecessary pressure on younger people in particular. Gyms have become a good deal more welcoming to beginners, though, which helps balance that out."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Sports & Exercise","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-084","_displayNo":"#084","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-084-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-084-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Gym training, running, football, badminton and yoga are popular. Social media encourages activities with visible results and apps make progress easy to share. Individual exercise has grown because people can choose their own schedule. However, it does not automatically provide the friendships and shared experience that come with playing regularly in a team.","phrases":[{"en":"visible results","meaning":"Improvements that people can see or recognise."}],"question":"What forms of exercise are popular among young people?","html":"Gym training, running, football, badminton and yoga are popular. Social media encourages activities with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">visible results</strong> and apps make progress easy to share. Individual exercise has grown because people can choose their own schedule. However, it does not automatically provide the friendships and shared experience that come with playing regularly in a team."},{"text":"Yes. Exercise is now connected with mental well-being, appearance and everyday health, not just competition. This attracts people who did not enjoy sport at school. Online images can create unrealistic expectations, particularly for young people, which is a drawback. However, many gyms have also become more welcoming to beginners, making it easier for them to start.","phrases":[{"en":"unrealistic expectations","meaning":"Hopes or standards that are not reasonably achievable."}],"question":"Have people's attitudes towards exercise changed in your country in recent years?","html":"Yes. Exercise is now connected with mental well-being, appearance and everyday health, not just competition. This attracts people who did not enjoy sport at school. Online images can create <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unrealistic expectations</strong>, particularly for young people, which is a drawback. However, many gyms have also become more welcoming to beginners, making it easier for them to start."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-084-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-084-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Inventions and the complexity of modern life","prompt":"What is the most important invention of the last century?\nCan technology make life harder?","answer":"Q: What is the most important invention of the last century?\nA: I&#x27;d say the internet, though the transistor&#x27;s a reasonable answer too, since nothing else would exist without it. The reason I&#x27;d choose the internet is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the sheer breadth of what it changed</strong> — communication, commerce, education, entertainment and access to information all work in a fundamentally different way now. It&#x27;s stopped being a product and become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">basic infrastructure</strong>, in much the same way as electricity or the road network. The honest problem with an answer this broad, though, is that it&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a bit of a cheat</strong> — you could just as easily break it down into the specific technologies underneath it, like fibre optics or mobile networks, and credit those instead.\n\nQ: Can technology make life harder?\nA: Definitely. The obvious problem is distraction, since a device designed to save time ends up consuming your attention unless you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set firm boundaries</strong> around it. Beyond that, there&#x27;s the expectation of being permanently available, which has quietly extended the working day for a great many people. And there&#x27;s a dependency issue: when a system fails, we often discover we&#x27;ve <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lost the ability to manage without it</strong>. The technology itself is fairly neutral, though; it&#x27;s really the habits people build around it that matter.","topic":"Technology","phrases":[{"en":"the sheer breadth of what it changed","context":"Q: What is the most important invention of the last century?\nA: I'd say the internet, though the transistor's a reasonable answer too, since nothing else would exist without it. The reason I'd choose the internet is the sheer breadth of what it changed — communication, commerce, education, entertainment and access to information all work in a fundamentally different way now. It's stopped being a product and become basic infrastructure, in much the same way as electricity or the road network. The honest problem with an answer this broad, though, is that it's a bit of a cheat — you could just as easily break it down into the specific technologies underneath it, like fibre optics or mobile networks, and credit those instead."},{"en":"basic infrastructure","context":"Q: What is the most important invention of the last century?\nA: I'd say the internet, though the transistor's a reasonable answer too, since nothing else would exist without it. The reason I'd choose the internet is the sheer breadth of what it changed — communication, commerce, education, entertainment and access to information all work in a fundamentally different way now. It's stopped being a product and become basic infrastructure, in much the same way as electricity or the road network. The honest problem with an answer this broad, though, is that it's a bit of a cheat — you could just as easily break it down into the specific technologies underneath it, like fibre optics or mobile networks, and credit those instead."},{"en":"a bit of a cheat","context":"Q: What is the most important invention of the last century?\nA: I'd say the internet, though the transistor's a reasonable answer too, since nothing else would exist without it. The reason I'd choose the internet is the sheer breadth of what it changed — communication, commerce, education, entertainment and access to information all work in a fundamentally different way now. It's stopped being a product and become basic infrastructure, in much the same way as electricity or the road network. The honest problem with an answer this broad, though, is that it's a bit of a cheat — you could just as easily break it down into the specific technologies underneath it, like fibre optics or mobile networks, and credit those instead."},{"en":"set firm boundaries","context":"Q: Can technology make life harder?\nA: Definitely. The obvious problem is distraction, since a device designed to save time ends up consuming your attention unless you set firm boundaries around it. Beyond that, there's the expectation of being permanently available, which has quietly extended the working day for a great many people. And there's a dependency issue: when a system fails, we often discover we've lost the ability to manage without it. The technology itself is fairly neutral, though; it's really the habits people build around it that matter."},{"en":"lost the ability to manage without it","context":"Q: Can technology make life harder?\nA: Definitely. The obvious problem is distraction, since a device designed to save time ends up consuming your attention unless you set firm boundaries around it. Beyond that, there's the expectation of being permanently available, which has quietly extended the working day for a great many people. And there's a dependency issue: when a system fails, we often discover we've lost the ability to manage without it. The technology itself is fairly neutral, though; it's really the habits people build around it that matter."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Technology","id":"speaking-part-3-085","_displayNo":"#085","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-085-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-085-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I would choose the internet because it has changed communication, shopping, learning and entertainment so widely. It now supports daily life like roads or electricity. However, that is a broad answer, since the internet depends on many other inventions, such as transistors and communication networks. Those deserve credit too, even if the internet is the change people notice most.","phrases":[{"en":"deserve credit","meaning":"Should be recognised for making an important contribution."}],"question":"What is the most important invention of the last century?","html":"I would choose the internet because it has changed communication, shopping, learning and entertainment so widely. It now supports daily life like roads or electricity. However, that is a broad answer, since the internet depends on many other inventions, such as transistors and communication networks. Those <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deserve credit</strong> too, even if the internet is the change people notice most."},{"text":"Yes. Devices can distract us, and being reachable all the time can extend the working day. We may also depend on systems so much that a failure leaves us unable to manage basic tasks. Technology can still be helpful, though. Clear boundaries and sensible habits often decide whether it saves time or makes life more complicated.","phrases":[{"en":"boundaries","meaning":"Limits that protect your time, privacy or well-being."}],"question":"Can technology make life harder?","html":"Yes. Devices can distract us, and being reachable all the time can extend the working day. We may also depend on systems so much that a failure leaves us unable to manage basic tasks. Technology can still be helpful, though. Clear <strong class=\"key-phrase\">boundaries</strong> and sensible habits often decide whether it saves time or makes life more complicated."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-085-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-085-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Life then and now, and the effect of electrical equipment","prompt":"What are the main differences between life in the past and life today?\nWhat are the impacts of electrical equipment on our life?\nHow will technology change our life in future?","answer":"Q: What are the main differences between life in the past and life today?\nA: The clearest difference is speed and choice. Information arrives instantly, travel takes hours rather than days, and household technology has removed a great deal of the physical labour that used to fill people&#x27;s time. What people had in the past was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a stronger local routine</strong> — you knew your neighbours and life followed a predictable rhythm. The trade-off is that they had far fewer options, and everyday tasks were exhausting in a way that&#x27;s hard to imagine now.\n\nQ: What are the impacts of electrical equipment on our life?\nA: Household appliances have transformed daily life more than people usually acknowledge, particularly for women, since washing and cooking once occupied most of the day. Refrigeration alone changed diet and health enormously by making it safe to store food. The costs are energy consumption and a kind of dependence — very few of us could manage a week without electricity. There&#x27;s also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the repair problem</strong>: modern appliances are sealed units, so when one fails, you replace it rather than fix it.\n\nQ: How will technology change our life in future?\nA: I&#x27;d expect automation to take over most routine decisions — scheduling, purchasing, basic diagnosis, the kind of thing a call-centre worker or a data-entry clerk does today — and services to become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far more personalised</strong>. The genuine questions aren&#x27;t technical, though. One is employment: if systems handle routine analytical work, many middle-income jobs disappear <strong class=\"key-phrase\">faster than people can retrain</strong>. The other is control. When a system decides who gets a loan or an interview, somebody needs to be able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">explain and challenge that decision</strong>.","topic":"Technology","phrases":[{"en":"a stronger local routine","context":"Q: What are the main differences between life in the past and life today?\nA: The clearest difference is speed and choice. Information arrives instantly, travel takes hours rather than days, and household technology has removed a great deal of the physical labour that used to fill people's time. What people had in the past was a stronger local routine — you knew your neighbours and life followed a predictable rhythm. The trade-off is that they had far fewer options, and everyday tasks were exhausting in a way that's hard to imagine now."},{"en":"the repair problem","context":"Q: What are the impacts of electrical equipment on our life?\nA: Household appliances have transformed daily life more than people usually acknowledge, particularly for women, since washing and cooking once occupied most of the day. Refrigeration alone changed diet and health enormously by making it safe to store food. The costs are energy consumption and a kind of dependence — very few of us could manage a week without electricity. There's also the repair problem: modern appliances are sealed units, so when one fails, you replace it rather than fix it."},{"en":"far more personalised","context":"Q: How will technology change our life in future?\nA: I'd expect automation to take over most routine decisions — scheduling, purchasing, basic diagnosis, the kind of thing a call-centre worker or a data-entry clerk does today — and services to become far more personalised. The genuine questions aren't technical, though. One is employment: if systems handle routine analytical work, many middle-income jobs disappear faster than people can retrain. The other is control. When a system decides who gets a loan or an interview, somebody needs to be able to explain and challenge that decision."},{"en":"faster than people can retrain","context":"Q: How will technology change our life in future?\nA: I'd expect automation to take over most routine decisions — scheduling, purchasing, basic diagnosis, the kind of thing a call-centre worker or a data-entry clerk does today — and services to become far more personalised. The genuine questions aren't technical, though. One is employment: if systems handle routine analytical work, many middle-income jobs disappear faster than people can retrain. The other is control. When a system decides who gets a loan or an interview, somebody needs to be able to explain and challenge that decision."},{"en":"explain and challenge that decision","context":"Q: How will technology change our life in future?\nA: I'd expect automation to take over most routine decisions — scheduling, purchasing, basic diagnosis, the kind of thing a call-centre worker or a data-entry clerk does today — and services to become far more personalised. The genuine questions aren't technical, though. One is employment: if systems handle routine analytical work, many middle-income jobs disappear faster than people can retrain. The other is control. When a system decides who gets a loan or an interview, somebody needs to be able to explain and challenge that decision."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Technology","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-086","_displayNo":"#086","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-086-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-086-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-086-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Life is faster and offers more choices now. Information arrives quickly, travel takes less time and machines do much household work. In the past, people often had stronger local routines and knew neighbours better. However, they also had fewer options and spent more time on tiring tasks. So I see benefits and losses rather than one period being completely better.","phrases":[{"en":"household work","meaning":"Tasks needed to run a home, such as cooking and cleaning."}],"question":"What are the main differences between life in the past and life today?","html":"Life is faster and offers more choices now. Information arrives quickly, travel takes less time and machines do much <strong class=\"key-phrase\">household work</strong>. In the past, people often had stronger local routines and knew neighbours better. However, they also had fewer options and spent more time on tiring tasks. So I see benefits and losses rather than one period being completely better."},{"text":"Appliances have saved enormous time and effort, particularly for those who did most cooking and washing. Refrigerators also make food storage safer and reduce waste. However, these machines use energy and make us dependent on electricity. Another problem is repair: some modern products are difficult to fix, so people replace them instead, creating extra cost and waste.","phrases":[{"en":"dependent on","meaning":"Needing something in order to function or manage."}],"question":"What are the impacts of electrical equipment on our life?","html":"Appliances have saved enormous time and effort, particularly for those who did most cooking and washing. Refrigerators also make food storage safer and reduce waste. However, these machines use energy and make us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dependent on</strong> electricity. Another problem is repair: some modern products are difficult to fix, so people replace them instead, creating extra cost and waste."},{"text":"I expect more routine tasks to become automatic and services to suit individual users more closely. The main difficulties may concern jobs and control rather than the technology itself. Workers may lose roles before retraining, and people need ways to question important automated decisions. A system choosing someone for a loan or interview should not be impossible to challenge.","phrases":[{"en":"automated decisions","meaning":"Choices made by a computer system with little or no direct human involvement."}],"question":"How will technology change our life in future?","html":"I expect more routine tasks to become automatic and services to suit individual users more closely. The main difficulties may concern jobs and control rather than the technology itself. Workers may lose roles before retraining, and people need ways to question important <strong class=\"key-phrase\">automated decisions</strong>. A system choosing someone for a loan or interview should not be impossible to challenge."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-086-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-086-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-086-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Inventions of the last fifty years and mobile phones","prompt":"What do you think are the most important inventions of the last 50 years?\nHow have mobile phones made communications easier?\nWhich household appliances do you think people need most?","answer":"Q: What do you think are the most important inventions of the last 50 years?\nA: The smartphone&#x27;s the obvious answer, because it collapsed a dozen separate devices into one — telephone, camera, map, bank, library and music player. For much of the world it was also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the first computer people ever owned</strong>. I&#x27;d add the internet itself, since the phone would be useless without it, and medical imaging, which transformed diagnosis. The smartphone stands out mainly because its effects reached almost everybody, including in poorer countries — though that&#x27;s not quite true even now, since plenty of people still can&#x27;t afford one or don&#x27;t have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reliable coverage</strong>, so the benefits are less universal than the answer makes them sound.\n\nQ: How have mobile phones made communications easier?\nA: They removed the two constraints that used to govern communication: location and cost. You can reach someone anywhere, instantly, and international calls that once cost a fortune are effectively free. For emergencies, and for families separated by migration, that&#x27;s genuinely transformative in a way older generations find hard to imagine. The side effect is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an expectation of immediate response</strong> — because people know you have the phone with you, not replying quickly is now read as rudeness.\n\nQ: Which household appliances do you think people need most?\nA: The refrigerator, without much question. It changed what people could safely eat, cut food waste enormously and reduced how often households had to shop. After that the washing machine, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">returned hours of every week</strong> to whoever was doing the laundry. Cooking appliances too, obviously. What these three share is that they replaced <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely heavy labour</strong> rather than merely adding convenience — though which one you&#x27;d rank first really depends on your circumstances, and someone hand-washing clothes for a big family would probably put the washing machine ahead of everything else.","topic":"Technology","phrases":[{"en":"the first computer people ever owned","context":"Q: What do you think are the most important inventions of the last 50 years?\nA: The smartphone's the obvious answer, because it collapsed a dozen separate devices into one — telephone, camera, map, bank, library and music player. For much of the world it was also the first computer people ever owned. I'd add the internet itself, since the phone would be useless without it, and medical imaging, which transformed diagnosis. The smartphone stands out mainly because its effects reached almost everybody, including in poorer countries — though that's not quite true even now, since plenty of people still can't afford one or don't have reliable coverage, so the benefits are less universal than the answer makes them sound."},{"en":"reliable coverage","context":"Q: What do you think are the most important inventions of the last 50 years?\nA: The smartphone's the obvious answer, because it collapsed a dozen separate devices into one — telephone, camera, map, bank, library and music player. For much of the world it was also the first computer people ever owned. I'd add the internet itself, since the phone would be useless without it, and medical imaging, which transformed diagnosis. The smartphone stands out mainly because its effects reached almost everybody, including in poorer countries — though that's not quite true even now, since plenty of people still can't afford one or don't have reliable coverage, so the benefits are less universal than the answer makes them sound."},{"en":"an expectation of immediate response","context":"Q: How have mobile phones made communications easier?\nA: They removed the two constraints that used to govern communication: location and cost. You can reach someone anywhere, instantly, and international calls that once cost a fortune are effectively free. For emergencies, and for families separated by migration, that's genuinely transformative in a way older generations find hard to imagine. The side effect is an expectation of immediate response — because people know you have the phone with you, not replying quickly is now read as rudeness."},{"en":"returned hours of every week","context":"Q: Which household appliances do you think people need most?\nA: The refrigerator, without much question. It changed what people could safely eat, cut food waste enormously and reduced how often households had to shop. After that the washing machine, which returned hours of every week to whoever was doing the laundry. Cooking appliances too, obviously. What these three share is that they replaced genuinely heavy labour rather than merely adding convenience — though which one you'd rank first really depends on your circumstances, and someone hand-washing clothes for a big family would probably put the washing machine ahead of everything else."},{"en":"genuinely heavy labour","context":"Q: Which household appliances do you think people need most?\nA: The refrigerator, without much question. It changed what people could safely eat, cut food waste enormously and reduced how often households had to shop. After that the washing machine, which returned hours of every week to whoever was doing the laundry. Cooking appliances too, obviously. What these three share is that they replaced genuinely heavy labour rather than merely adding convenience — though which one you'd rank first really depends on your circumstances, and someone hand-washing clothes for a big family would probably put the washing machine ahead of everything else."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Technology","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-087","_displayNo":"#087","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-087-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-087-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-087-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Smartphones stand out because they combine a phone, camera, map and many other tools. For many people, they were also the first computer they owned. I would include the internet and medical scanning too. However, the benefits are not universal: some people still lack affordable devices or a dependable connection, so these inventions have not reached everyone equally.","phrases":[{"en":"universal","meaning":"Applying to or available to everyone."}],"question":"What do you think are the most important inventions of the last 50 years?","html":"Smartphones stand out because they combine a phone, camera, map and many other tools. For many people, they were also the first computer they owned. I would include the internet and medical scanning too. However, the benefits are not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">universal</strong>: some people still lack affordable devices or a dependable connection, so these inventions have not reached everyone equally."},{"text":"They let us contact people quickly from almost anywhere, usually for much less than international calls once cost. This is especially useful in emergencies and for families living in different countries. However, easy contact creates pressure for immediate replies. People may see a slow response as rude because they assume the phone is always nearby.","phrases":[{"en":"immediate replies","meaning":"Answers sent straight away, without delay."}],"question":"How have mobile phones made communications easier?","html":"They let us contact people quickly from almost anywhere, usually for much less than international calls once cost. This is especially useful in emergencies and for families living in different countries. However, easy contact creates pressure for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immediate replies</strong>. People may see a slow response as rude because they assume the phone is always nearby."},{"text":"I'd put the refrigerator first because it keeps food safe, reduces waste and cuts shopping trips. Washing machines and cooking equipment are also essential because they replace heavy daily work. The order depends on the household, though. Someone washing clothes by hand for a large family might reasonably consider the washing machine the most important.","phrases":[{"en":"essential","meaning":"Extremely important or necessary."}],"question":"Which household appliances do you think people need most?","html":"I&#x27;d put the refrigerator first because it keeps food safe, reduces waste and cuts shopping trips. Washing machines and cooking equipment are also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">essential</strong> because they replace heavy daily work. The order depends on the household, though. Someone washing clothes by hand for a large family might reasonably consider the washing machine the most important."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-087-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-087-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-087-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How the internet will change our lives","prompt":"How do you think the internet will affect our lives in the future?","answer":"Q: How do you think the internet will affect our lives in the future?\nA: I think it&#x27;ll become almost invisible, in the sense that it&#x27;ll be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">built into everything</strong> rather than something we consciously log into. Healthcare, education, transport and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">government services</strong> are all moving online, and systems will increasingly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">anticipate what we need</strong> before we ask — a fridge that reorders milk before you run out, or a health app that flags a problem before you&#x27;d have noticed the symptoms yourself. The benefits are obvious, but there are two things societies will have to manage. One is privacy, because prediction requires collecting enormous quantities of personal data. The other is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dependence</strong> — if everything runs through a single network, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a failure or an attack</strong> becomes far more damaging than it would once have been.","topic":"Technology","phrases":[{"en":"built into everything","context":"Q: How do you think the internet will affect our lives in the future?\nA: I think it'll become almost invisible, in the sense that it'll be built into everything rather than something we consciously log into. Healthcare, education, transport and government services are all moving online, and systems will increasingly anticipate what we need before we ask — a fridge that reorders milk before you run out, or a health app that flags a problem before you'd have noticed the symptoms yourself. The benefits are obvious, but there are two things societies will have to manage. One is privacy, because prediction requires collecting enormous quantities of personal data. The other is dependence — if everything runs through a single network, a failure or an attack becomes far more damaging than it would once have been."},{"en":"government services","context":"Q: How do you think the internet will affect our lives in the future?\nA: I think it'll become almost invisible, in the sense that it'll be built into everything rather than something we consciously log into. Healthcare, education, transport and government services are all moving online, and systems will increasingly anticipate what we need before we ask — a fridge that reorders milk before you run out, or a health app that flags a problem before you'd have noticed the symptoms yourself. The benefits are obvious, but there are two things societies will have to manage. One is privacy, because prediction requires collecting enormous quantities of personal data. The other is dependence — if everything runs through a single network, a failure or an attack becomes far more damaging than it would once have been."},{"en":"anticipate what we need","context":"Q: How do you think the internet will affect our lives in the future?\nA: I think it'll become almost invisible, in the sense that it'll be built into everything rather than something we consciously log into. Healthcare, education, transport and government services are all moving online, and systems will increasingly anticipate what we need before we ask — a fridge that reorders milk before you run out, or a health app that flags a problem before you'd have noticed the symptoms yourself. The benefits are obvious, but there are two things societies will have to manage. One is privacy, because prediction requires collecting enormous quantities of personal data. The other is dependence — if everything runs through a single network, a failure or an attack becomes far more damaging than it would once have been."},{"en":"dependence","context":"Q: How do you think the internet will affect our lives in the future?\nA: I think it'll become almost invisible, in the sense that it'll be built into everything rather than something we consciously log into. Healthcare, education, transport and government services are all moving online, and systems will increasingly anticipate what we need before we ask — a fridge that reorders milk before you run out, or a health app that flags a problem before you'd have noticed the symptoms yourself. The benefits are obvious, but there are two things societies will have to manage. One is privacy, because prediction requires collecting enormous quantities of personal data. The other is dependence — if everything runs through a single network, a failure or an attack becomes far more damaging than it would once have been."},{"en":"a failure or an attack","context":"Q: How do you think the internet will affect our lives in the future?\nA: I think it'll become almost invisible, in the sense that it'll be built into everything rather than something we consciously log into. Healthcare, education, transport and government services are all moving online, and systems will increasingly anticipate what we need before we ask — a fridge that reorders milk before you run out, or a health app that flags a problem before you'd have noticed the symptoms yourself. The benefits are obvious, but there are two things societies will have to manage. One is privacy, because prediction requires collecting enormous quantities of personal data. The other is dependence — if everything runs through a single network, a failure or an attack becomes far more damaging than it would once have been."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Technology & Internet","id":"speaking-part-3-088","_displayNo":"#088","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-088-q01.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I think the internet will become built into more ordinary services, so people notice it less. Systems may order supplies automatically or warn of health problems earlier. That could be useful, but it requires collecting personal information. Greater dependence also means a breakdown or attack could affect more of life at once. Privacy and reliable systems will therefore become increasingly important.","phrases":[{"en":"breakdown","meaning":"A failure that stops a machine or system working."}],"question":"How do you think the internet will affect our lives in the future?","html":"I think the internet will become built into more ordinary services, so people notice it less. Systems may order supplies automatically or warn of health problems earlier. That could be useful, but it requires collecting personal information. Greater dependence also means a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">breakdown</strong> or attack could affect more of life at once. Privacy and reliable systems will therefore become increasingly important."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-088-q01.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Advertising online and how it changes what we buy","prompt":"What role does advertising play on the internet?\nHow do you think the internet will affect buying patterns in the future?\nWhat effect has the internet had on the way people generally communicate with each other?","answer":"Q: What role does advertising play on the internet?\nA: It funds almost everything people use for free — search, social media, email, video. That&#x27;s the bargain, though it&#x27;s not always made explicit: you&#x27;re not the customer, you&#x27;re what&#x27;s being sold. Targeting has become extraordinarily precise, which advertisers value and which makes many users uncomfortable, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the line between relevance and surveillance</strong> is genuinely thin. What I&#x27;d want is real control over the data rather than a consent box nobody reads — though I&#x27;ll admit most people, myself included, still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">click accept without reading it</strong>, so the market clearly isn&#x27;t demanding that control very loudly.\n\nQ: How do you think the internet will affect buying patterns in the future?\nA: Routine purchasing will become largely automatic — subscriptions and systems that reorder things before you notice you need them. For anything considered, people will rely more on reviews and comparison, which shifts power towards the consumer, though <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fake reviews undermine that considerably</strong>. Physical shops won&#x27;t disappear, but their role will change into places where you test or try something before buying it more cheaply elsewhere, which is a very difficult business model to sustain.\n\nQ: What effect has the internet had on the way people generally communicate with each other?\nA: It&#x27;s made communication immediate, cheap and global, which has obvious benefits. Two effects concern me, though. Messages have become shorter, so a lot of nuance goes missing and people misread tone constantly. And a great deal of communication is now <strong class=\"key-phrase\">public performance rather than conversation</strong> — people write for an audience, which changes what they&#x27;re willing to say. So we communicate far more often while arguably <strong class=\"key-phrase\">understanding each other less well</strong>.","topic":"Technology","phrases":[{"en":"the line between relevance and surveillance","context":"Q: What role does advertising play on the internet?\nA: It funds almost everything people use for free — search, social media, email, video. That's the bargain, though it's not always made explicit: you're not the customer, you're what's being sold. Targeting has become extraordinarily precise, which advertisers value and which makes many users uncomfortable, because the line between relevance and surveillance is genuinely thin. What I'd want is real control over the data rather than a consent box nobody reads — though I'll admit most people, myself included, still click accept without reading it, so the market clearly isn't demanding that control very loudly."},{"en":"click accept without reading it","context":"Q: What role does advertising play on the internet?\nA: It funds almost everything people use for free — search, social media, email, video. That's the bargain, though it's not always made explicit: you're not the customer, you're what's being sold. Targeting has become extraordinarily precise, which advertisers value and which makes many users uncomfortable, because the line between relevance and surveillance is genuinely thin. What I'd want is real control over the data rather than a consent box nobody reads — though I'll admit most people, myself included, still click accept without reading it, so the market clearly isn't demanding that control very loudly."},{"en":"fake reviews undermine that considerably","context":"Q: How do you think the internet will affect buying patterns in the future?\nA: Routine purchasing will become largely automatic — subscriptions and systems that reorder things before you notice you need them. For anything considered, people will rely more on reviews and comparison, which shifts power towards the consumer, though fake reviews undermine that considerably. Physical shops won't disappear, but their role will change into places where you test or try something before buying it more cheaply elsewhere, which is a very difficult business model to sustain."},{"en":"public performance rather than conversation","context":"Q: What effect has the internet had on the way people generally communicate with each other?\nA: It's made communication immediate, cheap and global, which has obvious benefits. Two effects concern me, though. Messages have become shorter, so a lot of nuance goes missing and people misread tone constantly. And a great deal of communication is now public performance rather than conversation — people write for an audience, which changes what they're willing to say. So we communicate far more often while arguably understanding each other less well."},{"en":"understanding each other less well","context":"Q: What effect has the internet had on the way people generally communicate with each other?\nA: It's made communication immediate, cheap and global, which has obvious benefits. Two effects concern me, though. Messages have become shorter, so a lot of nuance goes missing and people misread tone constantly. And a great deal of communication is now public performance rather than conversation — people write for an audience, which changes what they're willing to say. So we communicate far more often while arguably understanding each other less well."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Technology & Internet","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-089","_displayNo":"#089","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-089-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-089-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-089-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Advertising pays for many free services, such as search, email and videos. Companies value being able to reach very specific users, but this often involves collecting personal data. I would like people to have more meaningful control over that information. However, many of us accept conditions without reading them, so we do not always act on our concerns.","phrases":[{"en":"personal data","meaning":"Information connected with an identifiable individual."}],"question":"What role does advertising play on the internet?","html":"Advertising pays for many free services, such as search, email and videos. Companies value being able to reach very specific users, but this often involves collecting <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personal data</strong>. I would like people to have more meaningful control over that information. However, many of us accept conditions without reading them, so we do not always act on our concerns."},{"text":"Everyday purchases may become more automatic through subscriptions or systems that reorder items. For bigger purchases, reviews and comparisons can help buyers, although fake reviews create problems. Physical shops may become places to try products before ordering elsewhere. That gives consumers options, but makes it harder for shops to earn enough from providing the experience.","phrases":[{"en":"comparisons","meaning":"Ways of examining different options to understand their advantages and disadvantages."}],"question":"How do you think the internet will affect buying patterns in the future?","html":"Everyday purchases may become more automatic through subscriptions or systems that reorder items. For bigger purchases, reviews and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comparisons</strong> can help buyers, although fake reviews create problems. Physical shops may become places to try products before ordering elsewhere. That gives consumers options, but makes it harder for shops to earn enough from providing the experience."},{"text":"It makes contact quicker, cheaper and global, which is very useful. However, short messages can lose meaning or tone and create misunderstandings. Online communication is also sometimes written for an audience rather than one person, making people less open. We may send far more messages without necessarily understanding each other better, especially in sensitive conversations.","phrases":[{"en":"sensitive conversations","meaning":"Discussions where feelings or personal issues need careful handling."}],"question":"What effect has the internet had on the way people generally communicate with each other?","html":"It makes contact quicker, cheaper and global, which is very useful. However, short messages can lose meaning or tone and create misunderstandings. Online communication is also sometimes written for an audience rather than one person, making people less open. We may send far more messages without necessarily understanding each other better, especially in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sensitive conversations</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-089-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-089-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-089-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Public transport against the car","prompt":"Do you prefer travelling by public transport or by car?\nWhat are the benefits of travelling by public transport?\nIn your opinion, in what ways can governments encourage people to use public transport?","answer":"Q: Do you prefer travelling by public transport or by car?\nA: Public transport, when it&#x27;s reliable — and that qualification matters. The main advantage is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the time is usable</strong>: I can read or work rather than concentrating on traffic, so an hour on a train isn&#x27;t really lost. It&#x27;s also cheaper once you count fuel, parking and maintenance. I&#x27;d choose a car for journeys poorly served by public routes, or when I&#x27;m carrying things, or travelling late at night, or when I&#x27;m heading somewhere with a young child and a lot of luggage, since the flexibility of a car just makes that kind of trip easier.\n\nQ: What are the benefits of travelling by public transport?\nA: The main one is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">efficiency of space</strong>. A single bus replaces perhaps fifty cars, so congestion, pollution and the amount of land given over to roads and parking all fall dramatically. There&#x27;s a household benefit too, since running a car is one of the largest expenses most families have. And it provides <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuine independence</strong> to people who can&#x27;t drive — the elderly, teenagers and anyone with a disability — which is easy to overlook if you&#x27;ve always had a car. None of this works, though, if the service itself is unreliable — the efficiency is only theoretical if the bus never turns up on time.\n\nQ: In your opinion, in what ways can governments encourage people to use public transport?\nA: By making it genuinely competitive, which means frequency above all, since people will accept a great deal if they never have to check a timetable, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">integrated ticketing</strong> across buses and trains matters, as does safety, particularly at night. Beyond that, some restriction on driving is usually necessary, such as congestion charges. But imposing the restriction first, before the alternative&#x27;s actually good, simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes people resentful</strong> and turns the whole policy into a political liability.","topic":"Transport","phrases":[{"en":"the time is usable","context":"Q: Do you prefer travelling by public transport or by car?\nA: Public transport, when it's reliable — and that qualification matters. The main advantage is that the time is usable: I can read or work rather than concentrating on traffic, so an hour on a train isn't really lost. It's also cheaper once you count fuel, parking and maintenance. I'd choose a car for journeys poorly served by public routes, or when I'm carrying things, or travelling late at night, or when I'm heading somewhere with a young child and a lot of luggage, since the flexibility of a car just makes that kind of trip easier."},{"en":"efficiency of space","context":"Q: What are the benefits of travelling by public transport?\nA: The main one is efficiency of space. A single bus replaces perhaps fifty cars, so congestion, pollution and the amount of land given over to roads and parking all fall dramatically. There's a household benefit too, since running a car is one of the largest expenses most families have. And it provides genuine independence to people who can't drive — the elderly, teenagers and anyone with a disability — which is easy to overlook if you've always had a car. None of this works, though, if the service itself is unreliable — the efficiency is only theoretical if the bus never turns up on time."},{"en":"genuine independence","context":"Q: What are the benefits of travelling by public transport?\nA: The main one is efficiency of space. A single bus replaces perhaps fifty cars, so congestion, pollution and the amount of land given over to roads and parking all fall dramatically. There's a household benefit too, since running a car is one of the largest expenses most families have. And it provides genuine independence to people who can't drive — the elderly, teenagers and anyone with a disability — which is easy to overlook if you've always had a car. None of this works, though, if the service itself is unreliable — the efficiency is only theoretical if the bus never turns up on time."},{"en":"integrated ticketing","context":"Q: In your opinion, in what ways can governments encourage people to use public transport?\nA: By making it genuinely competitive, which means frequency above all, since people will accept a great deal if they never have to check a timetable, and integrated ticketing across buses and trains matters, as does safety, particularly at night. Beyond that, some restriction on driving is usually necessary, such as congestion charges. But imposing the restriction first, before the alternative's actually good, simply makes people resentful and turns the whole policy into a political liability."},{"en":"makes people resentful","context":"Q: In your opinion, in what ways can governments encourage people to use public transport?\nA: By making it genuinely competitive, which means frequency above all, since people will accept a great deal if they never have to check a timetable, and integrated ticketing across buses and trains matters, as does safety, particularly at night. Beyond that, some restriction on driving is usually necessary, such as congestion charges. But imposing the restriction first, before the alternative's actually good, simply makes people resentful and turns the whole policy into a political liability."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Transport","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-090","_displayNo":"#090","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-090-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-090-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-090-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I prefer public transport when it is reliable. I can read or work instead of concentrating on driving, and it often costs less after parking and maintenance are included. However, a car is more practical for poorly served places, late journeys or carrying a child and lots of luggage. My choice depends on the actual trip.","phrases":[{"en":"maintenance","meaning":"Work needed to keep something in good condition."}],"question":"Do you prefer travelling by public transport or by car?","html":"I prefer public transport when it is reliable. I can read or work instead of concentrating on driving, and it often costs less after parking and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maintenance</strong> are included. However, a car is more practical for poorly served places, late journeys or carrying a child and lots of luggage. My choice depends on the actual trip."},{"text":"Public transport moves many people using less road and parking space. It can reduce traffic and pollution, save households money and provide independence for people who cannot drive. However, those benefits depend on the service working properly. A bus that is unreliable may look efficient on paper but will not persuade people to leave their cars behind.","phrases":[{"en":"on paper","meaning":"In theory or according to a plan, rather than in real practice."}],"question":"What are the benefits of travelling by public transport?","html":"Public transport moves many people using less road and parking space. It can reduce traffic and pollution, save households money and provide independence for people who cannot drive. However, those benefits depend on the service working properly. A bus that is unreliable may look efficient <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on paper</strong> but will not persuade people to leave their cars behind."},{"text":"First, make services frequent, safe and easy to use, with tickets that work across different transport types. People need a realistic alternative before restrictions on driving are introduced. Charges may help afterwards, but applying them first can make people angry without changing travel habits. Good service should be the main reason to switch, not simply punishment for driving.","phrases":[{"en":"switch","meaning":"Change from one choice or method to another."}],"question":"In your opinion, in what ways can governments encourage people to use public transport?","html":"First, make services frequent, safe and easy to use, with tickets that work across different transport types. People need a realistic alternative before restrictions on driving are introduced. Charges may help afterwards, but applying them first can make people angry without changing travel habits. Good service should be the main reason to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">switch</strong>, not simply punishment for driving."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-090-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-090-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-090-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Getting to work, and how transport has improved","prompt":"Do you think getting to work is easier or more difficult than it used to be?\nHow have methods of transport improved?\nAre there any problems that have been created by new transport systems?","answer":"Q: Do you think getting to work is easier or more difficult than it used to be?\nA: It depends almost entirely on where you live. In cities with new metro lines it&#x27;s genuinely easier — you can check live times, plan a route on your phone and know within a few minutes when you&#x27;ll arrive, which removed a lot of the old uncertainty. But congestion has grown faster than the infrastructure in most places, and cities have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spread outwards</strong>, so people now commute much further than their parents did. My honest impression is that the technology improved while the journey itself got longer.\n\nQ: How have methods of transport improved?\nA: Safety is the clearest one — cars have airbags, crumple zones and automatic braking, and road deaths have fallen substantially in most developed countries despite far more traffic. Vehicles are also cleaner, with electric buses replacing diesel in a lot of cities. The less visible improvement is coordination: digital ticketing, live arrival information and route planning have made journeys <strong class=\"key-phrase\">predictable in a way they simply were not</strong> twenty years ago. Comfort hasn&#x27;t improved anywhere near as fast, but on the essentials, transport keeps getting better.\n\nQ: Are there any problems that have been created by new transport systems?\nA: Yes, and some are counter-intuitive. Building new roads tends to generate additional traffic rather than relieving it, because easier driving encourages more of it — engineers call this <strong class=\"key-phrase\">induced demand</strong>. Major roads also cut neighbourhoods in half, and the communities affected are usually the poorest. Ride-hailing has had a similar effect, adding vehicles to congested streets while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drawing passengers away from public transport</strong>. So a project that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">looks like progress on paper</strong> — a wider road, a new app — can still leave a city worse off once you look at the wider effects rather than just journey times.","topic":"Transport","phrases":[{"en":"spread outwards","context":"Q: Do you think getting to work is easier or more difficult than it used to be?\nA: It depends almost entirely on where you live. In cities with new metro lines it's genuinely easier — you can check live times, plan a route on your phone and know within a few minutes when you'll arrive, which removed a lot of the old uncertainty. But congestion has grown faster than the infrastructure in most places, and cities have spread outwards, so people now commute much further than their parents did. My honest impression is that the technology improved while the journey itself got longer."},{"en":"predictable in a way they simply were not","context":"Q: How have methods of transport improved?\nA: Safety is the clearest one — cars have airbags, crumple zones and automatic braking, and road deaths have fallen substantially in most developed countries despite far more traffic. Vehicles are also cleaner, with electric buses replacing diesel in a lot of cities. The less visible improvement is coordination: digital ticketing, live arrival information and route planning have made journeys predictable in a way they simply were not twenty years ago. Comfort hasn't improved anywhere near as fast, but on the essentials, transport keeps getting better."},{"en":"induced demand","context":"Q: Are there any problems that have been created by new transport systems?\nA: Yes, and some are counter-intuitive. Building new roads tends to generate additional traffic rather than relieving it, because easier driving encourages more of it — engineers call this induced demand. Major roads also cut neighbourhoods in half, and the communities affected are usually the poorest. Ride-hailing has had a similar effect, adding vehicles to congested streets while drawing passengers away from public transport. So a project that looks like progress on paper — a wider road, a new app — can still leave a city worse off once you look at the wider effects rather than just journey times."},{"en":"drawing passengers away from public transport","context":"Q: Are there any problems that have been created by new transport systems?\nA: Yes, and some are counter-intuitive. Building new roads tends to generate additional traffic rather than relieving it, because easier driving encourages more of it — engineers call this induced demand. Major roads also cut neighbourhoods in half, and the communities affected are usually the poorest. Ride-hailing has had a similar effect, adding vehicles to congested streets while drawing passengers away from public transport. So a project that looks like progress on paper — a wider road, a new app — can still leave a city worse off once you look at the wider effects rather than just journey times."},{"en":"looks like progress on paper","context":"Q: Are there any problems that have been created by new transport systems?\nA: Yes, and some are counter-intuitive. Building new roads tends to generate additional traffic rather than relieving it, because easier driving encourages more of it — engineers call this induced demand. Major roads also cut neighbourhoods in half, and the communities affected are usually the poorest. Ride-hailing has had a similar effect, adding vehicles to congested streets while drawing passengers away from public transport. So a project that looks like progress on paper — a wider road, a new app — can still leave a city worse off once you look at the wider effects rather than just journey times."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Transport","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-091","_displayNo":"#091","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-091-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-091-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-091-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"It depends on the place. New metro lines and live travel information can make commuting easier to plan. However, traffic has grown and cities have spread, so many people travel further than their parents did. My impression is that planning tools have improved while the journey itself may still take longer, especially where transport investment has not kept up.","phrases":[{"en":"commuting","meaning":"Regular travel between home and work or study."}],"question":"Do you think getting to work is easier or more difficult than it used to be?","html":"It depends on the place. New metro lines and live travel information can make <strong class=\"key-phrase\">commuting</strong> easier to plan. However, traffic has grown and cities have spread, so many people travel further than their parents did. My impression is that planning tools have improved while the journey itself may still take longer, especially where transport investment has not kept up."},{"text":"Vehicles have better safety features, such as airbags and automatic braking, and many are becoming cleaner. Digital tickets, route planning and live information also reduce uncertainty about a journey. Comfort may not have improved as quickly, but knowing how to travel and when to arrive is much easier. I think safety and coordination are the biggest gains.","phrases":[{"en":"coordination","meaning":"Organising different parts so they work well together."}],"question":"How have methods of transport improved?","html":"Vehicles have better safety features, such as airbags and automatic braking, and many are becoming cleaner. Digital tickets, route planning and live information also reduce uncertainty about a journey. Comfort may not have improved as quickly, but knowing how to travel and when to arrive is much easier. I think safety and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">coordination</strong> are the biggest gains."},{"text":"Yes. More roads can encourage extra driving instead of permanently reducing traffic. Large roads may also divide neighbourhoods, often affecting poorer communities. Ride-hailing can add vehicles and draw people away from buses. So a system that seems convenient for one journey may create wider problems. Governments need to examine the overall effects, not only individual travel times.","phrases":[{"en":"Ride-hailing","meaning":"Using an app or service to request a car and driver."}],"question":"Are there any problems that have been created by new transport systems?","html":"Yes. More roads can encourage extra driving instead of permanently reducing traffic. Large roads may also divide neighbourhoods, often affecting poorer communities. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Ride-hailing</strong> can add vehicles and draw people away from buses. So a system that seems convenient for one journey may create wider problems. Governments need to examine the overall effects, not only individual travel times."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-091-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-091-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-091-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Changes in transport and its future","prompt":"How has transport changed in recent decades?\nWill public transport become more popular?","answer":"Q: How has transport changed in recent decades?\nA: The biggest changes are speed and information. Journeys that once took a day now take a couple of hours, and more importantly you can compare routes, check live delays and book everything from a phone, which has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removed much of the uncertainty</strong> that used to come with travelling. The drawback is that private car ownership has risen enormously over the same period, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">urban congestion</strong> in most cities is considerably worse than it was thirty years ago.\n\nQ: Will public transport become more popular?\nA: It will, but only where it&#x27;s genuinely good. People switch when the service is frequent, clean and cheaper than driving — that&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the deciding factor</strong>, not environmental concern. Cities such as Vienna and Singapore have shown that if you invest properly, people use it willingly. Where governments simply raise parking charges without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improving the alternative</strong>, drivers complain and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carry on driving</strong> anyway. Reliability matters more than speed, in my experience — people will accept a slower journey if they can predict it.","topic":"Transport","phrases":[{"en":"removed much of the uncertainty","context":"Q: How has transport changed in recent decades?\nA: The biggest changes are speed and information. Journeys that once took a day now take a couple of hours, and more importantly you can compare routes, check live delays and book everything from a phone, which has removed much of the uncertainty that used to come with travelling. The drawback is that private car ownership has risen enormously over the same period, so urban congestion in most cities is considerably worse than it was thirty years ago."},{"en":"urban congestion","context":"Q: How has transport changed in recent decades?\nA: The biggest changes are speed and information. Journeys that once took a day now take a couple of hours, and more importantly you can compare routes, check live delays and book everything from a phone, which has removed much of the uncertainty that used to come with travelling. The drawback is that private car ownership has risen enormously over the same period, so urban congestion in most cities is considerably worse than it was thirty years ago."},{"en":"the deciding factor","context":"Q: Will public transport become more popular?\nA: It will, but only where it's genuinely good. People switch when the service is frequent, clean and cheaper than driving — that's the deciding factor, not environmental concern. Cities such as Vienna and Singapore have shown that if you invest properly, people use it willingly. Where governments simply raise parking charges without improving the alternative, drivers complain and carry on driving anyway. Reliability matters more than speed, in my experience — people will accept a slower journey if they can predict it."},{"en":"improving the alternative","context":"Q: Will public transport become more popular?\nA: It will, but only where it's genuinely good. People switch when the service is frequent, clean and cheaper than driving — that's the deciding factor, not environmental concern. Cities such as Vienna and Singapore have shown that if you invest properly, people use it willingly. Where governments simply raise parking charges without improving the alternative, drivers complain and carry on driving anyway. Reliability matters more than speed, in my experience — people will accept a slower journey if they can predict it."},{"en":"carry on driving","context":"Q: Will public transport become more popular?\nA: It will, but only where it's genuinely good. People switch when the service is frequent, clean and cheaper than driving — that's the deciding factor, not environmental concern. Cities such as Vienna and Singapore have shown that if you invest properly, people use it willingly. Where governments simply raise parking charges without improving the alternative, drivers complain and carry on driving anyway. Reliability matters more than speed, in my experience — people will accept a slower journey if they can predict it."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Transport","id":"speaking-part-3-092","_displayNo":"#092","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-092-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-092-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Travel has become faster, and information is much easier to get. People can compare routes, check delays and book through a phone, making trips more predictable. However, private car ownership has also increased. That means city traffic can be worse despite improvements in vehicles and planning tools, so progress has not removed every difficulty.","phrases":[{"en":"predictable","meaning":"Able to be expected or planned with reasonable confidence."}],"question":"How has transport changed in recent decades?","html":"Travel has become faster, and information is much easier to get. People can compare routes, check delays and book through a phone, making trips more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">predictable</strong>. However, private car ownership has also increased. That means city traffic can be worse despite improvements in vehicles and planning tools, so progress has not removed every difficulty."},{"text":"Yes, where services are frequent, clean, affordable and dependable. People usually change because transport works well for daily life, not just because it is greener. Higher parking fees alone will not be enough if the alternative is poor. In my experience, a journey people can predict may matter more than a faster one that is often delayed.","phrases":[{"en":"dependable","meaning":"Able to be trusted to work as expected."}],"question":"Will public transport become more popular?","html":"Yes, where services are frequent, clean, affordable and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dependable</strong>. People usually change because transport works well for daily life, not just because it is greener. Higher parking fees alone will not be enough if the alternative is poor. In my experience, a journey people can predict may matter more than a faster one that is often delayed."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-092-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-092-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Recommending a place and sightseeing in general","prompt":"Would you recommend the place you described to other people?\nDo you enjoy sightseeing in general?\nHave you visited a similar place before?","answer":"Q: Would you recommend the place you described to other people?\nA: Yes, but with a couple of conditions. I&#x27;d recommend it strongly to anyone interested in culture and landscape rather than nightlife, because that&#x27;s really what it offers. I&#x27;d also tell them to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">avoid the peak season</strong> if they possibly can — it gets so crowded that you lose the very thing you went for. And I&#x27;d mention respecting local customs, since places like that are damaged quite quickly when visitor numbers rise faster than the infrastructure can cope with.\n\nQ: Do you enjoy sightseeing in general?\nA: I do, though my approach has changed a great deal. I used to try to see everything, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">working through a list</strong> and photographing each item, and honestly I remember very little of it now. These days I&#x27;d rather visit three places properly than fifteen in a rush. I like reading some background beforehand so I understand what I&#x27;m looking at, and I try to leave time simply to walk around and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get a feel for</strong> how people actually live there — though I know plenty of people who prefer the list approach, and there&#x27;s nothing wrong with that if it&#x27;s genuinely what they enjoy.\n\nQ: Have you visited a similar place before?\nA: Yes, a few with comparable scenery, but what struck me was how different they felt despite the surface similarity. The scale was different, the food was different, and above all the way local people related to visitors varied enormously — some places felt genuinely welcoming, others clearly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resented the tourism</strong>. Comparing them was useful, because it made me notice what made each one distinctive rather than treating them as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">interchangeable destinations</strong> — though a comparison only takes you so far, and I try not to let one trip set my expectations for the next.","topic":"Travel & Tourism","phrases":[{"en":"avoid the peak season","context":"Q: Would you recommend the place you described to other people?\nA: Yes, but with a couple of conditions. I'd recommend it strongly to anyone interested in culture and landscape rather than nightlife, because that's really what it offers. I'd also tell them to avoid the peak season if they possibly can — it gets so crowded that you lose the very thing you went for. And I'd mention respecting local customs, since places like that are damaged quite quickly when visitor numbers rise faster than the infrastructure can cope with."},{"en":"working through a list","context":"Q: Do you enjoy sightseeing in general?\nA: I do, though my approach has changed a great deal. I used to try to see everything, working through a list and photographing each item, and honestly I remember very little of it now. These days I'd rather visit three places properly than fifteen in a rush. I like reading some background beforehand so I understand what I'm looking at, and I try to leave time simply to walk around and get a feel for how people actually live there — though I know plenty of people who prefer the list approach, and there's nothing wrong with that if it's genuinely what they enjoy."},{"en":"get a feel for","context":"Q: Do you enjoy sightseeing in general?\nA: I do, though my approach has changed a great deal. I used to try to see everything, working through a list and photographing each item, and honestly I remember very little of it now. These days I'd rather visit three places properly than fifteen in a rush. I like reading some background beforehand so I understand what I'm looking at, and I try to leave time simply to walk around and get a feel for how people actually live there — though I know plenty of people who prefer the list approach, and there's nothing wrong with that if it's genuinely what they enjoy."},{"en":"resented the tourism","context":"Q: Have you visited a similar place before?\nA: Yes, a few with comparable scenery, but what struck me was how different they felt despite the surface similarity. The scale was different, the food was different, and above all the way local people related to visitors varied enormously — some places felt genuinely welcoming, others clearly resented the tourism. Comparing them was useful, because it made me notice what made each one distinctive rather than treating them as interchangeable destinations — though a comparison only takes you so far, and I try not to let one trip set my expectations for the next."},{"en":"interchangeable destinations","context":"Q: Have you visited a similar place before?\nA: Yes, a few with comparable scenery, but what struck me was how different they felt despite the surface similarity. The scale was different, the food was different, and above all the way local people related to visitors varied enormously — some places felt genuinely welcoming, others clearly resented the tourism. Comparing them was useful, because it made me notice what made each one distinctive rather than treating them as interchangeable destinations — though a comparison only takes you so far, and I try not to let one trip set my expectations for the next."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Travel & Tourism","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-093","_displayNo":"#093","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-093-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-093-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-093-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, especially to people interested in culture and scenery rather than nightlife. I would suggest avoiding the busiest season because crowds can reduce the experience. Visitors should also respect local customs. If tourism grows faster than facilities can manage, a place can lose the qualities that made it attractive in the first place.","phrases":[{"en":"scenery","meaning":"The natural views and appearance of a place."}],"question":"Would you recommend the place you described to other people?","html":"Yes, especially to people interested in culture and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">scenery</strong> rather than nightlife. I would suggest avoiding the busiest season because crowds can reduce the experience. Visitors should also respect local customs. If tourism grows faster than facilities can manage, a place can lose the qualities that made it attractive in the first place."},{"text":"Yes, but I now prefer seeing fewer places properly. I used to rush through a long list and remembered very little. Reading some background and leaving time to walk around helps me understand how people live. Others enjoy a faster checklist approach, which is fine, but slower visits are more meaningful for me.","phrases":[{"en":"meaningful","meaning":"Having personal value or importance."}],"question":"Do you enjoy sightseeing in general?","html":"Yes, but I now prefer seeing fewer places properly. I used to rush through a long list and remembered very little. Reading some background and leaving time to walk around helps me understand how people live. Others enjoy a faster checklist approach, which is fine, but slower visits are more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meaningful</strong> for me."},{"text":"Yes, I have visited places with similar scenery, but they felt different because of the scale, food and local attitudes towards visitors. Comparing them helped me notice each place's character. However, I try not to let one trip set every expectation for the next. Similar-looking destinations can still offer quite different experiences.","phrases":[{"en":"destinations","meaning":"Places people travel to."}],"question":"Have you visited a similar place before?","html":"Yes, I have visited places with similar scenery, but they felt different because of the scale, food and local attitudes towards visitors. Comparing them helped me notice each place&#x27;s character. However, I try not to let one trip set every expectation for the next. Similar-looking <strong class=\"key-phrase\">destinations</strong> can still offer quite different experiences."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-093-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-093-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-093-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Technology at work and whether it costs jobs","prompt":"Which technologies are commonly used in workplaces?\nDoes technology make work easier?\nDoes technology cause people to lose their jobs?","answer":"Q: Which technologies are commonly used in workplaces?\nA: Most workplaces run on a fairly standard set of equipment — computers, phones, printers and photocopiers, with fax machines still hanging on in a few offices. The computer&#x27;s probably the most <strong class=\"key-phrase\">essential piece of equipment</strong> because we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rely on it for almost everything</strong>: email, reports, organising data, finding information online. Video calls have become just as essential now that teams are spread across different offices, and shared documents in the cloud have largely replaced sending files back and forth. The one exception is anything hands-on — a mechanic or an electrician still depends far more on physical tools than on a laptop.\n\nQ: Does technology make work easier?\nA: It does, on balance, because it removes a lot of friction from ordinary tasks. Email alone means a report or a query reaches a colleague in a different office, or a different country, in seconds rather than days. On the other hand, technology can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make life more difficult</strong> too, especially when it goes wrong — a dead internet connection or a crashed computer can bring an entire office to a stop for an afternoon. It also means work follows you home, because the same tools that let you work anywhere let your employer reach you anywhere.\n\nQ: Does technology cause people to lose their jobs?\nA: I think technology&#x27;s often responsible for people losing their jobs. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Machines have replaced people</strong> in areas like manufacturing and agriculture, and whenever a new technology&#x27;s introduced, there are redundancies. At the same time, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">jobs might be created</strong> thanks to a new technology — there&#x27;d be no computer programmers if the computer hadn&#x27;t been invented. The real problem is timing. Jobs disappear quickly and new ones appear slowly, and they rarely go to the same people, so an individual can be displaced permanently even while total employment holds up.","topic":"Work","phrases":[{"en":"essential piece of equipment","context":"Q: Which technologies are commonly used in workplaces?\nA: Most workplaces run on a fairly standard set of equipment — computers, phones, printers and photocopiers, with fax machines still hanging on in a few offices. The computer's probably the most essential piece of equipment because we rely on it for almost everything: email, reports, organising data, finding information online. Video calls have become just as essential now that teams are spread across different offices, and shared documents in the cloud have largely replaced sending files back and forth. The one exception is anything hands-on — a mechanic or an electrician still depends far more on physical tools than on a laptop."},{"en":"rely on it for almost everything","context":"Q: Which technologies are commonly used in workplaces?\nA: Most workplaces run on a fairly standard set of equipment — computers, phones, printers and photocopiers, with fax machines still hanging on in a few offices. The computer's probably the most essential piece of equipment because we rely on it for almost everything: email, reports, organising data, finding information online. Video calls have become just as essential now that teams are spread across different offices, and shared documents in the cloud have largely replaced sending files back and forth. The one exception is anything hands-on — a mechanic or an electrician still depends far more on physical tools than on a laptop."},{"en":"make life more difficult","context":"Q: Does technology make work easier?\nA: It does, on balance, because it removes a lot of friction from ordinary tasks. Email alone means a report or a query reaches a colleague in a different office, or a different country, in seconds rather than days. On the other hand, technology can make life more difficult too, especially when it goes wrong — a dead internet connection or a crashed computer can bring an entire office to a stop for an afternoon. It also means work follows you home, because the same tools that let you work anywhere let your employer reach you anywhere."},{"en":"Machines have replaced people","context":"Q: Does technology cause people to lose their jobs?\nA: I think technology's often responsible for people losing their jobs. Machines have replaced people in areas like manufacturing and agriculture, and whenever a new technology's introduced, there are redundancies. At the same time, jobs might be created thanks to a new technology — there'd be no computer programmers if the computer hadn't been invented. The real problem is timing. Jobs disappear quickly and new ones appear slowly, and they rarely go to the same people, so an individual can be displaced permanently even while total employment holds up."},{"en":"jobs might be created","context":"Q: Does technology cause people to lose their jobs?\nA: I think technology's often responsible for people losing their jobs. Machines have replaced people in areas like manufacturing and agriculture, and whenever a new technology's introduced, there are redundancies. At the same time, jobs might be created thanks to a new technology — there'd be no computer programmers if the computer hadn't been invented. The real problem is timing. Jobs disappear quickly and new ones appear slowly, and they rarely go to the same people, so an individual can be displaced permanently even while total employment holds up."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Work","id":"speaking-part-3-094","_displayNo":"#094","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-094-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-094-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-094-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Computers, phones, printers and shared online documents are common in offices. Computers support emails, reports, data and research, while video calls connect teams in different locations. However, needs vary by job. A mechanic or electrician still depends mainly on physical tools, so an office's most important technology is not necessarily essential in every workplace.","phrases":[{"en":"shared online documents","meaning":"Files that several people can access or edit through the internet."}],"question":"Which technologies are commonly used in workplaces?","html":"Computers, phones, printers and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shared online documents</strong> are common in offices. Computers support emails, reports, data and research, while video calls connect teams in different locations. However, needs vary by job. A mechanic or electrician still depends mainly on physical tools, so an office&#x27;s most important technology is not necessarily essential in every workplace."},{"text":"Overall, yes. Technology makes ordinary tasks quicker, such as sending a report to another country in seconds. However, an internet failure or computer crash can stop work completely. It also allows employers to contact staff at home. So it improves efficiency but can bring dependence and make it harder to separate work from personal time.","phrases":[{"en":"efficiency","meaning":"Doing work with less wasted time or effort."}],"question":"Does technology make work easier?","html":"Overall, yes. Technology makes ordinary tasks quicker, such as sending a report to another country in seconds. However, an internet failure or computer crash can stop work completely. It also allows employers to contact staff at home. So it improves <strong class=\"key-phrase\">efficiency</strong> but can bring dependence and make it harder to separate work from personal time."},{"text":"Yes, machines and software can replace jobs in areas such as farming and manufacturing. New technology also creates roles, including programming. The problem is that these changes do not happen at the same speed or help the same people. A worker may lose a job long before they can train for one of the new opportunities.","phrases":[{"en":"replace jobs","meaning":"Perform work that people were previously employed to do."}],"question":"Does technology cause people to lose their jobs?","html":"Yes, machines and software can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">replace jobs</strong> in areas such as farming and manufacturing. New technology also creates roles, including programming. The problem is that these changes do not happen at the same speed or help the same people. A worker may lose a job long before they can train for one of the new opportunities."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-094-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-094-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-094-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Preparing for an interview, and dangerous jobs","prompt":"What is the best way to prepare for a job interview?\nWhich jobs do you think are the most dangerous?\nHow can employers keep their staff happy?","answer":"Q: What is the best way to prepare for a job interview?\nA: Research is the foundation — understanding what the organisation actually does and what the role involves day to day, not just the job title. Beyond that, I&#x27;d prepare specific examples rather than general claims: saying you work well under pressure means nothing, whereas describing a situation where you did is convincing. Some rehearsal helps with nerves, but there&#x27;s a risk of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sounding memorised</strong>, so I&#x27;d practise the examples rather than scripting entire answers.\n\nQ: Which jobs do you think are the most dangerous?\nA: Mining and deep-sea fishing are consistently at the top of the statistics, along with construction, logging and firefighting. The common factors are heavy machinery, unstable environments and long shifts, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fatigue is where most accidents begin</strong>. What&#x27;s often overlooked is that danger isn&#x27;t always immediate, though — jobs involving chemicals, dust or constant noise can cause serious illness twenty years later, and those cases never make it into the annual accident figures that get all the attention.\n\nQ: How can employers keep their staff happy?\nA: Fair pay is the starting point, but it doesn&#x27;t buy satisfaction on its own — it just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removes a reason to leave</strong>. What matters more is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a manageable workload</strong>, being treated with respect, and having some genuine control over how you do the work. Opportunities to develop matter too, because people want to feel they&#x27;re getting better at something. Superficial perks definitely don&#x27;t work, though — a table-tennis table cannot <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compensate for</strong> a bad manager.","topic":"Work","phrases":[{"en":"sounding memorised","context":"Q: What is the best way to prepare for a job interview?\nA: Research is the foundation — understanding what the organisation actually does and what the role involves day to day, not just the job title. Beyond that, I'd prepare specific examples rather than general claims: saying you work well under pressure means nothing, whereas describing a situation where you did is convincing. Some rehearsal helps with nerves, but there's a risk of sounding memorised, so I'd practise the examples rather than scripting entire answers."},{"en":"fatigue is where most accidents begin","context":"Q: Which jobs do you think are the most dangerous?\nA: Mining and deep-sea fishing are consistently at the top of the statistics, along with construction, logging and firefighting. The common factors are heavy machinery, unstable environments and long shifts, since fatigue is where most accidents begin. What's often overlooked is that danger isn't always immediate, though — jobs involving chemicals, dust or constant noise can cause serious illness twenty years later, and those cases never make it into the annual accident figures that get all the attention."},{"en":"removes a reason to leave","context":"Q: How can employers keep their staff happy?\nA: Fair pay is the starting point, but it doesn't buy satisfaction on its own — it just removes a reason to leave. What matters more is a manageable workload, being treated with respect, and having some genuine control over how you do the work. Opportunities to develop matter too, because people want to feel they're getting better at something. Superficial perks definitely don't work, though — a table-tennis table cannot compensate for a bad manager."},{"en":"a manageable workload","context":"Q: How can employers keep their staff happy?\nA: Fair pay is the starting point, but it doesn't buy satisfaction on its own — it just removes a reason to leave. What matters more is a manageable workload, being treated with respect, and having some genuine control over how you do the work. Opportunities to develop matter too, because people want to feel they're getting better at something. Superficial perks definitely don't work, though — a table-tennis table cannot compensate for a bad manager."},{"en":"compensate for","context":"Q: How can employers keep their staff happy?\nA: Fair pay is the starting point, but it doesn't buy satisfaction on its own — it just removes a reason to leave. What matters more is a manageable workload, being treated with respect, and having some genuine control over how you do the work. Opportunities to develop matter too, because people want to feel they're getting better at something. Superficial perks definitely don't work, though — a table-tennis table cannot compensate for a bad manager."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Work","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-095","_displayNo":"#095","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-095-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-095-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-095-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Research the organisation and the actual work first. Then prepare specific examples showing your skills, rather than simply saying you are good under pressure. Practice can reduce nerves, but learning whole answers by heart may sound unnatural. I would rehearse explaining real examples clearly while leaving enough freedom to answer the question actually asked.","phrases":[{"en":"rehearse","meaning":"Practise something before a real event or performance."}],"question":"What is the best way to prepare for a job interview?","html":"Research the organisation and the actual work first. Then prepare specific examples showing your skills, rather than simply saying you are good under pressure. Practice can reduce nerves, but learning whole answers by heart may sound unnatural. I would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rehearse</strong> explaining real examples clearly while leaving enough freedom to answer the question actually asked."},{"text":"Mining, fishing, construction and firefighting can be very dangerous because of machinery, difficult conditions and tiredness from long shifts. However, danger is not always immediate. Dust, chemicals or constant noise can cause illness much later. Those slower harms may receive less attention than accidents, even though they can seriously affect workers' lives.","phrases":[{"en":"long shifts","meaning":"Extended periods spent working before a break or time off."}],"question":"Which jobs do you think are the most dangerous?","html":"Mining, fishing, construction and firefighting can be very dangerous because of machinery, difficult conditions and tiredness from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long shifts</strong>. However, danger is not always immediate. Dust, chemicals or constant noise can cause illness much later. Those slower harms may receive less attention than accidents, even though they can seriously affect workers&#x27; lives."},{"text":"Fair pay matters, but people also need respect, a manageable workload and some control over how they work. Chances to learn and improve can make a difference too. Small perks cannot make up for poor management. A workplace is more satisfying when everyday conditions are good, rather than when it offers entertainment while ignoring staff's real problems.","phrases":[{"en":"manageable workload","meaning":"An amount of work someone can reasonably complete."}],"question":"How can employers keep their staff happy?","html":"Fair pay matters, but people also need respect, a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">manageable workload</strong> and some control over how they work. Chances to learn and improve can make a difference too. Small perks cannot make up for poor management. A workplace is more satisfying when everyday conditions are good, rather than when it offers entertainment while ignoring staff&#x27;s real problems."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-095-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-095-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-095-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How computers changed writing, and the future of it","prompt":"What effect have computers had on the way we write?\nWhat are the advantages and disadvantages of email?\nWill writing remain an important skill in the future?","answer":"Q: What effect have computers had on the way we write?\nA: The biggest change is that writing became revisable. On paper, restructuring a piece meant copying the whole thing out again, so people planned carefully before they started. Now you can rearrange anything instantly, which is liberating but does encourage <strong class=\"key-phrase\">writing before thinking</strong>. Sharing and searching have transformed collaboration as well. The loss is at the level of detail, though — with autocorrect handling spelling, a lot of people have simply stopped noticing their own errors.\n\nQ: What are the advantages and disadvantages of email?\nA: It&#x27;s fast, essentially free, and creates <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a searchable record</strong>, which is genuinely valuable when you need to establish what was agreed. The disadvantages are mostly about tone and volume. Written messages strip out the signals that stop a conversation going wrong, so something meant neutrally reads as cold. And because sending costs nothing, people get copied into everything, so important messages <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get lost among dozens</strong> that never needed to exist in the first place.\n\nQ: Will writing remain an important skill in the future?\nA: More important, not less, I&#x27;d say. Artificial intelligence can produce <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a competent draft</strong>, but somebody still has to judge whether it&#x27;s accurate, whether the argument holds and whether it says what was actually intended — a manager still has to notice when an AI-drafted report gets a figure wrong or misses the actual point, and you can&#x27;t judge that if you can&#x27;t write yourself. There&#x27;s a deeper point too: writing something down is how you discover whether you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely understand it</strong>. That said, for routine writing — a quick email, a simple summary — nobody&#x27;s going to miss doing it by hand, so the skill that&#x27;ll matter is judgement, not typing practice.","topic":"Writing","phrases":[{"en":"writing before thinking","context":"Q: What effect have computers had on the way we write?\nA: The biggest change is that writing became revisable. On paper, restructuring a piece meant copying the whole thing out again, so people planned carefully before they started. Now you can rearrange anything instantly, which is liberating but does encourage writing before thinking. Sharing and searching have transformed collaboration as well. The loss is at the level of detail, though — with autocorrect handling spelling, a lot of people have simply stopped noticing their own errors."},{"en":"a searchable record","context":"Q: What are the advantages and disadvantages of email?\nA: It's fast, essentially free, and creates a searchable record, which is genuinely valuable when you need to establish what was agreed. The disadvantages are mostly about tone and volume. Written messages strip out the signals that stop a conversation going wrong, so something meant neutrally reads as cold. And because sending costs nothing, people get copied into everything, so important messages get lost among dozens that never needed to exist in the first place."},{"en":"get lost among dozens","context":"Q: What are the advantages and disadvantages of email?\nA: It's fast, essentially free, and creates a searchable record, which is genuinely valuable when you need to establish what was agreed. The disadvantages are mostly about tone and volume. Written messages strip out the signals that stop a conversation going wrong, so something meant neutrally reads as cold. And because sending costs nothing, people get copied into everything, so important messages get lost among dozens that never needed to exist in the first place."},{"en":"a competent draft","context":"Q: Will writing remain an important skill in the future?\nA: More important, not less, I'd say. Artificial intelligence can produce a competent draft, but somebody still has to judge whether it's accurate, whether the argument holds and whether it says what was actually intended — a manager still has to notice when an AI-drafted report gets a figure wrong or misses the actual point, and you can't judge that if you can't write yourself. There's a deeper point too: writing something down is how you discover whether you genuinely understand it. That said, for routine writing — a quick email, a simple summary — nobody's going to miss doing it by hand, so the skill that'll matter is judgement, not typing practice."},{"en":"genuinely understand it","context":"Q: Will writing remain an important skill in the future?\nA: More important, not less, I'd say. Artificial intelligence can produce a competent draft, but somebody still has to judge whether it's accurate, whether the argument holds and whether it says what was actually intended — a manager still has to notice when an AI-drafted report gets a figure wrong or misses the actual point, and you can't judge that if you can't write yourself. There's a deeper point too: writing something down is how you discover whether you genuinely understand it. That said, for routine writing — a quick email, a simple summary — nobody's going to miss doing it by hand, so the skill that'll matter is judgement, not typing practice."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Writing","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-096","_displayNo":"#096","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-096-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-096-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-096-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Computers make editing and reorganising writing much easier. People no longer need to copy everything again to change a structure. Sharing and searching also help people work together. However, easy editing may encourage starting before thinking, and automatic spelling correction can make people less aware of mistakes. The tools help, but careful attention is still needed.","phrases":[{"en":"editing","meaning":"Changing written work to improve or correct it."}],"question":"What effect have computers had on the way we write?","html":"Computers make <strong class=\"key-phrase\">editing</strong> and reorganising writing much easier. People no longer need to copy everything again to change a structure. Sharing and searching also help people work together. However, easy editing may encourage starting before thinking, and automatic spelling correction can make people less aware of mistakes. The tools help, but careful attention is still needed."},{"text":"Email is fast, cheap and creates a record that can be searched later. This is useful for checking agreements. However, written tone can be misunderstood, and too many unnecessary messages can hide important information. Copying everyone into every conversation increases that problem. Email works best when people think about both the wording and who actually needs the message.","phrases":[{"en":"wording","meaning":"The particular words chosen to express something."}],"question":"What are the advantages and disadvantages of email?","html":"Email is fast, cheap and creates a record that can be searched later. This is useful for checking agreements. However, written tone can be misunderstood, and too many unnecessary messages can hide important information. Copying everyone into every conversation increases that problem. Email works best when people think about both the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wording</strong> and who actually needs the message."},{"text":"Yes, because people still need to judge whether writing is clear and accurate, even when AI produces a first draft. Someone must notice a wrong number or weak argument. Writing also helps us discover whether we understand an idea ourselves. Routine typing may become less important, but the ability to organise and evaluate meaning will remain valuable.","phrases":[{"en":"evaluate","meaning":"Examine something carefully to judge its quality or usefulness."}],"question":"Will writing remain an important skill in the future?","html":"Yes, because people still need to judge whether writing is clear and accurate, even when AI produces a first draft. Someone must notice a wrong number or weak argument. Writing also helps us discover whether we understand an idea ourselves. Routine typing may become less important, but the ability to organise and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">evaluate</strong> meaning will remain valuable."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-096-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-096-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-096-q03.mp3"]}]);
